window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"rem-murmur":["Twelve songs in about forty-four minutes, built on Peter Buck's ringing, arpeggiated Rickenbacker, Mike Mills's melodic bass and Bill Berry's light, tidy drumming. Over the top, Michael Stipe sings half-buried in the mix, vowels smeared so that the words become texture. 'Radio Free Europe' and 'Talk About the Passion' are the handholds, but the record's identity is its haze: acoustic guitars, piano and backing harmonies layered into a warm, humid Southern murk that sounds both old and new.","R.E.M. formed in Athens, Georgia, in 1980, a college town whose scene also produced the B-52's. After a self-released single on the tiny Hib-Tone label and the Chronic Town EP, they recorded their debut album for I.R.S. Records with Mitch Easter and Don Dixon in Charlotte, North Carolina, early in 1983. The band had resisted the label's attempt to pair them with a slicker producer. In a year dominated by synthesisers and MTV, a guitar band from the South singing indistinctly was an unlikely proposition.","Listen to how often Mills's bass carries the actual tune while Buck plays chords around it, and how his high backing vocal sings a second, different melody against Stipe's. The guitar rarely takes a solo; Buck said he disliked them. 'Radio Free Europe' is a re-recording of the Hib-Tone single, faster and cleaner, and comparing the two shows how deliberately Easter and Dixon softened the band into something dreamier.","Rolling Stone named Murmur its album of the year for 1983, ahead of Michael Jackson's Thriller, which says much about how critics were hungry for an alternative. It sold modestly at first but became the founding document of American college rock, the network of campus radio stations and small clubs that R.E.M. toured relentlessly. Arguments about what Stipe was actually singing have never been settled; he has suggested that was partly the point.","Start with 'Perfect Circle', the slow song with two pianos, where the fog is at its most beautiful. Then play 'Radio Free Europe' loud and notice how the bass pushes the song forward. Don't strain to catch the words; let the voice work as another instrument. Afterwards, hear the Byrds' jangle it inherited and the Smiths' Hatful of Hollow from across the Atlantic."],"rem-automatic-for-the-people":["Twelve songs, mostly slow, mostly acoustic, about ageing, loss and memory. Mandolin, piano, accordion and a string section replace the electric jangle; 'Drive' opens in a hush, 'Everybody Hurts' builds from an arpeggio in the manner of an old soul ballad, and 'Nightswimming' is just piano, strings and voice recalling teenage summers. Only 'Ignoreland' and 'Man on the Moon', a song about the comedian Andy Kaufman, raise the pulse. It is a quiet record made by the biggest band in the world.","After Out of Time (1991) and 'Losing My Religion' had made R.E.M. global stars, the band set out to make a harder rock album and ended up with the opposite. Recorded in several studios including Criteria in Miami and Bearsville in New York, with Scott Litt producing, it drew string arrangements for several songs from John Paul Jones, once of Led Zeppelin. The title comes from the slogan of a soul-food restaurant in Athens, Georgia. The band did not tour it.","'Nightswimming' was built on a circling piano figure by Mike Mills; listen for the oboe that enters late and shadows the strings, one of the few wind sounds on the record. Listen to how 'Man on the Moon' quotes Elvis's vocal hiccups and wanders through Kaufman's world of wrestling and hoaxes. The record's closing trio moves from Kaufman's death-defying jokes to a funeral-quiet goodbye.","Released in October 1992, just as grunge was peaking, it was received as a brave swerve and sold many millions. Many critics and the band members themselves came to rank it as their finest record. 'Everybody Hurts' took on a long second life as a song of comfort, used in suicide-prevention campaigns. Some early fans missed the Southern haze of the 1980s albums; the album's stature has grown steadily since.","Hear it in order, at night. Notice how 'Drive' refuses to explode, and how 'Everybody Hurts' uses the simplest means to reach people at their lowest. 'Nightswimming' is the centre; listen to the piano and strings and the way Stipe's voice is, for once, perfectly clear. Compare Nick Drake's Bryter Layter and its string-lit melancholy."],"pixies-surfer-rosa":["Thirteen short songs, raw and room-shaped: David Lovering's drums sound as though recorded in a concrete hall, Joey Santiago's guitar squeals and scrapes, Kim Deal's bass is plain and propulsive, and Black Francis switches from a croon to a throat-tearing scream within a line. 'Bone Machine', 'Where Is My Mind?', 'Gigantic' (sung by Deal) and 'Cactus' are its landmarks. Studio chatter and giggles are left between songs, so the record feels like overhearing a band at work.","Pixies formed in Boston in 1986 after Charles Thompson (Black Francis) and Santiago met at university. Their first full album was recorded in about ten days late in 1987 by Steve Albini, then of the band Big Black, who disliked the term producer and aimed simply to capture the band in a room. The British label 4AD released it in March 1988; in America it was an import. Spanish phrases and surreal images drew on Francis's time as a student in Puerto Rico.","The lyrics mix Catholic imagery, UFOs, sex and violence into a private mythology; Francis has said much of it is sound as much as sense. Listen for the ambient spill: Albini placed microphones to pick up the room itself, which is why the snare explodes. 'Gigantic', co-written by Deal and Francis, is the only Pixies single led by her voice, and the one that hints at the Breeders she would later form.","It barely sold at first but became a musicians' record. Kurt Cobain said it was the model for Nirvana's sound and hired Albini for In Utero because of it. 'Where Is My Mind?' found a mass audience when it closed the film Fight Club in 1999. Albini publicly disparaged the band not long after, which only adds to the legend. It is now widely treated as one of the formative American rock albums of the decade.","Play 'Bone Machine' first and notice the drum sound before anything else. Then 'Where Is My Mind?': the ghostly wordless voice floating over the chords is Kim Deal. Listen for the lurch from whisper to howl in almost every song, then play Nirvana's In Utero, made by the same engineer five years later."],"pixies-doolittle":["Fifteen songs, most under three minutes, that alternate verses sung almost under the breath with choruses that crash in at full volume. 'Debaser' opens with a bass line and a manic shout; 'Wave of Mutilation' is surf rock gone strange; 'Here Comes Your Man' is a sunny pop song; 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' adds cellos and violins to a song about the environment and numerology. It is tighter, brighter and more melodic than Surfer Rosa without losing its oddness.","Recorded in late 1988 in Boston with the English producer Gil Norton, who pushed the band towards cleaner, shorter arrangements, much to Black Francis's irritation. It was released in April 1989 by 4AD in Britain and Elektra in America, the band's first major-label distribution there. Vaughan Oliver's sepia cover and booklet, pairing each song with an image, were part of 4AD's house style. Tensions between Francis and Kim Deal were already growing.","'Debaser' is a song about the 1929 surrealist short film Un Chien Andalou by Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí, which Francis admired. 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' assigns the numbers five, six and seven to man, the devil and God, a bit of home-made numerology dropped into an environmental song. Listen to how often the band stops dead just before a chorus, so that silence becomes the loudest thing in the song.","It reached the UK top ten and became Pixies' best-selling record, a college-radio standard in America. Kurt Cobain admitted that 'Smells Like Teen Spirit' was his attempt to write a Pixies song, borrowing the quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. Critics still debate whether Norton's polish improved the band or tamed it; many rank Doolittle and Surfer Rosa as a pair rather than choose.","Play 'Debaser', 'Tame' and 'Hey' in a row and track the dynamic switch each time. 'Tame' is the loud-quiet idea in its purest form, breath and scream alternating. Then put on Nevermind's first track and hear the template reused. Vinyl or the 2014 remaster both keep the dry, punchy sound."],"sonic-youth-evol":["Nine songs where noise begins to take the shape of pop. Guitars tuned to odd open chords chime, drone and clang; 'Tom Violence' and 'Shadow of a Doubt' drift like dream sequences, while 'Starpower', sung by Kim Gordon, is nearly a pop song with a warped undertow. The record ends with 'Expressway to Yr Skull', a long build that collapses into feedback. Compared with their earlier records, the atmosphere is spookier and more cinematic.","Sonic Youth came out of New York's downtown art and no-wave scene of the early 1980s. EVOL was their first album for the Californian punk label SST and the first with drummer Steve Shelley, whose steadier playing gave the songs a spine. Its imagery drew on B-movies and underground film: the cover is a still of Lydia Lunch from a Richard Kern film. The title is love spelled backwards.","On the original vinyl, 'Expressway to Yr Skull' ends in a locked groove, so the final drone repeats until the listener lifts the needle; the sleeve listed its duration as infinite. The guitar tunings were often achieved with cheap instruments and screwdrivers or drumsticks jammed under the strings, making each guitar a prepared instrument in the manner of Cage's prepared piano.","Received in the underground press as a step towards songcraft without surrendering noise. It sold modestly but established the band's college-radio following and the growing respect of musicians. Critics now tend to hear it as the start of the run that led to Daydream Nation, with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' as a live showpiece that anticipated much of the post-rock of the next decade.","Start with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and listen to how chords are made from guitars that ring in sympathy rather than being played in the usual way. Then try 'Starpower' for the pop side. Hear it alongside Glenn Branca's The Ascension to catch the harmonic clouds Thurston Moore and Lee Ranaldo absorbed in his guitar orchestras."],"sonic-youth-daydream-nation":["A double album of fourteen songs and more than seventy minutes, built on two guitars in unusual tunings that shimmer, grind and chime in a way no standard-tuned band can match. 'Teen Age Riot' opens with Kim Gordon murmuring before a clean, driving riff kicks in; 'Silver Rocket' breaks into a tunnel of noise; 'The Sprawl' and ''Cross the Breeze' stretch out; the closing 'Trilogy' runs together three linked pieces. Long instrumental passages feel like weather moving over the city.","Recorded in the summer of 1988 in New York with Nick Sansano and released that October on the independent Enigma label, it was the band's last album before signing to Geffen. It caught New York in the late Reagan years: the Lower East Side, suburban sprawl, the underground's contempt for mainstream culture. The cover reproduces Gerhard Richter's painting of a single candle, a strikingly still image for such loud music.","'Teen Age Riot' imagines a fantasy America in which J Mascis of Dinosaur Jr is president. The inner sleeves assign each member a symbol, a knowing joke at Led Zeppelin's fourth album. Listen to the guitars: each song often needed its own instrument set to a different tuning, sometimes with objects under the strings, so the band toured with a rack of guitars. The harmonies you hear are frequently overtones rather than played notes.","Critics received it as the peak of American underground rock, and it topped many year-end polls. It has since been added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry. Some find its length self-indulgent; others argue the long, drifting passages are precisely the point, where rock borrows the time scale of minimalism. Its influence on shoegaze, post-rock and indie guitar has been wide and lasting.","Start with 'Teen Age Riot' and listen to how the clean guitar melody rides above a bed of ringing overtones. Then play 'The Sprawl' through to its long instrumental coda. Compare Terry Riley's In C or La Monte Young's drones, which Moore and Ranaldo knew well, and notice how both use repetition to let harmonics bloom."],"glenn-branca-the-ascension":["Five instrumental pieces for a small guitar orchestra: four electric guitars, bass and drums. The guitars are strummed hard and fast on strings often tuned to a single pitch or a few, so that dense waves of sound build and pile up until the overtones begin to sing on their own. Pieces such as 'Lesson No. 2' and the title track climb steadily in intensity for many minutes, more like a slow, loud chord change than a rock song.","Branca came to New York from Pennsylvania in the mid-1970s through experimental theatre and the no-wave scene, where punk energy met gallery art. He formed bands and then larger ensembles of electric guitars, playing in clubs rather than concert halls. This first full album appeared on the small 99 Records label in 1981, with a cover drawing by the artist Robert Longo. His players included Lee Ranaldo, later of Sonic Youth; Thurston Moore also played in Branca's ensembles.","Many of the harmonies you hear are not being played by anyone: when many loud guitars ring on related pitches, the overtones combine to produce phantom tones and choirs. Branca rethought the guitar as a source of the harmonic series, a thread that runs from La Monte Young's sustained tones. Listen to the title track's last minutes, where the ear begins to hear bells that no one struck.","Rock critics heard it as a noise record; classical listeners argued over whether it belonged to minimalism. Branca went on to write symphonies for up to a hundred guitars, and John Cage reportedly found one of them oppressive, a notorious exchange that shows how far Branca stood from Cage's cool. Its main legacy runs through the players who passed through his ensembles, above all Sonic Youth.","Play the title track loud, sit still and wait for the phantom tones to appear. Then play Sonic Youth's 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and hear the idea shrunk to rock-song scale. Compare Reich's Drumming, where repetition also lets new patterns emerge that nobody is playing."],"husker-du-zen-arcade":["A double album of twenty-three tracks that follows, loosely, a boy who leaves an unhappy home and finds the outside world no better. Hardcore punk at breakneck speed sits beside acoustic songs, a piano interlude, backwards tapes and the fourteen-minute instrumental 'Reoccurring Dreams' that closes it. Bob Mould's guitar is a roaring wall of distortion that still carries melody; Grant Hart's drumming is frantic yet tuneful. 'Pink Turns to Blue' and 'Something I Learned Today' are the easiest ways in.","Hüsker Dü came from Minneapolis and toured constantly on the American hardcore circuit. They recorded Zen Arcade for SST in a few days in 1983, mostly in single takes because money and time were short. It appeared in July 1984, the same month SST released the Minutemen's double album Double Nickels on the Dime. A punk band making a double concept album was close to heresy in a scene that despised excess.","Most of the songs are first takes, which is why small mistakes and bleed survive; the band valued momentum over polish. The story ends by revealing that the whole journey was a dream, which is why the long instrumental is titled as it is. Hart and Mould split the songwriting, and learning to tell their voices apart is part of the fun: Hart the melodist, Mould the storm.","Critics outside punk took notice, and it won unusually strong reviews for a hardcore record, proof that the form could hold ambition. Some punks found it pretentious. Its influence has been long: melodic songs buried in distortion anticipate the Pixies, Nirvana and a whole generation of emotional punk. Many listeners find the story vague; the emotional arc carries the record better than the plot.","Try 'Something I Learned Today', then the Hart-written 'Pink Turns to Blue', to hear the melodies inside the noise. Then take the whole second side as a single sweep. Compare the Who's rock operas or Pink Floyd's concept records, and notice how Hüsker Dü does it in half the studio time."],"the-smiths-how-soon-is-now":["Almost seven minutes of throbbing, swampy guitar. Johnny Marr's rhythm part pulses on a heavy tremolo effect, bass and drums lock into a slow, hypnotic groove, and eerie slide-guitar harmonics wail above like sirens. Morrissey sings calmly, almost conversationally, about crippling shyness and a night at a club where no one talks to him. It sounds nothing like the jangle the Smiths were known for: dark, heavy and circular.","The Smiths, formed in Manchester in 1982, were the defining band of British independent rock in the 1980s, signed to Rough Trade. The song was recorded in 1984 with the producer John Porter and first appeared as a B-side, then on the compilation Hatful of Hollow that November, before Rough Trade issued it as a single early in 1985. Many felt it was wasted as a flip-side.","The tremolo effect was created by running the guitar through several amplifiers and matching their wobble to the song's tempo, a laborious process in a pre-digital studio; Marr has linked the idea to the shimmering tremolo of Bo Diddley. The wailing guitar is several slide parts layered with harmonics. The song's quiet, steady vocal against the churning music is its whole drama.","As a single it reached only the UK top thirty, partly because fans already owned it, but it became their most famous song in America, a staple of college and alternative radio, and a cover version later became a television theme. The band itself was divided about it. It is often cited as the moment British indie absorbed dance-floor hypnosis; its guitar sound has been endlessly analysed and imitated.","Listen on headphones to separate the layers: the throbbing rhythm guitar in the centre, the slide wailing at the edges, the bass pulling the whole thing forward. Then compare Bo Diddley's 'Who Do You Love?' and hear the old tremolo idea turned into a haunted engine."],"the-smiths-the-queen-is-dead":["Ten songs that swing from fury to tenderness to broad comedy. The title track storms in on Mike Joyce's rolling tom-toms and a wah-wah guitar; 'Bigmouth Strikes Again' gallops; 'I Know It's Over' is a slow, bleak ballad; 'Cemetry Gates' and 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' are witty and light. The close, 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', pairs a lush string arrangement with a romantic and morbid fantasy. Johnny Marr's guitars shimmer throughout; Andy Rourke's bass is unusually melodic.","Recorded in 1985 with engineer Stephen Street, the album was delayed for months by a legal battle between the Smiths and Rough Trade, and released in June 1986. Britain was in the middle of Thatcherism, the miners' strike just ended; Morrissey's title and lyrics jabbed at monarchy, the Church and the music industry. Rourke was fighting addiction and was briefly sacked during this period.","The album opens with a brief fragment of Cicely Courtneidge singing a wartime music-hall song, taken from the 1962 film The L-Shaped Room. The strings on 'There Is a Light' and elsewhere were played by Marr on an Emulator sampling keyboard, not by an orchestra, despite the band's anti-synthesiser reputation. The cover photograph shows the French actor Alain Delon.","Reaching number two in Britain, it was hailed on release and has regularly topped lists of the greatest British albums. Morrissey's later public statements have made the record harder for some to love, a debate about separating art from artist that continues. Musically, the consensus is firm: it is the Smiths' most complete statement, balancing Marr's arrangements with Morrissey's wit.","Start with 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', the song most fans name first. Then go back to the title track and notice how the drums, bass and guitar feedback make a single tidal sound. Enjoy the humour: 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' is a music-hall number at heart. Compare the Kinks' Village Green for a different vision of English decline."],"my-bloody-valentine-isnt-anything":["Twelve songs of warped, woozy guitar rock. Kevin Shields's guitar bends in and out of tune in slow waves; the male and female voices, his and Bilinda Butcher's, are sung softly and mixed low; Colm Ó Cíosóig's drums are fast and busy. 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)', 'Lose My Breath' and 'Feed Me with Your Kiss' show the range, from dreamy drift to punk ferocity. The sound is sensual and disorienting, like hearing music through a fever.","My Bloody Valentine, formed in Dublin and settled in London, spent several years as a fairly conventional indie band before the 1988 EP You Made Me Realise introduced their new sound. Isn't Anything, their first full album for Alan McGee's Creation label, was recorded that year in Wales. Its release in November 1988 pointed the way towards the scene the British music press would call shoegaze.","Shields had begun to develop his 'glide guitar' technique, strumming while holding the tremolo arm so that every chord slides slightly off pitch. The effect is audible everywhere here, but not yet as dominant as on Loveless. Listen for how the vocals are pushed back so far that they sit inside the guitars, making the singers part of the texture rather than the focus.","The British weeklies received it with enthusiasm, and the album has stayed in the shadow of Loveless while being admired in its own right; some fans prefer its rawer, more rhythmic energy. Musicians in the early 1990s treated it as a toolbox. Its reputation grew further when Loveless took years to follow and the band vanished for two decades.","Put on 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)' and listen for the queasy pitch-bending of the guitar. Then 'Feed Me with Your Kiss', which is far louder and faster. Compare Dinosaur Jr's You're Living All Over Me, which Shields admired, and hear how two bands made fuzz tender."],"my-bloody-valentine-loveless":["Eleven songs in which guitars melt into a single, swooning mass of sound. The chords seem to warp and bend like vinyl left in the sun; voices float above or inside, almost wordless; drums are often loops. 'Only Shallow' opens with a blast of noise, 'When You Sleep' is a woozy pop song, 'To Here Knows When' dissolves into a haze, and 'Soon', driven by a dance beat, ends the album in euphoria. It is loud but strangely soft.","Recorded between 1989 and 1991 in a long list of studios, as Kevin Shields searched for exactly the sounds he heard in his head, Loveless became notorious for its cost to Creation Records; figures of around a quarter of a million pounds have been quoted, though Shields disputes them. It came out in November 1991 at the high point of the British shoegaze scene. The band then went silent for over twenty years.","Many of the album's drum parts are samples and loops rather than live playing, because Ó Cíosóig was ill and unsettled for much of the sessions. Shields also sampled his own feedback and played it back on keyboards, so some of what sounds like guitar is a sampler. Butcher's vocals were reportedly often recorded soon after she woke, which helps explain their sleepy intimacy. Listen closely and the bending chords are always moving.","Critics were dazzled, but sales could not cover the cost, and Creation dropped the band. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now widely ranked among the most influential albums of its era, a touchstone for shoegaze, dream pop and ambient-leaning rock. Its absence of successors for twenty-two years made it a legend. Arguments continue about the best mastering; Shields supervised remasters in 2012 and 2021.","Play 'Only Shallow' loud and let the noise wash over you, then 'To Here Knows When' at lower volume, when it seems to breathe. Don't try to find words. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères or Eno's Discreet Music, where sound also becomes a slowly shifting cloud; Loveless is their rock cousin."],"slint-spiderland":["Six long songs that move between near silence and sudden eruptions. Clean, interlocking guitar figures repeat and shift in odd rhythmic patterns; Brian McMahan speaks or whispers stories more than sings them; bass and drums hold back until the music explodes. 'Breadcrumb Trail' describes a day at a carnival, 'Nosferatu Man' is jagged and heavy, and 'Good Morning, Captain', a sea story in fragments, ends in a scream. The mood is tense, lonely and unresolved.","Slint were four young musicians from Louisville, Kentucky, who emerged from the local hardcore scene. They recorded Spiderland with producer Brian Paulson in Chicago in 1990, and it was released by the Touch and Go label in March 1991. By then the band had already split up, so there was no tour, no press campaign and very little explanation. The cover photograph, taken by their friend Will Oldham, shows the members' heads above water in a quarry.","The songs are carefully composed, not jammed: guitar parts in unusual metres that fit together like clockwork, with repeats that change by one note or beat. The spoken passages are mixed very low, so you have to lean in, which makes the explosions more shocking. The sleeve included a brief notice seeking female vocalists, a quiet hint of the band's direction had it continued.","Barely noticed on release, it built a reputation by word of mouth and is now regarded as a founding text of post-rock and math rock, a word-of-mouth classic of the kind that the pre-internet decade produced. Critics have debated whether its bleakness is a pose or authentic adolescent unease. Bands such as Mogwai have cited it directly, and it was reissued in a large box set in 2014.","Play it from the start in a quiet room at a volume that lets you hear the whispers, and let the climaxes arrive as a shock. Focus on 'Good Morning, Captain': notice how long it waits before the full band comes in. Compare King Crimson's Red for another heavy, angular instrumental language."],"nirvana-smells-like-teen-spirit":["Five minutes built on four power chords. Kurt Cobain plays the riff alone, clean, before Dave Grohl's drums crash in and the band goes full volume; the verses drop to a quiet, sly bass-and-guitar pattern; the chorus explodes again with Cobain's voice shredding. A strange, sloppy guitar solo simply repeats the vocal melody. The lyric is a mocking jumble of apathy and slogans, hard to decode and designed to be.","Released as the first single from Nevermind in September 1991, the song was expected to be a college-radio track. MTV put its video, directed by Samuel Bayer and set at a chaotic high-school pep rally, on heavy rotation, and the song went global. Its title came from graffiti a friend, Kathleen Hanna of Bikini Kill, scrawled on Cobain's wall, referring to a deodorant brand he didn't know existed.","Cobain said he was trying to write a Pixies song, taking their quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. The guitar solo is almost exactly the vocal melody, a punk refusal of virtuosity. Listen to Krist Novoselic's bass in the verses: it carries the song, while the guitar plays just two scraped notes. Cobain also joked about the riff's resemblance to Boston's 'More Than a Feeling'.","It reached the top ten in the US and is widely held to have ended hair metal and brought alternative rock to the mainstream in one stroke. Cobain grew to resent it and at times refused to play it. Rolling Stone and others have placed it near the top of lists of the greatest songs. Its fame has made it easy to underrate how odd and ironic it actually is.","Listen for the switch from the clean opening to the full band at about seven seconds. Then focus on the verses: how little happens, and how much tension that creates. Compare 'Debaser' or 'Tame' from Pixies' Doolittle to hear the model, and notice what Nirvana added: heavier drums and a much bigger chorus."],"nirvana-nevermind":["Twelve songs of punk energy and pop melody, with 'Smells Like Teen Spirit', 'In Bloom', 'Come as You Are' and 'Lithium' making up the first half. Dave Grohl's drums are huge and precise, Krist Novoselic's bass is solid, and Kurt Cobain's guitar is often clean and chorused in the verses and heavily distorted in the choruses. 'Polly' is acoustic, 'Territorial Pissings' pure punk, and 'Something in the Way' closes with hushed guitar and cello.","Recorded at Sound City in Van Nuys in May 1991 with producer Butch Vig and mixed by Andy Wallace for Geffen's DGC label, the album aimed modestly to sell a few hundred thousand copies. Nirvana came from Aberdeen and Olympia, Washington, and had released Bleach on Sub Pop in 1989; Grohl had joined only months earlier. The album arrived in September 1991 and knocked Michael Jackson's Dangerous from number one on the American chart in January 1992.","'Something in the Way' was recorded with Cobain lying on a couch in the control room, because he could not get the quiet feel he wanted in the live room. Later pressings contain a hidden track, 'Endless, Nameless', a chaotic noise jam that begins after about ten minutes of silence after the last song. 'Polly' is a disturbing story sung from a criminal's point of view, handled with unsettling calm.","Its success reshaped the industry, sending labels searching for Seattle bands and turning underground rock into the mainstream. Cobain later complained that the mix was too slick, comparing it to a commercial record, and turned to Steve Albini for the next album. Debates about whether its polish betrayed punk continue, but its songwriting is widely admired, and it sits near the top of most greatest-albums lists.","Hear the first four tracks together to understand the album's impact, then skip to 'Drain You', which Cobain named as a favourite. Notice how nearly every song follows the quiet-loud template, yet each has a distinct melody. Afterwards play Pixies' Doolittle and Hüsker Dü's Zen Arcade to hear what Cobain loved."],"nirvana-in-utero":["Twelve songs, rawer and angrier than Nevermind. Grohl's drums sound enormous and live in the room, the guitars are more jagged, and Cobain's voice swings between despair and fury. 'Heart-Shaped Box' is slow and heavy, 'Scentless Apprentice' is a relentless assault, 'Dumb' and 'All Apologies' add cello and tenderness, and 'Pennyroyal Tea' is a quiet song that erupts. The songs mix confessional pain with sardonic comments on fame and the press.","After the vast success of Nevermind, Cobain wanted to return to a rougher sound and hired Steve Albini, who had made Pixies' Surfer Rosa. The band recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Cannon Falls, Minnesota, in February 1993, working quickly. Their label worried the record was uncommercial, and two songs were remixed by R.E.M.'s producer Scott Litt. The album was released in September 1993, less than a year before Cobain's death.","Albini recorded the band live and placed many microphones around the drums to capture the room; Grohl's snare seems to explode because of it. Cobain's lyrics refer to his medical troubles, the pressures of fame and his marriage. Some retailers stocked a version with a title altered on the back cover. 'Frances Farmer Will Have Her Revenge on Seattle' takes up the story of the Hollywood actress mistreated by her home city.","Released amid public argument between band, label and producer over its sound, it reached number one and has since been praised as Nirvana's bravest record, often preferred by critics to Nevermind. Albini's original mixes, later released on anniversary editions, are still debated against Litt's remixes. Cobain's death in April 1994 made the album's themes of pain and exposure impossible to hear neutrally.","Start with 'Heart-Shaped Box' and notice how the verses creep while the chorus explodes. Then 'All Apologies', whose cello makes it a farewell. Compare Pixies' Surfer Rosa for the same engineer's room sound, and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me, recorded at the same studio months earlier."],"nirvana-mtv-unplugged-in-new-york":["Fourteen songs recorded live in one set, acoustic and hushed. Nirvana play only a few of their hits, choosing instead deep cuts and covers: David Bowie's 'The Man Who Sold the World', the Vaselines' 'Jesus Doesn't Want Me for a Sunbeam', three songs by the Meat Puppets with two of that band's members joining, and, to finish, the traditional song Lead Belly made famous, 'Where Did You Sleep Last Night'. Grohl plays with brushes, a cellist adds weight, and Cobain's voice cracks and roars.","Recorded on 18 November 1993 at Sony Music Studios in New York for MTV's acoustic concert series, the performance was broadcast that December. Cobain insisted the stage be decorated with lilies and black candles, like a funeral. After his death in April 1994, the recording was released that November and topped the American chart. The set list was his choice, much to MTV's frustration at the lack of hits.","Listen to the final song, where Cobain pauses before the last line and his voice breaks into a howl; the performance was done in one take, and he refused an encore because he said he could not top it. Cobain had learned the song from Lead Belly's recordings, and he spoke of Lead Belly as his favourite performer. The Meat Puppets songs were a deliberate gesture to share his audience with a band he loved.","It won a Grammy for best alternative album and has often been ranked with Nevermind as Nirvana's best record. The funeral atmosphere, noticed at the time, became almost unbearable after his death. Some argue it is his finest singing. It was also a key moment in bringing Lead Belly and early folk blues to a new generation of listeners.","Hear the Lead Belly song first, then go back and listen to Lead Belly's own recording to hear how the old folk tune travelled. Notice Grohl's brushes and the cello's weight on 'Pennyroyal Tea' and 'Something in the Way'. Compare Johnny Cash's American Recordings, made the same year, another stripped-down record haunted by mortality."],"pearl-jam-ten":["Eleven songs of big, classic-rock-rooted grunge. Stone Gossard's riffs and Mike McCready's long, bluesy solos draw on Led Zeppelin and Hendrix more than punk; Jeff Ament's bass is fluid; Eddie Vedder's deep baritone, full of vibrato, is the signature. 'Once', 'Even Flow' and 'Alive' come early, 'Jeremy' tells the story of a troubled boy, and 'Black' is a slow, aching breakup song. The mood is earnest and anguished rather than ironic.","Gossard and Ament had been in Mother Love Bone, whose singer Andrew Wood died in 1990. They sent instrumental demos to a San Diego surfer and petrol-station attendant, Vedder, who wrote lyrics over them and was invited to Seattle. The album was recorded early in 1991 at London Bridge Studios and released by Epic in August, before Nevermind. It sold slowly at first before becoming one of the best-selling rock albums of the decade.","Vedder's first demo lyrics formed a loose trilogy about a son's discovery of family secrets, of which 'Alive' is the first part; he has said the story drew on his own life. 'Jeremy' is based on a real incident in a Texas school, handled in the song with restraint. Listen to McCready's solo in 'Alive', a long, soaring piece of classic rock that punk-minded listeners found almost shocking in 1991.","Its success made Pearl Jam one of the biggest bands in the world, and Kurt Cobain publicly dismissed them as careerists, though the two later made peace. Critics were divided at first between those who heard old-fashioned arena rock and those who heard genuine emotion. It was remixed by Brendan O'Brien in 2009, and fans still argue about which mix to prefer.","Start with 'Black', where Vedder's voice carries a slow build to a cathartic end. Then 'Alive' for the guitar solo. Notice how much of the sound comes from the 1970s rather than from punk; set it beside Nirvana's Nevermind to hear grunge's two faces in the same year."],"dinosaur-jr-youre-living-all-over-me":["Nine songs where extreme volume meets lazy, melodic singing. J Mascis drawls in a voice close to Neil Young's while his guitar roars through fuzz and Marshall amplifiers, erupting into long, melodic solos; Lou Barlow's bass is heavy and busy; Murph's drums are punk fast. 'Little Fury Things', 'Sludgefeast' and 'The Lung' define the sound, and the album closes with 'Poledo', a strange home-taped collage by Barlow that points towards lo-fi.","Dinosaur Jr came from Amherst, Massachusetts, from the hardcore punk scene, and moved towards a mix of punk energy and 1970s guitar rock. Their second album was recorded with Wharton Tiers in New York and released by SST late in 1987. They had been forced to add 'Jr' after a legal complaint from an older band called the Dinosaurs. Sonic Youth championed them.","Lee Ranaldo of Sonic Youth adds backing vocals to 'Little Fury Things'. Listen to the contrast between Mascis's guitar, which is enormously loud, and his voice, which barely rises above a murmur; the tension between the two is the band's whole character. 'Poledo', recorded by Barlow on cassette at home, shows the sound of the band he would form next, Sebadoh.","Received in the underground as a new kind of noisy melodic rock, the album inspired musicians more than it sold; Kevin Shields of My Bloody Valentine cited its influence. Internal tensions led to Barlow's departure after the next record. It is now seen as a key bridge from hardcore to alternative rock and a forerunner of grunge's sound.","Play 'Little Fury Things' loud and notice how the guitar solo sings a tune rather than showing off speed. Then compare My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything to hear the fuzz being turned dreamy, and Nirvana's Nevermind to hear the same mix made bigger."],"pavement-slanted-and-enchanted":["Fourteen songs recorded cheaply and messily, with out-of-tune guitars, drums that wobble in and out of time and Stephen Malkmus's half-sung, half-spoken vocals. Underneath the scruff are sharp melodies: 'Summer Babe (Winter Version)', 'Trigger Cut' and 'Here' are almost sweet, while 'Conduit for Sale!' and 'No Life Singed Her' are noisy and absurd. The lyrics are witty, fractured and allusive, full of odd phrases, and the album sounds casual yet carefully built.","Pavement began as a studio project for Malkmus and Scott Kannberg in Stockton, California. They recorded in the home studio of drummer Gary Young, for very little money, around the end of 1990 and into 1991. Cassettes circulated among critics before Matador Records released it in April 1992. It arrived just as grunge was going mainstream, and its offhand, low-budget sound was an alternative to the new corporate alternative.","Listen to Young's drumming: loose, occasionally falling out of time, and part of the band's appeal. The strongest influence is the English post-punk band the Fall, whose tape-hiss sound and declamatory vocals Malkmus admired. The guitars are often tuned slightly apart, making the chords sound blurry and bittersweet. Many fans first heard it on a dubbed cassette, which suits the sound.","Critics adored it, and it became a defining record of the lo-fi movement and 1990s indie rock. Some found its sloppiness affected; defenders argued that it was a rejection of rock's heroics. It has been widely reissued and remains a model for bands that want to sound unpolished and literate at the same time.","Start with 'Summer Babe' and 'Here', and notice how the melodies survive the noise. Then play 'Trigger Cut' and listen for the backing chants. Compare Pearl Jam's Ten from the year before to hear two opposite versions of American guitar rock in the early 1990s."],"pavement-crooked-rain-crooked-rain":["Twelve songs, cleaner and more tuneful than Slanted and Enchanted but still casual and witty. 'Cut Your Hair' is a catchy sendup of the music business, 'Gold Soundz' a wistful pop song, 'Range Life' a country-tinged daydream that mocks other bands, 'Stop Breathin' a slow drift, and 'Fillmore Jive' a long, sardonic farewell to classic rock. There is even a short jazz pastiche, '5-4=Unity'. The guitars are loose and bright; Malkmus's singing is relaxed and knowing.","By 1993 Gary Young had left, replaced on drums by Steve West, and percussionist Bob Nastanovich was more firmly part of the band. The album was recorded in New York and released by Matador in February 1994. That was the high point of grunge, two months before Kurt Cobain's death, and Pavement found themselves on MTV and in the mainstream conversation despite themselves.","'5-4=Unity' is a playful tribute to Dave Brubeck's 'Take Five', including its lopsided five-beat meter. 'Range Life' pokes fun at the Smashing Pumpkins and Stone Temple Pilots, which led to a real feud. The album's title and imagery nod to the California landscape and suburban life. Listen for the classic-rock guitar clichés Malkmus slips in ironically, especially in 'Fillmore Jive'.","Received as the band's most accessible record, it brought them their only real hit with 'Cut Your Hair'. Some early fans missed the noise, but it is generally ranked alongside or above Slanted and Enchanted. Critics debated whether its irony was a way of holding back feeling; others heard real tenderness in 'Gold Soundz'. It remains a touchstone for American indie rock.","Start with 'Gold Soundz' and notice how its melody is at once sad and cheerful. Then play '5-4=Unity' right after Brubeck's 'Take Five'. Finish with 'Fillmore Jive', whose long fade feels like the end of rock's golden age seen from a skate park."],"radiohead-the-bends":["Twelve songs of guitar rock, soaring and anxious. Three guitars (Thom Yorke, Jonny Greenwood and Ed O'Brien) interlock in shimmering layers; Yorke's falsetto rises over them. 'Planet Telex' and the title track are loud, 'Fake Plastic Trees' builds from acoustic guitar to a full band, 'High and Dry' and 'Just' became hits, and 'Street Spirit (Fade Out)' ends the album in quiet despair. It is bigger and more varied than their debut, and full of dread about modern life.","After their 1993 hit 'Creep', Radiohead feared being a one-hit band. They recorded The Bends in 1994 with the producer John Leckie, at Abbey Road and other studios, with Nigel Godrich engineering; Godrich would become their longtime producer. Parlophone released it in March 1995, in the middle of Britpop, but Radiohead's gloom set them apart from the celebratory mood of Oasis and Blur.","Yorke recorded the vocal for 'Fake Plastic Trees' in a single session after reportedly seeing Jeff Buckley perform in London, and Buckley's high, vulnerable style colours the album's singing. Greenwood's guitar on 'Just' is a dizzying run of notes climbing through the scale. 'Planet Telex' began as a late-night session with a drum loop, a hint of the electronic turns to come.","It sold slowly in America but was acclaimed in Britain and by fellow musicians, and its sound shaped a generation of British guitar bands from Coldplay on. Some fans still prefer it to OK Computer for its directness. It is usually treated as the bridge from their early grunge-influenced rock to the experiments of the later albums.","Start with 'Fake Plastic Trees' and listen to how the song grows, instrument by instrument. Then 'Street Spirit', whose arpeggiated guitar never changes. Compare R.E.M.'s Automatic for the People, which Yorke admired, for another major rock band exploring quiet despair."],"radiohead-ok-computer":["Twelve songs about technology, travel, alienation and dread. 'Airbag' opens with chopped-up drums; 'Paranoid Android' is a six-minute suite in several sections; 'Exit Music (for a Film)' moves from acoustic guitar to crushing distortion; 'Karma Police' and 'No Surprises' are gentle melodies with an edge. 'Fitter Happier', a list of lifestyle slogans read by a computer voice, sits in the middle. The sound is dense and spacious, guitars and keyboards blended into shimmering textures.","Recorded mostly in 1996 and early 1997 at St Catherine's Court, a Tudor mansion near Bath, with Nigel Godrich now co-producing, the album was released by Parlophone in May 1997. Britpop was at its height and the New Labour government was days old; the band's fears of globalisation, the internet and consumer life seemed prophetic. 'Exit Music' was written for Baz Luhrmann's film Romeo + Juliet.","'Climbing Up the Walls' ends with a cluster of strings written by Jonny Greenwood for sixteen players, each set a quarter-tone apart, a debt to Penderecki's Threnody. The drums on 'Airbag' were sampled and chopped, inspired by DJ Shadow. 'Fitter Happier' was spoken by the Macintosh text-to-speech voice. Listen to 'Let Down': the guitars play in a different meter from the drums.","Critics hailed it immediately, and it won a Grammy and was nominated for the Mercury Prize. It was added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry in 2015. Some found it portentous; others heard the defining rock album of the late 1990s, anticipating the digital age. Its influence on rock, and its reputation as a concept album, remain lively debates.","Play 'Paranoid Android' and follow its sections, as you might the movements of a symphony. Then play 'Climbing Up the Walls' right after Penderecki's Threnody to hear the debt. Notice the details: harpsichord-like guitars, Mellotron, backward sounds, all of which reward headphones."],"radiohead-kid-a":["Ten tracks that trade guitar rock for electronics, jazz and orchestral sound. 'Everything in Its Right Place' opens with warm synthesiser chords and fractured vocals; 'The National Anthem' has a bass riff and a chaotic brass section; 'How to Disappear Completely' floats on strings and ondes Martenot; 'Treefingers' is ambient; 'Idioteque' is a glitchy dance track. Yorke's voice is often processed until it becomes another instrument.","Exhausted by the fame of OK Computer, Thom Yorke had grown tired of rock and immersed himself in electronic music from the Warp label. The band recorded in Paris, Copenhagen, Gloucestershire and Oxford in 1999 and 2000, with Nigel Godrich producing, and released Kid A in October 2000 without singles or conventional videos. The album went to number one in America despite this. Early internet leaks and short animated clips promoted it.","Jonny Greenwood plays the ondes Martenot, the early electronic instrument heard in Messiaen's Turangalîla-Symphonie, on 'How to Disappear Completely', where his string writing slides in clusters descended from Penderecki. 'Idioteque' is built on samples from early computer music by Paul Lansky and Arthur Kreiger. The brass on 'The National Anthem' was told to play like a free-jazz band; Yorke cut up words and drew lines from a hat.","Critics and fans were divided at first; some called it a wilful retreat, others a landmark. Within a decade it was topping lists of the best albums of the 2000s. It is often credited with leading rock towards electronic music, and with showing that a big band could abandon a formula. Debate continues about whether it is truly radical or a smart fusion of existing ideas.","Start with 'How to Disappear Completely' and listen for the ondes Martenot's wavering tone over the strings; then hear it in Messiaen's Turangalîla. Play 'Idioteque' loud for the beat. Let 'Treefingers' be a pause. Greenwood's There Will Be Blood score is the next step."],"radiohead-in-rainbows":["Ten songs, warmer and more intimate than Kid A, often built on interlocking patterns. '15 Step' starts with a skittering beat in five; 'Nude' floats on strings; 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' builds from overlapping guitar arpeggios; 'Reckoner' has a falsetto melody over tambourine and piano; 'House of Cards' is slow and hazy; 'Videotape' closes with piano and quietly shifting drums. The mood is sensual and melancholy, closer to love songs than the band had come in years.","Out of contract with EMI, Radiohead announced In Rainbows with ten days' notice and released it as a download on 10 October 2007, letting buyers pay whatever they chose. A physical version followed at the end of the year. Produced by Nigel Godrich over long sessions in several places, including a country house in Wiltshire, it was a sensation in debates about digital music, piracy and the future of the record industry.","'Nude' had been played live since 1997 and was only now completed. The interlocking guitar lines of 'Weird Fishes' use the same principle of phase and pattern that runs through Steve Reich's music; Reich later wrote Radio Rewrite (2012) on songs from Radiohead, and Greenwood recorded Reich's Electric Counterpoint. Listen to '15 Step' for the sound of children cheering, and to 'Videotape' for a drum pattern that shifts its accent.","The pay-what-you-want release dominated news coverage, sometimes obscuring the music, but critics praised it as one of the band's best records and it later won two Grammys. Industry arguments about whether it helped or harmed musicians continue. Many fans rank it as their favourite Radiohead album, more human and approachable than Kid A.","Play 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' and follow the guitar lines as they overlap; then hear Reich's Electric Counterpoint, where one guitar is layered many times. 'Reckoner' is the heart of the record; notice how the falsetto floats over the rhythm. Listen late at night."],"pj-harvey-rid-of-me":["Fourteen songs, raw, violent and extremely dynamic. The title track opens with a whispered verse and a hammering chorus; '50ft Queenie' is a boastful, fast stomp; 'Man-Size Sextet' replaces the band with scraping strings; 'Dry' is slow and aching; and there is a snarling cover of Bob Dylan's 'Highway 61 Revisited'. Polly Harvey's voice ranges from a whisper to a howl, and her guitar is jagged. The songs explore sex, power and gender with dark humour.","Harvey grew up in rural Dorset, and her first album, Dry, appeared in 1992 on the independent label Too Pure. For her second, on Island, the trio of Harvey, bassist Steve Vaughan and drummer Rob Ellis recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Minnesota with Steve Albini late in 1992. The album was released in 1993; within months the band had broken up and Harvey continued alone.","Albini recorded the quiet passages very quietly and the loud ones very loud, so that the volume leaps can be startling at home; the vocals are often low in the mix. Harvey's love of Captain Beefheart shows in the jagged rhythms and bluesy growl, and she has spoken of growing up on her parents' records of Beefheart and the blues. Listen to how Ellis's backing vocals add ghostly falsetto.","Critics praised it as ferocious and original, though some thought Albini's production made it hard to hear. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize. Debate about whether Harvey was a feminist artist began here; she resisted labels, preferring to play characters. It remains one of the most intense rock albums of the 1990s and was made months before Nirvana's In Utero at the same studio.","Play the title track and let the quiet verses draw you in before the chorus erupts. Then hear 'Man-Size Sextet' for the strings, and '50ft Queenie' for sheer energy. Compare Captain Beefheart's Trout Mask Replica for the blues and angles she loved."],"pj-harvey-let-england-shake":["Twelve songs about war, England and its landscape, many of them centred on the Gallipoli campaign of 1915. Harvey plays autoharp and sings in a high, clear voice, far from her 1990s howl; the arrangements are sparse, with guitar, keyboards, drums and occasional samples of bugles and old records. 'The Words That Maketh Murder', 'The Glorious Land' and 'On Battleship Hill' are standouts. The tunes are catchy, even jaunty, against lyrics describing carnage.","Harvey spent several years writing, reading war poetry, history and soldiers' testimony from Gallipoli to Iraq and Afghanistan. The album was recorded in 2010 in a nineteenth-century church in Eype, Dorset, near her home, with longtime collaborators John Parish, Mick Harvey and Jean-Marc Butty. It was released in February 2011, while British troops were fighting in Afghanistan. The photographer Seamus Murphy made a short film for each song.","Harvey borrows from old records: the title track uses a sample of the 1950s novelty song 'Istanbul (Not Constantinople)', one song briefly quotes Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', and 'The Glorious Land' has a bugle call. Listen to how brisk tunes and a bright autoharp are set against descriptions of dead soldiers, a contrast that makes the horror more vivid. The recording church adds natural reverb.","It won the Mercury Prize in 2011, making Harvey the first artist to win it twice, and topped many critics' lists for the year. Critics compared it to the war poets such as Wilfred Owen. Some found its tone too detached; most heard it as a searching study of patriotism and violence and Harvey's most ambitious work.","Hear the title track and notice the sample running under the autoharp. Then 'On Battleship Hill', whose high, fragile vocal evokes a haunted landscape. Compare Britten's War Requiem, which sets Wilfred Owen, and Butterworth's A Shropshire Lad rhapsody, written by a composer who died at the Somme."],"neutral-milk-hotel-in-the-aeroplane-over-the-sea":["Eleven songs built on Jeff Mangum's strummed acoustic guitar and fervent, nasal voice, surrounded by fuzzed bass, flugelhorn and trumpets, singing saw, accordion and organ, all recorded with lots of distortion. 'The King of Carrot Flowers' opens in three parts, 'In the Aeroplane Over the Sea' is a sing-along waltz, 'Holland, 1945' is a loud rush, and 'Oh Comely' is an eight-minute solo performance. The songs mix love, death and surreal imagery.","Mangum was part of the Elephant 6 collective, a group of friends from Ruston, Louisiana, who made psychedelic-tinged indie pop. The album was recorded in 1997 in Denver with Robert Schneider of the Apples in Stereo, with Julian Koster, Jeremy Barnes and Scott Spillane, and released by Merge in February 1998. Mangum had been reading The Diary of Anne Frank, which affected him deeply and runs through the lyrics.","Anne Frank appears in several songs, though never named in a way that explains the album; Mangum imagines her as a child who might be reborn. The singing saw, played by Koster, is the eerie whistling sound. At the end of 'Oh Comely', someone in the room can be heard reacting to Mangum's performance, which was reportedly captured in one take.","Modestly received at first, it grew into a cult classic through word of mouth and the early internet, and by the 2000s was widely considered one of the best indie albums ever made. Mangum largely withdrew from public life, adding to its mystique. Some find his voice grating and the imagery overwrought; devotees hear a rare sincerity.","Start with the title track: notice the singing saw and the horns. Then 'Holland, 1945' for its joyful noise. Hear 'Oh Comely' alone at night. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for an intimate voice and guitar, and the Beatles' Sgt Pepper for the brass-band psychedelia."],"the-strokes-is-this-it":["Eleven songs, taut and minimal, in about thirty-six minutes. Two guitars, Nick Valensi's and Albert Hammond Jr.'s, play interlocking, clean, choppy lines; Nikolai Fraiture's bass pulses; Fabrizio Moretti's drums are precise and almost machine-like; Julian Casablancas sings through a distorted, lo-fi filter with a bored drawl. 'Last Nite', 'Someday', 'Hard to Explain' and 'The Modern Age' are the hits. It sounds casual, cool and effortless.","The Strokes were five friends from Manhattan who played Lower East Side clubs. Their debut EP created a buzz in Britain, and Rough Trade released the album in the summer of 2001, before RCA's American release in October. It arrived just after 9/11 in America, where 'New York City Cops' was dropped and the cover changed. Produced by Gordon Raphael in a small basement studio, it marked a return to lean guitar rock after years of nu-metal and electronica.","Casablancas recorded his vocals through a small amplifier to get the distorted, far-off sound; Raphael deliberately avoided modern gloss. Moretti's drums were recorded to sound like a drum machine, part of the band's goal of making rock sound like 1970s New York filtered through electronic music. 'Last Nite' so resembled Tom Petty's 'American Girl' that Petty later acknowledged it with amusement.","It was hailed as the saviour of rock and topped many polls of the decade's best albums, launching the garage-rock revival along with the White Stripes. Some critics found it derivative of the Velvet Underground and Television; others argued it was a synthesis rather than a copy. Its style influenced many British bands, including the Arctic Monkeys and the Libertines.","Play 'Last Nite' and listen to how the two guitars fit together like gears. Then 'Hard to Explain', with its machine-like drums. Hear it after Radiohead's Kid A, released a year earlier, to understand the two directions rock split into at the turn of the century."],"arcade-fire-funeral":["Ten songs, big, anthemic and emotional. Violins, accordion, piano and organ are woven around guitars and drums; many voices chant together. The four 'Neighborhood' songs imagine children escaping into snowstorms and tunnels; 'Wake Up' is a huge wordless chant; 'Rebellion (Lies)' drives on a pulsing bass; 'Une année sans lumière' is in French; 'In the Backseat', sung by Régine Chassagne, closes in grief. The mood is childlike, desperate and joyful at once.","Arcade Fire formed in Montreal around Win Butler and Chassagne, a married couple. While they were recording the album in 2003 and 2004, several relatives of band members died, including Butler's grandfather, the bandleader and steel-guitar pioneer Alvino Rey; hence the title. Merge released it in September 2004. Its success, driven by blogs and a glowing Pitchfork review, showed how the internet could make an indie band famous.","The strings were arranged by Owen Pallett and Sarah Neufeld, bringing a chamber-music sound. The band recorded in a Montreal studio with all members shouting and singing to create the choral effect. Listen for how the French-language song reflects Montreal's bilingualism and Chassagne's Haitian heritage. The 'Neighborhood' songs form a loose narrative of children growing up in a world of blackouts.","It was widely hailed as one of the best albums of the decade; David Bowie became a fan and performed with the band. Some critics found its emotion overblown; others heard a rare sincerity. It paved the way for 2000s indie rock's taste for big choruses and many-member bands, and led to later Grammy success with The Suburbs.","Start with 'Wake Up', and notice how the whole band sings the melody. Then 'Neighborhood #1 (Tunnels)', whose piano builds slowly. Compare Neutral Milk Hotel's In the Aeroplane Over the Sea for another cathartic, homemade indie sound, and Bowie's Heroes for the grandeur."],"bob-dylan-like-a-rolling-stone":["Six minutes of four verses and a chorus that keeps growing. It opens with a single crack of the snare drum, then piano, Mike Bloomfield's stinging guitar and, floating over everything, Al Kooper's organ, a high wavering line that answers each phrase. Dylan does not so much sing as sneer, pitching every chorus as a question thrown at a fallen socialite who has lost her place in the world. The song never resolves; it simply fades while still at full pressure.","Dylan wrote it in June 1965, back from a draining English tour, out of what he described as a long page of angry prose. Producer Tom Wilson cut it at Columbia's Studio A in New York over two days. Columbia hesitated to issue a six-minute single, since radio wanted three, but it went out in July and climbed to number two in America, kept from the top by the Beatles' 'Help!'. Five days after release Dylan played it, loud and electric, at the Newport Folk Festival.","Kooper was a guitarist who talked his way to the organ bench and did not really know the chords, so he watched the other players' hands and came in a fraction late on each change. That hesitation is the organ sound everyone remembers. The first day's takes tried the song as a waltz; the session tapes, since issued, let you hear it turn from three beats to four and find its swagger.","It was heard at once as a rupture: a pop single with the length, bile and imagery of a poem. Rolling Stone put it at the top of its list of the greatest songs in 2004 and again in 2021. The argument now is less about its worth than about blame: whether it ended the folk revival's innocence or simply told the truth about it. Its handwritten draft sold at auction in 2014 for about two million dollars.","Listen first for the opening drum shot and the organ entering behind the voice; then follow how each chorus gets angrier than the last. Hear it next to 'Blowin' in the Wind' to measure how far he travelled in two years, and next to the Rolling Stones' 'Satisfaction', released weeks earlier, for the other great rock single of that summer."],"bob-dylan-highway-61-revisited":["Nine songs, eight of them fully electric, with a blues band sound that is rough, bright and fast. 'Like a Rolling Stone' opens; 'Tombstone Blues' and the title track race; 'Ballad of a Thin Man' creeps over a slow piano and organ; 'Just Like Tom Thumb's Blues' is a weary border-town lament. The record closes with 'Desolation Row', eleven minutes of acoustic guitar and a Spanish-tinged second guitar, a carnival parade of figures from history, scripture and literature.","Made in New York in the summer of 1965, the weeks around Newport, with Mike Bloomfield on lead guitar and Al Kooper on keyboards; Bob Johnston took over as producer from Tom Wilson after the first single. U.S. Route 61 runs from New Orleans up through the Mississippi Delta to Duluth, Minnesota, where Dylan was born: the road of the blues migration, of Delta crossroads legends, of the pilgrimage north that made Chicago blues.","The title song begins with a comic retelling of Abraham ordered to sacrifice his son (Dylan's own father was named Abram), and its manic bursts come from a toy police siren credited to Dylan himself. 'Desolation Row' was recorded with Nashville guitarist Charlie McCoy, who happened to be visiting New York and improvised the lacework second guitar line on the spot.","Critics and fellow musicians took it as proof that rock could be art, and the Beatles, the Byrds and the Stones all listened closely. Some folk loyalists felt betrayed. Its standing has never really wavered; the debate is whether it or 'Blonde on Blonde' is the summit of the 1965 to 1966 run, and whether its surreal imagery is profound or brilliantly arbitrary.","Take side one straight through, then sit still for 'Desolation Row' as a single long journey. Notice how the band plays like a Chicago blues outfit that has read too many French poets. Follow with Robert Johnson's 'Cross Road Blues' and Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere' to hear the highway the album is named for. The mono mix is punchier."],"bob-dylan-blonde-on-blonde":["A double album of fourteen songs, softer and stranger than its predecessor. Organ and electric guitar swirl together into a silvery, nocturnal glow. 'Visions of Johanna' drifts through a sleepless night; 'I Want You' and 'Just Like a Woman' are sharp pop; 'Rainy Day Women #12 & 35' is a raucous, laughing brass-band romp; and the whole fourth side is given to 'Sad Eyed Lady of the Lowlands', an eleven-minute slow hymn to one woman.","Sessions began in New York in January 1966 with the Hawks, the group later known as the Band, and stalled. Producer Bob Johnston moved them to Nashville, where Dylan wrote in the studio while crack country session players such as Charlie McCoy, Kenny Buttrey and Wayne Moss waited, sometimes until dawn, playing cards. Robbie Robertson and Al Kooper came along from New York. It appeared in June 1966, among the first double albums in rock.","'4th Time Around' is a sly answer to the Beatles' 'Norwegian Wood', which had borrowed Dylan's own manner; John Lennon admitted feeling uneasy about it. Kenny Buttrey's drumming on 'Sad Eyed Lady' swells in near-panic because nobody knew how long the song would go on. Dylan later described the sound he was chasing as thin and wild and mercurial, which is exactly what the organ and harmonica do together.","Hailed at once and still widely ranked his finest record. Its reputation also made Nashville respectable to rock musicians, opening the road to country rock. Arguments continue about how much of the imagery is private, how much Sara Lownds, whom he had just married, lies behind 'Sad Eyed Lady', and whether the rambling side four is devotion or indulgence.","Start with 'Visions of Johanna' in the dark, then 'I Want You' for the bright pop Dylan. Listen for Garth Hudson-style organ and McCoy's bass harmonica in the corners. Play the Beatles' 'Rubber Soul' beside it to hear two camps trading ideas in real time. The 2010s mono reissues are worth seeking out."],"bob-dylan-live-1966":["A whole concert on two discs. The first half is Dylan alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica, playing long, hushed songs such as 'Visions of Johanna' and 'Desolation Row' to an attentive, reverent hall. The second half brings on the Hawks, loud and ferocious, for 'Tell Me, Momma', 'Ballad of a Thin Man' and more, while the audience slow-handclaps and heckles. It ends with a snarling 'Like a Rolling Stone'.","Recorded at the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, on 17 May 1966, during a British tour on which Dylan was booed nightly by folk audiences who felt he had sold out. The Hawks were Robbie Robertson, Rick Danko, Richard Manuel and Garth Hudson, with Mickey Jones on drums. A tape circulated for three decades as one of rock's most famous bootlegs, wrongly labelled as the Royal Albert Hall show; Columbia finally issued it officially in 1998, keeping the wrong name in quotation marks.","Just before the last song a voice from the audience shouts 'Judas!'. Dylan answers that he doesn't believe him and calls him a liar, then turns and tells the band, profanely, to play loud. At least two men have since claimed to be the heckler. Listen too to the acoustic half: the silence of the crowd there is part of the drama, because it is the same people.","Long before release, critics treated the bootleg as a founding document of rock's live power and of the folk-rock schism. Its official issue confirmed it; many rank it the greatest live rock recording. The debate now is about whose side history takes: the hecklers are usually cast as philistines, though some writers argue they were defending a real community Dylan had left behind.","Play one acoustic song, then jump to the electric 'Ballad of a Thin Man', and feel the shock the audience felt. Then save the last track for the end. Compare with Muddy Waters at Newport in 1960, and with Neil Young's 'Rust Never Sleeps', which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split as a choice rather than a fight."],"bob-dylan-blood-on-the-tracks":["Ten songs of love gone wrong, mostly built on Dylan's acoustic guitar with a light band. 'Tangled Up in Blue' opens, a story that jumps between times and pronouns; 'Simple Twist of Fate' is quiet and rueful; 'Idiot Wind' is a long, organ-driven storm of accusation; 'Shelter from the Storm' is a patient, circling ballad; 'Buckets of Rain' closes almost in a whisper. The long narrative 'Lily, Rosemary and the Jack of Hearts' is a Western told in fifteen verses.","Dylan recorded the album in New York in September 1974, then, dissatisfied, re-cut five songs in Minneapolis that December with local players gathered by his brother. His marriage to Sara was coming apart. He had also spent weeks that year in a painting class with Norman Raeben, who, he said, taught him to see past, present and future at once, which he credited for the album's shifting sense of time.","Test pressings of the New York version reached reviewers before the Minneapolis re-recordings replaced half of it, and the full September sessions were released in 2018 as 'More Blood, More Tracks'. Compare the two 'Idiot Wind's: in New York it is weary and hushed, in Minneapolis it is furious. Dylan has denied the songs are autobiography and pointed to Chekhov's short stories instead.","Initial reviews were mixed, some finding it slight, but within a few years it was ranked among his best, and it is often called the great breakup album. The autobiography question remains open: his son Jakob said it sounded like his parents talking, while Dylan insists on fiction. Fans still argue whether the New York takes were the better record.","Start with 'Tangled Up in Blue' and track who 'I' and 'she' are in each verse; the confusion is the point. Then hear 'Idiot Wind' and 'You're Gonna Make Me Lonesome When You Go' back to back: rage and tenderness a track apart. Schubert's 'Winterreise' is a surprisingly close cousin, a cycle of a jilted man walking through his memories."],"bob-dylan-time-out-of-mind":["Eleven songs in a murky, reverberant sound: organ, twanging guitars, doubled drums and a voice now cracked and worn, pushed through echo as if recorded in a swamp. The songs are slow blues and ballads about walking, waiting and ageing: 'Love Sick' creeps in over a ticking organ; 'Not Dark Yet' is a stately meditation on fading; 'Make You Feel My Love' is a plain ballad; and 'Highlands' closes the album with sixteen shambling minutes of wry monologue.","Recorded in early 1997 at Criteria Studios in Miami with producer Daniel Lanois, who had made Dylan's 'Oh Mercy' in 1989, and a crowded room of musicians. Dylan had issued no original songs for seven years. Just before release he was hospitalised with a serious heart infection, and the album's preoccupation with mortality was read through that, though it was finished beforehand. It won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Dylan was frustrated by the sessions and later said he wanted a sound closer to the old records he loved; the album is full of borrowed phrases and melodies from pre-war blues and country, a practice that grew on his later records. 'Make You Feel My Love' became a standard through covers by Billy Joel, Garth Brooks and Adele, the rare Dylan song others sing more than he does.","It was hailed as a comeback, the start of a late period many now rank alongside the 1960s. Lanois's atmospheric production divides listeners: for some it is the album's soul, for others a fog. Dylan went on to produce himself, under the alias Jack Frost. The borrowings, later more explicit, opened a lasting argument over his methods.","Begin with 'Not Dark Yet' at night. Then try 'Highlands' as you would a long short story. Listen for the old blues shapes under the fog; Muddy Waters and Charley Patton are close by. Schubert's 'Winterreise', a wanderer's cycle in a cold landscape, is an unexpected but apt companion."],"neil-young-after-the-gold-rush":["Eleven short songs alternating between fragile piano-and-voice ballads and loose, loud electric rock. Young's thin, high tenor is the signature. The title track is only piano, voice and a closing flugelhorn solo, a dream of knights, a burning world and silver spaceships. 'Southern Man' is a furious electric guitar assault on racism; 'Only Love Can Break Your Heart' is a gentle waltz; 'Don't Let It Bring You Down' is bleak and consoling at once.","Young made it in 1970, partly in a basement studio at his house in Topanga Canyon outside Los Angeles, between tours with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, whose 'Déjà Vu' had just been a huge success. He used members of Crazy Horse and an eighteen-year-old Nils Lofgren, a guitarist, whom he put on piano. Several songs grew from a screenplay by the actor Dean Stockwell for a film that was never made.","Lofgren's piano is simple because he had barely played the instrument; its plainness is part of the charm. 'Southern Man' drew a reply four years later from Lynyrd Skynyrd's 'Sweet Home Alabama', which names Young directly; he took it good-naturedly. The album also includes a cover, Don Gibson's country hit 'Oh, Lonesome Me', slowed into a dirge.","Rolling Stone's first review was cool, calling it uneven, but the album sold well and its reputation climbed steadily. It is now considered one of the defining records of the Laurel Canyon era. The title song's cryptic ecological vision has been widely covered, notably by Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris on 'Trio II'.","Play the title track first with nothing else going on, then 'Southern Man' straight after to hear Young's two voices, the choirboy and the wrecker. Compare Joni Mitchell's 'Blue' from the next year for the same canyon, a different temperament. Then hear 'Tonight's the Night' to see where this fragility went."],"neil-young-tonights-the-night":["A wake on record. Twelve songs played loose and ragged, slurred and off-key by design, with piano, guitars and Ben Keith's crying pedal steel. The title song opens and closes the album in two versions, an unsettling chant naming a dead friend. Between them come shambling barroom ballads such as 'Mellow My Mind', 'Tired Eyes' and 'Albuquerque', with voices cracking and tempos dragging throughout. It sounds like a late night that never quite ended.","Crazy Horse guitarist Danny Whitten died of an overdose in November 1972, days after Young had sent him home from rehearsals; roadie Bruce Berry died the same way the following year. In August and September 1973 Young gathered a band he called the Santa Monica Flyers at a rehearsal studio in Los Angeles and recorded late at night, often after heavy drinking. Reprise shelved the album; it came out only in June 1975.","One track, 'Come On Baby Let's Go Downtown', is a 1970 live recording from the Fillmore East with Whitten himself singing lead; the dead man is on his own memorial. Young kept the flubbed notes and cracked high notes on purpose. It belongs to what fans call the 'ditch trilogy', with 'Time Fades Away' and 'On the Beach', his deliberate swerve away from the success of 'Harvest'.","Critics were divided on release; some heard sloppiness, others unbearable honesty. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now often called his best album and a precursor to punk's and grunge's distrust of polish. Young himself said it was the closest he came to art. The argument over whether rawness is a style or a truth starts here.","Listen straight through, late, in one sitting, as one long wake. Hear 'Tired Eyes', half-spoken, and notice how the pedal steel does the weeping the voice will not. Compare Hank Williams, whose broken-hearted country it drinks from, and the grief of Nick Cave's 'Skeleton Tree' forty years later."],"neil-young-rust-never-sleeps":["An album of two halves. Side one is Young alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica: 'Thrasher', about leaving old companions, and 'Pocahontas', a dreamy history of dispossession. Side two brings on Crazy Horse at deafening volume: 'Powderfinger', a doomed young man defending his family's river, 'Welfare Mothers', 'Sedan Delivery'. The same song bookends it, as the acoustic 'My My, Hey Hey (Out of the Blue)' and the distorted, grinding 'Hey Hey, My My (Into the Black)'.","Most of it was recorded live on Young's 1978 tour with Crazy Horse, with the audience largely removed and some overdubs added. Punk had just arrived, and many 1960s stars felt pushed aside; Young instead embraced it, naming Johnny Rotten of the Sex Pistols in the bookend song. The title came from the band Devo, who adapted it from an advertising slogan for a rust-proofing paint.","The bookend songs argue that it is better to burn out than to fade away, an idea taken up by a whole generation; Kurt Cobain borrowed a line from it in his suicide note in 1994, which haunted Young. Listen to how the feedback on 'Into the Black' is played almost melodically: Crazy Horse's sludge is the sound grunge would later adopt as its own.","Hailed on release, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1979, and a concert film of the tour followed. It is regularly cited as a bridge between 1960s rock and punk, and Young as the 'godfather of grunge'. Debate lingers over whether the 'burn out' ethic is inspiring or dangerous, especially after Cobain.","Play the first track, then skip to the last, and hear one song change from a campfire tune to a wall of noise. Then give 'Powderfinger' full attention as a short story. Compare Dylan's 1966 Manchester concert, which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split under protest."],"joni-mitchell-blue":["Ten songs, spare and close-miked, sung in a voice that leaps without warning from low murmur to high clear octave. Mitchell accompanies herself on Appalachian dulcimer on 'All I Want', 'Carey', 'California' and 'A Case of You', on piano for the title song, 'River' and 'The Last Time I Saw Richard', and on open-tuned guitar elsewhere. The sound is intimate and unguarded; the songs are about travel, love affairs and a longing for home.","Recorded at A&M Studios in Hollywood in early 1971 and released that June, it followed a European journey in 1970, including a stay among hippies in the caves at Matala on Crete, the setting of 'Carey'. Mitchell was living in Laurel Canyon, the Los Angeles hillside community of songwriters, and had just ended relationships with Graham Nash and James Taylor, who plays guitar on three songs; Stephen Stills and pedal steel player Sneaky Pete Kleinow also appear.","'Little Green' is about the daughter Mitchell gave up for adoption in 1965, a secret that became public only in the 1990s, before they were reunited. 'River' opens and closes with the piano playing 'Jingle Bells' in a minor, melancholy key. Her open tunings, partly a way round the weakness polio left in her left hand, create chords no standard-tuned guitarist would find.","Reviews were warm but some were uneasy at its nakedness; Mitchell herself said she had no defences left while making it. Its standing only grew, and in 2020 Rolling Stone ranked it third among all albums. It is now the model of confessional songwriting, though Mitchell has resisted the word 'confessional' and insisted on craft over diary.","Start with 'A Case of You' and listen to the dulcimer's bright drone against her low voice. Then 'River' at Christmas time. Hear it beside Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' for the other great solitary record of the moment, and beside Schumann's 'Dichterliebe', a song cycle of love recalled and lost, for its oldest ancestor."],"joni-mitchell-court-and-spark":["Eleven songs, most of them with a full band of jazz players, polished and sophisticated, with woodwinds, electric piano and supple drums. The title song opens on solo piano; 'Help Me' is a lithe pop hit with jazz harmonies; 'Free Man in Paris' is a breezy portrait of a music-business friend longing to escape; 'People's Parties' and 'Down to You' are introspective; the record ends, unexpectedly, with a jazz vocalese cover, 'Twisted'.","Released in January 1974 on David Geffen's Asylum label, it was made with Tom Scott's L.A. Express, a group of Los Angeles jazz and studio musicians. It became the best-selling album of Mitchell's career, and 'Help Me' was her only top ten single in America. 'Free Man in Paris' is widely understood to be about Geffen himself. Mitchell was at the peak of her commercial power.","'Twisted' was written in the early 1950s by the jazz singer Annie Ross, setting words to a saxophone solo by Wardell Gray; Mitchell's version even includes a comic cameo from the duo Cheech and Chong. The arrangements' jazz harmonies, not the pop hooks, are the real signal: from here on she would move steadily into jazz, working with Jaco Pastorius, Wayne Shorter and eventually Charles Mingus.","Warmly received and commercially enormous, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1974. Some early fans missed the stark dulcimer of 'Blue'. It is now regarded as a model of how a songwriter can move into jazz-inflected pop without losing edge, and its polish now sounds less like compromise than like a new kind of precision.","Listen first to 'Help Me' for the way the melody leaps unexpectedly between registers, then to 'Down to You' for the orchestral side. Then hear 'Twisted' and seek out the Lambert, Hendricks and Ross version. Follow with 'Hejira' to see where the jazz leads."],"joni-mitchell-hejira":["Nine long, wandering songs of the road, built on Mitchell's chiming open-tuned guitar with very little else. On four of them Jaco Pastorius's fretless bass sings like a second voice, sliding, harmonising, chiming harmonics. There are no choruses in the usual sense; the melodies flow like speech over steady rolling guitar. 'Coyote' opens with a portrait of a restless man; 'Amelia' addresses the lost aviator Amelia Earhart; 'Refuge of the Roads' ends it.","Mitchell wrote most of the songs in 1976 while driving alone across America, from Maine back to Los Angeles, after touring with Bob Dylan's Rolling Thunder Revue. 'Coyote' is usually read as a portrait of the playwright Sam Shepard, also on that tour. 'Hejira' is the Arabic word for the Prophet Muhammad's flight from Mecca to Medina; she chose it for its sense of honourable escape.","'Furry Sings the Blues' is about visiting the aged Memphis blues man Furry Lewis on Beale Street, then being demolished; Neil Young plays harmonica on it. Lewis was reportedly unhappy with the song. Listen to Pastorius on 'Hejira' itself: he recorded his parts afterward, overdubbing melody lines that treat the bass as a lead instrument, a first in her work.","Received respectfully but with less excitement than its predecessors, it sold modestly. Over time it has become a musicians' favourite and for many critics her finest record, the point where her folk roots and jazz ambitions fully merge. Some still find its long, hookless songs monotonous; its admirers call that monotony the hypnotic hum of a highway.","Play 'Coyote' then 'Amelia' on a long drive. Focus on Pastorius's bass as a second singer. To hear the jazz he brought, try Miles Davis's 'Bitches Brew'; for the blues she was visiting, Mississippi John Hurt's 'Avalon Blues' is close in spirit to Furry Lewis."],"leonard-cohen-songs-of-leonard-cohen":["Ten songs sung in a low, level, almost unmusical voice over steady nylon-string fingerpicking, with discreet strings, soft female backing voices and odd touches such as a jaw harp. 'Suzanne', a slow, circling portrait of a woman by the Montreal waterfront, opens; 'Sisters of Mercy', 'So Long, Marianne' and 'Hey, That's No Way to Say Goodbye' follow. The songs are long, literary and patient; the voice never raises itself, and that restraint is the spell.","Cohen was thirty-three and already a known Canadian poet and novelist, whose 'Beautiful Losers' appeared in 1966. He turned to songs partly because books did not pay. Judy Collins recorded 'Suzanne' first, in 1966, and John Hammond signed him to Columbia. Producer John Simon completed the album in New York in 1967, over Cohen's objections to some of his arrangements. 'So Long, Marianne' is for Marianne Ihlen, with whom he had lived on the Greek island of Hydra.","The rolling guitar pattern owes a debt to flamenco: Cohen said a young Spanish guitarist in Montreal taught him a few chords before dying, not long after. Listen to how the melodies stay within a narrow range, like chanted verse, and how the backing voices sometimes carry the melody while Cohen intones below it, an arrangement idea he kept for fifty years.","It sold modestly in America and better in Britain and Europe, where Cohen quickly became a cult figure. Early critics found his voice dreary; later listeners heard gravity. Robert Altman's film 'McCabe & Mrs. Miller' (1971) used three of its songs. It is now regarded as a founding document of the literary singer-songwriter, and its songs are sung widely.","Hear 'Suzanne' first, paying attention to the guitar more than the words, then 'Master Song', and notice how the voice never hurries even when the song runs past five minutes. Compare Nick Drake's 'Five Leaves Left', also dark and hushed, and Hugo Wolf's Mörike songs, where the poem similarly governs the music."],"leonard-cohen-hallelujah":["A slow song in a rocking triple time, built on simple chords and a chorus of one word repeated. In Cohen's own recording, his deep, weathered voice is set against a synthesiser, a plodding rhythm and a gospel-tinged choir. The verses move between biblical stories (David the harpist, Samson and Delilah) and a love affair, mixing reverence and sensuality, with praise and doubt sitting side by side.","It first appeared on the album 'Various Positions' in 1984, produced by John Lissauer. Columbia's chief declined to release the album in America, and it came out there through a small label. Cohen said he wrote dozens of verses over several years, and he changed the verses he sang in concert, moving it from sacred to more worldly. He was fifty, his career in the doldrums, his record company unsure what to do with him.","The first verse describes its own harmony: as the words name the chord changes, the music makes them. John Cale's 1991 recording for a tribute album chose different verses and put it on piano; Jeff Buckley's 1994 version was based on Cale's, and it was Cale's version that played in the film 'Shrek' in 2001. The song everyone knows is a collaboration across three recordings.","Hardly noticed in 1984, it became over the next three decades one of the most covered songs in the world, sung at funerals, in talent shows and at Christmas. That ubiquity has its critics, including, at times, Cohen, who suggested it should be rested. The debate over which verses make the true song is unanswerable, since Cohen himself never fixed it.","Hear Cohen's original first, with its slightly dated synthesiser, then Cale's, then Buckley's, and notice how the meaning shifts with the verses chosen. Compare Mahalia Jackson's gospel for the tradition of praise it borrows from. Then return to the start and hear the song describe its own chords."],"leonard-cohen-im-your-man":["Eight songs framed in cheap synthesisers and drum machines, with Cohen's voice now a deep, dry bass murmur. 'First We Take Manhattan' is a menacing synth-pop march; 'Ain't No Cure for Love' a sly ballad; 'Everybody Knows' a grim catalogue of the world's rigged game; 'Take This Waltz' a Viennese waltz with violin; 'Tower of Song' a wry self-portrait of the songwriter's trade. Female backing voices, especially Jennifer Warnes and Anjani Thomas, carry the tunes.","Released in early 1988, it followed Jennifer Warnes's 1986 album of Cohen songs, 'Famous Blue Raincoat', which revived interest in him. Cohen co-produced with several collaborators, and co-wrote 'Everybody Knows' with his longtime singer Sharon Robinson. He was in his fifties and embracing irony, sharp suits and cheap keyboards, the opposite of his 1960s image as a mournful troubadour, and his voice had dropped to a register that made every line sound like a verdict.","'Take This Waltz' adapts a poem by Federico García Lorca, the poet after whom Cohen named his daughter; it first appeared on a Spanish tribute album for the fiftieth anniversary of Lorca's death. In 'Tower of Song' he imagines Hank Williams in the same building, a hundred floors above: a songwriter's lineage placed in a joke. The cheap keyboard solo there is played by Cohen himself, deliberately clumsy.","A critical and commercial comeback, especially in Europe and Canada. Some old fans found the synthesisers a betrayal; others heard wit and menace they had missed. Now it is often ranked among his best, and its songs, especially 'Everybody Knows' and 'Tower of Song', are cited as late-career models of irony and gravity combined.","Start with 'Tower of Song' and hear the jokes; then 'Everybody Knows' for the dread. Notice how the women's voices sing the melody while Cohen speaks under it. Then hear Hank Williams's 'I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry' to meet the man upstairs."],"van-morrison-astral-weeks":["Eight long songs that feel like one improvised dream. Morrison's acoustic guitar and voice are surrounded by jazz players: Richard Davis's double bass wanders freely, Connie Kay's drums whisper, vibraphone and flute drift in, strings float on top. There are no hooks and few choruses; songs like 'Madame George' and 'Cyprus Avenue' stretch to nine or ten minutes, circling, repeating a phrase until it becomes an incantation. The voice stammers, soars and scats.","Morrison, a young singer from Belfast who had fronted the band Them and had a hit with 'Brown Eyed Girl', was stuck in a contract dispute after his producer Bert Berns died in 1967. Living near Boston, he signed with Warner Bros. and recorded the album in three sessions in New York in autumn 1968 with producer Lewis Merenstein and jazz musicians he had just met. Strings and horns were added afterward.","The musicians say Morrison sang and played in a vocal booth, barely speaking, and they simply followed; most songs were done in one or two takes, so what you hear is them discovering the song. The streets are real: Cyprus Avenue is a leafy road in east Belfast. Listen for Davis's bass: he played as freely as in a jazz trio, and the whole record hangs on him.","It sold poorly at first and reviews were few, but it became a cult treasure. Lester Bangs's 1979 essay made the case that it had saved him. Now it routinely appears near the top of all-time album lists, though Morrison has been dismissive of the record's mystique and of the way it was made.","Hear it whole, in one sitting, perhaps on a long walk. Start with 'Sweet Thing' for joy, 'Madame George' for longing. Follow the bass rather than the voice. Compare Miles Davis's 'In a Silent Way', from months later, and Joni Mitchell's 'Hejira' for other open-ended musical journeys."],"van-morrison-moondance":["Ten tight, warm songs, all under five minutes, with horns, piano and organ, a joyful soul-and-rhythm-and-blues record. 'And It Stoned Me' recalls a childhood day of rain; the title song swings like a jazz standard with walking bass and flute; 'Crazy Love' is a falsetto ballad; 'Caravan' is a radio-worship stomp; 'Into the Mystic' is a quietly ecstatic song about sailing home. The mood is unforced celebration.","After 'Astral Weeks' failed commercially, Morrison produced this himself in New York in late 1969, with a regular band and a horn section. He was living in Woodstock, in upstate New York, where Dylan and the Band were neighbours. Released early in 1970, it was his first big album success and set the pattern for his long career. Where the earlier album had been improvised by strangers, this one was rehearsed and arranged, with Morrison firmly in charge.","Morrison has said he wanted to sound like the rhythm-and-blues and soul singers he grew up with in Belfast, Ray Charles especially, and listen to how he bends phrases like a soul shouter. The swinging title song, now a jazz-club standard, was originally conceived around a saxophone riff; Morrison was a saxophonist before he was a singer.","Well reviewed and popular, it has always stood slightly in the shadow of 'Astral Weeks' for critics, while being the better loved for many listeners. It shows that Morrison could be both mystic and hit-maker. Its classic mixture of soul, jazz and Irish folk was later called 'Celtic soul', and 'Into the Mystic' has become one of his most covered songs.","Put on 'Into the Mystic' first, then 'Caravan', loud. Notice how the horns answer his voice the way a gospel choir answers a preacher. Hear Otis Redding's 'Otis Blue' and Sam Cooke's 'Live at the Harlem Square Club' for his models."],"bruce-springsteen-born-to-run":["Eight songs, layered and huge, full of piano, glockenspiel, surging guitars and Clarence Clemons's saxophone. 'Thunder Road' opens on harmonica and piano, a young man urging a girl into his car; 'Backstreets' is operatic betrayal; the title song is a wall of sound with a four-count countdown; 'Jungleland' closes with nine minutes of street drama and a long saxophone solo. The tone is romantic, cinematic, desperate: escape from small-town New Jersey at any cost.","Springsteen's first two albums had sold poorly and Columbia was losing patience. The title song alone took months to record in 1974, at a studio in Blauvelt, New York, as he tried to pack in the dense Phil Spector sound of his youth. Critic Jon Landau, who had written that he had seen rock and roll's future in Springsteen, came in as co-producer. In October 1975 Springsteen was on the covers of Time and Newsweek the same week.","The title song has dozens of overdubbed guitars, several keyboards and strings stacked into a single roar, with the glockenspiel cutting through at the top; Springsteen has said he wanted Roy Orbison's voice and Dylan's words over Spector's sound. On the cover photograph he leans on Clemons: the image of a Black and white partnership was part of the message.","It was a hit and a critical triumph, though the media hype prompted a backlash and doubts about whether he was a record-company creation. Its reputation has never slipped; it is regularly ranked among rock's greatest albums. Some critics find its romanticism overwrought; others hear it as the last flourish of 1950s rock's teenage dream.","Play 'Thunder Road' first; then 'Jungleland', listening for Clemons's solo as the emotional climax. Follow how Roy Bittan's piano carries the melodic line. Compare Dylan's 'Like a Rolling Stone' and Elvis's 'Mystery Train' for the lineage, then his own stark 'Nebraska' for the opposite response to the same streets."],"bruce-springsteen-nebraska":["Ten songs recorded alone on a four-track cassette machine: voice, acoustic guitar, harmonica, a bit of mandolin and glockenspiel, all bathed in tape hiss and echo. The title song is told by a young killer on death row, calm and unrepentant; 'Atlantic City' is a doomed man's plea; 'Highway Patrolman' a brother torn between duty and blood; 'Reason to Believe' ends the album with a bleak half-smile. The sound is lonely, stark, American gothic.","Springsteen recorded these songs at home in Colts Neck, New Jersey, in early January 1982, as demos for the E Street Band. The band versions felt wrong, so he released the cassette itself, after struggling to master it for vinyl. He was reading Flannery O'Connor and had seen Terrence Malick's film 'Badlands', about the 1958 killings by Charles Starkweather, the subject of the title song. Reagan's America was in recession.","Some songs recorded at the same full-band sessions became the album 'Born in the U.S.A.', whose title song began life in this acoustic set. 'Mansion on the Hill' borrows a Hank Williams title. Warren Zanes's 2023 book 'Deliver Me from Nowhere' describes how dark Springsteen's state of mind was during the recording.","Critics were impressed but puzzled; it sold modestly without a tour or single. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now seen as a founding document of lo-fi home recording and of the modern Americana revival. Johnny Cash recorded two of its songs within a year. Some prefer the bright E Street Springsteen; many now think this is his best.","Hear the title song alone, late at night. Notice how the hiss and echo make the room part of the song. Then hear Woody Guthrie's 'Dust Bowl Ballads', its ancestor, and Johnny Cash's 'American Recordings', its descendant. Play 'Born to Run' straight after to hear the same highways without hope."],"tom-waits-swordfishtrombones":["Fifteen short songs for a bizarre junkyard band: marimba, pump organ, bass, bagpipes, clanging metal, muted brass and a double bass slapped like a drum. Waits growls, croons and barks. 'Underground' is a stomping march of subterranean creatures; 'In the Neighborhood' is a funeral brass-band hymn to his street; 'Frank's Wild Years' is a deadpan spoken story over organ; 'Soldier's Things' is a tender piano ballad of army junk for sale.","Waits had spent the 1970s as a jazzy, beatnik piano balladeer on Asylum Records. In 1980 he married Kathleen Brennan, who introduced him to Captain Beefheart and to the instrument-builder Harry Partch. Asylum reportedly would not release this new direction; Island Records did, in 1983. Waits produced it himself, a first, and wrote many of the arrangements around sounds he found rather than chords he knew.","Harry Partch's home-built instruments and hobo songs are the key: Waits hunted down odd percussion and chromelodeon-like organs to get that sound. 'Johnsburg, Illinois' is a tiny love song for Brennan's hometown. The album is the first of a trilogy about the character Frank, continued in 'Rain Dogs' and 'Franks Wild Years'.","Critics hailed a reinvention; some old fans were bewildered. It is now regarded as the turn that made Waits one of the most original voices in rock, and its 'junkyard orchestra' was widely imitated. Few argue now about its importance; the question is whether it or 'Rain Dogs' is the peak.","Start with 'In the Neighborhood' for the brass, then 'Underground' for the percussion. Listen for what is being hit. Then hear Harry Partch's 'Barstow' and Captain Beefheart's 'Trout Mask Replica' for the sources, and Kurt Weill's 'Threepenny Opera' for the cabaret in his voice."],"tom-waits-rain-dogs":["Nineteen songs in a rattling, lurching sound, a carnival of the urban lost. Marc Ribot's angular guitar leads, with marimba, accordion, trombone and banging percussion. 'Singapore' opens as a sailors' shanty; 'Clap Hands' is dark ritual; 'Cemetery Polka' a family portrait; 'Tango Till They're Sore' a drunken tango; 'Time' and 'Hang Down Your Head' are tender ballads; 'Downtown Train' is a straight rock love song. Waits's voice ranges from growl to lullaby.","Waits had moved to New York, and he recorded the album there in 1985 with a band including Ribot, Robert Quine, and Keith Richards of the Rolling Stones on three songs. It continues the sound of 'Swordfishtrombones' and the tale of lost wanderers in the city, the 'rain dogs' of the title, strays who can't find their way home after rain washes away the scent.","The cover photograph, by Anders Petersen, was taken in a Hamburg bar in the late 1960s. 'Downtown Train' became a hit for Rod Stewart in 1989, a telling measure of how good a pop song hides under the clatter. Listen for Richards's slack, bluesy guitar on 'Big Black Mariah': a Stones sound in a very un-Stones room.","It was widely hailed as his best work, and it often appears on lists of the great albums of the 1980s. Critics praised its variety and unity; a few found it too long. Its sound, with Ribot's guitar and the junkyard percussion, influenced a generation of alternative and Americana musicians.","Start with 'Time' for the balladeer, then 'Singapore' and 'Clap Hands' for the carnival. Listen for Ribot's guitar, never quite where you expect, and for the percussion, which is often something other than a drum. Hear Kurt Weill's 'Threepenny Opera' and the Rolling Stones' 'Let It Bleed' beside it."],"carole-king-tapestry":["Twelve songs, with King's warm, slightly husky voice and propulsive gospel-style piano at the centre and a small, relaxed band around them. 'I Feel the Earth Move' opens with a stomping piano riff; 'It's Too Late' is a gentle breakup song; 'You've Got a Friend' a hymn of loyalty; 'Beautiful' a morning pep talk. King also reclaims her own 1960s songs for other singers, 'Will You Love Me Tomorrow' and '(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman', slowed and made personal.","King had been a hit songwriter in New York's Brill Building world of the 1960s, writing with Gerry Goffin for the Shirelles, Aretha Franklin and others. After their divorce she moved to Laurel Canyon in Los Angeles and started singing her own songs. She recorded 'Tapestry' in January 1971 at A&M Studios with producer Lou Adler and friends including James Taylor, Joni Mitchell and her husband Charles Larkey.","Toni Stern wrote the words for 'It's Too Late' and 'Where You Lead'. Listen to the piano: King's left hand plays gospel bass lines that give the songs their drive. The cat on the cover, named Telemachus, was her own. James Taylor released his own version of 'You've Got a Friend' the same year, and it was his that topped the charts.","It topped the American album chart for fifteen weeks and won four Grammys, including Album of the Year. For years it was the best-selling album by a woman. Critics then and now praise its warmth; some find it too comfortable beside Mitchell's sharper self-exposure. It remains the model of the songwriter reclaiming her own songs.","Hear 'Natural Woman' in King's version and then Aretha Franklin's: the same song as private confession and public declaration. Then play 'I Feel the Earth Move' for the piano, and 'So Far Away' for the loneliness under the warmth. Compare Joni Mitchell's 'Blue', made the same year in the same studio."],"randy-newman-sail-away":["Twelve short songs, most for Newman's lazy, drawling voice and rolling New Orleans piano, many framed by a lush orchestra he arranged himself. The title song, with sweeping strings, is a slave trader's cheerful sales pitch luring Africans to America. 'Political Science' is a jaunty plan to bomb the world flat; 'God's Song' has God mocking humanity; 'Old Man' is a hushed deathbed farewell; 'You Can Leave Your Hat On' is a sly striptease.","Newman was the nephew of the Hollywood film composers Alfred, Lionel and Emil Newman, and grew up around studio orchestras; he had been a staff songwriter before recording. His 1968 debut had been orchestral and unsuccessful. This album, produced by Lenny Waronker and Russ Titelman for Reprise and released in 1972, was his breakthrough with critics, though not with the public.","Newman's great device is the unreliable narrator: nearly every song is sung in the voice of someone he despises or pities, and the lovely orchestral writing is the lure. Listen to the title track's strings, which echo the hymn-like Americana of Aaron Copland; the prettiness is the trap. Joe Cocker's rock hit version of 'You Can Leave Your Hat On' in 1986 turned irony into sincerity.","Critics adored it and it is often called his best album; sales were modest. The irony has always been a risk: listeners sometimes take the narrators at face value. Newman later found wide success writing film scores, especially for Pixar, which some see as a return to his family's trade and others as a waste of his bite.","Hear the title song, then 'Political Science' and 'God's Song', and ask who is speaking and whether Newman agrees with a word of it. Then listen to Copland's 'Appalachian Spring' and Gershwin's 'Porgy and Bess' for the American orchestral idiom he uses against itself."],"randy-newman-good-old-boys":["Twelve songs making a loose portrait of the American South, sung by Newman in character over piano, rhythm section and his own orchestral arrangements. 'Rednecks' opens with a Southern narrator mocking Northern liberals' hypocrisy about race; 'Birmingham' is a steelworker's proud hymn to his city; 'Louisiana 1927' recalls the Great Mississippi Flood with a slow, hymn-like orchestra; 'Kingfish' and 'Every Man a King' evoke Governor Huey Long; 'Marie' is a drunk man's tender apology.","Newman, who spent some childhood years in New Orleans, first conceived the album as a story about one man, Johnny Cutler, a demo version of which was later released. He said 'Rednecks' was sparked by seeing the segregationist Lester Maddox mocked on a television talk show. Released by Reprise in 1974 amid the Watergate crisis, it was his most commercially successful album so far.","'Every Man a King' was written in 1935 by Huey Long himself with the bandleader Castro Carazo; Newman quotes the populist anthem straight. 'Rednecks' uses a racial slur repeatedly as its narrator's own word, which is the point of the satire and a reason the song is rarely played. 'Louisiana 1927' became an anthem of mourning after Hurricane Katrina in 2005.","Critics praised its ambition and nuance, though 'Rednecks' has made some uncomfortable from the start. It is now seen as one of the most penetrating portraits of the South in popular music, mixing sympathy and scorn. Debate continues over whether his irony can be heard correctly without explanation, and over who is entitled to sing it.","Start with 'Louisiana 1927' and listen to the orchestra, a sort of Copland hymn gone mournful. Then hear 'Rednecks' and 'Birmingham', and notice the shift in voice. Play Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere', recorded after the same 1927 flood, for the Delta's own account."],"nick-cave-the-boatmans-call":["Twelve slow, spare songs, most built on Cave's piano and deep baritone with the Bad Seeds playing softly behind: brushed drums, organ, bass, a little violin. 'Into My Arms' opens with a prayer to a God the singer does not believe in; 'Brompton Oratory' sets a love song in a London church; 'People Ain't No Good' is a ballad of disillusion; 'West Country Girl' and 'Black Hair' are intimate portraits. There is almost no volume; everything is close.","Cave, an Australian who had fronted the violent post-punk band the Birthday Party and made a name with dark gothic ballads, was living in London. He had just released 'Murder Ballads' (1996), gleefully bloodthirsty. This record followed the end of relationships with Viviane Carneiro, mother of his son, and with the singer PJ Harvey. Released by Mute in March 1997.","Listen for the religious language: Cave was reading the Bible seriously, and many songs are addressed to God as much as to a lover. 'Into My Arms' was sung by Cave at the funeral of his friend Michael Hutchence of INXS later that year; he asked cameras to be turned off. The songs are in effect a cycle in the lieder tradition, one voice and a piano.","Hailed by many as a brave change of direction, and a surprise from the author of 'Murder Ballads'; a few old fans missed the noise. It is now widely considered his finest record and the beginning of his mature style. The debate about whether his religiosity is sincere or a literary pose has run through his career since.","Play 'Into My Arms' first, late, and notice how few notes the piano uses. Then hear it beside Leonard Cohen's early records, the obvious ancestor, and Schubert's 'Die schöne Müllerin', a cycle of hope turning to loss for one voice and piano."],"nick-cave-skeleton-tree":["Eight songs with almost no conventional structure: floating synthesiser drones, loops and electronic pulses by Warren Ellis, with Cave's voice speaking, half-singing, sometimes cracking. 'Jesus Alone' opens with a harsh electronic drone and a litany of images; 'Girl in Amber' circles around a frozen moment; 'I Need You' is a raw, repetitive plea; 'Distant Sky' is a duet with the Danish soprano Else Torp; the title track ends with an almost tender acceptance.","Recording began in late 2014. In July 2015 Cave's fifteen-year-old son Arthur died after falling from a cliff near their home in Brighton. Much of the writing had been done beforehand, but the album was finished in grief, partly in France. It was released in September 2016, along with Andrew Dominik's documentary 'One More Time with Feeling', about the making of it.","Many lyrics were written before the death and now seem uncannily predictive; Cave has written about how uncomfortable that is. Listen to how many vocals are improvised, with the slips left in. Else Torp is best known for early and sacred music, including Arvo Pärt; her pure voice in 'Distant Sky' is like a visitor from a different tradition.","Reviewers were overwhelmed, many calling it one of the most powerful records ever made about grief. Some found it too painful to revisit. It began a later, ambient-leaning phase in Cave's work, continued on 'Ghosteen' (2019). The difficulty of writing about it critically, given its circumstances, is itself part of its reception.","Hear it whole once, then return to 'Distant Sky'. Notice how the electronics hold everything in suspension, like breath held. Hear it beside Mahler's 'Kindertotenlieder', songs on the deaths of children, and Arvo Pärt's 'Cantus in memoriam Benjamin Britten', for other music made from loss."],"elliott-smith-either-or":["Twelve songs, most built on intricate fingerpicked acoustic guitar and a hushed, double-tracked voice, some filled out with bass, drums and electric guitar that Smith played himself. 'Speed Trials' opens; 'Between the Bars' is a slow, whispered waltz; 'Ballad of Big Nothing' and 'Rose Parade' have Beatles-style melodies; 'Say Yes' ends the album on a small, hopeful note. The melodies are complex and graceful even as the words are bleak.","Smith, who had played in the Portland, Oregon, band Heatmiser, recorded the album largely himself in Portland and released it on the independent label Kill Rock Stars in February 1997. The title comes from the philosopher Søren Kierkegaard. That year Gus Van Sant used several of its songs in the film 'Good Will Hunting', along with Smith's 'Miss Misery', which was nominated for an Oscar.","Listen to the doubled vocals: Smith sang each line twice and layered them, so the whisper is actually two voices. The chord progressions often move in unexpected directions, closer to the Beatles and to classical harmony than to folk. Smith's appearance at the 1998 Oscars in a white suit, performing to a huge audience, made him briefly and uncomfortably famous.","Critics praised it as his breakthrough; it sold modestly until 'Good Will Hunting'. After Smith's death in 2003 it gained a sadder aura. It is now regarded as a defining record of 1990s indie music, and his intimate, multitracked style influenced many later songwriters, including Sufjan Stevens and Phoebe Bridgers.","Start with 'Between the Bars' and 'Say Yes'. Pay attention to the guitar picking and to how the melody moves away from the obvious note. Compare Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' and the Beatles' 'Revolver', then Sufjan Stevens's 'Carrie & Lowell' for the next generation."],"elliott-smith-xo":["Fourteen songs with fuller arrangements than before: piano, strings, horns, drums, bright electric guitars, alongside the familiar whispered voice. 'Sweet Adeline' begins with just guitar and voice and bursts into a full band; 'Waltz #2 (XO)' is a stately waltz about a family's fracture; 'Baby Britain' and 'Independence Day' are bright pop; 'I Didn't Understand' ends the album with Smith's voice alone, multitracked into a choir with no instruments.","After the attention from 'Good Will Hunting', Smith signed to DreamWorks Records and made XO in Los Angeles with producers Rob Schnapf and Tom Rothrock, released in August 1998. He had more money and time than ever before and used them for Beatles-style arrangements with real strings and brass. It sold better than anything he had done, though he remained uneasy with the attention.","'Sweet Adeline' takes its title from an old barbershop song Smith's grandmother sang. 'Pitseleh' is a Yiddish endearment for a little one. Listen to 'I Didn't Understand': the close vocal harmony recalls the Beach Boys and the Beatles, with Smith singing every part. On 'Waltz #2', notice how the stately triple time sits against words about a mother and a stepfather, a sore subject he returned to often.","Critics welcomed the fuller sound; a few missed the bedroom intimacy. It is sometimes considered his most accomplished record, sometimes a slightly overproduced step away from his strengths. Its mixture of whispered voice and orchestral pop has been influential on a generation of indie songwriters, and the argument over bedroom versus studio Smith has never settled.","Hear 'Waltz #2' first, then 'Sweet Adeline', waiting for the moment the band crashes in after the quiet opening. Listen for how the voice stays whispered even when the arrangement is loud. Compare the Beatles' 'Abbey Road' for the arrangements, and 'Either/Or' for the same songwriter with less."],"sufjan-stevens-illinois":["Twenty-two tracks, many with long, whimsical titles, making a sprawling portrait of the state of Illinois through history, myth and personal memory. Stevens plays banjo, piano, guitar, woodwinds and glockenspiel, with brass, strings and a choir of cheerleaders. 'Chicago' is a joyous road song; 'John Wayne Gacy, Jr.' a quiet, disturbing portrait of a serial killer; 'Casimir Pulaski Day' a hushed song about a friend's death. Short instrumental interludes stitch it together.","Released by Stevens's own label, Asthmatic Kitty, in July 2005, it was the second of what he announced as a 'Fifty States' project, after 'Michigan' (2003); he later admitted it was mostly a joke. Stevens, from Detroit and living in Brooklyn, drew on history books, family stories and his Christian faith. The original cover showed Superman, which had to be changed after a legal complaint.","The piano, marimba and woodwind patterns repeat and shift like Steve Reich's and Philip Glass's minimalism, which Stevens admired; he also studied the oboe. 'Come On! Feel the Illinoise!' is partly in five beats to the bar. In 2024 the album became a Broadway dance-theatre show, 'Illinoise', directed and choreographed by Justin Peck, which won a Tony for its choreography.","It topped many critics' lists for 2005 and became one of the defining records of indie music in that decade. Some found it overlong and too twee. Its mixture of folk, orchestral pop and minimalism was widely imitated. Stevens's religious sincerity, rare in indie rock, remains part of the discussion.","Start with 'Chicago', then 'Casimir Pulaski Day', a song that sounds gentle until you follow the story. Listen for the repeating patterns under the melodies, and the brass that breaks in like a school band. Hear Steve Reich's 'Music for 18 Musicians' and Copland's 'Appalachian Spring' for its two American ancestors."],"sufjan-stevens-carrie-and-lowell":["Eleven quiet songs, mostly just fingerpicked acoustic guitar or banjo, with piano and soft synthesiser haze, and Stevens's voice doubled and whispered. 'Death with Dignity' opens; 'Should Have Known Better' moves from grief to a small light; 'Fourth of July' is a conversation with his dying mother; 'No Shade in the Shadow of the Cross' is almost unbearable; 'Blue Bucket of Gold' ends in a long, fading drone.","Stevens's mother, Carrie, who had left the family when he was a small child and struggled with mental illness and addiction, died of cancer in 2012. For a few summers in his childhood she had been married to Lowell Brams, who later co-founded Stevens's record label, Asthmatic Kitty. Stevens made the album in several places, some of it on a phone, and released it in March 2015.","The songs are full of Oregon place names from those summers with Carrie and Lowell. The doubled, whispered vocals are close to Elliott Smith's technique. The album's photographs, including the cover, come from the family's own snapshots. Listen to the closing drone of 'Blue Bucket of Gold': after all the words, the album dissolves into a long wash of sound, grief with nothing left to say.","It was received as one of the most moving albums of the decade, and appeared near the top of many year-end lists. Some found its sparseness a relief after the complexity of 'Illinois'. It is now often compared with Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' and Springsteen's 'Nebraska' as a classic of solitary, intimate recording.","Hear it whole, quietly, preferably on headphones so the doubled whisper sits close. Start with 'Should Have Known Better' and notice the shift in mood at the end, when a brighter figure enters. Compare Elliott Smith's 'Either/Or', and Fauré's 'Requiem' for a gentle, unfrightened music of death."],"the-teddy-bears-to-know-him-is-to-love-him":["A slow, hushed ballad in a lilting triple time, barely louder than a lullaby. Annette Kleinbard sings the lead in a small, pure voice close to the microphone while two young men hum and croon beneath her, and a guitar picks out gentle broken chords. There is no drama and no climax: the whole record is one devoted confession repeated, its title returning like a refrain in a hymn, and it is over in about two and a half minutes.","Phil Spector was a Los Angeles high-school student of eighteen when he wrote this, formed a trio with two friends, and paid for a session at Gold Star Studios. It came out on the small Doré label as a B-side, was flipped by radio, and reached number one in the United States late in 1958. For a teenager with no connections it was a staggering start, and it taught Spector that the producer, not the singer, could be the real author of a record.","The title came from the inscription on the gravestone of Spector's father, who had taken his own life when Phil was nine; Spector changed only the tense. Knowing that, the song stops being a teenage crush and becomes something odder: a son's private grief dressed in the clothes of a love song, sung by a girl who did not know where the words came from.","At the time it was one more soft teen ballad in a year full of them, and the Teddy Bears could not follow it up; the group was finished within a year. Its standing rose with Spector's. Historians now hear it as the seed of his method, a producer treating the recording studio as the instrument. The Beatles sang it in their early club and audition sets, and Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris recorded a fine trio version in 1987.","Listen for how dry and close the lead voice is: this is the opposite of the later Wall of Sound, with almost no echo at all. Then play it straight after Be My Baby, five years later, and hear the same producer's ear for a girl's voice swallowed by a cathedral of noise. The single mix, in mono, is the one to hear."],"the-drifters-there-goes-my-baby":["A rhythm-and-blues vocal group record with violins on it, which in 1959 was the whole shock. Ben E. King's lead is raw and pleading, the group moans behind him, and over a heavy, loping beat a string section plays a swaying, faintly Middle Eastern figure while a kettledrum thuds away underneath. The effect is lush and a little seasick at once, as if a doo-wop group had wandered into a film score.","The original Drifters had been sacked wholesale by their manager in 1958 and replaced by a Harlem group called the Five Crowns, with the young Ben E. King among them. Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, the writers of Hound Dog, were producing for Atlantic in New York and decided to dress this new line-up in strings, a device then reserved for white pop crooners. It reached the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart and the pop top ten.","Listen to the timpani: it is noticeably out of tune with the band, and nobody fixed it. Atlantic's Jerry Wexler reportedly thought the record was a mess and did not want it released. That woozy mismatch between the kettledrum, the strings and the group is exactly what makes it sound new; the accident became the texture that Phil Spector, who apprenticed with Leiber and Stoller soon after, would build a career on.","Listeners bought it in large numbers while some in the business found it pretentious for an R&B record. History has sided with its ambition. It is now routinely cited as the first rhythm-and-blues record with a real string arrangement, the bridge between doo-wop and the orchestral pop of the Brill Building years, and the model for Stand by Me, Spanish Harlem and every uptown soul record that followed.","Start by ignoring the singer and following the strings, which never settle into ordinary accompaniment. Then listen to the drummer and timpani fight over the beat. Compare it with Hound Dog, by the same writers three years earlier, to hear how far they travelled. A good mono Atlantic compilation of the Drifters is the way in."],"the-shirelles-will-you-love-me-tomorrow":["A mid-tempo ballad sung by Shirley Owens over a light Latin-tinged beat, with strings sweeping in behind and the other three Shirelles answering in soft harmony. The song is a single question, asked in the dark by a girl weighing whether to trust a boy for one night: will this still be love in the morning? The melody climbs to that question and leaves it hanging each time, and the record fades without an answer.","Gerry Goffin and Carole King, a young married couple writing for Don Kirshner's Aldon Music near the Brill Building, wrote it when King was eighteen. The Shirelles were four schoolfriends from Passaic, New Jersey, signed to Florence Greenberg's small Scepter label and produced by Luther Dixon. Owens at first thought the song too white and too country for the group. It went to number one in the United States in early 1961, the first by a Black girl group.","The strings were King's own arrangement, and she is often said to have played the kettledrum on the session herself. Listen too for what the song refuses to say: the subject is plainly sex, yet every line is a question about the future rather than a statement about tonight, which is how it passed radio censors in 1960 while every teenager understood it perfectly.","Some stations did balk at the subject, but it sold vastly and made the girl-group sound a commercial category. Its reputation has only grown: it is now heard as the first great statement of female uncertainty in rock-era pop, and as the song that made Goffin and King the model Brill Building team. King's own slower version on Tapestry in 1971 invited a long argument about which is the truer reading.","Listen for the strings first entering, and how Owens's voice is lighter than you expect, almost conversational. Then compare King's 1971 piano version, which turns a teenage question into a grown woman's memory. A clean mono Scepter transfer keeps the strings and the voices in their original balance."],"the-shirelles-baby-its-you":["A slow, aching ballad built on a wordless 'sha-la-la' chant from the group that opens the record and returns between verses like a sigh. Shirley Owens sings of loving a boy whom everyone warns her against, her voice cracking slightly at the top, over a thin, churchy electric organ and a patient shuffle. It is small and unpolished beside later girl-group records, and that intimacy is its charm.","It is an early Burt Bacharach song, written with Mack David, Hal David's elder brother, and Luther Dixon, who produced for Scepter and took his credit under a pseudonym. Bacharach was still best known as Marlene Dietrich's musical director and was only beginning his Brill Building career. The record reached the American top ten in 1962 and became a staple of the British beat groups' repertoire.","The session was reportedly built over Bacharach's own demo track, with the Shirelles' voices added on top, which may explain why the backing sounds so bare and different from their other records. Listen for the harmonic swerve in the middle section, a slightly unexpected chord change that already has Bacharach's fingerprint: melody that turns a corner just where your ear expects it to go straight.","It was a solid hit rather than a sensation, and its long afterlife came through covers. The Beatles recorded it on their first album in 1963, one of several Shirelles songs in their set, and that made it known to millions who never heard the original. Critics now use it to show how deep the Beatles' debt to American girl groups ran, a debt they paid openly in interviews.","Hear the opening chant as the record's real hook, then listen for how little is happening beneath Owens: organ, drums, bass, hardly anything else. Then play the Beatles' version with John Lennon singing and notice how closely they copied the arrangement, down to the chant. A good Shirelles anthology collects it with the other Scepter hits."],"the-marvelettes-please-mr-postman":["A bright, stomping two and a half minutes in which a girl begs the mailman to check his bag once more for a letter from her boyfriend. Gladys Horton's lead is urgent and a little rough, the group chants the title behind her, and the rhythm section pounds a heavy backbeat with handclaps and a honking saxophone. It sounds less like a polished pop record than a schoolyard game that got very loud.","The Marvelettes were high-school girls from Inkster, Michigan, who entered a talent contest and were sent to audition for Berry Gordy's young Motown company in Detroit. The song grew out of a blues by a local writer, William Garrett, reworked by the group and Motown staff. Released on Tamla, it became the company's first number one on the American pop chart in December 1961, proof that a Black-owned Detroit label could top the national market.","The drummer on the session is widely reported to have been the young Marvin Gaye, then a Motown session player hoping for a solo career; listen for how hard and simple the backbeat is. And notice that the song is a girl-group record from a label that would soon perfect a very different, more polished sound: the rough edges here are the raw material the Supremes would later smooth away.","It was a hit at once and has never needed rescuing. The Beatles recorded it on With the Beatles in 1963 and the Carpenters took it to number one again in 1975, which made it one of the few songs to top the chart in two different decades. Historians of Motown place it at the start of the company's national story, before the house style had settled.","Count how many times you hear the word of the title before the first verse ends; the song is almost all hook. Then listen to the handclaps and piano, the Motown engine in its crudest form. Compare the Beatles' cover, sung by Lennon at a harder tempo, and then the Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go three years later."],"the-drifters-up-on-the-roof":["A gentle, unhurried song about escaping a crowded tenement by climbing to the rooftop, where the city noise falls away and the stars are close. Rudy Lewis sings it softly, almost privately, the group harmonising behind him, and the strings rise and fall like a breeze across the tar. The melody lifts with the singer on the chorus, and the verses sink back to street level, so the song's shape acts out its story.","Gerry Goffin and Carole King wrote it in 1962, King composing the melody on the drive home, as a song of urban refuge for the Drifters, then Atlantic's most sophisticated vocal group. By now Rudy Lewis had replaced Ben E. King as lead. The production kept the strings-and-baion style Leiber and Stoller had developed for the group, and the record reached the American top five early in 1963.","Listen to how the arrangement opens up at the chorus, strings climbing as the singer climbs the stairs; the orchestration is a small piece of scene painting. The song also carries a quiet social point: its rooftop is the only private place available to a working-class Black New Yorker in a crowded building, a detail sung so softly that most listeners hear only a pretty love song.","It was a major hit and has become one of the most covered songs of the Goffin and King catalogue, recorded by Laura Nyro, James Taylor and King herself on Tapestry. Critics often name it the most lyrical of the Brill Building songs, the point where teenage pop began to write about place, class and solitude rather than dates and heartbreak alone.","Listen for the lift into the chorus and the way the strings follow the singer upwards. Then play Carole King's own version from 1971 for the writer's view, plainer and more wistful. Put it beside On Broadway, the Drifters' next great city song, to hear two sides of the same New York."],"francoise-hardy-tous-les-garcons-et-les-filles":["A slow, melancholy pop song in which a teenage girl watches all the couples of her age walking hand in hand and wonders when she will know what that feels like. Françoise Hardy sings in a low, breathy, almost toneless voice, never pushing, over a plain strummed guitar, a soft backbeat and a ghostly chorus of backing voices. It sounds shy rather than sad, and that shyness is its whole character.","Hardy was eighteen, a Paris student who had taught herself guitar and written the song herself, when Disques Vogue released it in 1962. The French youth craze called yé-yé was built on translated American hits; Hardy's own song stood apart. She sang it on television during the results broadcast of the October 1962 referendum, a vast audience, and the record went on to sell well over a million copies.","Listen to how little the voice does: Hardy barely varies her volume, and the melody moves in small steps within a narrow range. That flatness, which French critics at first took for inexperience, is exactly what made her the opposite of the brassy American girl groups and of the chanson tradition's big theatrical singers, and it prefigures a whole later line of cool, withdrawn female pop vocalists.","It made her a star overnight and an icon of French youth, which Hardy herself later found embarrassing, dismissing her early records as naive. Critics have since reversed her verdict: the song is now heard as a model of understated pop songwriting by a young woman writing her own material at a time when few did. English-speaking pop, from Bob Dylan to Blur, took notice of her.","Listen once to the words for the feeling, even without French: the loneliness is in the voice. Then notice how the backing voices sit far behind her, like the crowd she is watching. Compare Lesley Gore's American records from the next year for a completely different model of the teenage girl in pop."],"the-crystals-da-doo-ron-ron":["Two minutes and twenty seconds of joy at full pelt. A girl tells how she met a boy on a Monday, and the group answers every line with the nonsense syllables of the title, while behind them pianos, guitars, saxophones, castanets and handclaps pile into one roaring, echoing mass. Hal Blaine's drums hammer the beat, the saxophone takes a honking solo, and the whole thing sounds as if it were recorded in a stone hall.","Phil Spector wrote it with the husband-and-wife team Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich, and recorded it at Gold Star Studios in Los Angeles with the session players later nicknamed the Wrecking Crew. It came out on Philles, his own label, and was a top-three hit in America and Britain in 1963. By now Spector used the Crystals' name as a brand rather than a group; he could put any voice on the record he chose.","The lead is not by an original Crystal at first: Darlene Love reportedly sang a guide vocal, and Spector then had sixteen-year-old La La Brooks of the Crystals record the lead that was released. And the nonsense title is a placeholder that Barry and Greenwich never replaced. Listen too for the echo chamber on everything, drums included, which is what turns ordinary instruments into one blurred wall.","It was an immediate hit and has never lost its place as one of the purest examples of Spector's style. Some later critics find the Crystals records the most enjoyable of all his productions precisely because they are less grand than the Ronettes' or the Righteous Brothers'. The treatment of the group as interchangeable voices has become part of the darker reassessment of Spector the man.","Listen with the volume up, and try to pick out individual instruments; you mostly cannot, which is the point. Then find the saxophone solo, one of the few things that stands out of the mass. Hear it alongside Be My Baby from the same summer to feel Spector's two moods, the romp and the ache."],"the-ronettes-be-my-baby":["It begins with a drum alone: a heavy kick, two beats, then a snare crack, the most imitated opening in pop. Ronnie Bennett's voice enters, nasal and yearning with a little tremor, promising devotion to a boy she has just met, and around her the Wall of Sound rises: pianos and guitars doubled many times, castanets, strings, horns, a choir of backing voices, all washed in echo. The chorus arrives like a wave breaking.","Phil Spector wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich and recorded it at Gold Star in Los Angeles with his session players, among them Hal Blaine on drums, and with Sonny Bono and the young Cher reportedly among the backing voices. The Ronettes were three girls from Spanish Harlem, two sisters and their cousin, admired for their beehive hair and eyeliner. It reached number two in America in 1963. Ronnie later married Spector.","Listen to the fade: the strings keep climbing while the voices repeat the chorus, and the record seems to fall away into its own echo rather than ending. And listen to how the drum pattern of the opening returns every time the chorus lands. Brian Wilson has told of hearing it on a car radio and having to pull over; he spent years trying to answer it, and Don't Worry Baby and Pet Sounds are the result.","It was a huge hit at once, and its reputation has grown into near-universal agreement that it is one of the greatest pop singles ever made. The drum intro has been copied by everyone from the Jesus and Mary Chain to Amy Winehouse. The later accounts of Spector's cruelty to Ronnie, and his conviction for murder in 2009, now shadow how the record is heard, but not whether it is heard.","Start by listening only to the drums through the whole song, then play it again for the voice, and a third time just to feel the chorus arrive. Then listen to the Beach Boys' God Only Knows or Good Vibrations to hear what Brian Wilson learned. The original mono single mix is the only way it was meant to sound."],"phil-spector-a-christmas-gift-for-you":["Thirteen tracks: familiar seasonal songs such as White Christmas, Frosty the Snowman, Sleigh Ride and Santa Claus Is Coming to Town, each dragged into the Wall of Sound with sleigh bells, castanets, pounding drums and massed pianos, sung in turn by the Ronettes, the Crystals, Darlene Love and Bob B. Soxx and the Blue Jeans. One new song, Christmas (Baby Please Come Home), is the emotional centre, and the album ends with Spector himself speaking over Silent Night.","Spector recorded it through the late summer and autumn of 1963 at Gold Star, treating an album, then usually a few hits and filler, as a single designed whole. It was released on 22 November 1963, the day President Kennedy was shot in Dallas. In a country in mourning a jolly Christmas record sold poorly, and Spector was bitter about the failure for years.","Listen to Darlene Love on Christmas (Baby Please Come Home): the only original song on the record, written with Barry and Greenwich, and the one track where the Wall of Sound becomes truly desperate, the piano hammering like a fist on a door. And at the end, Spector's spoken thanks to his artists over Silent Night reveals how completely he thought of the album as his own.","Its commercial failure was reversed by reissues from the 1970s onward, most famously on the Beatles' Apple label in 1972, and it is now counted among the best Christmas albums in pop and a standard of seasonal radio. Critics note it as one of the first rock-era albums conceived as a whole. Darlene Love sang her song on American late-night television every December for nearly thirty years.","Start with Darlene Love's Christmas (Baby Please Come Home), then go to the Ronettes' Sleigh Ride with its horse whinny and the Crystals' Santa Claus Is Coming to Town. Listen for the sleigh bells mixed into the Wall. The original mono mix is preferable to later fake-stereo versions."],"lesley-gore-its-my-party":["A teenage girl's birthday party ruined because her boyfriend has gone off with another girl, and she insists on her right to cry about it. Lesley Gore sings in a bright, double-tracked voice that makes her sound both stronger and younger, over a shuffling beat with blaring horns and a group of backing singers. It is barely two minutes long, and its melodrama is played with a straight face and a wink at once.","Gore was a sixteen-year-old schoolgirl from New Jersey when Quincy Jones, then an executive at Mercury Records, chose the song from a pile of demos and produced it in New York in March 1963. He heard that Phil Spector was about to record it with the Crystals and rushed his version out within days. It reached number one in America, and Gore was performing on national television within weeks.","Listen to the double-tracking of Gore's voice, two takes laid on top of each other, a studio trick that thickens a young voice and gives it the slightly unreal shine of the record. Jones, who would later produce Michael Jackson's Thriller, was already using the studio to build a star rather than simply capture one. The race with Spector over the same song shows how fast the Brill Building machine moved.","Critics at the time treated it as disposable teen pop, and for years it was cited as the very model of sixties bubblegum. Later writers have been kinder, noting the precision of Jones's production and Gore's surprisingly assured voice. It spawned a sequel in Judy's Turn to Cry, and it is now often heard as the setup to which her next single, You Don't Own Me, delivered the punchline.","Listen to the horns in the intro and how quickly the song reaches its chorus. Then play You Don't Own Me, released just months later, and hear the same singer and producer turn from self-pity to defiance. A good mono Mercury compilation will have both."],"lesley-gore-you-dont-own-me":["A dark, slow-building declaration: a young woman telling a boyfriend that he does not possess her, cannot tell her what to do or how to dress, and that she will go her own way. It begins almost in a whisper over a minor-key vamp, then swells with each verse into a full orchestral climax, Gore's voice rising from level calm to open anger. Everything about it, from the melody to the arrangement, pushes forward.","Written by two Philadelphia songwriters, John Madara and David White, and produced by Quincy Jones with an arrangement by Claus Ogerman, it was released at the end of 1963. It reached number two in America early in 1964, held off the top only by the Beatles' I Want to Hold Your Hand, at the moment when Beatlemania was sweeping the American charts and the teen-girl market was changing.","Listen to the melody in the verses: it hovers over a few notes like someone controlling her temper, and only in the chorus does it leap. That structure is the song's argument. Gore herself later said she heard it as a humanist song rather than a narrowly feminist one; she came out as gay publicly only in 2005, which gives its insistence on independence a second meaning many listeners now hear.","It was a hit without being taken seriously as a statement until the women's movement of the late sixties and seventies adopted it, and it has been revived at political marches ever since, notably in a 2016 voting campaign video. It is now regarded as one of the first feminist anthems in American pop, a record whose meaning grew as the society around it caught up.","Hear the first verse as a quiet warning and follow how the strings and voices pile in behind Gore; the final chorus sounds nothing like the opening. Then play It's My Party, from the same year, and hear how far she travels. The mono single is still the most direct version."],"dionne-warwick-anyone-who-had-a-heart":["A torch song that refuses to sit still. The singer tells a lover that anyone with a heart would see how she loves him, and the music keeps catching and shifting under her, bars of uneven length making the rhythm stumble like a halting breath. Dionne Warwick sings it cool and exact, never oversinging, while Bacharach's arrangement moves from bare piano to sighing strings and massed voices at the climax.","Burt Bacharach and Hal David wrote it for Warwick, a church-trained singer from East Orange, New Jersey, whom they had discovered singing backing vocals on Drifters sessions in New York. They were writing almost exclusively for her by 1963, and Bacharach produced and arranged her records for Scepter. It reached the American top ten in early 1964, while Cilla Black's cover topped the British chart.","Try tapping your foot and it will fail you: the verse changes metre repeatedly, from four beats to five to seven-and-a-half, following the natural stress of David's words. Session musicians reportedly struggled to play it. Bacharach did this not for show but because he wrote the melody first as a vocal line, then let it take the time it needed, which is why the song feels so much like speech.","Pop audiences took the irregular rhythm in their stride and the record was a hit on both sides of the Atlantic. Musicians and critics came to treat it as the proof that Bacharach was a composer of real sophistication rather than a hitmaker; jazz players adopted it and later it was recorded by artists from Dusty Springfield to Luther Vandross. The British argument over whose version was better, Warwick's or Black's, ran for years.","Try counting the beats in the first verse; you will lose the count, and that is how the song works on you. Then listen for Warwick's restraint on the high notes. Compare Cilla Black's more dramatic version, and Brubeck's Take Five for another popular record that made an odd metre feel natural."],"dionne-warwick-walk-on-by":["A woman asks a former lover who passes her in the street to keep walking, so he will not see her cry. Warwick sings with complete control, pride holding grief in check, while the accompaniment keeps interrupting: a mournful two-trumpet figure answering her lines, a light piano, strings that swell and fall back, and backing singers who echo her like an inner voice. It is sad and elegant at once, and very cool in temperature.","Bacharach and David wrote it for Warwick and recorded it in New York in 1963; it was released in spring 1964 and went to the American top ten. Bacharach produced and arranged it himself, one of the first pop songwriters to control the whole record. The Beatles had arrived in America weeks earlier, and this was one of the few American records of that year to sound entirely independent of them.","Listen to the chord under the title phrase: a harmony that sounds unresolved and a little jazzy, which leaves the song hanging just as the singer's composure is. And notice the trumpets, which play a small falling phrase instead of a normal horn fanfare; they sound like someone sighing. Isaac Hayes's twelve-minute version in 1969 took that single chord and stretched it into a whole symphonic soul epic.","It was an instant hit and has only risen in estimation. It is regularly listed among the finest pop singles of the decade and is the central example critics use for Bacharach's distinctive harmonic language. Its afterlife is extraordinary: Isaac Hayes's version helped invent symphonic soul, and the song has been reinterpreted by the Stranglers, Cyndi Lauper and many others.","Start by listening for the trumpets answering Warwick, then for the hanging chord on the title. Then play Isaac Hayes's version on Hot Buttered Soul for a radical reimagining. For comparison with an older school of heartbreak, try Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours; this is the sixties answer to it."],"the-righteous-brothers-youve-lost-that-lovin-feelin":["It opens with a deep, slow baritone almost alone, Bill Medley accusing a lover of going cold, and builds over nearly four minutes into one of the largest sounds on any pop record. Strings, brass, massed pianos, drums and choir climb steadily until the middle section, where the music drops away to a stark, pounding beat and Medley and Bobby Hatfield trade desperate pleas, then everything rises again to a shouting climax.","Phil Spector wrote it with Barry Mann and Cynthia Weil, and recorded it in Los Angeles for Philles. The Righteous Brothers were two white singers from Orange County who sounded Black enough on radio that they were first heard as a soul act. Released at the end of 1964, it went to number one in America and Britain early the next year, just as British groups were dominating the charts.","It was far longer than radio then allowed, so Spector had the label print a false running time of 3:05, shaving nearly a minute. When Mann first heard the playback at the slow tempo he reportedly thought it was playing at the wrong speed. Listen to the middle section: the orchestra drops out almost completely, the drums pound alone, and the two voices overlap in something like church call and response.","DJs were at first bewildered by its length and slowness, and some thought it too gloomy for pop radio; it became a colossal hit anyway. It was later judged by the broadcasting-rights organisation BMI to be the most-played song on American radio and television in the twentieth century. Critics now treat it as Spector's grandest achievement and a template for the soul ballad as epic.","Listen to the opening with nothing else in your head, just the baritone and space. Then track how the arrangement accumulates, and brace for the breakdown in the middle. Compare the Walker Brothers' The Sun Ain't Gonna Shine Anymore, its direct British descendant. A mono transfer from the original master is essential."],"the-shangri-las-remember-walking-in-the-sand":["A girl remembers walking on a beach with a boy who has left her by letter, and the record plays the memory as if it were a film. It opens with seagull cries and crashing surf, a slow piano and the group's hushed voices, then Mary Weiss's lead, flat and unadorned, enters over a sparse rhythm. The song keeps collapsing into silence and rebuilding, and the gulls and waves return between the sections like flashbacks.","George 'Shadow' Morton, a self-invented producer with no formal training, wrote it and recorded it with the Shangri-Las, two pairs of sisters from Cambridge Heights in Queens, New York. It came out on Red Bird, the label newly set up by Jerry Leiber, Mike Stoller and George Goldner, and reached the American top five in 1964. The girls were teenagers still at school.","The seagulls and surf are the heart of the record's invention: sound effects, not instruments, carry the emotional weight, turning a pop single into a small radio play. Morton's method was cinema rather than music theory. The demo of the song is said to have had the teenage Billy Joel on piano, one of those details that sound invented but may well be true.","It was a hit and made the group stars, and its theatricality divided critics at first between those who found it melodramatic kitsch and those who heard real feeling. Opinion has swung strongly toward the latter. The Shangri-Las' records are now claimed as ancestors by punk and new wave; the New York Dolls and Blondie cited them, and Amy Winehouse drew heavily on their look and sound.","Listen in the dark, with the effects doing their work. Notice the long pauses: the song trusts silence. Then play Leader of the Pack, from later the same year, to hear Morton's method at its boldest. Compare Brian Wilson's use of found sound on Pet Sounds two years later."],"the-shangri-las-leader-of-the-pack":["A tragic teenage drama compressed into under three minutes. The girls question Mary Weiss about her boyfriend, she answers in spoken asides, then the song tells how her parents forced her to end it with the motorcycle-riding boy from the wrong side of town, how he rode off into the rain, and how he was killed. Revving engines, screeching tyres and a crash break into the music, and a thumping piano drives it to an anguished end.","Shadow Morton wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich for Red Bird, and it went to number one in America late in 1964. It belongs to a brief teen-tragedy craze of songs about young lovers dying, but it also plugged into a moral panic about motorcycle gangs and juvenile delinquency. The BBC declined to play it for a time, and it still reached the British top twenty.","Listen to the spoken dialogue at the start: the other girls ask the questions, and Weiss answers in a flat, unexcited speaking voice, a Queens teenager rather than a singer. That deadpan realism, against the lurid sound effects, is what keeps the record from being just a joke. The motorcycle noise was reportedly recorded with a real bike brought into or just outside the studio.","It was a massive hit and an easy target for parody, which it has attracted ever since. For years it stood as shorthand for sixties pop kitsch. Later critics have heard something more in it, a portrait of class and parental control in which the girl has no power at all, and punk bands from the Ramones onward adopted its leather-and-attitude image wholesale.","Hear it as a three-minute film, not a song: the setup, the conflict, the crash. Listen for the piano that drives everything. Then compare Remember (Walking in the Sand) for Morton's quieter method, and Lesley Gore's You Don't Own Me for a very different girl facing a very different pressure."],"dusty-springfield-i-just-dont-know-what-to-do-with-myself":["A woman alone after a break-up, unable to do anything that does not remind her of the lover who left. Dusty Springfield begins quietly and intimately over a sparse piano and a soft beat, then the orchestra and backing voices build behind her until, on the climactic chorus, she lets go with the huge, husky sound that was her trademark. The song is a Bacharach-David melody that keeps rising and falling like someone trying not to cry.","Bacharach and David had written it for Tommy Hunt in America; Springfield heard it and recorded it in London with producer Johnny Franz and arranger Ivor Raymonde for Philips. It reached number three in Britain in 1964. She had left the folk trio the Springfields the year before and was fast becoming the country's leading female pop singer, fiercely devoted to American soul and Brill Building writing.","Listen to the breath Springfield takes before the big chorus; she sings with a microphone technique borrowed from the soul records she loved rather than from British variety tradition, which is why she sounds so unlike other British pop singers of the day. Behind the lavish sound she was famously insecure about her own voice, fussing over every take, and her perfectionism shows in how exact the phrasing is.","It established her firmly as a singer of American material and the British rival to Dionne Warwick in Bacharach songs, a rivalry Warwick reportedly did not enjoy. Its reputation has endured; the White Stripes' bare garage-rock version in 2003 introduced it to a new generation and showed how strong the song is stripped of its orchestra.","Listen for the moment in the final chorus when Springfield opens up. Then compare Warwick's cooler Walk On By to hear two schools of singing the same writers. Finish with the White Stripes' version for contrast. The original mono single mix gives the voice its full weight."],"petula-clark-downtown":["An invitation to the lonely to go downtown, where the lights and noise and people will make them forget their troubles. It opens with a bright piano figure and a quiet verse, then climbs to a chorus that explodes with brass, strings and backing voices, the title shouted with delight. Petula Clark sings it with clear, warm optimism, and the arrangement keeps adding colour, like neon switching on street by street.","Tony Hatch, a staff producer at Pye Records in London, wrote it after a first trip to New York, when he stood in Times Square and was struck by its energy. Clark had been a British child star since the war and was then living in France. Released in late 1964, it reached the top of the American chart early in 1965, making her the first British woman to do so in the rock era, at the peak of the British Invasion.","Listen to the verse against the chorus: the verse is almost muted and tentative, the chorus loud and bright, so the song acts out the move from a dark room into the city lights. Hatch borrowed the scale of American orchestral pop, and the record is essentially a London answer to the Brill Building, written by a British staff songwriter working in the American way.","It was a worldwide hit and won a Grammy. Critics sometimes dismissed Clark as a family entertainer, and the song as easy listening, but its craft is widely admired now, and its vision of the city as refuge has made it a recurring choice in film and television. Hatch's later work with Clark is often counted among the best British songwriting of the decade.","Listen for the first entry of the brass at the chorus; the arrangement is built around that moment. Then compare the Drifters' Up on the Roof, where the city is something to escape. The original mono Pye single is punchier than later stereo mixes."],"france-gall-poupee-de-cire-poupee-de-son":["A bright, bouncing song in which a teenage singer describes herself as a wax doll stuffed with sound, singing of love she has never felt. France Gall's voice is high and girlish, the orchestra jaunty with brassy stabs and a driving beat, and the melody leaps about cheerfully. On the surface it is pure yé-yé candy; underneath, the words question the very pop industry that made the record.","Serge Gainsbourg wrote it for Gall, then seventeen, and it won the Eurovision Song Contest for Luxembourg in Naples in March 1965. Gainsbourg was then a respected but unfashionable songwriter of adult chanson looking for commercial success in the youth market. The win made both of them rich, and the song became a hit across Europe and beyond, recorded in several languages.","Listen to the title image: a doll of wax and sound, a singer who is merely a mouthpiece. Gainsbourg was writing an ironic comment on Gall herself, and on yé-yé stars in general, and giving it to her to sing to a huge television audience. The double meaning passed almost everyone by at the time, though not the knowing listeners who soon realised Gainsbourg was toying with his singers.","It won and it sold, but the song has also drawn lasting criticism for how Gainsbourg used a teenage singer, especially after his next song for her, Les sucettes, carried a double meaning she did not understand when she recorded it. Critics today admire the song's cleverness while treating its authorship as part of a troubling pattern. Gall moved on to a long, serious career.","Enjoy the song first as pure pop, with its bouncing orchestra. Then think about the title and hear the irony. Compare Françoise Hardy's Tous les garçons et les filles for a yé-yé singer writing her own feeling, and Gainsbourg's later Histoire de Melody Nelson for where his fascination with young muses went."],"the-walker-brothers-the-sun-aint-gonna-shine-anymore":["A vast, doom-laden ballad of loss. Scott Engel's rich baritone leads, joined by John Maus's harmony, over an arrangement that heaps strings, brass, choir and thundering drums into a wall of echo. The song says that without the lost lover the world has lost its light, and the arrangement makes that sound cosmic rather than personal: each chorus arrives like a storm front, and the singing never loses its stately, controlled gloom.","The Walker Brothers were three Americans, none named Walker, who moved to London in 1965 and found success there that had eluded them at home. The song, by Bob Crewe and Bob Gaudio of the Four Seasons, had been a minor American single for Frankie Valli. Produced by Johnny Franz with arranger Ivor Raymonde, it went to number one in Britain in March 1966 amid scenes of fan hysteria.","Listen to how closely the production copies Phil Spector's Righteous Brothers sound, from the slow tempo to the pounding drums and choir; the group's early British hits were deliberately modelled on it. But listen too to Engel's phrasing, already more detached and theatrical than a soul singer's; the future Scott Walker, the solo artist of deathly orchestral albums, is audible in embryo here.","It was the group's peak, and for a year they rivalled the Beatles for teenage adulation in Britain. Critics have sometimes seen it as a mere Spector copy, but its reputation has grown along with Scott Walker's, and it is now heard as the moment British orchestral pop reached its biggest and darkest.","Put it straight after You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin' and hear the debt. Then listen closely to Engel's voice, and follow it to Scott 4 three years later. The original mono single mix carries the full weight of the arrangement."],"dusty-springfield-you-dont-have-to-say-you-love-me":["A woman who once turned her lover away now asks only that he stay near, with no need to declare love. The song begins softly over strings and a slow beat, then Springfield's voice swells through each verse to a final chorus of enormous force, backed by a choir and a full orchestra. It is a torch song on the scale of Italian opera, and Springfield holds nothing back.","The melody is Italian: Io che non vivo (senza te), by Pino Donaggio, which Springfield heard at the Sanremo festival of 1965. Her friend Vicki Wickham and the manager Simon Napier-Bell, neither a professional lyricist, wrote English words to it in an evening. Produced by Johnny Franz in London, it became her only British number one in 1966 and a top-five hit in America.","Springfield was unhappy with the sound of the studio and reportedly recorded her vocal in a stairwell at the Philips building to get the echo she wanted. Listen to the natural reverb on her voice at the climax: that is concrete stairs, not a machine. Donaggio later became a film composer, working often with Brian De Palma, which is a nice irony for so cinematic a pop song.","It was her biggest British hit and remains her signature song. Some critics find its bombast too much beside the subtlety of Dusty in Memphis, three years later; others hear it as her greatest vocal performance. Elvis Presley recorded it in 1970, which says something about the reach of its melodrama.","Wait for the last chorus and listen for the stairwell echo on the voice. Then compare I Just Don't Know What to Do with Myself for her gentler style, and Dusty in Memphis for her quietest. The mono single is essential; stereo mixes lose the punch."],"ike-and-tina-turner-river-deep-mountain-high":["The last and largest of Phil Spector's great productions. Tina Turner sings of a love as deep as a river and high as a mountain, her voice raw and gospel-fierce, over an arrangement so thick it barely has edges: massed pianos, guitars, strings, horns, choir and pounding drums in a churning, galloping rhythm. In the middle the music breaks down to a staccato vocal passage before the whole mass rises again.","Spector wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich and recorded it at Gold Star in 1966 in sessions reputed to have cost a fortune and involved dozens of musicians. Only Tina sang; Ike Turner was reportedly paid to take the credit and stay away. It reached only number 88 in America, a failure Spector took as a personal rejection, though it made the British top three.","Tina Turner was reportedly made to sing the lead over and over for hours, until she was exhausted and drenched. Listen to her voice in the last chorus, fraying at the edges: that is the sound of a singer being pushed past control. Spector largely withdrew from the business after its American failure, so this is effectively the end point of his Wall of Sound era.","American radio found it neither Black enough for R&B stations nor white enough for pop, the usual explanation of its failure. In Britain it was admired at once, and George Harrison reportedly called it a perfect record. Its reputation has since become enormous, and Spector himself counted it his best work, though some listeners find it overbearing rather than overwhelming.","Listen once at high volume without trying to separate anything. Then listen again only for Tina's voice, especially in the breakdown. Compare the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations from the same year, the other studio epic of 1966, and Be My Baby for the style in its lighter youth."],"the-shangri-las-past-present-and-future":["Almost no singing at all. Mary Weiss speaks, slowly and coldly, about a love that is over and the fact that she will never let herself be touched by it again, while a piano and strings play the opening movement of Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata beneath her. The group hums and chants softly behind, and the music swells into a short, ballroom-like dance passage before sinking back into Beethoven's triplets and silence.","Shadow Morton wrote and produced it in 1966 as one of the last Shangri-Las records for Red Bird, which was already failing. The girl groups were being swept aside by the British Invasion and by folk-rock, and the Shangri-Las themselves were weeks from dissolving. Released as a single, it was a minor hit, and some American stations would not play it.","Listen to the music: it is Beethoven's first movement of the Moonlight Sonata, adapted almost literally, so a teenage pop single is built on one of the most famous piano pieces ever written. The chill in Weiss's voice suggests some trauma behind the words, which radio programmers found unsettling; the song never says what happened, and that silence is what makes it haunting.","It was barely noticed at the time, but has become a cult favourite, admired as one of the strangest and most daring records of the girl-group era, as close to a spoken-word art piece as to pop. Later writers have seen it as an ancestor of the gloomier strands of post-punk and of the darkest of Scott Walker's work.","Listen first for the Beethoven in the piano, then for the coldness of Weiss's voice against it. Then play the original sonata movement and hear what Morton borrowed. Compare Leader of the Pack from two years earlier to hear how far the Shangri-Las travelled."],"dionne-warwick-i-say-a-little-prayer":["A woman describing her day, from waking to dressing to riding the bus, and how at every moment she says a small prayer for her lover. The song moves quickly and lightly, Warwick singing precisely over a busy piano and a brisk beat, while the backing singers answer with their own gospel-flavoured refrain. The melody keeps changing its step, bar lengths shifting, so the song feels both rushing and graceful.","Bacharach and David wrote it for Warwick in 1966; the lyric was widely heard as the voice of a woman whose man is away in the Vietnam War, though David never said so directly. Bacharach reportedly thought the take too fast and did not want it issued as a single; Scepter released it anyway from an album in 1967, and it reached the American top five.","Listen for bars that are longer or shorter than normal, a Bacharach trademark: the song has measures of ten and eleven beats in places, disguised by the easy flow. And notice that the backing singers' refrain is the true hook, as in church. Aretha Franklin's version in 1968 took that gospel element and made it the whole song, a transatlantic hit that many prefer.","It was a hit, but Bacharach's own doubts and Franklin's quick cover mean it is often discussed as the song Aretha made her own. Warwick's version is now respected for its clarity and poise; the argument between the two readings, one composed and precise, one churchy and free, neatly sums up two ways of singing sixties pop.","Listen for the backing singers' refrain and how it overlaps Warwick's lines. Then play Aretha Franklin's version for the gospel reading. Try counting the beats in a verse and see where the pattern breaks. The original mono Scepter single is best."],"francoise-hardy-comment-te-dire-adieu":["A woman trying and failing to find the words to leave a lover. Françoise Hardy sings in her cool, low voice over a bright, bouncy orchestral arrangement with a clipped, almost comic rhythm, the gaiety of the music playing against the sadness of the words. Each line of the French lyric is packed with the same hard, clicking syllable, and the song sounds almost like a tongue-twister sung with a straight face.","The tune is American, from It Hurts to Say Goodbye by Arnold Goland and Jack Gold, first recorded by Vera Lynn. Hardy wanted to record it in French and asked Serge Gainsbourg to write the lyric; he took the challenge as wordplay. Released in 1968, it was a large French hit and became one of her signature songs, just as she was moving away from the yé-yé image toward more adult material.","Listen to the rhymes: Gainsbourg built the lyric around the sound of 'ex', repeated in word after word, so the song's sound itself is a stammer, a woman stuck on one syllable because she cannot say the one word she needs. It is a virtuoso piece of lyric writing hidden inside a light pop song, and only really audible once you know it is there.","It was a hit and has grown in reputation as one of the best Gainsbourg lyrics written for another singer. Critics now hear it as a meeting of two very different talents, Hardy's reserve and Gainsbourg's wordplay, and it has been widely covered, including by Jimmy Somerville in English and French versions.","Even without French, listen for the repeated clicking sound in the lyric. Then enjoy the contrast of the cheerful arrangement with the cool voice. Compare Tous les garçons et les filles for Hardy at eighteen, and Je t'aime moi non plus from the next year for Gainsbourg in a very different mood."],"serge-gainsbourg-je-t-aime-moi-non-plus":["A slow, languid duet over a softly swaying organ melody, bass and strings. Gainsbourg murmurs and Jane Birkin sighs in a high, breathy voice, the two exchanging fragments of a lovers' conversation in which each says the opposite of what they mean. The music barely changes over four minutes, the organ repeating its lazy tune while the voices become more intimate; it is a record of mood rather than of melody.","Gainsbourg wrote it in 1967 for Brigitte Bardot and recorded it with her, but at her request that version was held back. He re-recorded it with Birkin, his English partner, in London in 1968, and it was released in 1969. It was banned by the BBC and radio in several countries and condemned by the Vatican's newspaper, which only helped it to become a number one in Britain after Fontana withdrew it and a smaller label took it over.","The title itself is the key: 'I love you, me neither' is a paradox, and the song's joke is that the lovers' words contradict each other throughout. Listen, too, to the organ tune, which is sweet and almost churchy, a deliberate clash with the erotic content. Bardot's original version, finally released in 1986, is harder and more businesslike than Birkin's.","It was a scandal, which was the point, and a huge seller. For years it was treated as a novelty record, and its content is still discussed with unease. But critics have increasingly admired the craft of the arrangement and its influence on slow, sensual pop and later trip-hop. Its reputation now rests more on sound than on scandal.","Listen to the organ and the bass before paying attention to the voices, and hear how simple and hypnotic the music is. Then compare Histoire de Melody Nelson, two years later, where Gainsbourg pushes the same mood into a whole orchestral album."],"scott-walker-scott-4":["Ten songs, all Scott Walker's own, sung in his deep, rich baritone over arrangements that range from full orchestra and choir to spare guitar and harmonica. The album opens with The Seventh Seal, a song retelling Ingmar Bergman's film of a knight playing chess with Death, with a Morricone-like Western twang. Elsewhere there are songs about a Soviet leader's return, a war hero's hollow homecoming, and a dying old man, all delivered with calm, sombre grandeur.","By 1969 Walker, formerly the lead singer of the Walker Brothers, had released three solo albums mixing his own songs with covers, notably of Jacques Brel. He released Scott 4 under his real name, Noel Scott Engel, perhaps to escape his teen-idol past. It was his first album of entirely original songs. It failed to chart at all and was soon deleted, and Walker spent the next years singing others' material.","The song The Old Man's Back Again was prompted by the Soviet invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968; the record gives it a funky, ominous bass groove that makes the politics feel sinister rather than preachy. Listen too to the arrangements, much of the orchestral writing by Wally Stott, who later worked as Angela Morley: they mix film-score strings with pop rhythm in a way almost nobody else attempted.","It was a commercial failure and largely forgotten until the 1980s, when musicians including Julian Cope and David Bowie championed Walker, and it was reissued. It is now widely regarded as one of the finest British albums of its decade and the bridge between Walker's pop career and the avant-garde work of his later years. Some prefer Scott 3 for its orchestral sweep.","Start with The Seventh Seal, then The Old Man's Back Again. Listen to the voice: calm, grave, never raised. Then compare Nick Drake's Five Leaves Left from the same year, another orchestral singer-songwriter album ignored in its time, and David Bowie's Low for how Walker's influence travelled."],"serge-gainsbourg-histoire-de-melody-nelson":["Seven tracks in about twenty-eight minutes, telling a single story. Gainsbourg speaks rather than sings, in a low, smoky voice, over a slinky, prominent bass guitar, sparse drums and a swirling orchestral score. The narrator, driving his Rolls-Royce, knocks a red-haired English girl off her bicycle; a brief affair follows, and the album ends with her death in a plane crash and a vast finale for choir and orchestra.","Gainsbourg made it in 1970 and 1971 with the arranger Jean-Claude Vannier, who wrote the orchestral and choral parts, recording in Paris and London. Jane Birkin, then his partner, is the Melody Nelson of the story and appears on the cover. The album was a commercial disappointment when released in 1971, though admired by some French critics. Rock in France was then largely imported, and a forty-two-year-old chansonnier making a narrative rock album with an orchestra had no obvious audience.","Listen to the bass guitar: it carries the melody much of the time, placed far forward in the mix, while the orchestra swells and retreats around it. The last track, Cargo culte, uses the idea of Melanesian islanders building replica airstrips to summon supply planes, and makes it an image of grief and longing, with Vannier's choir giving it a sacred, almost requiem-like weight.","It has become Gainsbourg's most admired record, a cult album that found a new audience from the 1990s when Beck, Air, Portishead and others cited it. Its subject, a middle-aged man's obsession with a teenage girl, is now discussed with more discomfort than before. Critics generally treat it as a landmark in the concept album and in orchestral pop.","Listen to it in one sitting; it is short. Follow the bass through the first track, and wait for the choir in the final one. Then compare Scott Walker's Scott 4 for another orchestral auteur record of the period, and Je t'aime moi non plus for the same voice in a simpler setting."],"francoise-hardy-la-question":["A quiet, dreamlike album of love songs, most written by or with the Brazilian guitarist and singer Tuca. Hardy's low voice floats over nylon-string guitar, soft bass and strings that drift in and out like mist. The songs are slow and intimate, touched by bossa nova, with melodies that wander rather than resolve. The album is a single mood from start to finish: private, sensual, withdrawn.","By 1971 Hardy had left behind the yé-yé image and was recording more adult material. She worked with Tuca, a Brazilian musician living in Paris, and the album was recorded with a small group and string arrangements. It was released on Sonopresse and did not sell especially well, though it became treasured by her admirers. In France the singer-songwriter mood of the early seventies favoured quieter, more personal records, and this one pushed further into privacy than almost any of them.","Listen to how the strings never simply accompany; they hover and swell, sometimes in unusual harmonies, so the album sounds at once Brazilian and French. And notice how rarely Hardy raises her voice. The Brazilian bossa nova influence ties the record to Getz and Gilberto, whose cool, murmured style reached Europe in the early 1960s.","It was little noticed at the time, but has grown into Hardy's most admired album, often cited by musicians and critics as a model of intimate, atmospheric pop. Its reputation outside France rose after 2000, when a new generation discovered her through reissues. Some listeners still find it too uniform in mood, one long sigh; its admirers answer that the sameness is the design, an album meant to be heard whole rather than raided for singles.","Listen with headphones in a quiet room, from start to finish. Notice the guitar and the strings working together. Compare Getz/Gilberto for the Brazilian roots, and Tous les garçons et les filles to hear how far Hardy's voice travelled in nine years."],"sam-cooke-touch-the-hem-of-his-garment":["Two and a half minutes of quartet gospel, taken slowly. Cooke sings the story of the woman in the crowd who reached for Christ's robe, and the four Soul Stirrers behind him answer in close harmony on a single repeated phrase while a guitar keeps a soft shuffle. What you notice is the tone: light, unforced, with a little catch at the top of a phrase and a yodel-like turn that Cooke could place anywhere he wanted.","Cooke joined the Soul Stirrers in 1950, aged nineteen, replacing R. H. Harris, and by 1956 he was the biggest draw on the gospel circuit, singing to churches full of women who screamed. He wrote this himself for Specialty Records in Los Angeles. Within two years he had crossed to secular pop under a pseudonym, then under his own name, and the gospel world treated it as a defection. Almost every soul singer who followed made the same walk.","Listen to what Cooke does not do. Where a gospel shouter would tear the last chorus open, he stays inside a narrow, conversational range and lets the quartet supply the heat behind him. That restraint is the invention: he proved a gospel lead could hold a crowd by pulling back, and every soul ballad singer from Otis Redding to Al Green built on it.","In the churches it was simply a Soul Stirrers record among many. Judgement changed once critics went looking for the roots of soul and found that Cooke's gospel sides already contained the whole vocabulary — the melisma, the moan, the delayed entry — with no secular subject attached. The old argument about whether taking that voice to pop was betrayal or liberation has quietly been settled in favour of liberation.","Play it directly before 'A Change Is Gonna Come' and hear the same voice thirty seconds into the same technique with a different text. Notice the quartet's answering phrase, which never varies; the whole drama sits in Cooke's timing against it. Specialty's mono transfers are close and dry, which is the way to hear how little he is doing."],"sam-cooke-harlem-square-club":["Forty minutes recorded in a small Miami club, and it sounds like it: the room is packed, the band plays loud and slightly ragged, and the audience answers every line. Cooke works the crowd like a preacher, stretching 'Bring It On Home to Me' into a call and response that will not end, roughening his voice until it cracks. The polished crooner of the hit singles is nowhere present; this is a man sweating through a set.","Cooke was, by 1963, a pop star with an immaculate reputation on white television, and a chitlin-circuit headliner on Black stages where he sang entirely differently. RCA sent a mobile unit to Miami's Harlem Square Club in January to capture the second version. Hearing the tape, the label decided it could damage the crossover career it had built and shelved it. It stayed in the vault for twenty-two years, until 1985.","Listen for the moment he stops singing to talk to the room and the band keeps vamping under him, exactly as a gospel quartet lead would over a church organ. Also notice how little he uses the falsetto that made his records famous. The whole performance is pitched a shade lower and rougher, which tells you the studio smoothness was a decision, not a limit.","When it finally appeared in 1985 it reversed a critical consensus: Cooke had been filed as the great voice let down by soft arrangements, and here was proof that he could out-work Otis Redding on a stage. Some argued RCA's caution was ordinary commercial sense in 1963; others read it as the record industry sanding the Blackness off a Black artist. The second reading has largely won.","Start with 'Bring It On Home to Me' and count how many times he trades the phrase with the room before moving on. Then play the studio single of the same song and hear what was left out. The 1985 release is mono and close-miked, with the crowd nearly as loud as the band, which is the point."],"the-supremes-where-did-our-love-go":["Two and a half minutes built on one sound: a flat, wooden thump on every beat, like someone stamping on boards, with handclaps riding over it. Diana Ross sings low and almost without expression, half-spoken, while Florence Ballard and Mary Wilson answer in two-note sighs. A baritone saxophone doubles the bass. Nothing accelerates, nothing modulates, nothing is added after the first eight seconds; the record simply repeats until it stops.","Holland, Dozier and Holland wrote it for the Marvelettes, who turned it down. The Supremes, nicknamed the no-hit Supremes inside Motown after eight failed singles, got it by default and did not want it either. Ross disliked singing that low. It reached number one in August 1964 and was followed by four more consecutive number ones, which established both the group and Motown's claim to be the sound of young America rather than a Detroit race label.","The stamping is not a drum. It was made by musicians hitting boards with their feet in the studio, a trick Motown reused for years, and it is mixed almost as loud as the voice. Notice too that Ross's deadpan is a byproduct of the key: pitched where she is uncomfortable, she stops projecting, and that flatness became the sound everyone imitated.","Contemporary critics heard a novelty; rock writers of the seventies called Motown's assembly line the opposite of authenticity. The argument has inverted. The production line is now the point of interest, and this is the record where its method becomes audible — a hook, a rhythm and a hole where the emoting should be. Ballard's later removal from the group, and her death in poverty, colours every reissue.","Listen once for the stomp alone and you will hear how much of the record is percussion. Then follow the baritone saxophone, which is doing the work a guitar would do anywhere else. Compare it with the Temptations' 'My Girl' from the same months to hear the same studio making warmth instead of cold."],"the-temptations-my-girl":["It opens with a bass climbing four notes, then a guitar picking the same four, then finger snaps, and only after twenty seconds does David Ruffin come in, grainy and unhurried. The other four Temptations answer him in block harmony. Strings arrive for the second verse and lift the key at the end, a small shift that the whole record has been saving up for. Under three minutes, with no wasted bar.","Smokey Robinson wrote it with Ronald White as an answer to 'My Guy', which he had just given Mary Wells, and he built it for Ruffin's rough voice rather than for the group's smoother leads. Motown's writers were by 1964 competing internally for singles, and this was Robinson proving he could place a ballad against the Holland-Dozier-Holland dance records. It became the Temptations' first number one in early 1965.","The four-note bass phrase is the entire song in miniature — the tune, the guitar figure and the string line are all versions of it, which is why the record sounds inevitable rather than merely catchy. Listen also to the key change near the end: it happens under a held note, without a drum fill to announce it, so most listeners feel it before they notice it.","It was a hit at once and has never stopped being one, which has made it hard to hear. Critics who prefer the Temptations' later psychedelic records treat it as the pretty phase, and Ruffin's own later career, ended by addiction, gave the song a retrospective sadness. Scholarship on Motown now uses it as the standard example of an arrangement in which every part is a variation of one idea.","Try to ignore the singing for one play and follow only the bass. Then listen to how Ruffin lags fractionally behind the beat on every line while the backing voices sit exactly on it. Compare with the Supremes' 'Where Did Our Love Go' from the same studio, same season, entirely different temperature."],"sam-cooke-a-change-is-gonna-come":["A french horn opens alone, answered by strings and a slow drum, and the scale of the thing is orchestral before a word is sung. Cooke tells three verses of autobiography — born by the river, afraid to die, turned away from a cinema — each ending on the same phrase, and the arrangement grows behind him until the timpani are rolling. The bridge, where he asks his brother for help and is knocked back down, is the only place his voice breaks.","Cooke wrote it in 1963 after hearing Bob Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' and asking himself why a white man had written the movement's song, and after he and his party were refused rooms at a Louisiana motel and arrested for the fuss they made. He recorded it in Los Angeles in December 1963 with an arrangement by René Hall. It was issued as a single after he was shot dead in December 1964.","The orchestration is deliberately un-soulful: strings, horn and timpani are the furniture of a film score, and Cooke uses them so the song cannot be filed as a race record. Notice that the melody barely moves within each line — almost everything is one repeated note — so that the few rising phrases carry all the weight. He performed it publicly only rarely; he seems to have been uneasy with it.","It sold modestly at first and grew into the anthem it is now over decades, sung at marches, funerals and inaugurations. The debate is about ownership: whether a song written by a wealthy pop star in a Beverly Hills frame of mind belongs to the movement that adopted it, and whether the orchestral dress is dignity or camouflage. Nobody argues about the singing.","Listen first for the french horn, which returns at each seam and is the only instrument that answers the voice. Then go to the bridge and notice it is the one passage without strings. Play William Grant Still's Afro-American Symphony after it to hear another attempt to carry the same feeling in orchestral clothes."],"smokey-robinson-tracks-of-my-tears":["A guitar figure that sounds almost Spanish opens it, and everything else is arranged around that one curling phrase. Robinson sings high, in a light falsetto that never pushes, telling you that the smile is a mask and asking you to look closer at his face. The Miracles answer with a single long held word. The chorus arrives without any change of volume; the record refuses to get louder, which is why the image lands.","Marv Tarplin, the Miracles' guitarist, brought Robinson the guitar figure with no song attached, and Robinson carried it around for weeks before the phrase about tracks — the marks tears leave — gave him the title. He was by then Motown's vice-president as well as its most prolific writer, turning out singles for the Temptations and Mary Wells between his own. The record reached the American top ten in 1965.","The verse and chorus use almost the same melody; what changes is the harmony underneath, so the song seems to darken without moving. Listen for the way the backing voices enter a fraction early on the title phrase, overlapping the lead, which makes the line sound like something Robinson cannot stop the others from saying. Tarplin's guitar plays the hook alone at every seam, never doubled.","Robinson's reputation as a lyricist has always run ahead of his reputation as a singer, helped by Bob Dylan's much-repeated description of him as a great American poet, a line Robinson himself has treated as a joke. Critics divide over whether the extended metaphors are elegant or a touch too neat. The song is generally exempted from that argument; the image and the melody arrive together.","Follow the guitar rather than the voice for the first thirty seconds so you learn the figure, then let it disappear behind Robinson and see if you can still hear it. Notice the record never raises its voice. Try it beside the Temptations' 'My Girl', which Robinson also wrote, to hear his two registers side by side."],"curtis-mayfield-people-get-ready":["Under three minutes, quiet throughout, with a guitar figure that rocks between two chords like a train on rails and strings that enter so softly you may miss them. Mayfield sings high and close, and the two other Impressions fold their voices under rather than over his, so the trio sounds like one instrument. There is no chorus in the usual sense; the same eight bars come round, and each time the words move further from the railway and closer to judgement.","Chicago in 1964 and 1965: Mayfield was writing for the Impressions on Chicago's own soul label, ABC-Paramount sessions arranged by Johnny Pate, while the civil-rights movement was moving north into his city. The train to Jordan is old spiritual imagery, and Mayfield used it with the coded double meaning intact — a gospel train and a movement bus at once. Martin Luther King's organisers took the song up, and it was sung on marches.","There is no drum fill and no key change in the entire record; the only dynamic event is the strings swelling for four bars near the end. That flatness is the gospel structure showing through — a hymn does not build, it circulates. Listen also for the line that warns there is no room for the hopeless sinner, which Mayfield later said he was uneasy about, and which he softened in performance.","It was a hit and became a standard, covered by Rod Stewart, Bob Marley and Eva Cassidy among many others, usually louder and worse. Critics of Mayfield's Impressions years sometimes find the gentleness evasive next to the confrontation of his solo records; others argue the movement needed songs that could be sung by a thousand untrained voices, and that this one was engineered for exactly that.","Listen for the guitar's two-chord rock and count how many times it comes round unchanged. Then attend to the vocal blend: try to pick out the two lower Impressions, which is harder than it sounds. Follow it with 'A Change Is Gonna Come' to hear the movement's two great songs, one orchestral and one almost bare."],"otis-redding-otis-blue":["Eleven songs in thirty-three minutes, played by a five-piece house band with two horns and almost no overdubbing. The sound is dry and forward: Steve Cropper's guitar answers in single notes, Booker T. Jones's organ holds the chords, Al Jackson's drums drag very slightly behind. Redding sings three Sam Cooke songs, his own 'Respect', and a version of 'Satisfaction' in which the guitar riff becomes a horn line. Everything is loud, and nothing is smooth.","Recorded at Stax in Memphis in July 1965, in a single stretch of about twenty-four hours broken only so the musicians could play their regular engagements. Stax's method was the opposite of Motown's: no written arrangements, a racially integrated house band working out the parts on the floor, and a former cinema for a studio with a sloping floor that shaped the sound. Cooke had been dead seven months, and the covers are a wake.","Listen for the horns, which are arranged but never notated — the two players worked out lines by singing them to each other, which is why they breathe with Redding rather than against him. The room itself is audible: the sloping floor of the old Capitol Theatre gave Stax a slightly boxy ambience that engineers have tried and failed to reproduce. 'Respect' here is a man's demand; two years later it was answered.","It was Redding's breakthrough with white audiences and is still the usual first recommendation. The old critical split between Memphis grit and Detroit gloss, which this album was used to prove, now looks overdrawn: both were factories with house bands, and both made records to a formula. What survives the argument is the singing, and the fact that a major album was cut in about a day.","Start with 'I've Been Loving You Too Long' and listen to how Redding delays the first syllable of nearly every line. Then play 'Satisfaction' and notice the Rolling Stones' guitar riff reassigned to horns. A good mono transfer beats stereo here; the stereo split scatters a band that was playing as one thing."],"the-supremes-you-keep-me-hangin-on":["A guitar stutters the same high note over and over, fast and metallic, like a signal being tapped out, and it never stops for the whole record. Diana Ross sings harder than usual over it, close to a shout on the repeated set-me-free line, and the other two Supremes chop in on the offbeat. Horns punch in the gaps. The tempo is quick, the mood is not romantic at all, and the fade leaves the tapping still going.","Holland, Dozier and Holland were by 1966 writing at industrial speed and beginning to darken the group's material; the Supremes' run of number ones had made Motown a corporation with a charm school and a choreographer. This was the seventh of their eventual twelve chart-toppers. Within two years the writing team had left Motown in a dispute over royalties, and Florence Ballard had been forced out of the group.","The tapped guitar is sometimes described as Morse code, and Holland has said the idea came from exactly that association. Notice what it does structurally: because one element never varies, the arrangers can cut everything else away without losing the beat, and they do — the bridge drops to almost nothing but voice and tapping. The trick was copied for the next twenty years, most audibly by Vanilla Fudge, who slowed it to a crawl.","It was the Supremes record that rock audiences accepted, and it has aged into the one that sounds least like 1966. Kim Wilde's 1986 version restored it to number one in a synthesiser arrangement that made the mechanical basis explicit. The criticism that Motown treated singers as interchangeable parts has some purchase here, though the hard edge in Ross's delivery is hers and appears nowhere on the earlier hits.","Listen to the bridge, where the production pulls back and you can hear how little is holding the record up. Compare Ross's phrasing with 'Where Did Our Love Go' two years earlier: the same singer, twice the pressure. Then try Vanilla Fudge's slow version to hear how much of the song is tempo."],"otis-redding-try-a-little-tenderness":["It begins as a ballad — horns laid out long and slow, Redding almost murmuring the first verse — and then, about halfway, the drums double the pace and never come back down. The last ninety seconds are a controlled collapse: Redding shouting the same four words, the band hammering a two-chord figure, the horns stabbing on top, the singer improvising a string of exclamations over the fade. One song containing two entirely different records.","The song was written in 1932 by three British songwriters and had been crooned by Bing Crosby and Frank Sinatra as a piece of gentlemanly advice. Redding recorded it at Stax in 1966 with Isaac Hayes, then a staff pianist and arranger, shaping the horn parts. Stax's band was used to building a track on the floor; the acceleration was worked out in the room rather than written down.","The tempo shift is not a cut between two takes. Al Jackson simply moves from the backbeat to a fast pattern on the same pulse, so the underlying speed never changes — what changes is how often you hear it. Listen also to how Redding's final ad-libs fall on unexpected parts of the bar, dragging the band with him rather than following it.","Contemporary reviewers noted the audacity of taking a white crooner's standard and destroying it. Later writers have used the performance to argue that soul's real subject is effort — that the interest lies in hearing a voice run out of reserve. A minority still prefer the restraint of the first half and find the ending mannered. Live films from 1967 suggest Redding could not sing it any other way.","Play the Sinatra or Crosby version first, then this; the point is unmissable. Listen for the exact bar where the drums double and note that nobody signals it. Then hear Isaac Hayes's own long-form records, where the same instinct for a slow build gets eighteen minutes instead of three."],"aretha-franklin-i-never-loved-a-man":["Eleven songs, thirty-four minutes, and the crucial instrument is Franklin's own piano: she plays it on most of the record, which locks the band to her phrasing rather than the reverse. The title song is slow and accusatory, 'Do Right Woman' is a hymn in disguise, 'Dr. Feelgood' is close to a blues, and 'Respect' ends side one. Her sisters Carolyn and Erma sing behind her, so the backing voices are family harmony.","Franklin had spent six years at Columbia being given show tunes and jazz standards. Jerry Wexler signed her to Atlantic and took her to FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, in January 1967, put her at a piano in front of a white Southern rhythm section and let her lead. The session ended in a drunken argument after one day; the rest of the album was finished in New York with the same players flown up.","Listen to how the band follows the piano into places no arrangement would go — the small rhythmic hesitations before a chorus are Franklin's hands, not a chart. On 'Do Right Woman' almost everything except the organ and voices was added later in New York, which is why it floats free of the drums. The gospel training is audible in the way she answers her own lines.","It was received immediately as a career resurrection and has stayed near the top of every soul album list since. The debates concern the Muscle Shoals session itself, now a founding myth of integrated Southern recording, and how much credit Wexler deserves for a transformation that was mostly a matter of removing the material Columbia had imposed. Franklin's own accounts and Wexler's differ on details.","Start with the title track and listen only to the piano; it is the argument the voice then makes. Move to 'Dr. Feelgood' for the church left hand. Then compare a Columbia-era recording of hers from 1962 and hear what four years and one rhythm section changed."],"aretha-franklin-respect":["Two and a half minutes, fast, with the drums and a guitar snapping together on every offbeat. Franklin sings it as an ultimatum rather than a plea, and her sisters chant the sock-it-to-me line under her like a playground taunt. In the middle King Curtis takes a tenor saxophone solo lifted in feel from the Stax horn style. Then comes the spelled-out chorus, seven letters shouted one at a time, and the record never recovers its composure.","Otis Redding wrote and recorded it in 1965 as a working man's complaint: he brings home the money, so he wants respect when he gets in. Franklin cut her version at Atlantic in New York in February 1967, rearranged it with her sisters, and reversed the sex of the demand. It went to number one that summer, in the months of the Detroit and Newark uprisings, and was immediately heard as a civil-rights and a women's song at once.","The spelling of the title and the sock-it-to-me chant are Franklin's additions, worked out with Carolyn and Erma, and neither exists in Redding's song. Notice also the key detail of the bridge, where she lists what she is prepared to give; the demand is conditional, not absolute, which is why the record sounds like a negotiation rather than a slogan.","Redding is said to have remarked, after hearing it, that the girl had taken his song. The record is now so thoroughly a political emblem that the sexual bargaining in the lyric is often edited out of the public memory of it; recent scholarship has pushed back, insisting the song is about a specific relationship before it is about anything larger. Nobody disputes that the arrangement is entirely hers.","Play Redding's original first — it is a completely different song at the same tempo. Then listen to the two backing voices rather than the lead, because they carry the jokes. Finally note how short it is: everything is over in the time a later soul record would spend on an introduction."],"marvin-gaye-i-heard-it-through-the-grapevine":["An electric piano plays four heavy chords in near-silence, a tambourine ticks, and the record is already tense before anything happens. Gaye sings higher than his usual range, sounding cornered, while female voices circle behind him with a wordless figure. Strings enter low and stay low. The bass moves constantly. Nothing about the arrangement is dense, yet the record sounds crowded, which is exactly the subject: a man learning his private life is public knowledge.","Norman Whitfield and Barrett Strong wrote it and pushed it through the Motown quality-control meetings repeatedly. Gladys Knight and the Pips had a hit with a faster, gospel-driven version in 1967. Gaye's take was held back, buried on an album, then released as a single in late 1968 after radio picked it up, and became the biggest-selling Motown single to that point. Gaye was at the time still mainly known for duets.","Whitfield pitched the key deliberately high for Gaye, who had to strain, and the strain is the performance. Listen for the tambourine, which is doing the job of a snare drum and is placed unusually far forward in the mix. The wordless backing figure is the same shape as the vocal melody, a half-bar behind — the gossip arriving just after the news.","Its success embarrassed Motown's release committee and strengthened Whitfield's hand, which led directly to the label's psychedelic soul phase. Creedence Clearwater Revival's eleven-minute rock version in 1970 opened a long argument about whether the song belongs to Gaye's paranoia or to a swampy guitar groove; the argument is settled every time the original's first four chords play.","Listen to the first fifteen seconds twice before going on, because the whole record's atmosphere is set there. Then follow the bass, which never repeats a bar exactly. Compare Gladys Knight's earlier version, which is faster and happier and shows how much of the menace is tempo and key."],"the-temptations-cloud-nine":["A wah-wah guitar scratches out a rhythm before any voice appears, congas come in under it, and the beat is harder and more broken than any Motown record before it. The five Temptations trade lines rather than harmonise — one voice takes two words, another takes the next two — so the lead keeps moving around the group. The song is about a childhood in poverty and the escape a high offers, and the chorus is left ambiguous.","Norman Whitfield had heard Sly and the Family Stone and decided Motown's polish was about to become a liability. David Ruffin had just left the group, so the Temptations needed a new identity anyway, and Whitfield gave them one: no single lead, a psychedelic rhythm section, and lyrics about the street rather than the dance. Motown's executives worried the song endorsed drugs. It won the label its first Grammy in 1969.","Listen for how the vocal lines are cut across the beat rather than laid along it — Whitfield is treating five singers as a percussion section. The wah-wah pedal, then a new device, is used for rhythm rather than solos, which is how funk guitar would be played for the next decade. The lyric never says what the cloud is, which was the compromise that got it released.","The Grammy legitimised the change and Motown spent the next four years chasing it. Older fans, and some of the Temptations, complained that the group's singing had been subordinated to a producer's studio ideas, a complaint that grew louder as the records grew longer. Historians now treat this as the hinge between sixties soul and seventies funk, and as evidence that Detroit was listening hard to San Francisco.","Listen for the guitar in the first ten seconds; everything that follows is built on it. Then try to track a single Temptation through a verse and notice how quickly you lose him. Play 'My Girl' immediately afterwards — same group, four years, an entirely different idea of what a vocal group is for."],"otis-redding-dock-of-the-bay":["Waves and a gull, then a guitar figure that rocks gently between two chords, and a voice that does none of the things Redding was famous for. He sings quietly, in long relaxed phrases, about doing nothing at all. There is no climax, no shouted coda; the horns are reduced to two soft punctuations, and the record ends with whistling over the fade while the waves come back in.","Redding wrote the first verse on a houseboat in Sausalito in the summer of 1967, weeks after the Monterey Pop Festival had introduced him to a young white rock audience. He finished it at Stax with Steve Cropper in November and December. On 10 December 1967 his plane came down in a Wisconsin lake, killing him at twenty-six and most of his band. The single was released in January and became his only number one.","The whistling is generally reported to be a placeholder: Redding had not written a final verse and whistled instead, intending to return to it. The waves and gull were added afterwards by Cropper. Listen also to the bridge, where the chord movement briefly resembles a folk-rock song rather than a soul record — the Sausalito influence is audible in the harmony, not only the lyric.","Stax was reportedly unsure about releasing something so unlike him; its success and the circumstances of his death fused into a single story that has been hard to separate from the music. Critics argue about whether it signalled a real new direction or was a one-off experiment. What is clear is that the singer of 'Try a Little Tenderness' had decided he could hold attention without raising his voice.","Notice how long the phrases are and how late he enters each one. Follow the second guitar, which answers the first throughout. Then play 'Try a Little Tenderness' back to back with it to hear the two extremes of the same singer within eighteen months, and stay for the whistling."],"aretha-franklin-lady-soul":["Under thirty minutes and almost no filler. 'Chain of Fools' opens on a single tremolo guitar note before the band drops in; 'Ain't No Way' is a slow ballad with a soprano floating a wordless line high above Franklin's; '(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman' builds from piano to full gospel chorus. The band is the Muscle Shoals rhythm section again, and Franklin's piano and her sisters' harmonies remain the spine of the sound.","1968: a year of assassinations and burning cities, and Franklin's second Atlantic year, in which she was on the cover of Time and being called the Queen of Soul in print. The material came from several directions at once — Don Covay wrote 'Chain of Fools', Carole King and Gerry Goffin wrote 'A Natural Woman' with Jerry Wexler, and her younger sister Carolyn wrote 'Ain't No Way'. Eric Clapton played guitar on one track.","The soprano soaring above Franklin on 'Ain't No Way' is Cissy Houston, singing a countermelody rather than harmony, and it is one of the few places on any Franklin record where another voice is allowed to go higher than hers. Listen also to 'Chain of Fools', where the guitar's opening note was reportedly meant as a test tone and was kept because it set the mood.","It was a commercial and critical success on arrival and has remained the album people reach for after the debut. Later criticism has focused less on the record than on what surrounded it — the Queen of Soul framing, the political weight placed on a singer who preferred not to make speeches. The performances themselves have never needed defending.","Begin with 'Ain't No Way' and listen for the second soprano entering above the lead. Then 'Chain of Fools' for the guitar and the drum pattern. If you want to hear where the phrasing comes from, follow it with Margaret Bonds's Montgomery Variations, written out of the same church music and the same years."],"isaac-hayes-hot-buttered-soul":["Four tracks in forty-five minutes, which in 1969 was almost a provocation. 'Walk On By' runs twelve minutes and opens with strings and a distorted guitar before Hayes sings a note. 'By the Time I Get to Phoenix' runs eighteen, of which the first eight are a spoken monologue over an organ chord, telling the back story the song leaves out. Hayes's voice is a low, unhurried baritone; the band never hurries either.","Stax had just lost its entire back catalogue to Atlantic in a contract dispute and lost Otis Redding in a plane crash. In 1969 it responded by releasing twenty-seven albums at once to rebuild a catalogue from nothing, and gave its staff writer Isaac Hayes complete control of one of them. He used it to ignore every rule about single length. It outsold almost everything else Stax issued that year.","Both long tracks are covers of songs by white pop writers — Burt Bacharach and Hal David, and Jimmy Webb — and Hayes treats them as material to be dismantled rather than interpreted. Listen for the way the monologue on 'Phoenix' never quite resolves into the song; the band vamps on one chord for so long that when the melody finally arrives it feels like a memory. The fuzz guitar on 'Walk On By' is mixed as a lead instrument.","It was received as an eccentric gamble that worked, and it changed the economics of Black albums: soul artists could now be sold as album acts. Critics have argued since about the monologues, which some find hypnotic and others find close to self-parody, an impression the later Shaft persona did nothing to dispel. The arrangements are no longer in dispute; they set the pattern for orchestral soul.","Give 'By the Time I Get to Phoenix' the full eighteen minutes without skipping the talking, and time how long the organ holds before the band moves. Then compare Ravel's Boléro, another long crescendo over a fixed pattern. Hear 'Walk On By' beside Dionne Warwick's original to see what was removed."],"dusty-springfield-dusty-in-memphis":["Eleven songs, thirty-four minutes, sung very quietly. Springfield's voice is breathy and set close to the microphone, so the record works at conversational volume while the band plays full Memphis soul underneath — organ, horns, a rhythm section that swings without pushing. 'Son of a Preacher Man' is the famous one, but the character of the album is in the slow tracks, where she sings behind the beat and lets the arrangement do the rising.","Jerry Wexler brought her to Atlantic and to the Memphis studio where Elvis Presley and Neil Diamond were also recording, using the house rhythm section known as the Memphis Boys. Springfield, a British pop star raised on American records, was so intimidated by singing where Aretha Franklin had sung that she could not perform in the room; the backing tracks were cut in Memphis and her vocals were added later in New York.","Almost none of the album is her singing live with the band, and it is the reason the record sounds the way it does — a close, isolated voice floating on a group performance recorded a thousand miles away. Listen to the song selection too: Randy Newman, Goffin and King, Bacharach and David, all pop writers, arranged as Southern soul. The seam between those two worlds is the album's whole idea.","It flopped on release; the soul audience did not want a white English singer and the pop audience had lost interest. Its reputation was rebuilt slowly by critics, then decisively by the use of 'Son of a Preacher Man' in Pulp Fiction in 1994. The residual argument is about the word authenticity: whether the record is an act of homage or appropriation, and whether the answer changes because she sings so well.","Start with 'Just a Little Lovin'' rather than the hit, and notice how low she keeps the volume. Then play Aretha Franklin's Muscle Shoals sides for the contrast in attack. The 1999 expanded reissue adds outtakes that show how many passes it took to get that apparent ease."],"smokey-robinson-tears-of-a-clown":["The introduction sounds like a fairground: a bassoon burbling a circus figure against a high piccolo, over a tumbling percussion pattern. Then Robinson's falsetto arrives with a lyric about hiding grief behind a public face. The verse is fast and busy, the chorus opens out, and the carnival figure keeps returning between sections, so the joke and the confession alternate every eight bars. Three minutes, and none of it is slow.","Stevie Wonder and Hank Cosby built the backing track as an instrumental and gave it to Robinson at a Motown Christmas party because they could not find words for it. He heard a circus in it, thought of Pagliacci and wrote the lyric around the clown. It sat on a 1967 album for three years until Motown's British office issued it as a single, where it went to number one and forced an American release in 1970.","The bassoon is the giveaway. Motown almost never used one, and it is there because Wonder was writing for an imagined circus band rather than a soul group. Notice how the lyric quotes the operatic clown directly, and how the melody of the verse crowds so many syllables in that Robinson has to sing it almost as patter — the strain is the mask slipping.","Its three-year delay is the standard example of Motown's quality-control system failing, and of the British market rescuing an American record. Critics divide on the arrangement: some hear it as the most inventive thing Motown released, others as a novelty whose orchestration undercuts a serious lyric. It was the Miracles' only American number one, which surprises people who know how many hits Robinson wrote.","Listen once to the introduction alone and decide what kind of record you think is coming. Follow the bassoon after that; it never leaves. Then hear Leoncavallo's Pagliacci, whose weeping clown Robinson is openly borrowing, and 'The Tracks of My Tears' for the same idea with no costume at all."],"curtis-mayfield-curtis":["The opening track begins with a whispered congregation of voices before the band crashes in, and for eight minutes it warns that everyone is going to hell. 'Move On Up' is the opposite: a long, elated drive over congas and a punching horn line that keeps going for almost nine minutes in its full version. In between are slow songs with strings. Mayfield's own guitar, tuned to an open chord, threads through everything, and the falsetto is unchanged.","Mayfield left the Impressions in 1970 after thirteen years, and set up his own label, Curtom, in Chicago — Black-owned, which very few soul labels were. The gentleness of 'People Get Ready' had stopped matching the moment; this record answers the Black Power years directly, and the long tracks answer the new album market that Hot Buttered Soul had opened the year before. Johnny Pate arranged the horns and strings.","Mayfield tuned his guitar to an open chord and played, self-taught, in a way no trained guitarist would, which is why his rhythm parts sit oddly against the beat and cannot be transcribed convincingly. Listen for the congas, which carry the groove more than the drum kit does. The nine-minute 'Move On Up' was cut to a fraction for the single; the long version is the one to hear.","It was a commercial success and established that a soul singer could run his own label and write his own politics without a producer between him and the record. Critics at the time found the hellfire opener heavy-handed; the judgement has softened as the album's influence on seventies soul, and later on sampling producers, became clear. The falsetto, once considered a limitation, is now the signature.","Play the long 'Move On Up' and stay past the four-minute mark, where the horns take over completely. Then go back to the opening track for the whispering before the band enters. If you want the argument the record is making, put 'People Get Ready' from five years earlier immediately before it."],"marvin-gaye-whats-going-on":["Nine songs that run into each other without a gap, thirty-five minutes, beginning with party chatter and a saxophone that wanders in as if already mid-thought. Gaye multi-tracks his own voice into a small choir, so lead, harmony and murmured asides all belong to one man. Underneath, Motown's house band plays softly — congas, vibraphone, a bass line that never stops moving — with Detroit Symphony strings floated on top. The record almost never raises its voice.","Gaye had been Motown's smooth hit-maker through the sixties. By 1970 his singing partner Tammi Terrell was dead at twenty-four, his marriage was failing and his brother Frankie had returned from Vietnam with stories. Obie Benson of the Four Tops brought him the title song after watching police beat protesters in Berkeley. Berry Gordy thought a protest record would finish him and refused to release it; Gaye recorded nothing else until Motown gave way in May 1971.","The saxophone that opens the album was an accident — Eli Fontaine was warming up, Gaye heard the phrase and told him he was done. The voices at the party are partly Detroit Lions players Gaye knew. Listen for how the songs are stitched: the last chord of one becomes the first of the next, so there are no gaps and nowhere to applaud, which is why the album cannot be heard in pieces.","It sold immediately and was recognised at once as proof that a soul singer could sustain an album-length argument; Gordy's resistance has since become part of the legend, and he has spent decades explaining it. Later debate concerns the mixes — the album exists in more than one, and reissues have made the case for each — and how much of the seamless flow was designed in advance rather than found at the desk.","Hear it whole, in order, once, without skipping. Then notice that the title song and 'Inner City Blues' bracket the sequence and share material, so the record closes a circle. Play 'Mercy Mercy Me' twice: once for the words, once for the vibraphone. It works best quietly, late."],"isaac-hayes-theme-from-shaft":["A hi-hat opens alone, hissing in even sixteenths, and stays there for a full minute. A wah-wah guitar joins, scratching a two-note figure; then flute, then horns, then strings, then a bass line, each entering on top of what is already running and none of them leaving. Hayes does not sing until the piece is three-quarters over, and then mostly talks, answered by female voices who supply the punchlines. Five minutes of accumulation.","Gordon Parks was directing Shaft for MGM, a detective film with a Black hero made largely by a Black crew, and Stax wanted the soundtrack. Hayes, whose Hot Buttered Soul had made him the label's biggest solo act, wrote and produced the score, expecting also to be cast in the lead. The single reached number one at the end of 1971 and won the Academy Award for Best Original Song the following spring.","The wah-wah pedal is played by Charles Pitts, and the figure he scratches is deliberately unmusical — two notes, no melody, pure rhythm — which is precisely why it became the sound of urban film scoring for a decade. Listen also to how the arrangement is nothing but layers added in sequence: no section is ever taken away until the very end.","The Oscar made Hayes the first Black composer to win in that category and confirmed that soul musicians could write for film, a door that stayed open. The blaxploitation cycle it helped launch has been argued over ever since, for trading in the images it also celebrated. The theme itself is now so widely parodied that its construction — a five-minute crescendo with no chorus — is easy to overlook.","Count the entries: hi-hat, guitar, flute, horns, strings, bass, voice, in that order, and notice that nothing ever drops out. Then hear Duke Ellington's Harlem for another orchestral portrait of the same streets thirty years earlier, and Superfly for the answer Curtis Mayfield gave a year later."],"al-green-lets-stay-together":["Thirty-three minutes of restraint. Al Jackson's drums are recorded dry and slightly muffled, always a shade behind the beat; the horns play soft sustained chords rather than stabs; the organ hums. Green sings in three registers — a soft baritone, a light falsetto and an unpitched sigh — and slips between them mid-word. The title song's opening phrase, sung almost under his breath before the band arrives, is the sound the whole record is built around.","Hi Records in Memphis, produced by Willie Mitchell in a converted cinema on South Lauderdale Street with the Hodges brothers as the rhythm section and Al Jackson, from Booker T. and the MG's, on drums. Mitchell had spent two years persuading Green to stop imitating Otis Redding and sing quietly instead. Stax was in decline; Hi became the city's second sound, and this album, with a number one single, made Green the biggest soul star in America.","The drum sound is a deliberate piece of engineering: Jackson's kit was damped with tape and towels and recorded with very few microphones, so the beat has no ring at all and the voice can float above it. Listen for the moment in the title track where Green abandons words and just breathes in rhythm; Mitchell kept those breaths in the mix rather than editing them out.","It was received as the year's most sensual record and has been treated ever since as the high point of Memphis soul after Stax. Criticism has centred on repetition — Green and Mitchell made several albums to a very similar recipe — and on how to weigh the later gospel career against it. Green himself has said the songs were always closer to church than they sounded.","Put the title song on and listen only to the drums; once you hear how far behind the beat they sit, the whole record opens. Then 'I've Never Found a Girl' for the falsetto. Compare with Otis Redding's 'Dock of the Bay' for two Memphis singers discovering what quiet does."],"the-temptations-papa-was-a-rollin-stone":["The album version runs nearly twelve minutes and no one sings for the first four. A bass figure repeats, hi-hat ticking above it; a wah-wah guitar enters, then a lone muted trumpet, then strings scraping high and dissonant, then handclaps — each layer added over an unchanging foundation. When the voices finally arrive they trade single lines about a father who died on the day the singer was born. The instrumental sections return between every verse.","Norman Whitfield had been extending Motown records for four years and here pushed the method to its limit, after first cutting the song with the Undisputed Truth. The Temptations reportedly resented being made to wait four minutes to sing on their own single. Motown had moved its headquarters to Los Angeles, the Detroit era was over, and the record's cinematic length matched the blaxploitation scores then dominating Black radio.","The strings are not doing what strings usually do: they are placed high and left unresolved, functioning as tension rather than sweetness, which is unusual anywhere in Motown. Listen for the trumpet, which states a three-note phrase and then vanishes for minutes at a time. The single edit cuts the song roughly in half and removes most of what makes it strange.","It won three Grammys, including one for the instrumental B-side, which rather made the Temptations' point about who the record belonged to. It is now cited as the end of Motown's Detroit period and the single most sampled Motown production. The argument that remains is the old one between producer and performer: whether a twelve-minute track with four minutes of vamping serves the group or the man at the desk.","Insist on the twelve-minute album version. Time the entries — bass, hi-hat, guitar, trumpet, strings — and notice nothing is ever removed. Then play 'My Girl' for the same group eight years earlier, and Isaac Hayes's Shaft theme for the other great exercise in pure accumulation."],"curtis-mayfield-superfly":["A film score that works as an album because Mayfield wrote songs rather than cues. Congas and a hi-hat set almost every track moving; horns answer the falsetto; strings arrive to make the street sound tragic rather than glamorous. 'Freddie's Dead' is an instrumental in the film and a song here. 'Pusherman' introduces the dealer in the first person, which is the record's central device: Mayfield sings as the people the film only shows.","Gordon Parks Jr. was making a film about a Harlem cocaine dealer trying to get out. Mayfield, running his own Curtom label in Chicago, saw the script, thought it glamorised the trade, and wrote a commentary rather than an accompaniment. He recorded it before the film was finished. The album was released ahead of the picture and became one of the very few soundtracks to earn more than the film it belonged to.","The lyrics contradict the images they accompany. In the film the dealer is stylish; in the songs he is a dead man walking, and the most seductive groove on the record carries the bleakest words. Listen for Mayfield's guitar, tuned to an open chord and played with his thumb, sitting slightly across the conga pattern. The strings quote the same three-note falling phrase in several tracks.","It was a huge commercial success and gave blaxploitation its conscience; critics who attacked the cycle for selling dealers and pimps generally exempted this record, because it says the opposite of the film. Its afterlife has been enormous in hip-hop, where the rhythm tracks have been sampled for four decades. The persistent question is whether a warning that grooves this well can function as a warning at all.","Play 'Pusherman' and read the words while it goes; the gap between the two is the whole album. Then 'Freddie's Dead' for the string writing. Gershwin's Porgy and Bess makes a useful companion — another ambitious portrait of a Black neighbourhood that drew the same argument about who it was for."],"stevie-wonder-talking-book":["Ten songs in which Wonder plays most of the instruments himself, including the drums. The sound is new: a clavinet, an electric keyboard with a hard plucked attack, played through effects until it functions as a rhythm guitar, with synthesiser bass underneath. 'Superstition' is the demonstration — layered clavinet parts, a horn section, and drums that Wonder recorded first, alone. 'You Are the Sunshine of My Life' sits at the other end, soft and jazz-inflected, sung partly by other voices.","Wonder turned twenty-one in 1971, let his Motown contract lapse, and renegotiated one that gave him ownership of his publishing and complete artistic control — almost unheard of for a Black artist at the label. He then worked with Malcolm Cecil and Robert Margouleff and their enormous custom synthesiser, TONTO, at a rate of several songs a week. He toured with the Rolling Stones that year, which took his music to a white rock audience.","The sleeve carries the album title in braille, the only major pop record of the period to do so, and it is not decoration: Wonder insisted on it. Musically, listen for how 'Superstition' has no bass guitar at all — the low end is a keyboard — and how the drum track was laid down before anything else, so the whole song hangs off a performance by the singer.","It was the record that established Wonder as an album artist rather than a singles act, and the beginning of the run of five albums usually treated as his classic period. Critics noted at the time how strange it was for one man to play everything; the counter-argument, that the records lose the friction of a band, is still occasionally made about the later albums and rarely about this one.","Listen to 'Superstition' for the drums first and the clavinet second, which reverses how everyone hears it. Then 'Maybe Your Baby' for seven minutes of the same instrument doing something dirtier. Follow with Innervisions, recorded within months, to hear the method turned on politics."],"marvin-gaye-lets-get-it-on":["Eight songs, thirty-two minutes, and a completely different climate from the album before it. A wah-wah guitar opens the title track with three notes that everyone recognises; congas and a loose drum pattern keep the tempo slow; strings and horns swell and retreat. Gaye stacks his own voice again, but now the overdubs are moans and encouragements behind the lead. 'Distant Lover' turns the mood inside out, a slow plea sung almost entirely in falsetto.","Gaye had moved to Los Angeles and was recording at Motown's West Coast studio. He was separating from Anna Gordy, Berry Gordy's sister, and had met Janis Hunter, who was seventeen. Ed Townsend, who co-wrote the title song, had drafted it about recovery from addiction; Gaye rewrote it as an argument for sex. Released two years after What's Going On, it outsold it, and fixed his public image for the rest of his life.","The three-note guitar hook is played with a wah-wah pedal held nearly still, so it sounds vocal rather than funky — the guitar is doing an impression of the singer. Listen also for how Gaye buries his own spoken and sighed overdubs low in the mix; on headphones there is a second, private performance running under the sung one. The record's sound is deliberately warmer and murkier than its predecessor's.","It was an enormous commercial success and was immediately used to file Gaye as a seduction artist, which flattened the reputation the previous album had built. Later writing has been more interested in the unease audible in the songs, and in how the marriage that was ending would produce the bitter divorce album of 1978. The title track's ubiquity has made it almost impossible to hear as music.","Skip the title track first time and start with 'Distant Lover', where the falsetto and the strings do the work. Then return to the opening for the guitar hook alone. Headphones matter here more than on any other Gaye record, because of what is hidden under the lead vocal."],"al-green-call-me":["Ten tracks, the same Hi Records band and the same damped drum sound, but the writing has widened. 'Here I Am (Come and Take Me)' rides a fast, light groove; 'Call Me (Come Back Home)' is a slow plea with horns sighing behind it; 'Jesus Is Waiting' ends the record with eight minutes of gospel that never quite becomes a sermon. Two country songs sit in the middle, sung straight. Green's falsetto is used more sparingly and lands harder.","Memphis, 1973, with Willie Mitchell producing and the Hi rhythm section intact. Green was at his commercial peak and beginning to be uneasy about it; the religious material on this album is not a late addition but the centre of it, and within a few years he would be ordained and largely leave secular music. Recording a Hank Williams song and a Willie Nelson song on a soul album was, in Memphis in 1973, a quiet act of border-crossing.","The two country covers are the key to the record. Green sings 'I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry' almost without ornament, which exposes how close honky-tonk phrasing and soul phrasing already were. Listen also to 'Jesus Is Waiting', where the band plays a secular groove under an entirely sacred lyric, and to the organ, which is playing church chords throughout the love songs.","It is frequently argued to be the better album than Let's Stay Together, more varied and less reliant on a single hit, though it has never been as famous. Critics have used it to show that the division between soul and country was a marketing arrangement rather than a musical one. The gospel material, once heard as a curiosity, now looks like the announcement of what came next.","Start with 'Here I Am' for the groove, then jump to the country covers and hear how little he changes them. Finish with 'Jesus Is Waiting' and its eight minutes. Dusty in Memphis makes the obvious pairing: two records made in the same city out of songs from somewhere else."],"stevie-wonder-innervisions":["Nine songs, forty-four minutes, most of them played entirely by one man. 'Too High' opens with scat singing over a jittery bass; 'Living for the City' runs seven minutes and breaks in the middle for a piece of street theatre with recorded traffic and a wrongful arrest; 'Higher Ground' is built on a clavinet run through a wah-wah pedal; 'He's Misstra Know-It-All' closes it gently. The synthesiser bass is everywhere and there is almost no guitar.","Wonder was twenty-three and working at enormous speed with the TONTO synthesiser system and its two operators. The subjects had changed: drugs, racism, the mechanics of poverty, and a portrait of a politician widely taken to be Richard Nixon. Three days after the album's release, in August 1973, a car he was travelling in struck a logging truck in North Carolina and he spent days in a coma; he lost his sense of smell and nearly his life.","The dramatised sequence in 'Living for the City' — a man arriving in New York, arrested within minutes — uses actors and street recordings and was cut from the single, so most people who know the song have never heard its point. Listen also to how much of the album's low end is synthesiser rather than bass guitar, and to 'Higher Ground', written and recorded within hours, weeks before the crash.","It won the Grammy for album of the year and consolidated the argument that Wonder was the most complete musician in popular music. The accident and the near-prophetic 'Higher Ground' attached a mythology to the record that critics have since tried to peel away. The lasting debate is about the one-man method: whether the absence of a band is the source of the album's coherence or the reason it sometimes sounds airless.","Hear 'Living for the City' in the full album version, including the spoken sequence; then hear the single edit and notice what was removed. 'Higher Ground' repays listening for the clavinet alone. Play it after Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, which it answers directly and rather less gently."],"stevie-wonder-songs-in-the-key-of-life":["Two long-playing records plus a bonus disc, over a hundred minutes, and the range is deliberate: big-band tribute ('Sir Duke'), a school-yard memoir over one of the hardest grooves he ever cut ('I Wish'), a synthesiser string quartet describing a slum ('Village Ghetto Land'), a fourteen-year-old's jazz-fusion instrumental, a lullaby for his newborn daughter, and a seven-minute declaration of love that changes key repeatedly. Wonder plays much of it and sings all of it.","He had threatened to retire and emigrate, then signed what was reported as the largest recording contract to that point, and spent two years in the studio with dozens of musicians passing through. It arrived in 1976, in the American bicentennial year, as a summing-up: Black American music surveyed from swing through gospel to synthesiser funk by a man who had been a Motown child star fourteen years earlier.","'Village Ghetto Land' has no real instruments at all: the string quartet is a synthesiser, and the mismatch between a polite classical minuet and a lyric about rats and rotting food is the entire device. 'Isn't She Lovely' includes a recording of his daughter being bathed. 'Sir Duke' names Ellington, Basie, Glenn Miller and Louis Armstrong outright, and the horn line imitates a swing band in unison.","It entered the American chart at number one, which almost nothing did in 1976, and won album of the year. The standing criticism is length: two records and an EP contain material most artists would have edited, and the sequencing asks a lot. The counter-case is that the sprawl is the subject, and that no shorter record could have held a survey of a whole tradition.","Do not attempt it in one sitting. Take side one for 'Love's in Need of Love Today' and 'Village Ghetto Land' back to back — the softest and the sharpest thing on the album. Then 'Sir Duke', and then Ellington himself, whom the song is paying for. Keep the bonus disc for later; 'All Day Sucker' is worth finding."],"james-brown-live-at-the-apollo":["A single show, captured whole: James Brown and the Famous Flames at the Apollo Theater in Harlem, fronted by a big R&B band with a hard horn section. It runs like a revue, not a playlist. Short songs are cut together with no gaps, the band hits every cue on the singer's signal, and the centre of the record is the long slow ballad Lost Someone, stretched to ten minutes of pleading, silence and screams from the crowd before the band tears into a medley of his hits.","Brown's label, King Records of Cincinnati, run by Syd Nathan, saw no point in a live album: live records did not sell, and singles were the business. Brown believed his stage show was the real product and paid for the recording himself. It was taped in October 1962 during a week of shows at the Apollo, the proving ground of Black entertainment in New York, at a time when rhythm and blues was still largely kept off white radio.","Listen to the opening. Before a note is sung, the master of ceremonies runs through Brown's song titles and billings in a rising incantation, and the band is already vamping underneath him: the show is built as a ritual of anticipation. Then notice the audience. The shrieks from women near the stage are part of the drama, and during Lost Someone Brown plays them like an instrument, holding the band back until the room answers him.","It was a surprise hit, climbing high on the pop album chart and staying there for over a year, bought by listeners who played it straight through at parties. That success forced the industry to take both live albums and Black album buyers seriously. It now appears on nearly every list of the great live records, and critics tend to agree it catches Brown at the hinge point, still a soul balladeer, already a bandleader of military precision.","Play it loud and from the start, without skipping: the whole point is momentum. Listen to how the band responds to Brown's shouted cues rather than to written bar lengths. Then compare Sam Cooke's Harlem Square Club set from the following year, rawer and gentler, and Brown's own later funk sides, where the song dissolves entirely into the groove."],"james-brown-papas-got-a-brand-new-bag":["Just over two minutes of a new idea. The horns stab short bright chords, the guitar scratches a tight choked rhythm high on the neck, and the whole band leans on the first beat of every bar instead of the backbeat that R&B had always stressed. Brown's voice is barked and percussive, the words almost secondary to the attack. The tune barely moves harmonically; the excitement comes from rhythm, from where the accents land and from the silences between them.","By 1965 Brown was the hardest-touring act in Black America, with a large, rigorously drilled band that he fined for missed cues. He was looking for a sound that would carry his stage show onto a three-minute single. The song, about an older man showing he can still keep up with new dances, arrived as soul was crossing into the pop charts, and it became Brown's first top-ten pop hit and earned him his first Grammy.","The record is faster than the band played it. The master tape was sped up for release, which raised the pitch and tightened the feel, giving Brown's voice its slightly strained edge. The full take was also longer and was split into two parts across the single. Listen too for the guitar: the scratchy muted strumming, played by Jimmy Nolen, would become the signature texture of funk guitar for the next decade.","Brown himself called it a turning point, and historians broadly agree, though they argue about whether this or Cold Sweat two years later is the first true funk record. The consensus now is that this is the hinge: still shaped like a twelve-bar song, but already built around rhythm rather than melody. Its influence on dance music, from Motown to Afrobeat, is hard to overstate.","Ignore the words and count along: feel how the weight lands hard on the first beat, then how much space follows. Listen to the guitar in isolation for one pass and the horns for another. Then play Cold Sweat and hear the same ideas with the song form stripped away."],"james-brown-cold-sweat":["Seven minutes built on almost nothing: a bass figure, a scratching guitar, a horn riff and a drum pattern that stutters around the beat, locked together and repeated. There is effectively one chord for long stretches. Brown sings, grunts and calls out, then hands the floor to a saxophone solo and, famously, to the drummer, who plays a short break alone. Everything that had been accompaniment in rhythm and blues has become the substance.","The arrangement came together with the band's musical director, the saxophonist Alfred 'Pee Wee' Ellis, who shaped it from an older Brown ballad. It was recorded at King's studio in Cincinnati in 1967, the year of the Detroit and Newark riots and of psychedelia's summer. Released as a two-part single, it went to the top of the R&B chart and into the pop top ten, a startling result for a record without a chorus to speak of.","Ellis said the horn line was inspired by Miles Davis's So What, from Kind of Blue: the same idea of a short riff answering a phrase, turned from cool jazz into something clipped and physical. Listen too for the moment Brown calls on the drummer, Clyde Stubblefield, to take a solo: it is one of the first isolated funk breaks on record, a template for the drum breaks later hip-hop producers would hunt for.","Many writers name it the first fully formed funk record, the point at which harmony stops moving and rhythm takes over. Musicians at the time were startled by how little happened harmonically and how much rhythmically. It is now taught as a foundation text of groove-based music, and Ellis's role, long under-credited, is increasingly acknowledged.","Put on the full seven-minute version rather than the single edit. Listen first to the bass and the kick drum together, then to the way the guitar and horns fill only the gaps they leave. Follow it with Kind of Blue's opening track to hear the borrowed idea, and with Funky Drummer to hear where it led."],"james-brown-say-it-loud":["A funk groove with a chant at its centre. Brown half-speaks, half-shouts verses about dignity, work and self-reliance, and a chorus of children answers him with the title, turning the song into a call-and-response rally. The band keeps the tight interlocking pattern of Cold Sweat, horns jabbing, guitar scratching, bass circling, so that the message rides on the body rather than on a melody. It is a protest song with no lament in it at all.","It was recorded in Los Angeles in the summer of 1968, months after the assassination of Martin Luther King. In April Brown had been credited with helping keep Boston calm by letting his concert be broadcast live on television the night after the killing. The children were recruited locally for the session. The song arrived as the phrase 'Black is beautiful' and the Black Power movement were reshaping how a generation spoke about itself.","Notice who sings the chorus: not the band or backing singers but kids, which makes the statement sound like something being taught to the next generation, not argued with the current one. The verses also say nothing about violence or grievance; they talk about building, owning and working. The pride is framed as economic self-determination, a theme Brown would return to throughout the next decade.","It topped the R&B chart for weeks and reached the pop top ten, yet Brown later said it cost him part of his white audience and some radio play. Some in the Black Power movement thought him too cosy with politicians; others heard the song as the moment soul music said the word Black with pride on national radio. That second view now dominates.","Listen to it as a chant, not a song: notice how short each exchange is, and how the children's answer lands on the beat like a drum. Then play Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready, from only three years earlier, to hear how far and how fast the tone of Black popular music moved."],"james-brown-funky-drummer":["A long, loose jam in which the song is almost a pretext. The band settles into a slinky groove, Brown ad-libs and directs, a saxophone takes a turn, and then, near the end, everything drops away except the drummer. For about twenty seconds Clyde Stubblefield plays alone: a light, bouncing pattern with ghost notes skittering around a steady backbeat, relaxed and precise at once. It is those twenty seconds, not the song, that became one of the most recycled pieces of music ever recorded.","Recorded in Cincinnati in November 1969, it was released early in 1970 as a two-part single and did modestly on the R&B chart. It was, by Brown's standards, a minor record. Its second life began in 1986, when a Polydor compilation called In the Jungle Groove issued the full unedited version, just as affordable digital samplers were letting hip-hop producers loop short passages of old records.","Listen to what Stubblefield plays with his left hand on the snare: the quiet in-between strokes, not the loud ones, give the pattern its bounce, and that softness is what made it so easy to loop under a rapper. Brown can be heard naming the drummer and telling the band to hold back, arranging the famous break in real time rather than planning it.","The break has been sampled on hundreds or, by some counts, over a thousand records, from Public Enemy and Eric B. and Rakim to LL Cool J and much of 1990s dance music. It has become the central case in arguments about sampling and credit: Stubblefield, as a salaried sideman, received no royalties for its reuse, and said so often before his death in 2017.","Listen to the whole nine minutes first, so the break arrives as a surprise, then loop those twenty seconds and hear how much is in them. Then play It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back or Paid in Full and try to catch Stubblefield's pattern buried underneath."],"james-brown-sex-machine":["Two voices trading lines over a small, lean band. Brown calls, his long-time partner Bobby Byrd answers, and underneath a teenage bassist plays a rubbery, constantly moving line against a guitar that chops the same short figure over and over. Horns are nearly absent. Midway Brown asks the band to change section and they move to a new chord and back again, the only harmonic event in the track. The subject is the dance floor and desire, handled with a wink.","In March 1970 most of Brown's band walked out over pay and conditions. Within days he had hired a young Cincinnati group led by the brothers William 'Bootsy' and Phelps 'Catfish' Collins, then still in their teens, and renamed them the J.B.'s. This was one of the first records made with them, in Nashville that spring, with John 'Jabo' Starks on drums. It was released as a two-part single and later recut as a pseudo-live track.","Listen to Bootsy Collins's bass: it barely repeats itself, bubbling and sliding under the static guitar, and that restless line is what the young players brought that the old band did not. Brown's question to the band about going to the bridge, and Byrd's answers, are not lyrics so much as stage directions, a live rehearsal captured on tape and turned into the hook.","It was a big R&B and pop hit and has become one of Brown's signature pieces. Critics tend to rank it just behind Cold Sweat as a funk touchstone, and more than a few prefer its lighter, bouncier feel. Bootsy's short stay with Brown, only about a year, is now seen as the bridge that carried Brown's discipline into the looser world of Parliament-Funkadelic.","Follow the bass alone for one play, then the call-and-response between Brown and Byrd. Count how long the band stays on one chord. Then play Parliament's Mothership Connection and hear Bootsy and the Brown horn players, a few years later, let the same groove sprawl."],"james-brown-funky-president":["A mid-tempo, heavy-footed groove with a hard, dry drum sound, bright horn punctuations and Brown talking more than singing. The track is a state-of-the-nation address in funk form: hard times, rising prices, and a new man in the White House. Brown's advice is practical rather than angry, urging listeners to pool money, buy land and build businesses. The drums open the track bare, and that clean, weighty pattern is what later ears seized on.","It was released on Brown's 1974 album Reality, months after Gerald Ford replaced Richard Nixon, during recession and oil shock. Brown was by then a figure in national politics as well as music, courted by politicians of both parties and criticised for it. The early seventies were his most prolific funk years, working with the trombonist and arranger Fred Wesley, as disco was about to change what dance floors wanted.","Listen to the opening drum pattern on its own: short, clean, with space around each hit. That is why it was sampled for Eric B. and Rakim's debut single Eric B. Is President, whose title is itself a nod to this record. The economic message also echoes Say It Loud: pride expressed as ownership and self-help rather than protest.","It was a solid R&B hit but little noticed by the white press at the time, when Brown was often treated as a spent force. Its reputation grew with hip-hop, which mined its drums repeatedly. Critics now read it alongside Gil Scott-Heron and Curtis Mayfield as a sharp piece of mid-seventies social commentary, though some find the politics muddled.","Listen to the first few seconds several times and then play Eric B. Is President to hear what the producers kept and what they threw away. Then return to the whole track and follow Brown's talk as a sermon over a groove, not a song with verses."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-everyday-people":["A two-and-a-half-minute pop song that sounds like a nursery rhyme and is about prejudice. The band's voices, male and female, take turns and then join in unison over a bouncing piano and a steady beat. The song lists the small differences that divide people, long hair and short, rich and poor, and shrugs them off with a playground chant. Its tone is warm and teasing rather than preachy, which is exactly why it carried so far.","Released in late 1968, the year of the King and Kennedy assassinations and of riots in American cities, it reached number one on the pop chart early in 1969. Sly and the Family Stone were themselves the argument: a San Francisco band of Black and white musicians, men and women, which was still rare enough in 1968 to be a statement. It preceded the album Stand!, released that spring.","Listen to Larry Graham's bass: for most of the song it plays essentially one note, over and over, while the chords shift above it. It is a deliberately simple part that keeps the song grounded. The song also popularised a common American phrase about different strokes for different people, a saying that passed into everyday speech from this record.","It was the band's first number one and has hardly left the air since, used in advertising, children's television and campaigns. Some critics find it slight next to the band's heavier funk, but most admire the craft: an argument for tolerance with no sanctimony. It was reworked in 1992 by Arrested Development, keeping the spirit and changing the sound.","Listen for the order in which the voices enter and how they change each time. Notice the bass barely moving. Then play Stand! as a whole album to hear how the same band could be both this sunny and much tougher."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-stand":["Eight tracks that fuse soul vocals, rock guitar, gospel and the new funk rhythm into one sound. The title song is a short anthem of defiance; I Want to Take You Higher is an explosion of shouted voices and fuzz guitar; Everyday People is sunny pop. Side two ends with a thirteen-minute instrumental jam in which Sly sings through a wah-wah effect. The voices constantly swap lead, so the album sounds like a crowd rather than a star with a backing band.","Sly Stone was a former San Francisco radio DJ and producer who had built his band as a model of integration. Stand! came out in May 1969, a few months before the band's triumphant set at Woodstock, and made them stars with rock audiences as well as soul listeners. Its politics, about race, self-belief and mutual suspicion, reflected a year of assassinations, Black Power and the counterculture colliding.","The title track ends with a sudden, much slower gospel coda, and it sounds different because it was reportedly recorded later with outside session musicians rather than the whole band. Listen too for Cynthia Robinson's trumpet cries and ad-libs: she was a woman playing lead horn in a funk band in 1969, and her voice is one of the band's signatures.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and is often called their most complete statement, the moment their optimism and their anger were in balance. A few critics find the long jam self-indulgent; most hear it as a seed of later funk and hip-hop. Its fusion of rock and soul shaped Miles Davis, the Temptations' producer Norman Whitfield, George Clinton and, later, Prince.","Start with I Want to Take You Higher, loud, then go back to the start and take the album in order. Listen for how often the lead voice changes hands. Then play Jimi Hendrix's Electric Ladyland and the Temptations' Cloud Nine to hear the same rock-soul border crossed from each side."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-thank-you":["A lean, mid-tempo groove that feels both celebratory and weary. The guitar chops a clipped rhythm, the horns punctuate, and the band's voices sing a gratitude chorus with a slightly sardonic edge. The star is the bass: Larry Graham hits the strings with his thumb and pops them with his fingers, making the bass sound half like a drum. The misspelled title, written as it is sung, announces a band having fun with language.","Released at the end of 1969 as a single, it went to number one early in 1970, with Everybody Is a Star on the other side. It did not appear on a regular studio album at the time. The band were at the height of their fame after Woodstock, but Sly's growing drug use, pressure from Black activists and record-company demands were pulling the group apart.","The verses name-check the band's own earlier hits, turning the song into a wry look back at their success. Two years later Sly remade it as the slow, druggy Thank You for Talkin' to Me Africa on There's a Riot Goin' On, the same song drained of energy. Listen to Graham's bass technique, which he developed to cover missing drums in his mother's church band: it is one of the first recorded examples of slap bass.","Hugely popular at the time, it became a key text for bassists: slap bass was taken up across funk, disco and later rock, so Graham's playing here is often called one of the most influential bass parts on record. Janet Jackson sampled it for Rhythm Nation in 1989. Critics hear its mixed mood as the band's turn from optimism.","Follow the bass line from start to finish and notice when it slaps and when it glides. Then play Thank You for Talkin' to Me Africa from There's a Riot Goin' On to hear the same song seen through a darker lens."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-theres-a-riot-goin-on":["The sound of a party ending. The grooves are slower, the textures murky and dry, the voices slurred and half-buried. Family Affair, the hit single, is a quiet, eerie song built on a primitive drum machine and a sinuous electric piano. Elsewhere the funk is heavy and stripped down, and a long slow remake of the band's own Thank You closes the record. It is funk turned inward: claustrophobic, ambivalent, full of mood rather than slogans.","Two years after Stand! and Woodstock, Sly was holed up in Los Angeles, using heavy drugs, missing concerts, and under pressure from the label for new music. The band was fragmenting; much of the album was made by Sly alone, overdubbing parts at home and in the studio. It came out in November 1971, after the killings at Kent State and Jackson State, and as the optimism of the late 1960s curdled.","The title track is listed on the sleeve as lasting no time at all: it is silent, a joke and a statement, perhaps an answer to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On from earlier that year. The muddy sound is partly the result of Sly recording over the same tapes again and again until they wore. Listen for the Maestro Rhythm King drum machine ticking under Family Affair, one of the first uses of a drum box on a hit record.","Reviewers were baffled and some hostile, yet it went to number one on the album chart. Its reputation has only grown: it is now routinely named among the greatest albums of its era and a source for everything from Miles Davis's On the Corner to D'Angelo's Voodoo. The debate is whether its darkness is artistic vision or the sound of a collapse.","Listen at night, on headphones, from beginning to end. Start by noticing the drum machine on Family Affair, then compare the closing Thank You remake with the original single. Then play What's Going On from the same year: two very different answers to the same American moment."],"parliament-mothership-connection":["Seven tracks of spacious, slow-rolling funk presented as a broadcast from outer space. George Clinton plays a cosmic DJ, Star Child, arriving to bring the funk back to Earth. The grooves are thick with interlocking parts: Bootsy Collins's elastic bass, Bernie Worrell's synthesizers, a horn section led by former James Brown players, and a dozen voices chanting and joking. Give Up the Funk, with its crowd chant about tearing the roof off, is the best-known track.","Clinton ran two overlapping bands, Parliament and Funkadelic, with shared personnel and separate record deals. Parliament signed to Casablanca, the Los Angeles label that was also building disco. For this album he brought in the horn players Maceo Parker and Fred Wesley from James Brown's band. Released in December 1975, it was followed by a tour featuring a giant spaceship prop that descended onto the stage.","The title track borrows a line and melody from the spiritual Swing Low, Sweet Chariot, turning the chariot that carries souls home into a spaceship: an Afrofuturist image of deliverance and a link between Black sacred song and science fiction. Listen for Worrell's synthesizer in the background, bending and swooping like a theremin in a science-fiction film.","It was Parliament's first gold album and made P-Funk a mass phenomenon. Critics at the time found it silly; the fashion now is to read its science-fiction mythology as a serious strand of Afrofuturism, next to Sun Ra. It was heavily sampled in the late 1980s and early 1990s, from De La Soul to Dr. Dre's G-funk.","Start with Give Up the Funk and listen to how many separate parts are playing without cluttering. Then go back to the title track, and when you hear the spiritual, play the Fisk Jubilee Singers' Swing Low, Sweet Chariot. Finish with Sun Ra's Space Is the Place."],"parliament-flash-light":["A slow, heavy funk track whose bass line is played not on a bass guitar but on synthesizers. Bernie Worrell's Minimoog lines bounce and wobble low in the mix, stacked several at once, under a chanting chorus that trades nonsense syllables and a group sing-along. The drums are simple and steady, the guitar sparse; the energy comes from the synthetic bass and the chants passing between voices.","It appeared on the album Funkentelechy vs. the Placebo Syndrome, released in late 1977, part of Clinton's mythology in which Star Child defeats the villain Sir Nose, who refuses to dance. Issued as a single, it topped the R&B chart in 1978, knocking disco aside for a while. Synthesizers were becoming affordable, and this record showed how they could replace instruments rather than decorate them.","The bass line is built from several separately recorded Minimoog parts layered together, which gives it that thick, slightly unstable wobble no single instrument could produce. Bootsy Collins, normally the bassist, is reported to have played drums. Listen for the chanting in the chorus, which has almost no words, just rhythm and sound.","It became one of the most influential synthesizer bass lines ever recorded, a direct ancestor of 1980s electro and of the whining synth bass of West Coast G-funk. Critics now credit Worrell as one of the great under-recognised keyboard innovators of the era, a conservatory-trained pianist who brought classical technique and a sense of orchestral colour into funk.","Listen through headphones and try to separate the layered Moog lines, some low and round, some buzzing. Notice how little the drums do by comparison. Then play Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock and hear the same idea, electronic bass driving a dance record, taken a step further toward the machine."],"funkadelic-maggot-brain":["The album opens with its most famous track: a ten-minute guitar solo by Eddie Hazel, slow, grieving and increasingly ecstatic, over a quiet, repeated figure. After Clinton's brief spoken introduction the rest of the band drop almost out of hearing. The other six tracks move between gospel-inflected soul, hard rock riffing and chaotic, noise-laden psychedelia, ending with the sprawling Wars of Armageddon. It is funk at its most rock-minded and most experimental.","Funkadelic were George Clinton's Detroit-based band, recording for the small Westbound label. They were steeped in Hendrix and Sly Stone and played loud to both Black and white crowds. Released in 1971, the album came out as the counterculture fractured and the Vietnam War dragged on. The cover photograph, a woman's head apparently buried in soil and screaming, set a tone of apocalyptic dread.","Clinton later said he told Hazel to play as if he had just learned his mother had died, and then as if he discovered she was alive. The track was reportedly recorded with the full band, whose parts were then pulled down in the mix to leave Hazel almost alone. Listen for the echo and phasing on the guitar, which makes one player sound like an entire choir.","It sold modestly, and critics at the time were divided by its mix of genres. The title track has since become a touchstone for guitarists, often named among the greatest solos recorded, and the album is now regarded as the peak of Funkadelic's early work. Some find the second half uneven next to its opening.","Listen to the title track in a dark room, start to finish, without interruption, and follow how Hazel's phrases grow from sighs into cries. Then play Can You Get to That for the gospel side of the band. Afterwards try Hendrix's Electric Ladyland and Frank Zappa's Hot Rats, and ask how Hazel's lyricism differs from their virtuosity."],"funkadelic-one-nation-under-a-groove":["Funkadelic at its most polished and accessible. The title track is a seven-minute mid-tempo groove, with a gentle chant about a dance floor as a united nation and synthesizer lines weaving through the choir of voices. The rest moves from comic funk to heavy guitar rock, including a track whose title openly asks who says a funk band cannot play rock. It is funk as community, a sprawling party record with a utopian streak.","By 1978 Funkadelic had moved to Warner Bros. and was in competition with Clinton's other band, Parliament, and with disco, which dominated the charts. Walter 'Junie' Morrison, formerly of the Ohio Players, became a key writer and keyboard player. Released that autumn, the title track spent six weeks at number one on the R&B chart and the album became the band's biggest seller.","Original copies came with a bonus seven-inch record of extra tracks, including a live version of Maggot Brain: the band glancing back at its earlier self. Listen for Morrison's keyboard parts, which carry much of the title song's melodic work, a real shift from the guitar-driven sound of the early Funkadelic records.","It was praised on release and is widely regarded as Funkadelic's best album, or at least its most complete. Critics note how it answers disco without surrendering to it, keeping the groove danceable but loose and human. It has been sampled throughout hip-hop, and its title has passed into common use as shorthand for the ideal of the dance floor as a place of unity.","Start with the title track and listen to how many voices share the chant, then to the synthesizer threading through them. Then go back to Maggot Brain and hear how far the band travelled in seven years. Finish with Chic's Good Times, from the following year, to compare funk's sprawling unity with disco's cool elegance."],"earth-wind-and-fire-thats-the-way-of-the-world":["Eight tracks of bright, disciplined, jazz-inflected soul and funk. Shining Star is a tight, punchy funk song built on unison vocals and stabbing horns; the title track is a slow, warm anthem of optimism; Reasons is a falsetto ballad that shows off Philip Bailey's soaring voice. Throughout, the horns are arranged with jazz-like precision, the rhythm section is crisp, and Maurice White's kalimba, an African thumb piano, adds shimmering colour.","The album was recorded as the soundtrack to a film of the same name, about the music industry, starring Harvey Keitel. The film flopped; the album, released in March 1975 by Columbia, went to number one, and Shining Star topped the singles chart. The arranger Charles Stepney, from Chicago, helped shape the band's sophisticated sound, before his early death the next year.","Listen for the kalimba, often tucked behind the horns: White used it on stage and on record as a symbol of the band's African and spiritual aspirations. Shining Star ends with its title sung a cappella, the band suddenly naked after the full funk arrangement. The album was released well before the film, so most listeners never connected it with the movie at all.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and remains its most admired album, and Shining Star won a Grammy. Critics praised the craftsmanship; a few found the relentless positivity bland beside grittier, angrier funk. Over time the band's precision and spiritual aspiration have made them a touchstone for later soul and R&B producers, and the album is a staple of lists of the decade's best.","Start with Shining Star and listen for the horns hitting exactly with the voices. Then play Reasons and follow Bailey's falsetto as it climbs. Listen for the kalimba glinting in the background. Then compare Parliament's Mothership Connection, also from 1975: Earth, Wind & Fire's cosmos is spiritual and sleek, Parliament's comic and gloriously chaotic."],"earth-wind-and-fire-september":["Three and a half minutes of pure uplift. A bright guitar figure, a bouncing bass and swinging horns carry a song of remembered love and dancing, sung in a mix of Maurice White's warm lead and Philip Bailey's falsetto. The chorus is built on a nonsense syllable, repeated and harmonised, that anyone can sing. The mood is so relentlessly joyous that it has become a default soundtrack for celebrations.","Recorded in 1978 for the compilation The Best of Earth, Wind & Fire, Vol. 1, it was released as a single that autumn and became a major international hit. White wrote it with the band's guitarist Al McKay and the songwriter Allee Willis. Disco was at its peak, and the song's four-on-the-floor bounce and horn arrangements sit on the border between funk and disco.","Willis wanted to replace the nonsense syllable in the chorus with a real word; White refused, telling her, as she recalled, never to let the lyrics get in the way of the groove. The song names a date in September, and that day is now marked every year online as a kind of informal holiday. Listen to how the horns answer the vocals, almost like a second chorus.","It was a hit on release and has become one of the most streamed and licensed songs of its era, a fixture of weddings, films and sports arenas. Critics once dismissed it as lightweight; now many admire it as a perfectly engineered piece of joy. Some find its ubiquity exhausting, but few deny its craft.","Listen for the moment the horns first enter, then sing the chorus. Then play That's the Way of the World to hear the same band in a more serious mood, and Chic's Le Freak to hear another route from funk to disco."],"the-meters-cissy-strut":["A three-minute instrumental played by a four-piece band, with space as the main ingredient. The guitar and bass play a short, angular riff in unison, the organ fills in above, and the drums skip and syncopate around them with a looseness that comes straight from New Orleans street parades. Nothing is crowded; each player leaves gaps for the others. The result feels both lazy and precise, a groove that seems to walk rather than run.","The Meters were the house band for the New Orleans producer Allen Toussaint, playing on records by Lee Dorsey, Betty Harris and others. In 1969 they began releasing instrumentals under their own name on the Josie label. New Orleans had its own tradition of parade drumming and piano rhythm-and-blues, and the Meters brought these into funk at the same time as James Brown was developing his own version in Cincinnati.","Listen to Joseph 'Zigaboo' Modeliste's drumming: he often plays against the beat rather than on it, echoing the second-line rhythms of New Orleans brass bands. That rhythmic looseness is what makes the Meters sound different from Brown's tight band. The same unison riff structure, guitar and bass doubling each other, would become a funk staple.","It was a modest hit but made the Meters' reputation among musicians. Their records have since been sampled extensively in hip-hop, and they are regarded as one of the essential funk bands, sometimes called the originators of New Orleans funk. Many critics consider them underrated compared with Brown and Sly.","Listen to the drums first, and try to count along: notice where the accents fall. Then play Cold Sweat to compare Brown's tight version of funk with the Meters' looser one, and Fats Domino's The Fat Man for the New Orleans piano rhythm behind both."],"the-meters-rejuvenation":["The Meters with voices, a fuller sound and longer grooves. The album moves between tight, singing funk such as People Say and Hey Pocky A-Way, whose chant comes from Mardi Gras Indian tradition, and the eleven-minute It Ain't No Use, a slow-burning jam with extended guitar work. The drumming is still loose and syncopated, but the arrangements are richer and the songs have hooks. It feels like a band at the peak of its confidence.","By 1974 the Meters had moved to Reprise, a Warner Bros. label, and were recording with Allen Toussaint producing at his new Sea-Saint studio in New Orleans. They had backed Dr. John and Labelle and were soon opening for the Rolling Stones. The album came out as funk reached its commercial peak and disco was emerging, and the band hoped at last for a national hit of their own.","Hey Pocky A-Way draws on the chants of the Mardi Gras Indians, the Black carnival societies of New Orleans, and the Meters' Art and Cyril Neville had family ties to that tradition. Listen for the percussion that comes straight from parade music, and for the clavinet, a keyboard with a sharp, percussive tone that was central to 1970s funk.","Critics praised it on release, but it sold modestly, and the band never broke through nationally as its peers did. It is now frequently named the Meters' best album and one of the essential funk records. Some writers consider the Meters the most musically subtle of all the funk bands, and their influence is heard in New Orleans brass bands and jam bands to this day.","Start with Hey Pocky A-Way and listen to the percussion bubbling under the chant. Then give yourself eleven minutes for It Ain't No Use and let the long groove settle. Afterwards go back to Cissy Strut to hear how much the band's sound grew in five years, from four players and a riff to a full, singing band."],"chic-le-freak":["A sleek disco record with a funk band underneath. Nile Rodgers plays a tight, chiming guitar figure, Bernard Edwards a supple bass line, Tony Thompson steady drums, and strings glide above them. The chorus is a chant naming a new dance, sung by the band's vocalists in cool, precise unison. It is elegant rather than sweaty, with every part placed exactly.","Rodgers and Edwards wrote it after being turned away from Studio 54 in New York on New Year's Eve 1977, despite an invitation. They went home and wrote a riff with a much ruder chorus aimed at the club's door policy, then softened it into a dance-craze title. Released that autumn on Atlantic, it became the label's biggest-selling single to that date.","The song began as an angry reaction to the club's exclusivity, and it still carries a trace of that in its cool distance. Listen for Rodgers's guitar playing: the rhythmic 'chucking' technique, choked chords played in short bursts, would become his signature, later heard on records by David Bowie and Daft Punk.","It was a huge hit, topping the American chart, and remains a disco standard. Rock critics at the time often dismissed disco as faceless, but Chic's musicianship earned respect even then. It is now seen as one of disco's finest records and a model of how to make a dance record that is both simple and sophisticated, and its origin story has become part of disco legend.","Listen to the guitar and bass together first, then notice how the strings float above without crowding them. Count how few chords the song uses. Then play Good Times to hear Chic strip the formula down even further, and James Brown's Papa's Got a Brand New Bag to hear where that choked rhythm guitar came from."],"chic-good-times":["Eight minutes of cool, understated groove. Bernard Edwards's bass line walks and bounces through the song, carrying it; Nile Rodgers's guitar chops sparely above; strings and piano add colour. The vocals, sung in a detached, almost ironic tone, celebrate carefree fun. The track is built on repetition, with the bass line and the guitar pattern cycling almost unchanged, and a long instrumental break in the middle.","Released in June 1979 as the lead single from the album Risqué, it became Chic's second American number one. The country was sliding toward recession, with petrol queues and high inflation, so the song's celebration of good times carried an ironic undertone. Disco was at its commercial height and about to face a furious backlash, symbolised by the Disco Demolition Night riot at a Chicago baseball stadium that July.","The lyrics borrow phrases from Depression-era songs, a knowing nod to an earlier time of hardship masked by cheerful music. Within months the bass line was replayed by the Sugar Hill house band as the backing for Rapper's Delight, the first hip-hop hit; Rodgers and Edwards threatened legal action and won songwriting credit. Queen's Another One Bites the Dust, from 1980, is widely heard as borrowing its bass line.","It was a hit and has become one of the most influential dance records ever made. Critics now regard it as Chic's finest moment and its bass line as one of the most borrowed in pop. It is also a key text in debates over borrowing and credit, since the first rap hit was built on it before anyone had agreed the rules for such reuse.","Follow the bass line all the way through, and notice how it changes slightly each time while the guitar barely moves. Then play Rapper's Delight, where a studio band replays it for fifteen minutes under three rappers, and Grandmaster Flash's Adventures on the Wheels of Steel, where the original record is cut and scratched live on turntables."],"donna-summer-love-to-love-you-baby":["A seventeen-minute disco track built on a slow, steady groove and Donna Summer's breathy, intimate vocal. Strings swell and fall back, the rhythm section keeps a relaxed pulse, and the song drifts through repeated sections rather than following a verse-and-chorus plan. Its sensual tone is its defining feature, handled as mood and texture rather than explicit statement. It is disco as extended atmosphere.","Summer, an American singer living in Munich, recorded it with the producers Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte. A short version was released first in Europe. Neil Bogart of Casablanca Records in Los Angeles reportedly asked for a much longer version to play at parties, and the seventeen-minute track filled one side of the album. Released in late 1975, it became an international hit.","The long version was a new format in itself: a single piece that took up a whole side of a record, designed for dance floors where DJs needed long tracks. Listen for how little changes over seventeen minutes: the art is in the gradual build and release, a technique that would shape club music for decades.","It was a hit and a controversy, with some radio stations reluctant to play it because of its sensual tone. Many critics dismissed it as a novelty or a gimmick. Later writers see it as a pioneering extended dance track, the start of Summer and Moroder's partnership, and an early sign that Summer was a far more controlled and versatile singer than its reputation suggested.","Give it the full seventeen minutes, as intended, ideally in a dim room. Notice how the strings and rhythm change only slowly, and how the tension builds and releases. Then play I Feel Love, from two years later, to hear the same team replace the band with machines, and Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul for an earlier long slow burn."],"donna-summer-i-feel-love":["A pulsing electronic track built almost entirely on a Moog synthesizer. A rapid, repeating bass line, doubled by an echo, drives the song forward; shimmering synthesizer lines rise above it; and Donna Summer's voice floats high and airy over the machine. There is no chorus in the conventional sense, only a hypnotic cycle. It sounds futuristic even now: cold, sensual and relentless.","It was made at Musicland Studios in Munich in 1977 by Summer with the producers Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte, for I Remember Yesterday, a concept album whose songs moved through the decades of popular music; this closing track stood for the future. Everything except the kick drum was played on a Moog modular synthesizer, a room-sized instrument few pop producers dared use. Released as a single that summer, it became an international hit.","Keeping the Moog in time with the recording tape was a real technical problem; the studio's engineer devised a way to lock it to a click track, making the machine's pulse perfectly steady. The kick drum was played by a human drummer, Keith Forsey. By a much-repeated account, Brian Eno heard it and told David Bowie it was the sound of the future.","It was a hit and has come to be seen as one of the most important records ever made, the ancestor of electronic dance music, house, techno and synth-pop. Critics once treated disco as disposable; this record helped change that view. Some debate how much credit belongs to Summer and how much to Moroder, but most now emphasise the collaboration.","Listen on headphones and follow the bass line and its echo, which together make a gallop no human bassist could sustain. Notice how Summer's voice seems to float rather than drive. Then play Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express, from the same year, and Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear other kinds of pulsing repetition."],"donna-summer-bad-girls":["A double album that moves from hard-edged disco to rock and ballads. Hot Stuff opens with a guitar riff and a rock beat; the title track is a brassy, driving disco number with whistles and chants; Dim All the Lights builds from a slow ballad to a fast disco climax. The album closes with synthesizer-heavy tracks in the style of I Feel Love. Summer's voice ranges from soft to powerful, proving her as a singer, not just a disco figure.","Released in April 1979 on Casablanca, at disco's peak and just before its backlash, it was Summer's most successful album, spending weeks at number one in America. It was made in Los Angeles with Moroder and Bellotte. Summer wrote the title song herself, about women working the streets; the record company reportedly considered giving it to another singer before she recorded it.","Dim All the Lights was written by Summer alone. Listen for the long note she holds at its turning point, one of the most famous sustained notes in pop. Hot Stuff's guitar solo is by Jeff Baxter, formerly of Steely Dan and the Doobie Brothers, a sign of the album's reach toward rock radio.","It was praised on release and is widely regarded as Summer's best album, the point where she became a full pop star rather than a disco voice. Critics admired its range and its rock crossover, and Hot Stuff won her a Grammy in a rock category. Some have debated the title track's handling of its subject; most now hear it as sympathetic rather than exploitative.","Start with Hot Stuff and hear how the rock guitar sits on a disco beat. Then play Dim All the Lights and wait for the long held note at its turning point. Finish with the synthesizer tracks at the end of the album, and then I Feel Love, to hear where their sound came from."],"giorgio-moroder-from-here-to-eternity":["A short album of synthesizer disco whose first side runs as one continuous suite, each song flowing into the next without a break for the DJ to cover. Sequenced bass lines pulse underneath, bright synthesizer melodies ride on top, and Moroder's own voice comes through a vocoder, flattened into something robotic. There are no strings, no horns and almost no guitar. It is disco remade from electronic sound, cool, clean and relentlessly steady.","Moroder, a South Tyrolean producer based in Munich, made it in 1977, the same year as I Feel Love, while synthesizers were moving from university studios and progressive rock into pop. Released on Casablanca, it found its audience mainly in European clubs. Moroder was already Donna Summer's producer and would soon win an Academy Award for his score to Alan Parker's film Midnight Express.","The sleeve carries a note announcing that only electronic keyboards were used on the recording, a boast at a time when synthesizers were widely treated as novelties or sound effects. Listen for the vocoder on Moroder's voice, and for the way the suite's seams are hidden: the album was designed to be played in clubs as a single unbroken piece.","It was a modest seller and is now valued mainly as a pioneering document, an early statement that dance music could be made without a band. Critics sometimes find it thin and a little mechanical next to I Feel Love, where Summer's voice gives the machine a heart, but its influence on synth-pop, Italo disco and electronic dance music is widely acknowledged.","Play side one straight through as one piece and notice the moments where a song changes without the beat ever stopping. Listen for the vocoder voice. Then play I Feel Love to hear the same machinery with a great singer on top, and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for German electronic music with a very different temperament."],"prince-dirty-mind":["Thirty minutes of lean, punky funk, played almost entirely by Prince himself. The sound is spare and raw: clipped guitar, bright synthesizer lines, a drum machine or simple kit, and Prince's voice leaping from falsetto to growl. Songs are short and fast, combining funk rhythm with new-wave energy. The subject matter is frankly sexual, handled with provocation and humour, and the album closes with a call for unity.","Prince was twenty-two and based in Minneapolis, with two albums behind him. He made this one largely alone in a home studio, and Warner Bros. released the demos with little change in October 1980. It came out as disco was collapsing and new wave was rising, and Prince was looking for a sound that would cross the line between Black and white radio.","Many of the tracks were originally demos, and the rawness is the result: Prince decided the rough versions had more energy than polished remakes. Listen for the synthesizer lines, which take the place of horns in a traditional funk band. This new sound, spare and synth-driven, would be called the Minneapolis sound.","It was praised by rock critics, some of whom compared its brazenness to punk, but sold only modestly. Its sexual frankness was controversial, and some radio stations would not play it. It is now seen as the album where Prince found his own voice and as the founding record of the Minneapolis sound, the template for the Time, Vanity 6 and much 1980s pop-funk.","Listen to the whole album in one go; at thirty minutes it is over quickly. Notice the synthesizers standing in for horns and the way the guitar plays tight funk rather than rock solos. Start with When You Were Mine and Uptown. Then play 1999 to hear how Prince stretched the same sound into long grooves."],"prince-1999":["A double album of long, synthesizer-driven funk grooves. The title track is a party song about celebrating in the face of nuclear fear; Little Red Corvette is a pop-rock hit; Delirious is a rockabilly-funk romp. Many tracks stretch to seven or nine minutes, built on a Linn drum machine and layered synthesizers. The sound is bright, mechanical and sensual, the grooves hypnotic.","Released in October 1982 on Warner Bros., it was Prince's commercial breakthrough, helped when MTV, then criticised for ignoring Black artists, put the video for Little Red Corvette into regular rotation. Prince recorded much of it alone in Minneapolis and Los Angeles, playing most instruments himself. The Reagan-era arms race and a renewed fear of nuclear war were in the air, and the title song turns that dread into a party.","On the cover, the words 'and the Revolution' are written backwards inside the lettering of the title, the first hint of the band that would be fully credited on Purple Rain. Listen for the Linn LM-1 drum machine, whose crisp, dry claps and snares give the album its synthetic snap, and for the several voices trading lines in the title song, perhaps a nod to Sly and the Family Stone.","It was a big hit, and critics praised its ambition even as some found the double-album length indulgent. It is now seen as one of the defining albums of the 1980s and a template for the fusion of funk, pop and electronic sound. Its title song gained a second life as the millennium approached, when it became the obvious soundtrack for New Year's Eve 1999.","Start with the title track, then Little Red Corvette, and notice how the drum machine and synthesizers carry the groove. Then give time to one of the long tracks such as Automatic or Lady Cab Driver. Afterwards go back to Dirty Mind to hear the sound in its lean first form, and Donna Summer's I Feel Love for the machine pulse it builds on."],"prince-sign-o-the-times":["An eighty-minute double album that ranges across funk, soul, rock, pop, gospel and psychedelia. The title track is a spare drum-machine piece about social problems; If I Was Your Girlfriend explores gender and intimacy through a sped-up voice; The Cross is a gospel-rock hymn; Adore is a lush soul ballad. It is Prince's most wide-ranging album, a panorama of American music.","Prince disbanded the Revolution in 1986 and was working on several projects at once, including an album called Dream Factory, a record credited to an alter ego named Camille, and a triple album called Crystal Ball. Warner Bros. refused to release a triple; the double album that came out in March 1987 drew from all of them. It arrived amid the crack epidemic and the AIDS crisis, which the title song names directly.","Several songs were meant for Camille, a character sung with the tape sped up so Prince's voice sounds higher and androgynous. Listen for that voice on If I Was Your Girlfriend, where it deepens the song's play with gender. The title track, by contrast, is almost bare: a drum machine, a synthesizer bass line and short guitar interjections, the social report delivered with no decoration at all.","Critics hailed it as Prince's finest album, and it regularly appears near the top of lists of the greatest albums of the 1980s. It sold less than Purple Rain, and the accompanying concert film did poorly in America. Debate now centres on the vaults: the abandoned projects it came from have been partly released, and fans still argue which version would have been best.","Start with the title track and notice how little is playing. Then hear If I Was Your Girlfriend, Housequake for pure funk, and The Cross, which builds from a quiet folk-like chant to a roaring gospel-rock climax. Then play Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, another sprawling double album that tries to hold all of Black American music."],"rick-james-street-songs":["An album of hard-hitting funk that its maker called punk funk. Super Freak is built on a fat, strutting bass line and stabbing synthesizers; Give It to Me Baby rides a thumping groove with horns shouting back; Ghetto Life sketches James's Buffalo childhood; Fire and Desire is a long, slow duet with Teena Marie. The sound is punchy and loud, horns and synthesizers jostling over a heavy rhythm section, and James's voice swaggers throughout.","Rick James, from Buffalo, New York, had signed to Motown in the late 1970s and was already a star. Street Songs, released in April 1981 on Motown's Gordy label with his Stone City Band, was his biggest success, topping the R&B album chart. It came out as disco's collapse left funk briefly dominant again, just before drum machines and MTV reshaped Black pop, and James would soon attack MTV publicly for ignoring Black artists.","Super Freak's bass line had a second life in 1990, when MC Hammer built U Can't Touch This on it; James sued and won a share of the credit. Listen closely to Fire and Desire, where Teena Marie, a white singer he produced and championed at Motown, matches him phrase for phrase: it is as much a contest as a love song.","It was a big hit and is widely regarded as James's best album. Critics praised its energy, though some found its boasting and swagger crude, and his later troubles have shadowed his reputation. It is now seen as a key record of early-1980s funk, a bridge between the P-Funk era and the electro and hip-hop that followed.","Start with Give It to Me Baby and listen to the horns answering the bass. Then Super Freak, and notice how simple the bass line is. Then hear Fire and Desire as a duet between equals. Afterwards play Prince's Dirty Mind to hear a leaner, stranger approach to the same punk-funk idea."],"betty-davis-they-say-im-different":["Eight tracks of raw, heavy funk with a hard-rock edge. Betty Davis's voice growls, rasps, shrieks and purrs over grinding, distorted guitars, a thick, prowling bass and drums that hit hard and dry. The songs are bold and sexually confident, told from the point of view of a woman who sets the terms, and the sound is deliberately rough rather than polished. The title track slows down to pay tribute to the blues singers she counted as ancestors.","Betty Davis, born Betty Mabry, had been married briefly to Miles Davis at the end of the 1960s and introduced him to the music of Jimi Hendrix and Sly Stone, nudging him toward the electric sound of Bitches Brew. This, her second album, was released in 1974 on the small Just Sunshine label. Her frankness about desire was too much for many radio stations of the day, and some shows were met with protests.","Davis largely produced the album herself, which was very rare for a woman in 1974, and she wrote every song. Listen to the title track's roll call of blues figures: she places her own shocking funk in a line of descent from earlier Black women and men who were also called too loud and too much. Her voice is often mixed as another rhythm instrument rather than floating above the band.","It sold poorly and was largely ignored or disapproved of at the time, and Davis had left music entirely by the end of the decade. Reissues from the 2000s onward transformed her reputation. She is now seen as a pioneer of funk-rock and of a woman's self-possession in pop, and a documentary and a stream of admiring younger musicians have given her the recognition she missed.","Start with the title track, then turn up something harder such as Shoo-B-Doop and Cop Him and notice how her voice attacks the beat. Then play Funkadelic's Maggot Brain and Sly and the Family Stone's There's a Riot Goin' On, the two records she sits most comfortably beside."],"michael-jackson-thriller":["Nine songs in forty-two minutes, and seven of them became American top-ten singles. The record moves from the churning Afro-disco of Wanna Be Startin’ Somethin’ through the horror-film camp of the title track, with Vincent Price’s spoken coda, to the hard-rock guitar of Beat It and the taut, minimal groove of Billie Jean. What binds it is Jackson’s voice used as percussion — hiccups, gasps and clipped yelps sitting exactly in the pocket of the beat — and Quincy Jones’s spotless, layered production.","Jackson was twenty-four, already a star after Off the Wall (1979) but determined to make an album with no filler. Sessions ran through 1982 at Westlake Recording Studios in Los Angeles with Jones producing and Bruce Swedien engineering; Rod Temperton wrote the title song, members of Toto played on several tracks and Eddie Van Halen dropped in a guitar solo. The release coincided with the rise of MTV, then playing almost no Black artists, and the Billie Jean video helped break that barrier.","The chant that closes Wanna Be Startin’ Somethin’ is adapted from Manu Dibango’s 1972 Soul Makossa, a Cameroonian makossa hit — a piece of African popular music hiding at the climax of the best-selling album ever made; the borrowing was later settled out of court. Listen too for how many songs fade on a vamp rather than ending: Jones built the record for dancing, and the grooves are allowed to keep turning.","It won eight Grammys in one night in 1984 and is generally cited as the best-selling album in history. Critics at the time admired its polish more than its depth, and some rock writers resented a Black pop star taking over rock radio with Beat It. Later judgment has settled on it as the record that reunited a racially segregated pop market; argument now centres on the man rather than the music.","Play Billie Jean, then Human Nature, back to back: one is all tension, the other all floating air, and together they show the album’s range. Then hear the side-one opener at high volume and follow the bass and handclaps. The original vinyl mix is warmer than several loud remasters; Swedien’s later reissues are a fair compromise."],"michael-jackson-billie-jean":["A song built from almost nothing: a dry, ticking drum pattern, a walking bass line that repeats for nearly five minutes, a few stabs of synthesizer and, later, a cushion of strings. Jackson sings in a tight, breathy murmur that swells into cries on the chorus. The story is a paternity accusation told by a narrator who insists the child is not his, and the music never resolves the unease — the groove simply keeps circling while the voice grows more agitated.","Jackson wrote it alone and made a detailed home demo before the Thriller sessions. Released as a single in January 1983, it went to number one in the United States and Britain. Its video, with pavement slabs lighting up under his feet, became one of the first by a Black artist in heavy rotation on MTV. In March 1983 he performed it at the Motown 25 television special and first showed the backward-gliding dance step later called the moonwalk.","The introduction runs for almost half a minute before a word is sung. Quincy Jones wanted it cut; Jackson refused, saying the introduction was what made him want to dance. Jones also reportedly pushed for a different title, worried listeners would think of the tennis player Billie Jean King. Notice how the bass and drum figure never change through the verses: all the drama comes from what is laid on top.","It is regularly ranked among the greatest pop recordings, and engineers still study Bruce Swedien’s drum sound, recorded with a specially built platform and covers to keep each hit separate. Some critics hear in the lyric a paranoid view of women and fame that recurs across Jackson’s work. The Motown 25 performance is often treated as the moment he stopped being a former child star and became the dominant entertainer of the decade.","Listen once only to the bass and once only to the hi-hat — each is a complete piece of music. Then notice where the strings enter and how they darken rather than sweeten the track. Watch the Motown 25 performance afterwards to see how the song was meant to be danced."],"madonna-like-a-prayer":["Madonna’s most personal album opens with its title song, where a chugging rock guitar gives way to a full gospel choir and a church organ, and closes with a track that plays a prayer backwards. Between them sit the brassy dance anthem Express Yourself, the bubbly Cherish, the brooding Oh Father about her father, Till Death Do Us Part about a failing marriage, and Promise to Try, a quiet piano song for her mother, who died when Madonna was five.","She was thirty, newly divorced from Sean Penn and rethinking her Catholic upbringing. Co-written and produced with Patrick Leonard and Stephen Bray, the album featured the Andraé Crouch choir on the title track and Prince duetting on Love Song. Pepsi had paid for the title song to launch in a television advertisement, but when the video — burning crosses, a Black saint, stigmata — appeared the next day, religious groups protested and Pepsi pulled the campaign.","The first pressings of the album were scented with patchouli oil, meant to evoke the incense of church, and came with an insert of facts about safe sex at the height of the AIDS crisis. Listen to the last seconds of the title song: the choir keeps going after the band drops away, as if the service has outlasted the pop song.","Reviewers who had dismissed Madonna as a clever marketer largely conceded that this was a serious, coherent album; Rolling Stone called it as close to art as pop music gets. The outrage over the video now looks like a case study in how she used controversy as form. Debate continues about whether the confessional songs or the dance tracks are its real centre.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the choir first answers her voice. Then play Promise to Try to hear how bare she was willing to be. Compare the gospel writing with Aretha Franklin’s church recordings to hear what she borrowed and what she changed."],"madonna-ray-of-light":["Thirteen songs that fold trance, ambient washes, trip-hop and acoustic guitar into mainstream pop. The title track is a hurtling rave song with a squealing synthesizer line; Frozen is slow and orchestral, a cold string riff over a crawling beat; Shanti/Ashtangi sets a Sanskrit yoga invocation to a club rhythm; Little Star is a lullaby for her daughter. Madonna’s voice is lower, rounder and steadier than on any earlier record.","Madonna had given birth to her daughter Lourdes in 1996, taken formal vocal lessons for the film of Evita and begun studying Kabbalah and yoga. She turned to the British producer William Orbit, whose home-made electronic records she admired, and the sessions in Los Angeles ran for months as Orbit’s ageing equipment broke down. Released in March 1998 on her own Maverick label, it arrived as electronic dance music was moving into the American mainstream.","The title song is built on Sepheryn, a 1971 folk song by the obscure British duo Curtiss Maldoon, credited on the sleeve — a hippie tune turned into a rave anthem. Drowned World/Substitute for Love samples an easy-listening record by the San Sebastian Strings, a Rod McKuen project. Listen for the gentle crackle and hiss Orbit left in: the machines are meant to sound handmade.","It won four Grammys and was received as a reinvention, the first Madonna album critics discussed chiefly as sound. Some electronic musicians grumbled that she had borrowed an underground style; many more credited her with carrying it to a mass audience. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often named her best album, though admirers of Like a Prayer dispute that.","Hear Frozen on good headphones for the way the strings and beats occupy separate spaces. Then play the title track loud. Compare with Björk’s Homogenic from the previous year, which uses strings and beats to very different ends. Notice how rarely her voice is doubled or buried; Orbit keeps it dry and close."],"kate-bush-hounds-of-love":["Two halves. Side one, Hounds of Love, holds five songs, among them Running Up That Hill, driven by a booming drum machine and a synthesizer cello figure, and Cloudbusting, powered by marching strings. Side two, The Ninth Wave, is a continuous seven-part suite about a woman adrift at night in freezing water, moving through dream, hallucination, a witch trial and a view of the Earth from space before she is rescued at dawn.","Bush had built a studio at her family home in Kent and took full control of production. The Fairlight sampler, then an expensive novelty, let her play orchestral and found sounds from a keyboard. After the commercial disappointment of The Dreaming, EMI worried; Hounds of Love went to number one in Britain, displacing Madonna’s Like a Virgin. Running Up That Hill’s original title, A Deal with God, was changed at the label’s request.","Cloudbusting is drawn from Peter Reich’s memoir of his father, the psychoanalyst Wilhelm Reich, whose rain-making machine and arrest the song describes. Hello Earth quotes Tsintskaro, a Georgian folk song Bush heard on the soundtrack of Werner Herzog’s Nosferatu, sung here by a male choir. The title song opens with a line of dialogue sampled from the 1957 horror film Night of the Demon.","It is usually treated as her best album and one of the high points of 1980s art pop, with The Ninth Wave compared to a song cycle. In 2022 Running Up That Hill, used in the television series Stranger Things, returned to the top of the British chart thirty-seven years after release — a rare case of a new generation finding a song on its own terms.","Play side two in one sitting, in the dark if possible, as a single piece. Listen for the ice and radio voices in Under Ice and Waking the Witch. Then return to side one and hear how every song uses drums as the lead instrument."],"kate-bush-the-dreaming":["Ten densely packed songs in which Bush sings in a dozen voices — growling, shrieking, cockney, childlike. Sat in Your Lap opens with thunderous tom-toms and brass; There Goes a Tenner is a comic bank robbery in music-hall style; Pull Out the Pin sees the Vietnam War through a Viet Cong soldier’s eyes; Night of the Swallow brings in Irish pipes and fiddle; Houdini imagines the escapologist’s wife; Get Out of My House ends in braying animal noises.","This was the first album Bush produced herself, made over more than a year in several London studios with heavy use of the new Fairlight sampler. She was twenty-four, famous since Wuthering Heights in 1978, and tired of being marketed as a whimsical ingénue. EMI was alarmed by its density, and it sold far less than her earlier records, pushing her to build her own home studio for the next album.","Get Out of My House was inspired by Stephen King’s The Shining, and its layered voices act out a person and a haunted house fighting each other. Listen to how often the drums are the melody: the album is built more from rhythm and texture than from chords. The title song features a didgeridoo.","British reviewers were divided and some called it the sound of an artist losing control. Its reputation has risen sharply; many musicians, Björk among them, have named it a favourite, and it is now often ranked with Hounds of Love. A few of its portrayals of other cultures sit less comfortably today, and listeners still disagree about them.","Start with Sat in Your Lap for the drums, then Houdini for the drama. Expect to be disoriented on first hearing; the second time the shapes emerge. Hear it before Hounds of Love to understand where that album’s methods came from."],"prince-purple-rain":["Nine songs made as the soundtrack to Prince’s semi-autobiographical film. Let’s Go Crazy opens with a mock sermon over church organ before exploding into guitar rock; When Doves Cry is funk stripped to drum machine, keyboards and voice; Darling Nikki is lurid and theatrical; the closing Purple Rain is a slow gospel-rock ballad nearly nine minutes long, ending in a soaring guitar solo and a wordless falsetto cry.","Prince and his band the Revolution were based in Minneapolis, far from the music industry. Warner Bros. backed a film on the strength of his album 1999. Several songs, including the title track, were recorded live at a benefit concert at the First Avenue club in August 1983 and then overdubbed; it was guitarist Wendy Melvoin’s first show with the band. The album spent twenty-four weeks at number one in the United States.","When Doves Cry has no bass line. Prince recorded one, decided the track sounded too conventional, and removed it, leaving a hole at the bottom of the mix that radio had never heard in a hit. Darling Nikki led Tipper Gore to found the Parents Music Resource Center, whose campaign produced the Parental Advisory sticker on American records.","It won an Academy Award for original song score and made Prince a superstar on the scale of Michael Jackson. Critics praised its fusion of rock, funk and gospel; rock purists and funk purists each felt it belonged to the other side. It now appears near the top of most lists of the greatest albums, and the title song became his signature and his farewell anthem.","Play When Doves Cry and listen for the missing bass. Then hear the title track, waiting for the moment the guitar solo gives way to the strings and the falsetto. Watch the film’s concert scenes: it shows how the band played the music live."],"whitney-houston-i-will-always-love-you":["Houston’s recording of Dolly Parton’s 1974 country farewell song opens with her voice completely alone for about three-quarters of a minute. Piano and strings enter quietly, a saxophone solo bridges the middle, and then a single drum hit launches the final chorus, raised a step in key and sung at full power. The song is a gentle goodbye; Houston turns it into a slow, controlled build to one enormous climax.","It was recorded for The Bodyguard (1992), the film in which Houston starred opposite Kevin Costner, who is credited with suggesting the unaccompanied opening. The producer was David Foster; Kirk Whalum played the saxophone. The soundtrack became one of the best-selling albums ever, and the single spent fourteen weeks at number one in the United States, a record at the time.","It was not the first choice: the plan had been to record What Becomes of the Brokenhearted until that song turned up in another film. Parton’s version is a quiet, tender country waltz; listen for how Houston keeps the melody almost intact but stretches individual syllables into long ornamental runs rooted in her gospel training at New Hope Baptist Church in Newark.","It became the template for the big pop ballad and for a generation of televised singing competitions, where its final key change is a rite of passage. Some critics find the performance overwhelming or showy; others hear an extraordinary piece of breath control and pacing. Parton has said the royalties from it made her very comfortable.","Hear Parton’s original first, then Houston’s: notice what stays the same and how the arrangement pushes toward one moment. On a second listen, follow only the breathing and the dynamics in the unaccompanied opening. Seek the full soundtrack version rather than the shorter radio edit, which trims the unaccompanied opening."],"whitney-houston-i-wanna-dance-with-somebody":["A bright, bouncing dance-pop song with a cowbell-and-synthesizer introduction, a skipping drum-machine beat and an exuberant chorus that Houston hits with gospel force. The arrangement is dense and glossy — layered keyboards, handclaps, chattering backing voices — and in the closing minutes she improvises over the repeated hook, pushing higher each time. Underneath, a busy synthesizer bass line keeps the whole thing skipping forward at a brisk tempo.","It was the lead single from her second album, Whitney, released on Arista in 1987 under the guidance of label head Clive Davis. Written by George Merrill and Shannon Rubicam of the duo Boy Meets Girl, who had also written How Will I Know, and produced by Narada Michael Walden, it went to number one in the United States and Britain and won a Grammy. It was the fourth of seven consecutive American number ones, still a record.","Under the party surface the verses describe loneliness at the end of a day and a longing for a partner who will stay; the celebration is a wish, not a fact. Listen to how the ad-libbed ending changes the mood from bright to almost desperate. The song later lent its title to the 2022 film of Houston’s life.","Some critics thought it too close to How Will I Know and too slick, and her label’s crossover strategy was criticised by those who felt she was being steered away from Black audiences. Its standing has grown with time: it is now a wedding and karaoke staple and is often heard as a song of bittersweet longing.","Listen for the moment in the last chorus when she leaves the melody behind. Then compare it with Robyn’s Dancing On My Own, which takes the same lonely-dancer idea and plays it in a minor key. The extended single mix gives the ad-libs more room."],"abba-the-winner-takes-it-all":["A ballad sung by Agnetha Fältskog over a rolling piano figure that repeats almost unchanged. The verses are long and conversational, the chorus builds with stacked backing voices, and the song climbs slowly without ever breaking into a dance beat. It is essentially a monologue: one partner, after a divorce, speaking to the other with dignity and sadness. It lasts nearly five minutes.","Written by Benny Andersson and Björn Ulvaeus and recorded at their Polar Music studio in Stockholm, it was the first single from the album Super Trouper in 1980, reaching number one in Britain and many other countries. Ulvaeus and Fältskog had divorced the year before; Ulvaeus has said the song is not a literal account, but it was hard for audiences, and for Fältskog singing it, to hear it otherwise.","An early demo reportedly had a different title and lyric; Ulvaeus has said he wrote the final words very quickly, late at night, and handed them to Fältskog. Listen to how the melody of the verse lies low and then jumps: she has to reach for the top notes, and the strain is part of the effect. The piano figure never stops, like a clock.","Critics who had dismissed ABBA as bubblegum found themselves taking the song seriously; it is now often voted the group’s best. The revival of ABBA’s reputation from the 1990s onward, including the Mamma Mia! stage musical, placed it at the emotional centre of their catalogue. Some find the lyric’s even-handedness more devastating than any accusation.","Listen for how the backing voices enter one by one. Then hear it after Adele’s Someone Like You or Robyn’s Dancing On My Own, both of which descend from this kind of dignified heartbreak. Then play the full Super Trouper album to hear the song in its setting."],"abba-the-visitors":["Nine songs, cooler and darker than any earlier ABBA album. The title track is a tense synthesizer piece about a Soviet dissident waiting for a knock at the door; Soldiers is about the arms race; Slipping Through My Fingers is a mother watching her daughter grow up; When All Is Said and Done, sung by Anni-Frid Lyngstad, faces the end of a marriage; One of Us is a lonely, lilting single. Synthesizers dominate and the old bright harmonies are used sparingly.","By 1981 both couples in ABBA had divorced, and the group was barely functioning socially. The album was recorded at Polar Music in Stockholm, which had installed a digital recorder, and it is often cited as one of the first albums pressed on compact disc. It was their last album of new material until Voyage in 2021; the group stopped recording the following year.","When All Is Said and Done was written by Ulvaeus about Andersson and Lyngstad’s divorce and then sung by Lyngstad herself. Listen in the title track for how the melody stays trapped in a narrow range, the music as claustrophobic as the room it describes. Slipping Through My Fingers was prompted by Ulvaeus and Fältskog’s daughter heading to school.","It was their least commercially successful album at the time and was often called cold. Later critics have revalued it as their most mature and adventurous work, a bridge between their pop craft and the stage musicals Andersson and Ulvaeus went on to write. Some fans still prefer the sunnier albums.","Start with the title track and listen for the synthesizer’s ticking pulse, then play Slipping Through My Fingers for tenderness. Hear it next to Fleetwood Mac’s Tango in the Night, another band turning broken relationships into polished records. A good remaster will bring out the detail of the early digital recording."],"fleetwood-mac-tango-in-the-night":["Twelve glossy, shimmering songs shared among the band’s three writers. Lindsey Buckingham’s Big Love is a jittery guitar song with gasping vocal answers; Christine McVie’s Everywhere and Little Lies are bright, chiming love songs; Stevie Nicks’s Seven Wonders has a sweeping, airy chorus. Synthesizers, sampled marimbas and layered voices give the record a sheen very different from the band’s 1970s sound.","Fleetwood Mac had not released a studio album in five years. The project grew out of a Buckingham solo album and was recorded largely at his home studio in Los Angeles, with Buckingham and Richard Dashut producing. Band members were struggling with addiction and exhaustion, and Nicks was present only for short stretches. It became the group’s second-best-selling album, and Buckingham left the band shortly after its release.","The breathy gasps in Big Love that sound like a woman answering Buckingham are his own voice, sped up and processed, not Nicks’s. Listen too for how much of the percussion is sampled and played on keyboards rather than hit by Mick Fleetwood: the album is a studio construction more than a band performance.","Reviewers were polite rather than excited; its polish was often compared unfavourably with Rumours. Its reputation has since risen, especially among younger musicians who admire its production, and Everywhere has become one of the band’s most-streamed songs. The argument is whether it is a great band record or a great Buckingham record with guest vocalists.","Start with Everywhere for the chiming texture, then Big Love for Buckingham’s guitar. Listen on headphones to catch how many small sounds are tucked into each track. Compare with Kate Bush’s Hounds of Love, also made in a home studio with sampled sounds."],"bjork-debut":["Eleven songs that set Björk’s voice — whispering, yelping, then suddenly enormous — against club beats, strings, a harp and a saxophone ensemble. Human Behaviour watches people as an animal might; Venus as a Boy has lush, swooning strings with an Indian film-music feel; Big Time Sensuality is pure house music; Like Someone in Love is a jazz standard sung with harp alone. The mood is curious, playful and wide-eyed.","Björk had been the singer of the Icelandic band the Sugarcubes and moved to London in 1993 to make a solo record. She chose Nellee Hooper, producer for Massive Attack and Soul II Soul, and plunged into the city’s club culture. The album was released on the independent One Little Indian label and was a surprise commercial success across Europe and in the United States.","There’s More to Life Than This was recorded in the toilets of a London club during a night out: you can hear the party muffled behind a closed door, and the door opening and shutting. The Anchor Song closes the album with just voice and a brass choir of saxophones, a hint of the orchestral ambitions to come.","It was widely praised and won her two Brit Awards, but some critics treated her as an eccentric novelty and wrote about her accent and appearance rather than the music. The album now reads as a key document of the early-1990s moment when club culture, trip-hop and pop overlapped in London.","Start with Human Behaviour for the timpani and the voice, then Like Someone in Love to hear how she treats a standard. Listen to the club recording on headphones, following the door. Finish with The Anchor Song and its saxophones, which point toward the orchestral writing of her later albums."],"bjork-homogenic":["Ten songs built on a strict pairing: an eight-piece string ensemble and harsh, crackling electronic beats. Jóga opens with lush strings over beats that sound like rock being split; Bachelorette is a sweeping, cinematic ballad; Hunter is a cold march; Pluto is a burst of distorted noise; All Is Full of Love ends the album in stillness. Björk said she wanted a sound for Iceland itself: the strings as its landscape, the beats as its volcanic ground.","After a stalker mailed a bomb to her London home in 1996, Björk left the city and recorded in a studio in southern Spain. She worked with Mark Bell of the electronic group LFO, alongside Guy Sigsworth, Howie B and Markus Dravs, and the Brazilian arranger Eumir Deodato arranged the strings for the Icelandic String Octet. It was the first of her albums made as a single, unified concept.","Many of the beats were made by distorting and chopping acoustic sounds until they crack and hiss like ice or lava. Listen in Jóga for the moment the strings seem to hold still while the beat tears underneath them. The album’s title is ironic: its unity comes from putting two opposites side by side.","Critics received it as a bold turn away from the eclectic pop of her first albums, though its austerity puzzled some. It has since become many listeners’ favourite Björk record, and its strings-and-beats method was widely copied. The video for All Is Full of Love, with two robots embracing, is shown in art museums.","Listen on good speakers or headphones so the low beats and the strings are both clear. Start with Jóga, then Bachelorette. Compare with Sibelius’s Fifth Symphony for another Nordic attempt to turn landscape into sound. Then watch the All Is Full of Love video with the sound up."],"bjork-vespertine":["A hushed, wintry album of twelve songs about intimacy. Beats are tiny clicks and rustles — shuffled cards, footsteps in snow, crackling paper — surrounded by harp, celesta, custom-built music boxes, choir and strings. Hidden Place opens with a whisper; Cocoon is barely audible; Pagan Poetry builds to an overwhelming, exposed climax; Sun in My Mouth sets a poem by E. E. Cummings; Unison closes with an ensemble swell.","Björk wanted music for the private sphere, made for headphones and home computers at a moment when listening was moving from shops to laptops. She worked with the San Francisco electronic duo Matmos and the harpist Zeena Parkins, with strings arranged by Vince Mendoza. It followed her harrowing role in Lars von Trier’s Dancer in the Dark (2000) and was toured in opera houses and concert halls with a harp, choir and orchestra.","Listen for the rhythms made from household sounds: Aurora’s beat is built from someone walking in snow. Harm of Will sets words by the filmmaker Harmony Korine. The music boxes were specially made, with punched cards that had to be designed note by note. Cocoon is mixed so close that her breathing is part of the rhythm.","Critics loved its intimacy and detail, and it is often named her finest album. Some found it too quiet or too private after the big drama of Homogenic. It is now regarded as a key record in the crossover between glitch electronica and song, and between pop and the concert hall.","Listen alone, on headphones, at night. Follow one tiny sound through a whole song. Then hear Arvo Pärt’s Spiegel im Spiegel for another kind of hush, or Meredith Monk’s Dolmen Music for voices used as landscape. The live album from the tour shows how the songs grew in concert halls."],"amy-winehouse-back-to-black":["Eleven short songs in thirty-five minutes, in the sound of 1960s girl groups and soul — booming drums, baritone saxophone, tambourine, echoing piano — with a voice that sounds decades older than its twenty-three years. Rehab is a jaunty refusal; You Know I’m No Good is a sly confession; the title track is a funeral march for a relationship; Love Is a Losing Game is a spare, sad ballad. The lyrics are blunt, witty and specific.","Winehouse wrote the songs after a breakup, and recorded with two producers: Mark Ronson, who brought in the Brooklyn soul band the Dap-Kings, and Salaam Remi, who handled the jazzier tracks. Released on Island in October 2006, it made her famous worldwide and won five Grammys in 2008. Her struggles with alcohol and drugs, and relentless tabloid coverage, followed; she died in 2011 at twenty-seven.","Tears Dry on Their Own is built on the chords and rhythm of Ain’t No Mountain High Enough, the Marvin Gaye and Tammi Terrell hit, and credits its writers — the happiest Motown duet turned into self-reliance after heartbreak. Listen for the Dap-Kings’ deliberately old-fashioned recording: few microphones, lots of room sound, like a 1960s session.","It was hailed at once, and it opened the door to a wave of British soul singers including Adele and Duffy. Critics debate whether its retro sound was homage or pastiche, and whether its frankness about addiction was later misread as glamour. After her death it came to be heard partly as a document of her struggles, which admirers argue distorts its humour and craft.","Start with the title song and listen for the tolling piano and the reverb on the drums. Then play Love Is a Losing Game, the quietest track. Follow it with the Supremes and Dusty Springfield to hear her sources. The deluxe edition adds demos that show how the songs began."],"amy-winehouse-frank":["A debut of jazz-inflected songs with hip-hop beats underneath: Stronger Than Me scolds a passive boyfriend; In My Bed and Take the Box deal with a breakup; there is a version of the standard There Is No Greater Love and the vocalese showpiece Moody’s Mood for Love. Winehouse’s voice is supple and conversational, swinging behind the beat like a jazz singer, and the lyrics are sharp, funny and unguarded.","Winehouse was nineteen, a product of London’s stage schools and jazz clubs, and a fan of Sarah Vaughan and Dinah Washington. Island released the album in October 2003 after she worked mainly with the producer Salaam Remi. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and Stronger Than Me won an Ivor Novello award for songwriting. Her band played live in the studio on several tracks, and she co-wrote every original song on the record.","Moody’s Mood for Love is itself a remarkable object: King Pleasure’s 1950s vocalese setting of words to James Moody’s saxophone solo on I’m in the Mood for Love. Winehouse sings the solo line by line — a nineteen-year-old channelling a bebop saxophonist. She later said she was unhappy with the album’s final mixes and had not approved all of them.","It was well reviewed but sold modestly until Back to Black made her famous. Critics now hear it as the more jazz-rooted, less styled record, and some prefer it for that reason. Its hip-hop production is sometimes called dated beside the more carefully period-styled sound of its successor. Admirers point to its wit and to the ease of her phrasing.","Start with Stronger Than Me for the voice and the attitude. Then find Moody’s Mood for Love and try to hear the saxophone solo underneath the words. Then play Ella Fitzgerald for comparison. Notice how often a sly joke lands in the middle of a sad line."],"beyonce-crazy-in-love":["The song is built on a blazing horn fanfare sampled from a 1970 soul record, played over a crisp, slamming drum beat. Jay-Z raps the opening verse; Beyoncé enters with a stuttering, syllable-chopping hook and a dizzy, breathless verse about losing control in love. The track barely changes harmony: its energy comes from the horn stabs and the rhythm of her delivery.","It was the lead single from Dangerously in Love, Beyoncé’s first solo album, released while she was still a member of Destiny’s Child. The producer Rich Harrison had built the horn-sample beat months earlier; Beyoncé wrote the vocal hooks with him in the studio. It spent eight weeks at number one in the United States and won two Grammys, launching her solo career.","The horn loop comes from Are You My Woman (Tell Me So), a 1970 single by the Chicago soul group the Chi-Lites, credited on the record. Listen for how the sample is chopped and repeated so that a brief fanfare becomes a relentless riff. Beyoncé has said the hook was written in a couple of hours, the stuttering phrase coming from a comment about how she looked.","It was received as an instant classic, and many critics rank it among the best singles of the 2000s. Some wrote it off as a well-produced club hit; its reputation as the song that made her a solo artist has only grown. It is a model of the early-2000s blend of soul sampling and pop songcraft.","Listen for the moment the horns first drop out and the voice carries the rhythm alone. Then find the Chi-Lites original and hear how different it sounds at its own tempo. Then listen to the whole Dangerously in Love album, which sets the single among slower, smoother ballads, and compare with the Destiny’s Child records that came before."],"beyonce-lemonade":["Twelve songs that move through the stages of discovering a partner’s infidelity — suspicion, anger, apathy, forgiveness — and outward to Black women’s history and inheritance. The sound changes with each stage: breezy reggae-tinged pop (Hold Up), ferocious garage rock with Jack White (Don’t Hurt Yourself), brass-band country (Daddy Lessons), piano balladry (Sandcastles), a gospel-rock protest with Kendrick Lamar (Freedom) and the defiant bounce music of Formation.","Released in April 2016 alongside an hour-long film on HBO, it was made with a large team of collaborators including Diplo, Jack White, James Blake, Ezra Koenig and the poet Warsan Shire, whose verse links the film’s chapters. It came amid the Black Lives Matter movement; the film features the mothers of Trayvon Martin, Michael Brown and Eric Garner holding photographs of their sons.","Don’t Hurt Yourself samples the drums of Led Zeppelin’s When the Levee Breaks — itself a reworking of a 1929 Memphis Minnie blues — so a Black woman reclaims a rhythm first taken from Black music. Hold Up interpolates the Yeah Yeah Yeahs’ Maps. Listen for how the album moves from private betrayal to collective grief without ever announcing the shift.","It was greeted as her most ambitious work, winning a Peabody Award for the film. When Adele’s 25 beat it to the Grammy for Album of the Year, Adele said on stage that Lemonade should have won, and the award’s treatment of Black artists became a major controversy. Debate continues over how autobiographical it is.","Watch the film first, then listen to the album straight through. Notice how each genre carries a mood. Then play Marvin Gaye’s What’s Going On, another soul album that moves from private life to public crisis. Listen closely for the samples and interpolations, which carry the story of Black music across the whole album."],"beyonce-renaissance":["Sixteen tracks sequenced as a continuous DJ set, with no gaps between songs. It moves through house, disco, ballroom, Afrobeats and bounce: Break My Soul rides a classic 1990s house piano, Cuff It is sunny disco-funk, Alien Superstar is a strutting catwalk track, and Summer Renaissance closes by interpolating the pulsing synthesizer of Donna Summer’s I Feel Love. The album is about pleasure, release and the dance floor as a place of freedom.","Released in July 2022 after pandemic lockdowns, it was presented as the first act of a three-part project. Beyoncé dedicated it to her late Uncle Jonny, a gay man who introduced her to house and disco, and to the Black and queer pioneers who created those scenes in Chicago, Detroit and New York. It won the Grammy for Best Dance/Electronic Album.","Break My Soul samples Robin S.’s 1993 house hit Show Me Love and Big Freedia’s New Orleans bounce track Explode; the album credits dozens of writers and samples, a history lesson hidden in the liner notes. Listen for the joins between tracks: each transition is mixed like a club set, not a playlist.","It was widely praised as a celebration of Black queer dance culture, though some house-music veterans questioned whether a superstar record could honour an underground scene without flattening it. It lost Album of the Year to Harry Styles, renewing arguments about the Grammys. Its continuous form has been praised as a statement about how dance music is actually experienced.","Play it without shuffle, from start to finish, loud. Listen for the voices of ballroom emcees and bounce artists. Then play Robyn’s Dancing On My Own and Madonna’s Ray of Light for two other pop albums that owe everything to the club."],"taylor-swift-1989":["Thirteen synthesizer-driven pop songs, with gated drums, sparkling keyboards and layered vocals recalling the 1980s pop of the year Swift was born. Blank Space parodies her own media image as a serial romancer; Style is a slinky, guitar-funk song; Out of the Woods is a pounding, anxious chant; Shake It Off is a brassy dismissal of her critics; Clean closes quietly with a sober, healing ballad.","Swift had begun as a teenage country singer in Nashville; with 1989 she announced a complete move to pop. She worked with the Swedish hit-makers Max Martin and Shellback, with Jack Antonoff and Ryan Tedder, and with Imogen Heap on Clean. She had moved to New York, and the album opens with Welcome to New York. In 2014 she also pulled her catalogue from Spotify in a dispute over payments to musicians.","Swift has described Clean as written about sobriety from a relationship, using the language of addiction recovery. The re-recorded 1989 (Taylor’s Version), released in 2023, is part of her campaign to own her own recordings after her early masters were sold; listening to both shows how carefully the arrangements were rebuilt.","It won the Grammy for Album of the Year and was widely admired for its craft. Some critics missed the narrative detail of her country songs and felt the Max Martin production sanded her down. Ryan Adams covered the whole album in a folk-rock style in 2015, an early sign that serious critics were beginning to treat her songwriting as the point.","Start with Out of the Woods for the drums, then Style for the groove, then Clean. Compare with Madonna’s 1980s records or Fleetwood Mac’s Tango in the Night to hear what it borrows. Hear the 2023 re-recording next to the original and listen for the small differences in her voice."],"taylor-swift-folklore":["Sixteen quiet songs, mostly piano- or guitar-based, with soft strings, muted drums and hazy synthesizers. Many are told through characters rather than Swift herself: the last great american dynasty tells the story of the socialite Rebekah Harkness, whose Rhode Island house Swift bought; cardigan, august and betty form a three-song teenage love triangle heard from each side; exile is a duet with Bon Iver; epiphany imagines her grandfather in the Second World War.","Swift wrote and recorded the album during the Covid-19 lockdown of 2020, working remotely with Aaron Dessner of the indie band the National and Jack Antonoff. It was announced only hours before release in July 2020. Some songs were co-written by William Bowery, later revealed as a pseudonym of her then-partner Joe Alwyn. It won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","The triangle songs mirror each other: details from one reappear from a different character’s view in another, so a small phrase or image changes meaning as you move between them. Listen for how Dessner’s piano patterns, borrowed from his indie-rock work, drive the songs more than any beat. Several songs share images and phrases that return like motifs.","It was received as a bold artistic turn, and many critics who had resisted Swift praised its restraint and storytelling. Some indie-rock listeners felt it borrowed the National’s sound without its edge. It is often named her best album and was followed within months by a companion, evermore. Its sales showed that a quiet record could still dominate charts.","Listen to cardigan, august and betty in sequence as a story. Then play exile for the contrast of two voices. Follow with Nick Drake’s Pink Moon for another spare, intimate record made in a small room. Choose headphones: the mixes are soft and detailed."],"lady-gaga-bad-romance":["A pounding electropop song with a dark, minor-key synthesizer riff, a nonsense-syllable chant for a hook and a chorus that rises into a full-throated rock vocal. A spoken French bridge and a sudden gear change into the final chorus give it the drama of a stage number. The subject is a lover who is also bad for the singer: desire bound up with danger and obsession.","It was the lead single from The Fame Monster, a 2009 expansion of Gaga’s debut, co-written and produced with RedOne. Its video, directed by Francis Lawrence and set in a white bathhouse, became the most-viewed on YouTube at the time and helped establish the video platform as the new MTV. Gaga, a New York performance artist and songwriter, was building a theatrical persona in the tradition of Bowie and Madonna.","The lyric name-checks three Alfred Hitchcock films — Psycho, Vertigo and Rear Window — and Gaga has linked the song’s obsession to their themes of voyeurism and fatal desire; listen for how the synthesizer riff has the stabbing insistence of a Bernard Herrmann score. In the video she wears pieces from Alexander McQueen’s final runway collection, including his towering armadillo shoes.","It was a global hit and won two Grammys; critics hailed it as the best pop single of 2009. Some found her image more interesting than her music, while others argued that the persona was the art. It is now regarded as a defining song of late-2000s pop, and its video as a landmark of the YouTube age.","Listen for the change in key before the last chorus. Then watch the video with the sound up. Play Bernard Herrmann’s Psycho score afterwards to hear the family resemblance. Then hear the rest of The Fame Monster, a short, eight-song collection where each track dramatises a different fear, and compare it with Madonna’s Like a Prayer."],"robyn-dancing-on-my-own":["A song with a pulsing, insistent synthesizer line that runs from start to finish over a punchy dance beat. Robyn sings about standing alone in a club, watching the person she loves with someone else. The chorus builds with layered synthesizers and her voice pushing to the top of its range, and the song never becomes happy: the dancing is a way of surviving.","Robyn had been a Swedish teen pop star in the 1990s before leaving her major label and founding her own label, Konichiwa Records, in 2005. Dancing On My Own was the lead single from Body Talk Pt. 1, part of a series of three EPs released in 2010, co-written and produced with Patrik Berger. It was a hit in Europe and became a cult favourite in the United States.","Listen for how little the arrangement changes between verse and chorus: the lift comes almost entirely from her voice climbing and from extra synthesizer layers stacking up, so the song feels like the same night getting louder rather than going anywhere new. The song has been covered as a slow ballad by the British singer Calum Scott, revealing the grief beneath the dance track.","It was widely hailed as one of the best pop songs of its decade, topping at least one major magazine’s end-of-decade list. Critics praised its combination of dance-floor energy and heartbreak, sometimes called crying on the dance floor. It has influenced many younger pop artists, from Lorde to Charli XCX.","Play it loud and let the synthesizer pulse carry you. Then listen to Calum Scott’s ballad version to hear the lyric naked. Follow with ABBA’s The Winner Takes It All to hear the Swedish pop heartbreak tradition behind it. Listen to the whole of Body Talk afterwards."],"adele-21":["Eleven songs about the end of a relationship, sung in a powerful, raw voice. Rolling in the Deep opens with a stomping beat and a gospel-style chorus; Rumour Has It is a bluesy, handclapping put-down; Set Fire to the Rain is a big string-drenched ballad; Someone Like You closes the album with just piano and voice, a moment of resigned grief. The sound mixes soul, gospel, country and pop balladry.","Adele wrote the album at twenty-one, after a breakup, and worked with producers including Paul Epworth, Rick Rubin and Ryan Tedder; Dan Wilson co-wrote Someone Like You. She had heard a lot of American country and rockabilly — she has named Wanda Jackson — while touring. Released by XL Recordings in 2011, it became the best-selling album of the 21st century and won six Grammys.","Someone Like You was written with Dan Wilson in a day or two, with the piano figure played by Wilson. Listen for the tiny breaks in her voice on the album recording. Later in 2011 she had surgery on her vocal cords, and the voice on 21 is a snapshot of an instrument under strain.","It was universally acclaimed and sold beyond all expectations at a time when album sales were collapsing. Some critics called it old-fashioned, but most praised its directness. Her performance of Someone Like You at the 2011 Brit Awards, with only piano, is remembered as one of the most moving moments in British television pop.","Listen to Rolling in the Deep and Someone Like You back to back: one is all force, the other all restraint. Then hear Dusty Springfield and Etta James for her lineage. Then play the whole album in order, noticing how the mood shifts from anger to acceptance over its eleven songs."],"lorde-royals":["A startlingly spare track: deep bass, finger snaps, a slow kick drum and many overdubbed layers of Lorde’s own voice, singing in low, cool tones. The song is a teenager’s shrug at the luxury and excess paraded in mainstream pop and hip-hop, set against the ordinary life of her friends in a New Zealand suburb. It has no guitars or keyboards to speak of; voice and rhythm do all the work.","Lorde (Ella Yelich-O’Connor) was sixteen when she co-wrote the song with the Auckland producer Joel Little. It appeared first on an EP released free online and then on her debut album, Pure Heroine. It reached number one in the United States — the first solo New Zealand artist to do so — and won the Grammy for Song of the Year.","The title came from a National Geographic photograph of the baseball player George Brett, wearing a Kansas City Royals shirt, signing baseballs. Listen for how many vocal layers fill the track: she is essentially a choir of one, doing the work that synthesizers usually do. Joel Little built the track in his Auckland studio around her voice, leaving deliberate space in the mix.","It was hailed as a fresh voice against pop excess. Some critics argued that its list of luxury items singled out hip-hop imagery and carried an uncomfortable racial edge; others said it was simply a teenager noticing what was on the radio. The debate is still cited in discussions of pop and cultural critique.","Listen on headphones and count the layers of her voice. Then hear Grandmaster Flash’s The Message to hear what the song is reacting to in hip-hop’s own tradition of social realism. Then play the whole of Pure Heroine, which extends the same sound across an album."],"lorde-melodrama":["Eleven songs imagined as a single house party after a breakup, from the first rush to the lonely walk home. Green Light starts as a quiet piano ballad and bursts into a pounding house-piano anthem; Sober and Homemade Dynamite capture the giddiness of the night; Liability is an exposed piano confession; Writer in the Dark is almost a theatrical aria; Perfect Places closes with a wistful question about where happiness lives.","Lorde was twenty, living partly in New York after the success of Pure Heroine. She worked closely with the producer Jack Antonoff over two years. Released in June 2017, it topped the American album chart and was nominated for the Grammy for Album of the Year; many critics named it the best album of the year. She has described the album as a study of solitude and of the extremes of feeling that come after heartbreak.","Lorde has synaesthesia and has said she hears songs as colours; she chose sounds to fit those colours. Listen in Green Light for how the piano changes from a sad ballad instrument into a dance instrument in one beat. Hard Feelings/Loveless is two songs joined at a sudden shift in mood, like a party turning.","It was received with broad acclaim and is now regarded as one of the defining pop albums of the late 2010s, praised for its emotional honesty and ambitious structure. Some found the production too close to Antonoff’s work with other artists. Its reputation as a cult coming-of-age record has only grown.","Play it straight through, ideally late at night. Start with Green Light, then Liability. Then hear Kate Bush’s Hounds of Love for another album that shapes a night into a story. Listen for the returning idea of a party in the lyrics and the sounds."],"charli-xcx-pop-2":["Ten tracks of exaggerated, glittering, distorted pop, packed with guest singers and rappers. Voices are pitch-corrected until they sound like machines; synthesizers glitch and shriek; songs lurch between bubblegum melody and industrial noise. Backseat with Carly Rae Jepsen is a dreamy car-ride song; Femmebot is a robotic dance track; I Got It is a chaotic posse cut; Track 10 dissolves into distorted, melting vocals. The whole feels like pop music on fast-forward.","Charli XCX had been a mainstream hitmaker but grew frustrated with her label’s pressure. She released Pop 2 as a mixtape in December 2017 with A. G. Cook of the London label PC Music as executive producer, and producer SOPHIE among the collaborators. It became a founding text of what critics would call hyperpop, and a touchstone for young queer fans online.","Listen to Track 10, which begins as one song and warps into another as though a file were corrupting. Its guest list is a snapshot of the queer and underground pop world of 2017, including Kim Petras, Caroline Polachek, Mykki Blanco and Pabllo Vittar. Many of the voices are processed so heavily you cannot tell who is singing.","Initially a cult record, it was praised by critics for its daring and is now regarded as a blueprint for the hyperpop that followed. Some listeners found it abrasive and unfinished; admirers argue that is the point, a rebuke to over-polished pop. It is often called her artistic breakthrough. It also made a star of its producers.","Start with Backseat for its soft beauty, then Track 10 for its chaos. Listen on headphones to catch the details in the processing. Then hear Kraftwerk’s Computer World to trace the machine voice back to its roots. Hear Brat next to see where the ideas went."],"charli-xcx-brat":["Fifteen tracks of hard, bass-heavy club pop, bratty and confessional by turns. 360 and Club Classics are swaggering party anthems; Sympathy Is a Knife and I Might Say Something Stupid admit to jealousy and insecurity; So I is a tribute to the late producer SOPHIE; Girl, So Confusing is about a fraught friendship with another singer; I Think About It All the Time weighs whether to have children. The beats are loud and blunt, the lyrics unusually frank.","Released by Atlantic in June 2024 and made with A. G. Cook, Finn Keane, George Daniel and others, it drew on the London and New York club scenes. Its lime-green cover in plain type spawned a summer-long meme; when Charli wrote that Kamala Harris was brat, the Harris campaign adopted the colour and style. It won Grammys for Best Dance/Electronic Album and Best Dance Pop Recording.","The remix album that followed included a new version of Girl, So Confusing with Lorde, in which the two singers address the tension openly — a rare case of a pop feud resolved on record. Listen for how many tracks cut off abruptly, as if someone pulled the plug on the party.","It was one of the most acclaimed and culturally ubiquitous albums of 2024, praised for combining club energy with emotional honesty. Some argued the meme overshadowed the music; others that the meme was part of the art. It is regarded as the moment hyperpop’s ideas reached the mainstream. Critics also noted its unusually candid songs about fame, rivalry and envy among women in pop.","Play 360 loud, then So I quietly. Then hear the Lorde remix of Girl, So Confusing. Compare with Robyn’s Body Talk for another album of sadness on the dance floor. Listen for how the confessional songs sit right next to the party tracks, and play it loud."],"sade-diamond-life":["Nine songs played slowly and quietly by a four-piece band that never raises its voice. Sade Adu sings low and almost without vibrato, as if confiding rather than performing; Stuart Matthewman's saxophone answers her in short, smoky phrases; Paul Denman's bass carries most of the melody underneath. Smooth Operator, Your Love Is King and Hang On to Your Love are the songs people know, and the sound they share, all space and restraint, is what the record is remembered by.","Made in London in 1983 and 1984 with the producer Robin Millar, by a band that had split off from a funk group called Pride. Adu, born in Nigeria and raised in Essex, had studied fashion design and came to singing late. British pop in 1984 was loud, synthesised and theatrical; a record built on jazz chords, real instruments and understatement sounded like a deliberate step sideways, and it sold across Europe and then America.","The closing track is a cover of Timmy Thomas's Why Can't We Live Together, a 1972 soul hit built on a cheap organ and drum machine; the band keep its hypnotic stillness and its plea for tolerance. Listen also to how rarely the drums play a full kit pattern. Much of the album's intimacy comes from what the drummer leaves out, so the bass line becomes the real rhythm section.","It won the BRIT Award for best British album, and the band took the Grammy for best new artist. Some critics at the time filed it as tasteful wine-bar music for the new professional class. Later writers, and a generation of R&B singers from Maxwell to Frank Ocean, heard something harder to copy: a whole emotional world made out of holding back.","Play it at low volume first, as it was meant, and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then compare Smooth Operator with Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On to hear how differently the two treat seduction: his all heat, hers cool and watchful. Any standard CD or vinyl pressing is fine."],"sade-love-deluxe":["The band's fourth album is darker and more spacious than their debut. No Ordinary Love opens it with a slow, heavy bass figure and a guitar line that seems to hang in the air; Kiss of Life and Cherish the Day follow in the same unhurried pulse. The arrangements have thinned out to a few elements with a great deal of silence between them, and Adu's voice sits almost dry and close to the microphone.","Released in 1992, when American R&B was dominated by the hard, busy drum machines of new jack swing. The band had spent long periods apart and worked slowly, a habit that would stretch the gaps between their albums to eight and ten years. No Ordinary Love found a second life the following year when it was used in the film Indecent Proposal.","The album ends with Pearls, a stark song about a woman in Somalia scraping for grains of rice, set over little more than strings and voice; it sits in the middle of an album of love songs as a deliberate shift of gaze. On Feel No Pain, listen for the loose, almost hip-hop drum loop, a sign the band were hearing the music around them after all.","It sold strongly and was well reviewed, though some critics complained the band had simply refined the same mood. In hindsight it reads as the moment the Sade sound became a template, the source of the slow, bass-led, atmospheric ballad that neo-soul and later alternative R&B singers treated almost as scripture.","Start with No Ordinary Love on good speakers or headphones and listen to how much low end there is under that quiet voice. Then play Cherish the Day, and after it Frank Ocean's Blonde, to hear a direct descendant. Late at night is the right hour."],"janet-jackson-control":["Nine tracks of hard, bright, machine-driven funk. Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis build each song from punchy programmed drums, stabbing synthesiser chords and bass lines that snap rather than roll, and Janet Jackson rides on top with a light voice made tough by attitude and spoken asides. What Have You Done for Me Lately, Nasty, When I Think of You and The Pleasure Principle are the hits; the title track opens the record as a statement of independence.","Jackson was nineteen, had just ended a brief marriage and had parted from her father as manager. A&M sent her to Minneapolis to work with Jam and Lewis, former members of Prince's circle who had been fired from the Time. Away from Los Angeles and her famous family, she co-wrote much of the album. It topped the Billboard album chart and turned her from 'Michael's sister' into a star on her own terms.","Listen to the spoken introductions and interjections: the record keeps breaking the fourth wall, with Jackson addressing the listener directly and the producers leaving in studio chatter. The last track, Let's Wait Awhile, is a soft ballad arguing for patience, which sits pointedly against the swagger of Nasty. The album's sequencing is doing the same work as its title.","Critics praised it at once, and it has held up as one of the key records of the decade. Its production approach, drum machines played like an aggressive live band, fed straight into new jack swing and much of the R&B and pop of the next fifteen years. Debate now is mostly about how much credit belongs to Jackson and how much to Jam and Lewis; the answer is plainly both.","Put on Nasty and listen to how the drums seem to hit harder than any real kit could, then to the gaps the arrangement leaves for her voice. Follow it with Rhythm Nation 1814 three years later to hear the same team turn outward from the personal to the social."],"janet-jackson-rhythm-nation-1814":["A concept album in two halves. The first is industrial dance music about the state of the world: Rhythm Nation, State of the World and The Knowledge are clanging, metallic tracks about poverty, drugs, illiteracy and racism, stitched together by short interludes. The second half turns to love and pleasure with Miss You Much, Escapade and the slow Come Back to Me. The sound throughout is dense, percussive and militarily precise.","Her label wanted a sequel to Control; Jackson and Jam and Lewis, working again in Minneapolis, made a record about social problems they were seeing on the news. It came with a long black-and-white film whose drilled, uniformed choreography became as famous as the music. The album produced seven top-five American singles across 1989, 1990 and 1991, a record at the time.","The title track is built partly on a sample from Sly and the Family Stone's Thank You (Falettinme Be Mice Elf Agin), tying its call for unity to a 1969 funk anthem of the same spirit. The number 1814 is the year the words of the American national anthem were written. Black Cat, a hard-rock track in the middle of the album, is one Jackson wrote alone.","Some reviewers found the social message vague next to its sheer commercial power; others were surprised a pop star of her rank would try it at all. Its reputation has grown steadily. It is now heard as one of the most ambitious big-budget pop albums of its decade, and its sound and staging run through the work of Beyoncé and many who followed.","Start with the title track and pay attention to the clanks, sirens and sampled noises in the mix; this is dance music built from scrap metal. Then watch the film, because the choreography is part of the work. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for an earlier, gentler soul record about the same state of the nation."],"janet-jackson-the-velvet-rope":["A long, dark, intimate album about depression, self-worth, sexuality and loss, laid out like a night inside a private club. Short interludes connect songs that range from the trip-hop groove of Got 'til It's Gone to the house music of Together Again and the slow, whispered title track. Jackson's voice is often recorded close and soft, and the production by Jam, Lewis and René Elizondo is thick with atmosphere.","Jackson had just signed one of the largest recording contracts in history with Virgin, and was, by her own later account, in the grip of a serious depression. The album addresses it openly, alongside domestic abuse, homophobia and AIDS. In the late 1990s few mainstream pop stars wrote so directly about mental illness or about gay lives.","Got 'til It's Gone is built on a sample of Joni Mitchell's Big Yellow Taxi, with Mitchell's own voice repeating a fragment through the track, and a guest verse from Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest. Together Again, which sounds like the happiest record on the album, is in fact an elegy for friends lost to AIDS; the joy is a memorial.","Reviews were largely strong, but it sold less than its predecessors and some listeners were put off by its frankness. Its standing has climbed since, and it is now often called her best album and a forerunner of the confessional, genre-crossing R&B of the 2010s. Its treatment of sexuality has made it a touchstone for queer listeners.","Play it straight through, in the dark, as a single sequence; the interludes matter. Listen to Got 'til It's Gone next to Joni Mitchell's original to hear what a sample can do to a song's meaning. Then set Together Again beside it and notice how the tempo hides the grief."],"tlc-crazysexycool":["The trio's second album moves from the bright, cartoonish hip-hop pop of their debut to something slower, smokier and more grown-up. T-Boz's low, husky voice leads Creep and Red Light Special; Chilli's lighter tone takes other songs; Left Eye's rapid rap verses cut across them. Waterfalls, with its live horns and warning stories, is the centre. Interludes, including guest spots from Atlanta rappers, give it the feel of a mixtape.","Made in Atlanta for LaFace, the label run by L.A. Reid and Babyface, with producers including Dallas Austin, Jermaine Dupri, Sean Combs and the local team Organized Noize. The group were in turmoil: Lisa Lopes was facing criminal charges, and within a year of its enormous success the three filed for bankruptcy, having earned remarkably little from their contracts. It was the moment Atlanta became a centre of American R&B.","The title divides the group into three parts, T-Boz the cool, Chilli the sexy and Left Eye the crazy, and the album is built around those roles. Listen to Creep for the muted trumpet sample that loops through the whole song; it gives a song about infidelity a jazz-club melancholy.","It sold over ten million copies in America and won the Grammy for best R&B album. It has since been credited with defining the sound of 1990s female R&B groups and, through Organized Noize, with bringing the Atlanta sound of OutKast into the mainstream. Its story of commercial triumph and financial ruin is now a standard case in debates about artists' contracts.","Begin with Creep, then Waterfalls, then Red Light Special, to hear how differently three producers used the same three voices. Pay special attention to T-Boz: her low alto was unusual at the top of the charts. Follow it with Destiny's Child's The Writing's on the Wall to hear who learned from it."],"tlc-waterfalls":["A mid-tempo song with a warm, live-sounding groove, wah-wah guitar and a brass section, carrying two grim little stories. In the first a young man is drawn into selling drugs and is killed; in the second a man with HIV passes it on. Between them the chorus advises staying within your limits, and Left Eye's rap verse turns the message toward hope and self-belief.","Produced by Organized Noize, the Atlanta team behind OutKast, it was released in 1995 as the third single from CrazySexyCool and spent seven weeks at number one in America. Its video, directed by F. Gary Gray, used then-expensive digital effects, with the singers standing on water, and won the MTV award for video of the year. AIDS was still, in 1995, a leading cause of death for young Americans.","The sound is closer to 1970s Southern soul than to 1990s drum machines: Organized Noize built it around live instruments, including a horn line that could sit on an old Stax record. That warmth is what lets such a bleak song become a singalong. Listen also to how the two story verses are sung in the third person, keeping the singers as witnesses rather than preachers.","It was greeted as a rare hit that carried a public-health message without sounding like one, and it is still one of the best-known songs of the decade. Some critics have found the moral simplistic. It is routinely cited as proof that mainstream R&B in the 1990s could engage directly with the crises of its audience.","Listen once for the horns and the bass and once for the stories. Then play Curtis Mayfield's Pusherman from Super Fly, another soul record about the drug trade, and notice how both wrap hard content in an irresistible groove. Watch the video once; it is part of how the song was received."],"tlc-no-scrubs":["A crisp, mid-tempo dismissal of men who have nothing to offer but want everything. Chilli sings the lead over a clipped, stuttering beat and a looping minor-key guitar figure, and the chorus lands like a flat refusal. Left Eye adds a rap verse in some versions. It is short, precise and relentlessly catchy.","The lead single from FanMail, the group's first album after their bankruptcy and a long break. It was written by Kandi Burruss and Tameka Cottle of the Atlanta group Xscape with the producer Kevin 'She'kspere' Briggs, who were then shaping late-1990s R&B with a run of songs about money and self-respect. It spent four weeks at number one in America and won two Grammys.","Listen to the gaps in the drum pattern: Briggs leaves beats out and pushes others early, a stop-start style that Timbaland and Darkchild were also developing and that would define R&B around 2000. The same writing team wrote Destiny's Child's Bills, Bills, Bills the same year, and the two songs are close cousins in both sound and attitude.","It provoked answer records from male rappers, and debate about whether it was empowering or simply snobbish about money. It has become one of the defining songs of late-1990s R&B, much covered and still a staple of any party that plays music from the period.","Play it next to Destiny's Child's Say My Name and notice the shared jittery rhythm. Then listen only to the guitar loop: it is the song's melancholy undertow, and it keeps the track from sounding merely smug."],"destinys-child-the-writings-on-the-wall":["The Houston group's second album, full of tight four-part harmony and quick, clipped vocal lines that fall across jittery programmed beats. Bills, Bills, Bills, Bug a Boo, Jumpin', Jumpin' and Say My Name are the hits; slower songs let the group's gospel-trained harmonies spread out. Beyoncé Knowles leads most tracks, with Kelly Rowland, LaTavia Roberson and LeToya Luckett close behind.","Managed by Knowles's father, Mathew, the group had been rehearsing since childhood. The album was made with a roster of the most sought-after R&B producers of the moment, including Kevin 'She'kspere' Briggs, Rodney Jerkins and Missy Elliott. Before it had finished its run, two of the four members had left in a bitter dispute and been replaced, one of the most public line-up changes in pop.","The album is framed as a book of rules for relationships: a spoken opening sets it up, and many track titles carry a mock-scriptural commandment in brackets. Listen to how the vocal lines are written almost as percussion, with syllables packed tightly and cut short; this rapid-fire singing became a signature of Beyoncé's later style.","It sold many millions and made the group the leading R&B act of the turn of the century. Critics at the time were friendly but not reverent. The album is now heard as the foundation of Beyoncé's solo career and as a summary of late-1990s R&B production at its sharpest.","Start with Bills, Bills, Bills and Say My Name, then go back and listen only to the harmonies. Compare with TLC's CrazySexyCool: similar Southern roots, but here the singing is the rhythm section. Then jump to Solange's A Seat at the Table to hear where the family took it."],"destinys-child-say-my-name":["A suspicious lover interrogates a partner on the phone, over one of the most intricate beats of its time. Rodney Jerkins' production keeps shifting under the singers: the drums skip and stumble, the chords change colour, and the song's sections feel as if they have been cut and re-spliced. The harmonised chorus is a simple demand, repeated until it sounds like an accusation.","The fourth single from The Writing's on the Wall, released early in 2000 in the middle of the group's line-up split. The video introduced Michelle Williams and Farrah Franklin as new members, and the departing LaTavia Roberson and LeToya Luckett reportedly learned of their replacement from it. The song reached number one in America and won two Grammys.","Listen to the harmony under the verses: Jerkins moves the chords in unexpected directions, which is why the song feels unsettled even when the beat is steady. The track's most distinctive sound, a sharp clicking percussion, sits almost where a hi-hat would be but never quite behaves like one.","It was praised immediately as one of the most inventive R&B productions of the era, and it has stayed on critics' lists of the best songs of the 2000s. The drama around its video has become part of its story, and part of the myth of Beyoncé's rise.","Listen with headphones to the drum programming alone; it is doing something new every few bars. Then compare with TLC's No Scrubs from a year earlier, and with the slower, broken-down beats of Frank Ocean or SZA a decade later."],"dangelo-brown-sugar":["Ten warm, slow-burning songs played mostly by D'Angelo himself on Fender Rhodes electric piano, organ and bass, over drums that nod to hip-hop without its hardness. His voice layers on itself in close harmony, murmuring and falsetto by turns. The title track opens the album with a lazy, rolling groove; Lady, Me and Those Dreamin' Eyes of Mine and a cover of Smokey Robinson's Cruisin' follow.","D'Angelo, from Richmond, Virginia, was twenty-one, the son of a Pentecostal minister, with a gospel-trained ear and a hip-hop generation's taste in drums. His manager and label head Kedar Massenburg used the phrase 'neo-soul' to sell him and, soon, Erykah Badu. The term stuck to a whole movement. Contemporary R&B was then dominated by slick, heavily produced ballads; this sounded handmade.","The title track was co-produced with Ali Shaheed Muhammad of A Tribe Called Quest, and the rhythm feel of that group's records runs underneath the album. Listen to the background vocals: D'Angelo stacks his own voice in the manner of Marvin Gaye's 1970s records, so that a choir of one surrounds the lead.","It sold well, earned Grammy nominations and was received as a return to the virtues of 1970s soul. Critics now tend to hear it as a starting pistol: without it the neo-soul of Badu, Maxwell, Lauryn Hill and Jill Scott would sound quite different. Some argue that the neo-soul label always undersold how new it was.","Play the title track and follow the Rhodes piano, which holds the whole song together. Then put on Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On and hear the family resemblance in the layered voices. Move on to Voodoo, five years later, to hear the same man take everything further."],"dangelo-voodoo":["Thirteen long, loose, deeply funky tracks recorded live by a small band. The drums lag behind the beat, the bass pushes against them, and D'Angelo's voice is often buried in the mix like another instrument. Playa Playa opens with a slow, swampy groove; Devil's Pie, Left & Right, The Line, Chicken Grease and Untitled (How Does It Feel) follow, alongside a cover of Roberta Flack's Feel Like Makin' Love. It is hazy, sensual and hard to hurry.","Made over about four years at Electric Lady Studios in New York, the studio Jimi Hendrix built, with a loose collective later called the Soulquarians: Questlove on drums, James Poyser on keys, Pino Palladino on bass, J Dilla's ideas about timing, and guests including Roy Hargrove. The sessions involved endless jamming on old Prince and Parliament records. The album went to number one in America and won a Grammy.","The rhythmic trick is everywhere and easy to miss: the drums, bass and voice each sit at slightly different points around the beat, a deliberately 'drunk' feel borrowed from J Dilla's programmed beats and played by humans. Listen to the snare on Left & Right, which arrives late every time. Devil's Pie was produced by DJ Premier, the one track built on a sample in the old hip-hop manner.","Early reviews were mixed to strong, with some listeners finding it too murky and slow. Its standing has risen every year since, and it is now widely named among the best albums of its decade. Musicians study its rhythmic feel the way jazz players study Kind of Blue. Its long shadow was lengthened by D'Angelo's fourteen-year silence afterwards.","Put it on loud, not quiet, and listen to the drums and bass on Playa Playa rather than the singing. Once the lag in the rhythm clicks, the whole record opens. Then play Prince's Sign o' the Times era and Sly Stone's There's a Riot Goin' On if you know them, and Robert Glasper's Black Radio after."],"dangelo-untitled-how-does-it-feel":["A slow, simmering ballad that builds for seven minutes from a soft falsetto plea to a raw, full-throated climax. The guitars and keyboards churn gently, the drums stay steady, and D'Angelo's layered voices rise until the last minutes, where the song breaks into something close to a scream. It is an unashamed homage to Prince's slow jams.","Written with Raphael Saadiq and released from Voodoo in 2000. Its video, directed by Paul Hunter, is a single continuous shot of D'Angelo, apparently naked, singing to the camera. It made him a sex symbol overnight. He later said that the attention and the expectation it created made performing unbearable, and it is widely seen as one reason he withdrew from public life for over a decade.","The song is built to sound like Prince at every level: the falsetto, the guitar tone and the arc from whisper to shriek. D'Angelo has described it as a tribute. Listen near the end for the moment the band drops almost out and the voice carries the song alone before the last surge.","It won the Grammy for best male R&B vocal performance and remains his best-known song. Its video is still cited in discussions of how the music industry treats Black male sexuality, and of how an image can overwhelm an artist. Musically it is admired as a masterclass in slow build.","Listen to it all the way through without skipping; the last ninety seconds are the point. Then find a live version from the 2000 tour to hear how the band stretched it. Compare with Al Green's Call Me for another soul singer who could make restraint burn."],"dangelo-black-messiah":["A thick, murky, guitar-heavy funk album that sounds as if it were recorded inside a haze. D'Angelo's voice is again half-buried, the guitars grind and chime, and the rhythms lurch. 1000 Deaths opens with sampled speech and a heavy, distorted groove; Really Love turns to a soft acoustic ballad with flamenco-tinged guitar; The Charade and Another Life take the soul tradition back to its gospel and protest roots.","After fourteen years of silence, the album was released with almost no warning in December 2014, brought forward in response to the protests in Ferguson and after the death of Eric Garner. It is credited to D'Angelo and the Vanguard, his touring band, with Questlove, Pino Palladino and co-writers including Q-Tip and Kendra Foster. It won the Grammy for best R&B album.","D'Angelo wrote a note explaining that the title does not refer to him but to people, to a collective of those rising up. The Charade's references to being denied breath were widely heard as pointing at Eric Garner's final words, though the song was written before his death. Listen for the vintage recording gear: much of it was tracked on analogue tape.","Reviews were rapturous and it appeared on almost every critics' list of the year. Some listeners found it too murky after the clarity of Voodoo. It is now heard alongside Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly and Solange's A Seat at the Table as part of a wave of Black American protest albums in the mid-2010s.","Start with Really Love for its warmth, then go back to 1000 Deaths for its fury. Listen to it next to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On and Curtis Mayfield's Curtis, whose tradition of soul as social commentary it knowingly continues."],"erykah-badu-baduizm":["A slow, jazzy, deliberately unhurried debut. Badu's voice, thin, grainy and oddly phrased, floats over upright-sounding bass lines, dry drums and soft keyboards. On & On opens with a spare, looping groove and a lyric full of mystical numerology; Next Lifetime, Appletree and Otherside of the Game follow. The record sounds closer to a small jazz club than to 1990s R&B radio.","Badu, born Erica Wright in Dallas, had worked as a teacher and performed in Dallas clubs before Kedar Massenburg, D'Angelo's manager, signed her. Several tracks were recorded in Philadelphia with members of the Roots. The album entered the American chart near the top and won the Grammy for best R&B album, confirming neo-soul as a movement rather than a single artist.","On & On draws on the teachings of the Five-Percent Nation, a Black nationalist offshoot of the Nation of Islam whose vocabulary of numbers and 'knowledge of self' runs through 1990s hip-hop; the song can seem abstract until you know that. Listen also for the flute and the way her phrasing lags and bends the beat, which drew instant comparisons to Billie Holiday.","Critics were taken with her at once and the Billie Holiday comparison followed her everywhere, sometimes to her annoyance. The album's reputation is secure; debate is mostly about whether her later, stranger records surpass it. Many consider it the defining neo-soul debut.","Start with On & On, then Otherside of the Game, and listen to how much space the band leaves. Then play Billie Holiday's God Bless the Child and hear where the comparison came from, and where it stops."],"erykah-badu-mamas-gun":["Badu's second studio album is warmer, looser and more varied than her first. Penitentiary Philosophy opens with rock-tinged funk; Didn't Cha Know rides a woozy, hazy loop; Bag Lady advises letting go of emotional baggage; Cleva celebrates plainness. It ends with Green Eyes, a ten-minute suite in three parts that moves from sparse jazz balladry to full band as it traces the stages of heartbreak.","Made at Electric Lady Studios in New York with the same Soulquarians collective who were finishing D'Angelo's Voodoo: Questlove, James Poyser, J Dilla and others. The overlap in players, rooms and months was deliberate; the collective traded ideas across albums. It was released on Motown in late 2000, and Bag Lady became a major hit.","Didn't Cha Know was produced with J Dilla and built on a sample from the jazz-fusion group Tarika Blue; its stumbling drum feel is his signature. A.D. 2000 is a quiet elegy for Amadou Diallo, the unarmed man shot by New York police in 1999, though the song never states the facts outright.","It was well received and is now often ranked above her debut as her finest album. Green Eyes is admired as one of the most ambitious pieces of songwriting in the neo-soul movement. It is regularly paired with Voodoo as the twin peak of the Electric Lady sessions.","Play Green Eyes last and all the way through; its three sections are best heard as one act. Then play Didn't Cha Know alongside D'Angelo's Left & Right to hear the shared rhythmic feel of the collective."],"erykah-badu-new-amerykah-part-one":["A dense, psychedelic, politically charged album built largely from samples and beats by hip-hop producers. Amerykahn Promise opens with a brassy 1970s funk cover; The Healer drifts over a slow Madlib beat; Master Teacher and Soldier look at Black American life, faith and survival; Honey closes in a lighter mood. It sounds like a radio dial turning through a troubled country.","Made in the last year of the George W. Bush presidency, with producers including Madlib, 9th Wonder and the Los Angeles team Sa-Ra Creative Partners. Badu recorded much of it on a laptop and had embraced the beat-driven, sample-based underground of the 2000s. It is dedicated in part to J Dilla, who had died in 2006; Telephone addresses him.","Amerykahn Promise is a cover of The American Promise by the group RAMP, a 1970s band linked to Roy Ayers, so the album opens with a borrowed voice from Black radicalism of an earlier decade. The Healer names hip-hop as bigger than religion or government; listen for Madlib's hazy, unquantised drums, a direct inheritance from Dilla.","It was widely praised as her boldest album and one of the most adventurous major-label records of its year. Some fans missed the song-shaped warmth of her earlier work. It now reads as a forerunner of the sample-rich, socially minded Black music of the 2010s, from Kendrick Lamar to Solange.","Treat it as a mixtape and listen straight through. Start again at The Healer and notice how little happens, and how much that little does. Then go to De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising for an earlier record built on the same collage principle."],"maxwell-urban-hang-suite":["A concept album that follows a single love affair from first meeting to a proposal of marriage. Maxwell sings mostly in a soft, airy falsetto over live, gently swinging grooves: warm bass, muted guitar, Fender Rhodes and discreet horns. Sumthin' Sumthin', Ascension (Don't Ever Wonder) and Whenever Wherever Whatever are the best known. The whole is continuous, adult and deliberately unhurried.","Maxwell, from Brooklyn, of Puerto Rican and Haitian descent, was in his early twenties. The album was finished in 1994 but shelved for over a year by Columbia, who were unsure how to sell it. Among its collaborators was Stuart Matthewman of Sade, and Leon Ware, who had written and produced Marvin Gaye's I Want You. When finally released it went platinum.","Leon Ware's presence is the key: Sumthin' Sumthin' carries the lush, sensual texture of the Gaye album he made in 1976, and the whole record is in a sense a sequel to it. Matthewman's involvement links it directly to Sade. Listen to the transitions between songs: the story is told as much by moods as by words.","Critics welcomed it as a romantic, musicianly alternative to the hard R&B of the mid-1990s, and it was nominated for a Grammy. It is now counted, with Brown Sugar and Baduizm, as a founding neo-soul album. Maxwell's long gaps between records have kept its reputation high.","Listen straight through, in order, as a story. Then play Sade's Love Deluxe and Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On to hear the two parents of this sound. A good system helps; the bass and the Rhodes carry the warmth."],"lauryn-hill-the-miseducation-of-lauryn-hill":["An album that moves freely between singing and rapping, hip-hop beats and live soul, reggae and gospel. Lost Ones is a hard, rapped opening; Ex-Factor a slow ballad of a relationship coming apart; To Zion a song to her newborn son with Carlos Santana on guitar; Doo Wop (That Thing) a street-corner warning set to horns and harmonies. Between songs, a teacher talks with a classroom of teenagers about love.","Hill, from New Jersey, had been the singer and one of the rappers of the Fugees, whose album The Score had been one of the biggest records of 1996. She made this, her only solo studio album, partly at Bob Marley's Tuff Gong studio in Kingston, Jamaica, while pregnant and after the group had begun to fall apart. It won five Grammys, including album of the year, the first for a hip-hop record.","The title alludes to Carter G. Woodson's 1933 book The Mis-Education of the Negro, about how schooling shaped Black Americans' sense of themselves. The classroom interludes were recorded with real students. The musicians who worked on it, credited as New Ark, later sued for credit as co-writers; the case settled. D'Angelo sings with her on Nothing Even Matters.","It was acclaimed at once and has never really been doubted. Its standing grew further because Hill never made a follow-up studio album, and it is now a fixture on all-time lists. The credit dispute remains a live question in how its authorship is described.","Play Ex-Factor for the singing and Lost Ones for the rapping, then To Zion for everything at once. Listen through the classroom interludes, which frame the album as lessons. Afterwards play Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace to hear where the gospel in her voice comes from."],"lauryn-hill-doo-wop-that-thing":["A brisk, bouncy warning in two halves: the first verse cautions young women against men who want only one thing, the second turns on young men who care more about looks and money than character. Hill raps the verses in a quick, conversational flow and sings the chorus over a rolling piano, punchy horns and 1950s-style doo-wop backing harmonies. It sounds joyful and old-fashioned at once, and that sweetness is what lets the lecture land.","The first single from The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill. It entered the American chart at number one, a rare feat at the time, and won Grammys for best R&B song and best female R&B vocal performance. Its video shows two block parties side by side in split screen, one in 1967 and one in 1998, with Hill performing in both.","The title is an ironic nod to doo-wop, the street-corner harmony style of the 1950s, and the backing singers use its vocabulary while the verses speak with 1990s directness. The split-screen video makes the point explicit: the same problems, thirty years apart. Listen for the moment the two halves of the arrangement, old and new, lock together.","Critics liked it immediately, and its debut at the top of the chart proved that a song with an explicit moral could also be a commercial hit. It is now regularly named among the best songs of the 1990s. Some listeners find its moralising stern, and its even-handedness, scolding both sexes in turn, has been read both as fairness and as conservatism. Its musical wit, fusing a 1950s harmony group with a 1990s rap verse, is rarely questioned.","Watch the video once, then listen with eyes closed and hear the two eras in the music. Then go back to The Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go to hear the tradition she was pointing at."],"alicia-keys-songs-in-a-minor":["An R&B debut built around the piano. Keys plays on almost every track, from the classical-sounding introduction to the gospel chords of Fallin' and the soft balladry of A Woman's Worth. Her voice is husky and strong, and the drums often have the thick, dusty feel of hip-hop. She also covers Prince's How Come You Don't Call Me.","Keys, from Hell's Kitchen in Manhattan, trained as a classical pianist and signed her first record deal as a teenager with Columbia. When that deal stalled, Clive Davis took her to his new label J Records. She appeared on the Oprah Winfrey Show before the album came out, and it sold over twelve million copies worldwide. It won five Grammys, including best new artist.","The opening track, Piano & I, is Keys playing a phrase from Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata before the beat drops in; the whole album is framed as the story of a classical pianist finding her way into soul. The title is a pun: A minor is a key, and she was a minor when she wrote many of the songs.","It was a huge commercial success and well reviewed, though some critics thought it earnest and derivative of the 1970s. It is now seen as the moment neo-soul crossed into the pop mainstream, and as the record that made the piano central to R&B again.","Start with Piano & I and then Fallin', and listen for the classical training in her left hand. Then play the first movement of the Moonlight Sonata itself, and afterwards Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, to which the album owes a great deal."],"alicia-keys-fallin":["A slow, swaying gospel-blues ballad in a lilting triple feel. Keys plays a rolling piano figure that repeats almost unchanged, and sings over it in a husky voice about a love that keeps pulling her back despite herself. The arrangement is spare: piano, drums and backing voices that rise in the choruses. By the end she is almost shouting.","The first single from Songs in A Minor. Clive Davis promoted it with an unusual appearance on the Oprah Winfrey Show before release, and it rose to number one in America, where it stayed for six weeks. It won the Grammy for song of the year, a rare honour for an R&B ballad by a new writer.","Listen to the piano: it keeps circling round the same few minor chords, a pattern that owes as much to blues and old gospel as to modern R&B. The rhythm has the slow triple sway of a 1950s soul ballad, which is why the song sounds older than it is. Its opening seconds were designed to announce 'a pianist', not 'a producer'.","It was praised as a return to real musicianship in pop R&B at a moment when the charts were dominated by producers' beats, and a twenty-year-old playing her own piano on television became part of the story. Some critics found it dramatic without being deep, all build and no surprise. It remains her signature song and a standard for singing contests, which has done its reputation no favours, though her own live versions keep it raw.","Listen to the piano alone, then to the voice alone; they are two different songs of equal weight. Then compare it with Etta James's I'd Rather Go Blind, another slow minor-key song of helpless love."],"jill-scott-who-is-jill-scott":["A warm, conversational debut that moves between singing, spoken poetry and scat. Scott's voice is full and flexible, sliding from a murmur to a roar within a line. A Long Walk describes a simple date in loving detail; Gettin' in the Way is a firm warning to a rival; The Way and He Loves Me (Lyzel in E Flat) are soft, slow songs of contentment. The grooves are live and relaxed.","Scott came from the Philadelphia spoken-word scene, where poets and musicians performed together in small clubs. She had co-written The Roots' You Got Me, which won a Grammy with Erykah Badu singing her part. This debut was produced largely by DJ Jazzy Jeff's company A Touch of Jazz, in Philadelphia, and released on the new independent label Hidden Beach.","He Loves Me (Lyzel in E Flat) is one of the rare pop songs to name its own key in the title, a nod to the jazz and classical music around which Scott grew up. Listen for how often her lines move from singing to speaking in mid-sentence: the album is poetry performance as much as soul.","It was received warmly and sold well, and it is a defining record of the Philadelphia strand of neo-soul. Some critics found its domestic, everyday focus refreshing, others slight; its reputation has settled on the side of warmth and craft.","Start with A Long Walk, then He Loves Me, and notice how much of each song is spoken. Then play Erykah Badu's Baduizm and Lauryn Hill's Miseducation for the neo-soul circle she belonged to."],"frank-ocean-channel-orange":["A sprawling debut album of character sketches and confessions. Thinkin Bout You is a falsetto love song over sparse synthesisers; Super Rich Kids portrays bored, wealthy teenagers; Pyramids is a ten-minute journey from ancient Egypt to a modern strip club; Bad Religion is a confession to a taxi driver, set against organ and strings. Ocean's voice moves from croon to falsetto to a nearly spoken murmur, and the arrangements are lush but never crowded.","Ocean, raised in New Orleans and a member of the Los Angeles collective Odd Future, had already made a name with a mixtape and songwriting for others. Days before release in July 2012, he published an open letter describing his first love as a man. At the time, R&B and hip-hop had few openly queer stars, and the letter gave the album a context it could not escape.","The title refers to synaesthesia: Ocean has said he associated the summer he first fell in love with the colour orange. Listen to how the album is framed by television sounds, with channels switching between tracks, as if you were flicking through stations. Bad Religion and Forrest Gump address love for a man without saying so outright.","Critics greeted it with near-unanimous acclaim and it won the Grammy for best urban contemporary album. It is now seen as the album that opened R&B up to wider subjects, stranger structures and queer lives, and as the start of the alternative R&B era.","Start with Thinkin Bout You, then Bad Religion, then Pyramids. Listen to how often the songs end in a different place from where they started. Then go back to Stevie Wonder's Innervisions, another album of character sketches in soul form."],"frank-ocean-pyramids":["A ten-minute song in two halves. The first is a pulsing, synthesiser-driven tale of Cleopatra stolen from her palace; midway, the music drops away and returns as a slow, woozy R&B groove set in a modern-day strip club called the Pyramid, where a woman who shares Cleopatra's name works. The tempo, the texture and the whole century change; the story continues. It ends with a long guitar solo.","Released as a single shortly before Channel Orange, and the album's clearest statement of ambition. Long R&B songs had been rare on the radio since the 1970s, when Isaac Hayes and Stevie Wonder stretched tracks past ten minutes; this one refused radio length outright at a time when streaming was only beginning to make such refusals affordable. Ocean produced it with his collaborator Om'Mas Keith, and John Mayer adds the closing guitar.","The two halves share a name and a woman: the song is a comparison between two kinds of queenship, one ancient, one debased. Listen for the arpeggiated synthesiser in the first half, which owes a lot to 1980s dance music and to Kraftwerk's machine pulses, before the second half lurches into slow soul.","It was admired as one of the boldest singles of its year and is often cited as the moment Ocean showed his range, turning an R&B single into something closer to a short film. Some critics found it overlong and the Egyptian half more concept than song. Over time the second half, slow and sordid, has come to be heard as the real heart of it. It remains one of the defining long-form songs of 2010s R&B.","Listen through without skipping to the switch at about the midpoint, which is the point. Then compare with Stevie Wonder's long, multi-part songs from the 1970s, and with Kraftwerk's Computer World for the synthesiser pulse."],"frank-ocean-blonde":["A drifting, fragmented, often drumless album of memories and longing. Nikes opens with pitched-up vocals over a soft beat; Ivy is a guitar ballad about a fading first love; Self Control, Solo and White Ferrari are hushed and open-ended, often with guitar or organ alone. Ocean's voice is sometimes sped up, sometimes low and close. Many songs end without resolving; the album closes with the gospel-tinged Futura Free.","Ocean released it himself in August 2016, a day after delivering a visual album, Endless, to fulfil his contract with Def Jam, and so owned it outright. He had spent four years making it, with contributors including Beyoncé and André 3000, largely out of view. The title on the cover reads Blond, and the difference between the two spellings is often read as an allusion to gender.","White Ferrari quietly borrows lines and melody from the Beatles' Here, There and Everywhere, and its writers are credited. Listen also for the spoken interludes, including a voicemail from Ocean's mother warning against drugs, and for how few tracks have steady drums: the missing pulse is the album's most radical choice.","It was acclaimed at once and topped many best-of-the-decade lists. Ocean declined to submit it for Grammys, criticising the awards' treatment of Black artists. It is now widely considered the defining album of alternative R&B and one of the most influential albums of the century so far.","Listen late at night, in order, with headphones. Start with Ivy, then Self Control, then White Ferrari. After White Ferrari, play the Beatles' Revolver and find Here, There and Everywhere to hear how completely he transformed it."],"solange-a-seat-at-the-table":["A soft-spoken, deeply personal album about Black identity, anger and healing. Cranes in the Sky lists the ways the singer has tried to escape sadness, over a gentle groove and strings; Don't Touch My Hair is a quiet refusal of other people's intrusions; Mad and F.U.B.U. address rage and ownership. Solange's voice is light and airy, and the grooves are slow, warm and full of space.","Solange, Beyoncé's younger sister, had been making music for a decade with modest success. She recorded much of this in New Iberia, Louisiana, with Raphael Saadiq co-producing. Released in September 2016, in the middle of the Black Lives Matter protests, it went to number one in America. Cranes in the Sky won a Grammy.","The album is threaded with spoken interludes from her parents, Tina and Mathew Knowles, and from Master P, the New Orleans rapper and label founder, who talks about independence and ownership. These voices give the album the feel of a family conversation. Cranes in the Sky was written some eight years earlier and held back until it fit.","It was met with near-unanimous praise and appeared at the top of many best-of-the-year lists. Critics noted that its anger is delivered almost in a whisper, the opposite of the anthemic protest song, and that choice is what most reviews returned to. It was released months after Beyoncé's Lemonade, and comparison was inevitable. It is now seen as a major statement of the mid-2010s, and as the moment Solange stepped fully out of her sister's shadow.","Start with Cranes in the Sky, then Don't Touch My Hair. Listen to the interludes; they are part of the argument. Then play Frank Ocean's Blonde, released the same year, for another quiet, bold R&B record."],"sza-ctrl":["A candid, funny and anxious album about being young, insecure and in love. Supermodel opens with guitar and a list of grievances; Love Galore, Drew Barrymore, The Weekend and 20 Something are slow, woozy songs about jealousy, self-doubt and wanting to be chosen. SZA's voice is loose, slurring and conversational, and the beats are soft, spacious and often guitar-led.","SZA, born Solána Rowe in St Louis and raised in New Jersey, had been signed to Top Dawg Entertainment, Kendrick Lamar's label. The album was delayed for years while she argued with the label. When it arrived in June 2017 it was an immediate success and earned five Grammy nominations.","The album is threaded with spoken interludes from SZA's mother and grandmother, who talk about control, and whose voices frame the album's question: how much of life can anyone control? Listen to the guitar: many songs are built around it rather than keyboards, an unusual choice in modern R&B.","It was met with acclaim and topped many best-of-the-year lists. Critics admired its candour and its refusal to present the singer as glamorous. It is now seen as one of the defining R&B albums of the late 2010s, and a blueprint for the confessional, genre-blurring R&B that followed.","Start with Supermodel, then Drew Barrymore and The Weekend. Listen for the spoken interludes. Then compare it with Frank Ocean's Channel Orange, whose openness about the singer's inner life it continues."],"the-weeknd-house-of-balloons":["A mixtape of dark, slow, reverb-soaked songs about parties, loneliness and excess. The Weeknd's high, pleading voice floats over cavernous drums and blurry synthesisers. High for This, What You Need and The Morning are the best known; the title track rides a sample from a British post-punk band. The mood is hazy, seductive and uneasy.","Abel Tesfaye, a Toronto singer born to Ethiopian immigrant parents, released this for free online in March 2011 with producers Doc McKinney and Illangelo. He gave no interviews and hid his face, so the music spread through blogs and a recommendation from Drake. The mixtape was later reissued as part of a compilation called Trilogy. Drug use and casual sex are its constant subject, treated with numbness rather than celebration.","The title track samples Siouxsie and the Banshees' Happy House, and The Party & The After Party uses Beach House's Master of None; alternative rock samples in R&B were rare, and they give the album its dreamlike haze. Listen for how the reverb swallows the drums, making even the fastest tracks sound half-asleep.","It was acclaimed by online critics and made the Weeknd famous almost overnight. Along with Frank Ocean's early work, it is credited with defining alternative R&B. Some listeners have found its view of women and excess troubling, a debate that has followed his career.","Listen late at night, with headphones, from start to finish. Pay attention to the samples and how they are bent into R&B. Then compare with Sade's Diamond Life, a very different take on seduction, and with Frank Ocean's Channel Orange."],"the-weeknd-blinding-lights":["A bright, driving synth-pop song with a pounding drum machine and a looping keyboard hook. The Weeknd sings of racing through a city at night to reach someone he needs. The sound is pure 1980s: gated drums, shimmering synthesisers and a relentless pulse. It is short, catchy and always moving forward.","Released in November 2019 as a single from the album After Hours, with production by Max Martin and Oscar Holter. It became one of the biggest songs of the century, spending ninety weeks on the American chart and topping Billboard's list of the greatest Hot 100 songs of all time in 2021. It also became one of the most-streamed songs in history.","Listen to the drum machine: it has the sharp, gated sound of 1980s pop, and the keyboard hook recalls a-ha's Take On Me. The song is part of a larger story on After Hours, in which the Weeknd plays a character spiralling through a night in Las Vegas, and the video shows him bloodied and dazed.","It was a huge commercial success and widely admired as a polished pop song; arriving just before the pandemic lockdowns, its nocturnal rush through empty streets took on an unintended resonance. Some critics found it more nostalgic than original. Its chart records have since made it a reference point in debates about how streaming measures popularity. It is now seen as the moment the Weeknd moved fully from alternative R&B into global pop.","Listen to the drum machine and the synthesiser hook, then play Kraftwerk's Computer World to hear where that machine pulse began. Then compare it with House of Balloons to hear how far the Weeknd travelled in eight years."],"sugarhill-gang-rappers-delight":["Fifteen minutes of three young men from New Jersey rapping in turn over a live band's replay of Chic's Good Times: bass, hi-hat, a scratchy rhythm guitar, almost nothing else. Wonder Mike opens with the hip hop, the hippie chant; Big Bank Hank and Master Gee follow with boasts, a joke about a terrible dinner at a friend's house, and a Superman routine. Nobody sings a note. The groove simply keeps going and the voices keep passing the baton.","Sylvia Robinson, a singer turned label owner, had heard rapping at a New York club and decided to put it on a record. The Bronx MCs who invented the form worked live and saw little reason to make records, so Robinson auditioned three men she found around Englewood, New Jersey, one of them rapping while working at a pizzeria. Her house band played the Chic groove rather than sampling it, because sampling barely existed in 1979.","Big Bank Hank's verse was not his own. He had been working with Grandmaster Caz of the Cold Crush Brothers and used lines from his notebook; the boast that spells out C-A-S-A-N-O-V-A is Caz's own name, and Caz was never credited or paid. Nile Rodgers and Bernard Edwards, hearing their bassline coming out of a club, took action and were added to the songwriting credit.","Bronx originators were scornful: outsiders with no standing had beaten them onto vinyl. It sold enormously anyway and travelled far past New York. Judgment has settled on two claims that are both true, that it was not the first rap record and that it was the one that made the rest possible. The Chic settlement also set early terms for how borrowed music would be paid for.","Find the full-length twelve-inch rather than the radio edit; endurance is the point. Notice how the band stiffens and loosens behind the voices in a way no drum machine would. Then play Chic's Good Times straight afterwards and hear how much emptier the rap version deliberately leaves the middle of the mix, so that speech has room."],"grandmaster-flash-adventures-on-the-wheels-of-steel":["Seven minutes of one man cutting between records in real time, with no band and almost no rapping. Flash drops the needle on a groove, snatches it back, stutters a single word into a rhythm, and slams into a different record mid-bar. Fragments of Chic, Blondie's Rapture, Queen's Another One Bites the Dust and his own group's records collide and hand off to each other. The join is always audible, and that is the music.","By 1981 Flash had spent years building a technique in Bronx parks and community rooms: a cue system so he could hear the next record before the crowd did, and a way of marking the label with crayon to find a break instantly. Sugar Hill let him document it. The takes had to be played straight through, because multitrack editing would have defeated the purpose, and a turntable record had no commercial precedent at all.","Listen for the moment Blondie's Debbie Harry names Flash in the lyric of Rapture, which he then cuts up: the record is quoting a record that is quoting him. A children's spoken-word disc also turns up in the collage, chopped into nonsense. Everything you hear is the sound of vinyl being physically dragged, so the pitch bends slightly on almost every entry.","Radio had nowhere to put it and it sold modestly. Critics at the time treated it as a novelty document of a party trick. It is now read as the founding statement of turntablism, the argument that a record player is an instrument and that existing recordings are raw material. DJ battle culture, scratch records and the entire sampling aesthetic descend from these seven minutes.","Count the sources on a first pass and then stop counting and listen to the rhythm of the cuts instead: Flash is phrasing, not just switching. Compare it with Cage's radio piece to hear two different centuries deciding that found sound is material, then play a modern DMC battle routine to hear where the sport went."],"grandmaster-flash-the-message":["A slow, heavy mid-tempo groove, a rubbery synthesizer figure looping under it, and a voice describing broken glass, rats, urine on the stairs and a repossessed car. The refrain, don't push me, 'cause I'm close to the edge, arrives like a held breath. Melle Mel's closing verse follows a child from birth to a prison cell in about a minute. The track ends not with music but with a spoken scene of the group being stopped by police.","Sugar Hill's percussionist Ed Fletcher, known as Duke Bootee, brought most of the words and the track; Melle Mel supplied the last verse and the delivery. Several of the Furious Five wanted nothing to do with it, believing audiences came to dance and boast, not to hear about the rent. Flash himself is barely present. New York in 1982 was cutting services, and the South Bronx had become national shorthand for urban collapse.","The final thirty seconds are a drama, not a song: the crew greet each other on a corner, a squad car pulls up, and the record fades on them being put in it. Nothing is explained. Notice too that the famous refrain is sung, not rapped, by Fletcher, which is why the hook sits so oddly against the verses around it.","It was the first rap record widely treated as journalism, and it gave critics who had dismissed the form a way in, which annoyed people who had liked hip-hop when it was only about dancing. The complaint that it invented a market for misery is as old as the record. So is the answer: it also proved rap could carry an argument.","Play it after Rapper's Delight to feel the temperature drop. Follow the last verse only, from the birth of the child to the cell, and time it. Then listen to how little the backing track changes across seven minutes, and how that refusal to develop becomes the point: nothing here moves."],"grandmaster-flash-white-lines":["A bassline that climbs and drops in a four-note hook carries the whole record; over it Melle Mel warns about cocaine while sounding thrilled by it. Backing voices chant the title and a hissed freeze, rock. Two worlds alternate in the verses, a dealer in a Cadillac who beats the charge and a kid who gets eight years for a small amount. The groove never lets up for either of them.","By 1983 Flash had sued Sugar Hill and left; the record still went out under his name, which he objected to. Cocaine had moved from glamour to epidemic in New York, and the anti-drug song was becoming a commercial category. Sugar Hill's house band, as usual, replayed rather than sampled the borrowed part, a habit that would shortly become an expensive legal problem for the label.","That bassline comes from Cavern by Liquid Liquid, a downtown New York post-punk group; Sugar Hill's musicians copied it without clearance. The lawsuit that followed helped destroy both Liquid Liquid's label and Sugar Hill itself. Listen also to how the chorus voices are pushed almost into the red, a distortion that was cheap studio compression rather than a choice, and became part of the record's sound.","It was a hit in Britain long before America caught up, and its warning has always been undercut by how good the track feels, a tension critics have argued over ever since. Grandmaster Flash's absence from a record bearing his name remains a standing example of how labels treated artists in the era. It has been sampled, covered and re-recorded continuously.","Start with the bass and try to ignore the words for a chorus, then let the words back in and notice the mismatch. The twelve-inch version has room the edit does not. Play Liquid Liquid's Cavern afterwards to hear the same figure played by a very different band in a very different room."],"afrika-bambaataa-planet-rock":["No band, no guitars, no live drums: a Roland drum machine ticking out a hard, dry pattern, a synthesizer melody riding above it, a vocoder chanting party people, and the Soulsonic Force calling a crowd to the floor. The middle breaks down into handclaps and orchestral stabs before the machine snaps back. It sounds less like a song than like a transmission, and the voices behave as if they are answering it.","Bambaataa had been a Bronx gang leader who turned his organisation into the Universal Zulu Nation and his record collection into a philosophy: anything with a break was fair game, rock, calypso, television themes, German electronics. Producer Arthur Baker and keyboardist John Robie built the track with him in New York, at the moment the TR-808 drum machine had become cheap and unfashionable, which is exactly why it was available.","The melody is lifted from Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and the rhythm from their Numbers, welded together deliberately rather than quoted in passing. Kraftwerk were not asked, and the settlement came later. Listen for the 808's bass drum, which is not a sample of a drum at all but a tuned sine tone decaying, the sound that would define dance music for the next forty years.","It was a huge club record instantly and a foundational one in hindsight: Detroit techno and Miami bass both point back to it, and so does most electro. Bambaataa's own standing is now contested, after allegations of sexual abuse made public in 2016 led the Zulu Nation to remove him from his role, and writers have had to separate the record's importance from the man's.","Play Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express first, then this, and hear a cool European machine record turned into a hot American dance record by little more than tempo and attitude. Focus on the handclap breakdown, where the track almost stops. Then jump forward to early Detroit techno and notice how little had to change."],"afrika-bambaataa-looking-for-the-perfect-beat":["Eight minutes built from the same machines as Planet Rock but arranged like a suite: sections arrive, take over, and give way, with synth fanfares, sirens, a vocoder chorus and stretches where only the drum machine and a bass pulse remain. The Soulsonic Force chant the title as a mission statement. Where the earlier record had one idea repeated, this one keeps promising a bigger one around the next corner.","Tommy Boy wanted a follow-up, and Arthur Baker and John Robie were by then fluent in the equipment rather than improvising with it. Electro had become a genre within a year, with records coming out of New York, Miami and London, and the competition was to be more extravagant. Twelve-inch singles aimed at club DJs allowed lengths and structures that radio would never have permitted.","The track keeps deferring its own arrival, which is the joke in the title: the perfect beat is always the next section. Notice how the vocoder voice is used as an instrument in the arrangement rather than as a singer, dropping in for a bar and vanishing. The sirens are synthesized, not sampled, which is why they bend in ways a real siren never would.","It never matched Planet Rock commercially and has always lived in its shadow, though DJs and producers often prefer it for the arrangement. Critics who found electro thin in the mid-eighties have largely come round, hearing in it the template for a dance record built as a sequence of drops rather than verses and choruses.","Listen for structure rather than hooks: mark where each new section enters and see how rarely anything returns. Play it against Planet Rock to hear ambition replacing shock. Then follow the thread to Miami bass and early Detroit records, which took the drum machine and threw the fanfares away."],"run-dmc-sucker-mcs":["A drum machine, a handful of scratches, and two voices. There is no bassline, no chords, no chorus in the usual sense. Run and DMC shout in hard, clipped phrases and finish each other's lines, one voice landing on the last word of the other's. Three minutes, over almost before it has settled. After the layered party records of the previous four years it sounds like someone has switched the lights on.","Joseph Simmons and Darryl McDaniels were middle-class kids from Hollis, Queens, not South Bronx veterans. Run's brother Russell Simmons and producer Larry Smith put them on Profile, a small New York independent. The B-side of their first single, this was built on a hard programmed rhythm rather than a disco band, in a year when the available machines finally made that cheaper and louder than hiring players.","The lyric is a list of ordinary life, a Cadillac, a college, a bedroom, delivered with the aggression usually reserved for boasting about wealth. Listen to the trading: Run and DMC frequently overlap by a syllable, an effect they rehearsed rather than stumbled into, and which almost no earlier rap record attempts. The scratches are the only element that is not a machine.","It is routinely called the first new school record, the moment hip-hop stopped dressing like disco and started sounding like itself. Old school performers heard it as crude; younger MCs heard a blank page. Every stripped-back rap production of the following decade, and a good deal of the argument that less instrumentation means more force, starts here.","Play it immediately after Rapper's Delight and count the instruments in each. Then listen only to the vocal interplay, following where one voice hands over to the other. The best way to hear its influence is to put on a bare Def Jam record from three years later and notice that nothing essential has been added."],"run-dmc-run-dmc":["Under half an hour, nine tracks, and almost no decoration. Hard programmed drums, scratching from Jam Master Jay, and two voices barking in unison or in relay. It's Like That lays out unemployment and war in flat declaratives with a shrug of a refrain. Rock Box drops a distorted electric guitar into the middle of a rap record, doubling the drum machine's aggression. Hard Times and Jam-Master Jay keep the same skeletal frame.","Profile could not afford, and Russell Simmons did not want, a session band. The group came from Hollis, Queens, dressed in what they already wore, black hats, Adidas with no laces, leather, and refused the sequins and spacesuits of the previous generation. Rock radio was closed to rap; putting a guitar on Rock Box was partly a musical choice and partly a way of forcing a door.","The guitar on Rock Box was played by a session musician, Eddie Martinez, not by anyone in the group, and it is mixed as a rhythm instrument rather than a lead, sitting exactly where a horn stab would go. Notice that Jam Master Jay's scratching is arranged as punctuation at line ends, not as a solo: the DJ is treated as a third voice in the conversation.","It became the first rap album to sell in the hundreds of thousands and to be discussed as an album rather than a collection of twelve-inches. Rock critics used Rock Box as permission to write about hip-hop, which some in the scene resented as approval on the wrong terms. The record's plainness, once taken for poverty of means, is now heard as its argument.","Listen to It's Like That and Rock Box back to back: the same machine, two different strategies for getting heard. Keep an ear on how much silence the mix leaves around each drum hit. Then play Raising Hell to hear the same group with Rick Rubin making the emptiness even bigger."],"run-dmc-raising-hell":["Twelve tracks of enormous, dry drums and two voices at full volume. Peter Piper rides a loop of tubular bells against a hard break; My Adidas turns a pair of shoes into a chant; It's Tricky borrows a new wave guitar riff and rides it; Walk This Way drags a rock band into the room. Proud to Be Black closes with a history lesson. The whole record is louder, cleaner and funnier than anything rap had made.","Rick Rubin, then a student running Def Jam out of a dormitory, co-produced with Russell Simmons and pushed the group towards fewer elements played bigger. Rap was still assumed to be a singles form with no album audience. The record's success, a multi-platinum seller and a Rolling Stone cover, ended that assumption and made hip-hop a business that major labels could no longer pretend not to see.","The bells that run through Peter Piper come from Bob James's instrumental Take Me to the Mardi Gras, a break already famous in the parks long before it reached a record. My Adidas was not a paid endorsement when it was written; the sponsorship deal came afterwards, when the company's representatives watched an arena full of people hold their shoes in the air.","It was the first hip-hop album to reach a mass rock audience, and the crossover brought the familiar complaint that the music had been diluted for white listeners. Time has largely dismissed that: the record is harder, not softer, than its predecessors. What is still argued is whether Walk This Way opened a door for rap or simply rescued Aerosmith.","Begin with Peter Piper for the bells and the trading, then My Adidas for the chant. Notice throughout how much of the arrangement is silence between hits. Compare the drum sound here with the same group two years earlier: fewer elements, far more room, and every one of them enormous."],"run-dmc-walk-this-way":["The guitar riff from Aerosmith's 1975 record starts alone, then a drum machine lands on top of it with twice the weight. Run and DMC trade the original's tongue-twisting verses in hard alternating phrases while Steven Tyler sings the chorus and Joe Perry plays over them. Two bands are in the room at once and neither yields; the track works because the riff was already a rhythm loop before anyone rapped on it.","Rick Rubin proposed it. Run and DMC knew the Aerosmith break from park DJs and had rapped over it for years without knowing the band's name, and they initially thought recording it was embarrassing hillbilly music. Aerosmith, by 1986, were a spent commercial force. The video, in which a wall between a rock rehearsal room and a rap session is knocked down, did as much work as the record.","Listen to how Run and DMC deliver the original lyric: they keep Tyler's syllable count but flatten the melody out of it entirely, which exposes how close his phrasing already was to rapping. Note also that the drum machine does not follow the live drums, it replaces them, so the rock band is effectively playing over a hip-hop rhythm rather than the other way round.","It went top five, put rap on rock radio and MTV for the first time in force, and revived Aerosmith's career outright. Purists on both sides called it a stunt. The rap-rock genre it licensed produced a great deal of bad music over the following fifteen years, which has made some critics colder on it, but the collision itself still sounds deliberate rather than accidental.","Play Aerosmith's original first and listen only to the drums, then hear how the remake removes their swing and replaces it with a grid. Watch the video once for the argument it makes visually. Then try Licensed to Ill, made months later, where the same producer takes the joke much further."],"ll-cool-j-radio":["A teenager's voice, enormous drums and very little else. I Can't Live Without My Radio is a boast about a boombox delivered as if the boombox were a weapon; Rock the Bells rides a hammering pattern and a scratched bell; I Need a Beat is almost all space. The album is short, fast and monomaniacal, and the rapping is unusually agile, sliding between double-time bursts and slow, taunting lines.","James Todd Smith was sixteen when Def Jam, then run by Rick Rubin from a New York University dormitory with Russell Simmons, released his first single. Radio was the label's first album. Rubin's production philosophy was subtraction: a drum machine turned up until it distorted, one or two other elements, nothing to hide behind. The back cover credit reads that the record was reduced, not produced.","That credit, reduced by Rick Rubin, is the whole method stated in two words. Listen to how often a track drops to voice and kick drum alone for a bar before the rest slams back; the effect depends on the listener expecting something to fill the gap. The boombox on the cover is not decoration either, this is music mixed to sound correct out of a single plastic speaker on a street.","It sold strongly and established both an artist and a label. Critics noticed that a sixteen-year-old could carry an album alone, which nobody had proved. LL's later move into ballads and pop divides opinion about his legacy, but the argument that he was the first great solo technician on the microphone usually starts with this record.","Start with Rock the Bells, at volume, and pay attention to the drum machine's decay: the sound is as much room as hit. Then follow the voice alone, noting how he changes speed within a single verse. Compare with Run-D.M.C.'s debut, where two voices do what one is doing here."],"ll-cool-j-mama-said-knock-you-out":["The title track opens with a shouted warning not to call it a comeback and then never lets go: a stuttering drum loop, a low horn stab, and a voice pushed to the edge of hoarseness. Around it the album alternates snarling battle records like The Boomin' System and Murdergram with slower turns such as Around the Way Girl, where charm replaces threat. Marley Marl's drums are wetter and heavier than the dry Def Jam sound LL began with.","LL's previous album had been badly received and he had been booed by a hometown crowd, an unusual public humiliation for an artist still in his early twenties. He went to Marley Marl, the Queensbridge producer whose sampled and layered drums had reshaped New York rap. The competition by 1990 included Rakim, KRS-One and Public Enemy, and a reputation built in 1985 counted for nothing.","The title is literal: it comes from his grandmother, who told him to knock them out when he complained about being written off. The vocal on the title track is deliberately kept slightly out of control, with breaths and cracks left in, where his earlier records were clean. Notice that the hook is not sung at all, it is shouted, which was still unusual for a lead single.","It restored him commercially and critically and won a Grammy, and the title track became shorthand for any return from the dead. Critics who had filed him under teen novelty had to revise. The album is long and uneven, and the ballads still divide listeners, but nobody argues about the first three minutes.","Begin with the title track and listen for the moment the voice tears. Then play The Boomin' System for Marley Marl's drums, which are the real story: dense, compressed, with the room baked in. Set it beside Radio from five years earlier to hear how much the technology of sampling changed what a producer could do."],"public-enemy-yo-bum-rush-the-show":["Slower and heavier than the rap around it, with a booming low end and guitar that snarls rather than riffs. Chuck D's baritone is already fully formed, unhurried and declamatory; Flavor Flav circles him with yelps and interjections that turn statements into arguments. You're Gonna Get Yours is a car record with menace in it, Public Enemy No. 1 sets out the group's name and stance, Timebomb ticks. The album moves at a deliberate, bulldozing pace.","Chuck D had been a graphic design student and radio DJ on Long Island, older than most rappers and initially reluctant to sign. Def Jam wanted the voice. The group arrived with an apparatus already designed: a logo of a figure in a rifle sight, a security detail called the S1Ws who drilled on stage, and a clock the size of a dinner plate around Flavor Flav's neck. None of it was conventional showbusiness.","The tempos are notably slower than contemporary hip-hop, a decision the Bomb Squad reversed completely on the next record; hearing them side by side shows a production team working out what it wanted. Listen for how Flavor Flav is mixed, usually slightly off-centre and behind Chuck, so his comments sound like heckling from the wings rather than a second lead.","Reviews were respectful rather than excited, and sales were modest; the consensus was that something formidable had arrived but had not yet gone off. That judgment still holds, which is why the record is more often cited than played. Its reputation improved once critics went back to find where the group's political framing began.","Play Public Enemy No. 1 for the voice alone, which is already the most authoritative in rap. Notice the weight of the low end on a good system. Then go straight to It Takes a Nation of Millions and hear the tempo jump by a third and the samples multiply; the same group, one year, an entirely different noise."],"public-enemy-it-takes-a-nation-of-millions":["A wall of sirens, shrieking saxophone loops, scratching and hard drums, faster than any rap album before it. Chuck D declaims over the top in a voice pitched to carry across a stadium; Flavor Flav interrupts, mocks and occasionally takes a whole track. Bring the Noise, Don't Believe the Hype and Rebel Without a Pause come at you almost without gaps. Black Steel in the Hour of Chaos slows everything to a lone piano loop and tells a prison-escape story.","The Bomb Squad, Hank Shocklee, Keith Shocklee, Eric Sadler and Chuck D, built the tracks by layering dozens of short samples until the result behaved like distortion. Sampling technology had just become affordable and clearance law had not caught up, so a producer could use forty sources on one track. The politics came from Chuck's reading of Black nationalism, aimed at an audience that mainstream radio was not serving.","The album opens with a live recording made at the Hammersmith Odeon in London, so the first thing you hear is a British crowd. The screaming horn loop that powers Rebel Without a Pause comes from The Grunt by The J.B.'s, pitched up until it is nearly unbearable. The piano under Black Steel is lifted from Isaac Hayes's Hyperbolicsyllabicsesquedalymistic, on Hot Buttered Soul.","It was received almost immediately as a landmark and has stayed near the top of critical lists ever since. The controversies are separate from the music: Professor Griff's antisemitic statements the following year, and the argument over whether the group's rhetoric survives close reading. Producers point out that no record like this can legally be made now, because clearing its samples would be impossible.","Listen to Rebel Without a Pause once for the sax loop and once for everything underneath it. Then play Black Steel for contrast, a single piano and a story. If you know Reich's Different Trains, made the same year from sampled speech, play them in sequence: two very different people discovering that recorded voice is musical material."],"public-enemy-fight-the-power":["A marching drum loop, layers of horn and guitar fragments stacked until they buzz, and Chuck D calling out over the top with Flavor Flav answering. The chorus is a chant rather than a melody. Midway through, the verses turn from general defiance to specific targets, dismissing Elvis Presley and John Wayne as heroes who were never theirs. A saxophone wails across the last section. Five minutes that never stop accelerating in feel, though the tempo never changes.","Spike Lee asked the group for an anthem for Do the Right Thing, a film about a Brooklyn block on the hottest day of the year that ends in a police killing and a riot. In the film it blasts from a character's boombox for most of the running time, becoming the thing the argument is about. It arrived in a summer of high-profile racial violence in New York.","The title is taken from the Isley Brothers' 1975 song of the same name, a soul record with the same refusal in it. Branford Marsalis's saxophone was played live into the collage rather than sampled. Listen to how the Bomb Squad tune their loops slightly against each other so the texture beats and shimmers; nothing in the track is quite in tune with anything else, and that friction is deliberate.","The Elvis and John Wayne lines drew furious responses and are still the most quoted in the group's catalogue; Chuck D's defence was that he was describing whose heroes they were, not denying their talent. The song has since been used at protests across four decades, which some read as vindication and others as domestication. It remains the group's most widely known recording.","Watch the opening credits of Do the Right Thing first, then listen to the track alone and notice how much it still implies a street. Focus on the saxophone entry near the end. Then play the Isley Brothers' song of the same title to hear a demand made in 1975 in a completely different voice."],"public-enemy-fear-of-a-black-planet":["Twenty tracks, many under two minutes, running into each other with radio chatter and snatches of speech between them, so the record plays like a broadcast rather than a sequence of songs. 911 Is a Joke is Flavor Flav alone, funny and furious about ambulances that do not come. Welcome to the Terrordome is the densest thing the Bomb Squad ever built. Burn Hollywood Burn brings in guests to attack film casting. Fight the Power closes it.","The group made it while under sustained attack, following Professor Griff's antisemitic remarks to a newspaper and the firing, rehiring and disbanding that followed. Chuck D's lyrics respond directly to the press coverage. Sampling lawsuits were beginning in earnest, and this is close to the last major record made under the old assumption that anything on a record could be used.","The short tracks and the linking chatter are structural: the album is designed to be heard whole, with no gaps to skip into, and several pieces make no sense on their own. Listen for how often a sample is used only for its texture, a half-second of guitar or crowd noise, rather than for a recognisable hook. Welcome to the Terrordome reportedly carries around twenty separate sources.","It sold better than its predecessor and was praised as an achievement in production even by people who objected to the lyrics, some of which, in Terrordome, were widely read as repeating the offence the group was defending itself against. Chuck D has disputed that reading. Judgment now tends to hold the production as extraordinary and the politics as a genuine and unresolved problem.","Listen to the first third without pausing, so the transitions do their work. Take 911 Is a Joke as the way in, since Flavor Flav's comic timing carries an argument the serious tracks state flatly. Then compare the density with Yo! Bum Rush the Show: three years, and the same group has gone from a groove to a collage."],"eric-b-and-rakim-eric-b-is-president":["A slow, walking bassline, a hard unhurried drum pattern, scratching at the line ends, and a voice that does not raise itself once. Rakim enters with a calm announcement that he came in the door, said it before, and never lets the volume or the tempo of his delivery vary. Where every other rapper in 1986 shouted on the beat, he speaks across it, placing rhymes inside lines rather than only at their ends.","Eric Barrier was a DJ from Queens looking for an MC; William Griffin, a teenager from Wyandanch on Long Island who had taken the name Rakim, was a saxophone player and a student of the Nation of Gods and Earths. They recorded for a tiny label before 4th & B'way picked it up. The sampler was new enough that a loop still counted as a whole production idea.","The bassline is lifted from Over Like a Fat Rat by Fonda Rae, looped almost without alteration, which is why the track breathes like a band rather than a machine. Listen to where Rakim's rhymes fall: several land in the middle of a line, so the ear hears a pattern with no obvious downbeat. He also breathes in the wrong places on purpose, cutting a phrase where a shout would have landed.","MCs recognised it instantly as a change of standard, and the years afterwards are full of rappers describing the day they first heard it. Critics were slower, because nothing about the record announces itself. It is now the usual starting point for any argument about who changed rap technique, and the B-side, My Melody, is often preferred by specialists.","Play it after any 1985 rap single and listen to the difference in volume and heart rate. Then follow only the rhymes and try to hear where they land relative to the drums. The Fonda Rae original is worth a listen straight afterwards, to hear what a full arrangement sounds like before it was reduced to a loop."],"eric-b-and-rakim-paid-in-full":["Loops of old soul and funk, cut close and left largely alone, with scratching for punctuation and a voice that stays level throughout. I Ain't No Joke and My Melody are technical displays delivered without a raised syllable. I Know You Got Soul rides a bassline with a horn stab. Eric B. Is President is included. Two instrumentals give the DJ the floor. Nothing on the record is cluttered, and the spaces are as calculated as the words.","Recorded quickly and cheaply in New York by a duo with no track record, at the point when the Akai sampler put the contents of a record collection under one pair of hands. Rakim's vocabulary comes partly from the teachings of the Nation of Gods and Earths, common in Queens and Long Island, which is why so much of the imagery is about knowledge, precision and self-command rather than money or violence.","I Know You Got Soul takes its title, hook and feel from Bobby Byrd's record of that name, and the title track's bassline comes from Dennis Edwards's Don't Look Any Further. Listen to how Rakim's syllables sit slightly behind the drum, never quite on it, which is what makes an unhurried delivery sound like pressure rather than laziness.","The album's influence was immediate among rappers and slower among critics, but it has ended up on nearly every list of the essential hip-hop records. A British remix by Coldcut, which spliced the title track with Ofra Haza's Israeli vocal, became famous and was disliked by the duo, who felt it turned their record into something else; it is now studied as an early example of the remix as authorship.","Start with I Know You Got Soul, then I Ain't No Joke, and keep an ear on the pauses: Rakim leaves more silence than anyone before him. Play Bobby Byrd afterwards to hear the soul record underneath. The Coldcut seven-minute remix is worth hearing once, as an argument the artists lost."],"eric-b-and-rakim-follow-the-leader":["Faster and busier than the debut, with more instruments and more moving parts: bass guitar, keyboard stabs, layered breaks. The title track opens with a rumbling low figure and Rakim describing a journey out past the planets. Microphone Fiend sets an addiction metaphor over a hard swinging loop; Lyrics of Fury is a display of sustained rhyming at speed. The voice is the same instrument as before, only now it has more to push against.","Success had moved the duo from an independent to a major label with a budget, and rap production was changing monthly as samplers gained memory. Rakim was by now the MC other MCs measured themselves against, which raised the technical stakes on every verse. The album arrived in the same extraordinary year as It Takes a Nation of Millions and Slick Rick's debut.","Notice how many verses have no chorus to land on: Rakim will rhyme continuously for a minute and a half, breaking only for a scratch. The title track's imagery of travelling beyond the solar system is not decoration but a running conceit about leaving competitors behind. Listen also to the bass, which is played rather than sampled on several tracks, a sign of the bigger budget.","It sold well and was widely admired, though some listeners missed the starkness of Paid in Full, and the debate about which is the better record has never resolved. Its status among rappers is undisputed: Microphone Fiend and Lyrics of Fury are among the most quoted and rewritten verses in the form.","Take Microphone Fiend first for the swing, then the title track for the sustained rhyming. Try following one verse with the lyric in front of you to see how the internal rhymes stack. Then return to Paid in Full and decide for yourself whether more production helped."],"boogie-down-productions-criminal-minded":["Cheap, hard and fast. Scott La Rock's tracks are mostly a drum machine, one loop and a scratch, and KRS-One rides them in a hectoring Jamaican-inflected sing-song that slips between rapping and toasting. South Bronx is a geography lesson delivered as a threat. The Bridge Is Over is almost nothing but a piano figure and a voice demolishing rivals by name. 9mm Goes Bang tells a shooting story over a reggae rhythm.","KRS-One was living in a Bronx shelter where Scott La Rock worked as a counsellor. The record was made for a tiny label with almost no money. It is also a battle document: Queensbridge's Juice Crew had released a record that KRS read as claiming hip-hop began in Queens, and most of this album is the answer. Four months after its release, Scott La Rock was shot dead.","The piano loop that carries The Bridge Is Over is a lift from a Billy Joel song, played at a jaunty tempo entirely at odds with the contempt in the lyric. Note how often KRS drops into a Jamaican cadence: his father was Trinidadian and he had absorbed the sound-system tradition, which is where the practice of naming and destroying an opponent on record actually comes from.","It is the founding document of the recorded rap battle, and also of the argument about whether gunplay on a record cover is reportage or marketing; the sleeve, with the pair posed with weapons, has been cited in both directions for decades. Scott La Rock's murder changed the reception overnight and pushed KRS towards the teaching persona of the next record.","Play South Bronx and The Bridge Is Over together for the full argument, then find MC Shan's record that provoked them. Listen to how little is on any of these tracks. The reggae inflection is clearest on 9mm Goes Bang, which explains more about the album's rhythm than anything else on it."],"boogie-down-productions-by-all-means-necessary":["The same lean production, now turned towards instruction. My Philosophy sets out a manifesto against rappers who say nothing, over a loping break and a wandering bassline. Stop the Violence answers the killing of Scott La Rock without naming him much. Illegal Business connects the drug trade to the people policing it. KRS-One's delivery still swings between lecture, toast and taunt, and he refers to himself throughout as the Teacher.","Made within a year of his partner's murder, for a bigger label, by a man who had decided that being the best battle rapper was not enough. Crack had transformed New York's street economy and the political response was mass incarceration. KRS began appearing at universities, an unusual move for a rapper in 1988, and started the Stop the Violence Movement with other New York artists.","The cover deliberately recreates a famous photograph of Malcolm X holding a rifle and looking out of a window, with KRS in his place; the title alters Malcolm's own phrase. Listen for how much of the album is addressed to other rappers rather than to a general audience, which is why the tone is so often corrective rather than persuasive.","It was taken as proof that hip-hop could argue rather than only assert, and it helped establish the conscious rap category that later artists have both claimed and resented. KRS-One's habit of certainty has drawn steady criticism, and some of the album's positions have aged poorly, but the willingness to change position in public is part of the record's character.","Start with My Philosophy, one of the most quotable verses in the form, and follow the argument rather than the hooks. Then play Criminal Minded from the year before to hear the same voice with a completely different purpose. Stop the Violence is best heard knowing who is absent from the record."],"de-la-soul-3-feet-high-and-rising":["Twenty-four tracks including a running game-show skit in which the group are asked absurd questions. The samples come from everywhere: a children's educational song, soft rock, French language instruction records, psychedelic soul. Three voices, none of them aggressive, talk over each other and finish each other's jokes. Me Myself and I bounces on a fat funk loop; Eye Know whistles; Say No Go turns a pop hit inside out. It is the first rap album that is funny on purpose and all the way through.","Three teenagers from Amityville on Long Island, working with Prince Paul of Stetsasonic, who produced the record as a collage and treated the studio as a place for sketches. Rap in 1989 was hardening into militancy and gangster narrative; this arrived in bright colours with flowers on the sleeve and a coined slogan, the D.A.I.S.Y. age, which the group spent the next two years denying meant they were hippies.","The chorus of The Magic Number is taken from Schoolhouse Rock's Three Is a Magic Number, a children's television song; Eye Know whistles over Steely Dan's Peg; Say No Go is built on Hall and Oates's I Can't Go for That; and Me Myself and I rides Funkadelic's Knee Deep. The Turtles sued over an unlicensed fragment, a case that helped establish that every sample must be cleared.","Reviews were rapturous and the hippie label stuck anyway, to the group's lasting irritation. Its real legacy is procedural: the Turtles settlement changed the economics of sampling permanently, and the album itself was unavailable on streaming services until 2023 because the original clearances did not cover formats nobody had imagined. A generation therefore knew it only by reputation.","Let the skits play rather than skipping them; the album is designed as a broadcast. Start with The Magic Number and Eye Know. Then try tracing three or four samples to their sources and notice that almost none are funk records, which is precisely what made this sound new. Compare with Paul's Boutique, made the same year on the opposite coast."],"de-la-soul-de-la-soul-is-dead":["Longer, darker and more elaborate than the debut, with a framing device in which a group of kids find the album on cassette and mock it between tracks. Ring Ring Ring is an exasperated comedy about answering machines and demo tapes. Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa tells a story of sexual abuse and revenge without a single joke. Prince Paul's production is denser, the humour is sourer, and the sleeve shows a broken flowerpot.","Two years of being called hippies, dressed in daisies by magazines, and expected to be cheerful had curdled. The album is partly an act of image destruction, made while the Native Tongues circle around them, A Tribe Called Quest, Jungle Brothers, Queen Latifah, was at its height. Sample clearance costs had risen sharply since the debut, which is audible in how much of the record is played rather than lifted.","The kids in the interludes eventually dismiss the tape and throw it away, which is the album's own review of itself. Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa is narrated in the ordinary comic voice used everywhere else on the record, which is what makes the ending land so badly on a first hearing; nothing in the music warns you.","Critics admired it and many fans found it exhausting at seventy-four minutes. It has risen steadily in reputation since, particularly for its willingness to break its own franchise, and Millie is now widely cited as one of the first rap narratives about abuse told from a bystander's position. The debut is better loved; this one is more often called braver.","Do not treat it as a singles record. Play the first twenty minutes with the skits intact, then Millie Pulled a Pistol on Santa on its own, twice. Afterwards return to 3 Feet High and Rising and hear how the same three voices sound when they still expect to be liked."],"a-tribe-called-quest-peoples-instinctive-travels":["Loose, bright and wandering. Q-Tip's nasal, conversational voice carries most of it, with Phife Dawg appearing only occasionally. I Left My Wallet in El Segundo is a shaggy travel story; Bonita Applebum is a slow, blatant seduction over a soft psychedelic loop; Can I Kick It? rides a famous bassline and a call-and-response the group barely bother to vary. Nothing is hurried, and the drums sit low in the mix where other records put them front and centre.","Four friends from St. Albans, Queens, still teenagers, part of the Native Tongues circle around De La Soul and the Jungle Brothers. Their label had a hit-making department and no idea what to do with an album that contained no obvious single. Sampling was still cheap enough for a group with no budget to build a record from records, though not for much longer.","Can I Kick It? is built over the bassline of Lou Reed's Walk on the Wild Side; Reed is reported to have taken the entire publishing, so the group earned nothing from their best-known early song. Listen to how thin the drums are compared with the New York records around them, a deliberate softness that the next album would reverse completely.","It was received as charming and slight, an assessment that hardened once The Low End Theory appeared and made it look like a sketch. Critics have since come back to the looseness, hearing an alternative to hardness that no other group in 1990 was offering. Bonita Applebum has drawn later scrutiny for its lyrics, which is part of any honest account of it.","Take Bonita Applebum first for the texture, then Can I Kick It? for the bassline, then Lou Reed's original. Notice how much space the mix leaves at the bottom. Play The Low End Theory afterwards and hear a group deciding that bass is the whole point."],"a-tribe-called-quest-the-low-end-theory":["Almost nothing but bass, drums and two voices. Most tracks have three or four elements at most: an upright bass figure, a dry kick and snare, a fragment of horn, and Q-Tip and Phife Dawg trading verses in completely different timbres, one nasal and reflective, the other clipped and combative. Check the Rhime is a rooftop argument between friends. Scenario ends the record with a posse of guests and the most explosive verse on it.","The group rebuilt after a debut that had been liked rather than respected, with Phife stepping up from a guest to a full partner. Engineer Bob Power gave the record a clarity unusual for hip-hop then. Jazz was the deliberate reference point, not as decoration but as a claim of lineage, at a moment when rap's commercial centre was moving towards harder and louder productions.","Ron Carter, who played bass with Miles Davis's second great quintet, plays live on Verses from the Abstract; he agreed only after being assured the lyrics would be clean. The album's mixing is the real trick: by removing mid-range clutter the producers made two voices and a bass sound bigger than a wall of samples. Scenario's closing verse belongs to Busta Rhymes, then nineteen.","It was immediately and almost unanimously praised, and has stayed on best-of lists ever since. The jazz rap label it helped create was soon overused, applied to any record with a saxophone sample, and the group disliked it. What is still discussed is how much the album's clean low end shaped the sound of nineties New York production generally.","Listen on something with real bass; the record is unintelligible on a phone speaker. Start with Buggin' Out for the interplay between the two voices, then Verses from the Abstract for Ron Carter. Then play a Miles Davis quintet record from the sixties and hear where the group thought they were standing."],"a-tribe-called-quest-midnight-marauders":["Warmer and more tuneful than its predecessor, with a synthesized female voice introducing tracks like a museum audio guide and commenting on the tempo. Award Tour is a travelogue with a bright keyboard hook; Electric Relaxation is a slow, sly seduction over a soft electric piano loop; Oh My God brings in Busta Rhymes to shout across a stuttering beat. Phife is at his sharpest here, and the two voices are balanced as equals throughout.","Recorded as New York hip-hop entered its most competitive stretch, with Wu-Tang Clan's debut appearing the same month and Nas six months away. The group were now established enough to be a reference point rather than a novelty, and the sleeve, a grid of dozens of rappers' and DJs' faces, was an explicit claim about belonging to a lineage rather than standing outside it.","The linking voice is credited as a tour guide and gives the album's sampling rate and running commentary, a small joke about the record as a piece of equipment. Listen for how the two MCs hand over mid-line rather than at verse ends, a technique they had used sparingly before and now deploy constantly. Q-Tip produced most of it himself.","It sold better than anything they had made and is the album most often named as their best by listeners who came later, while critics tend to split between this and The Low End Theory. Its reputation has grown with the years, partly because the drums and keyboard textures became the default sound of warm East Coast production for a decade.","Play Award Tour and Electric Relaxation back to back for the two moods. Then listen to how Q-Tip and Phife divide a single verse between them. If you want to hear its children, put on a mid-nineties Pete Rock or J Dilla production and count the similarities."],"beastie-boys-licensed-to-ill":["Three nasal New York voices shouting over enormous drums and metal guitar. Rhymin and Stealin opens on a thunderous rock break with a pirate chant; Paul Revere runs a drum machine pattern backwards under a tall tale about the Wild West; No Sleep till Brooklyn is a heavy metal parody that works as heavy metal; Fight for Your Right is a beer commercial that became an anthem. The record is loud, juvenile and impeccably timed.","A New York hardcore punk band turned rap group, working with Rick Rubin, who liked both genres and heard no reason to separate them. Def Jam's distribution deal with Columbia gave it a reach no rap record had had. It became the first rap album to top the American album chart, sold to suburban teenagers in enormous quantities, and turned three art-school kids into a touring scandal.","The drums under Rhymin and Stealin are Led Zeppelin's When the Levee Breaks, and the riff behind them is Black Sabbath's Sweet Leaf; Kerry King of Slayer plays the solo on No Sleep till Brooklyn. Paul Revere's beat is a drum machine pattern recorded and then reversed, which is why it lurches rather than swings.","It was enormously successful and widely deplored, and the group's own later view was that the misogyny and the frat-party persona had been a joke that the audience took literally and that they had helped make real. Adam Yauch apologised in print years afterwards. Critics still disagree about whether the record's craft can be separated from what it licensed.","Play Paul Revere for the reversed beat and the storytelling, which is the best thing here. Then Rhymin and Stealin for the sheer size of the drums. Afterwards go to Paul's Boutique and hear the same three people three years later, having decided to be something else entirely."],"beastie-boys-pauls-boutique":["A record built almost entirely out of other records, hundreds of them, layered three and four deep so that a single bar may contain a drum break, a bassline, a guitar squeal and a half-second of dialogue from four different decades. The three voices trade lines in a constant stream of references. It closes with B-Boy Bouillabaisse, a suite of nine short pieces run together, which functions as a summary of everything the album has been doing.","The group had left Def Jam after a dispute, moved to Los Angeles and signed to Capitol, a label that expected another Licensed to Ill. They worked instead with the Dust Brothers, whose existing instrumental tracks were already dense collages the group rapped over rather than trimmed. Nobody was clearing much of anything in 1989, and the window in which this was affordable closed within a year.","The drums the group rap over on The Sounds of Science come from the Beatles, and several more Beatles fragments run through the record. Listen for how often a sample is used for a single hit or breath rather than a loop, so the texture keeps flickering. The album cover is a panoramic photograph of a real Manhattan street corner, printed as a wraparound so the shop of the title only appears when the sleeve is opened out.","It sold poorly, the label effectively abandoned it, and the group were written off. Within five years it was being called one of the great sample records; within twenty it was standard on best-album lists. It is also cited as an argument against sample clearance law, since a record of this density could not be licensed today at any realistic price.","Do not try to identify everything on a first listen; let the density be the experience. Start with Shake Your Rump, then Shadrach. Afterwards, one of the many published sample maps will double the record. If you know Berio's Sinfonia, play its quotation-stuffed third movement next and enjoy the family resemblance."],"slick-rick-the-great-adventures-of-slick-rick":["An English accent, several character voices, and a set of stories told in the third person with a moral at the end. Children's Story follows a robbery from bravado to a body in the street in three minutes, the narrator speaking as if reading to a child at bedtime. Mona Lisa is a comic pick-up scene. Hey Young World is an address to listeners younger than the narrator. The production is light and springy, and everything serves the telling.","Richard Walters was born in London and moved to the Bronx as a child, which is why he sounds like nobody else on a rap record. He had become famous in 1985 as MC Ricky D on a single with Doug E. Fresh before signing to Def Jam. Rap in 1988 was dominated by political declamation and technical display; a storyteller in gold chains and an eyepatch was a different proposition entirely.","He performs the whole cast: victim, robber, police, bystander, shifting register mid-line, which is why the stories work without any narration telling you who is speaking. Children's Story ends on the narrator's voice returning to the bedtime frame, so the violence is bracketed by a lullaby. The eyepatch covers an eye blinded in infancy by broken glass; it is not a costume choice.","It sold well and made him one of rap's most imitated voices, and Children's Story has been quoted, sampled and remade continuously since. His conviction and imprisonment in the early nineties, and a long immigration fight afterwards, interrupted the career at its peak, which has made the album feel like a complete statement by default. Critics now treat him as the form's foundational narrator.","Listen to Children's Story once for the story and once for the voices, counting the characters. Then Mona Lisa for the comedy. If you want to hear where this belongs in a longer tradition, play a narrative ballad like Schubert's Erlkonig, where one voice also plays every part in a chase that ends badly."],"slick-rick-la-di-da-di":["One voice rapping and one mouth making the drums. There is no band, no sampler and no turntable: Doug E. Fresh supplies the rhythm entirely with his mouth while Slick Rick, then billed as MC Ricky D, narrates a morning in the life of a young man who wakes, bathes, dresses, and is pursued down the street by a woman and her mother. It is funny, unhurried, and five minutes long with no chorus.","Released as the flip side of The Show on a small New York label, and an instant street hit. Human beatboxing was a live technique born of not owning equipment, and by 1985 it was a recognised discipline with its own stars. The pairing of a London-raised storyteller with the most celebrated beatboxer in New York was arranged by their label rather than by long friendship.","Listen to how Doug E. Fresh's beat changes to follow the story, dropping out for a punchline and thickening when the chase starts; the drum part is a performance, not a loop. Rick's use of a sung fragment as a refrain, and his habit of voicing the women's dialogue in a different register, both become standard practice in rap storytelling afterwards.","It is among the most sampled, quoted and directly recreated records in hip-hop, borrowed in full by later artists and used as the basis for hit singles. Critics at the time treated it as a novelty because of the beatboxing. Later writing has taken it more seriously, as the record where rap narrative and rap comedy arrive fully formed with no production to lean on.","Listen once for the story and once for the mouth drums alone, ignoring the words. Then find Snoop Dogg's version from 1993, which keeps the structure and changes almost nothing else, to hear how completely later rappers absorbed it. Any decent remaster is fine; the original was recorded cheaply and sounds it."],"dr-dre-the-chronic":["Dr. Dre's first solo album is an hour of slow, rolling grooves built from seventies funk, rebuilt rather than simply looped: session players replay bass lines and keyboard parts, and a high, whining Moog lead floats over the top like a siren heard from a car window. A young Snoop Doggy Dogg is on most of the tracks, his drawl as much a part of the sound as the synthesizer. Skits and interludes stitch the songs into a single long ride through Los Angeles.","Dre had left N.W.A and Ruthless Records in a bitter contract dispute and co-founded Death Row Records with Suge Knight; no major label would touch the venture until Interscope agreed to distribute it. The album appeared in December 1992, months after the Los Angeles riots that followed the Rodney King verdict, and it carried the city's anger and its hedonism side by side. Several tracks are aimed squarely at Dre's former partner Eazy-E.","Listen to how little is actually sampled. Where the East Coast chopped records, Dre hired musicians to replay Parliament-Funkadelic and Leon Haywood figures, which gave him cleaner bass, room to change keys and fewer legal headaches. The chanted hook of 'Let Me Ride' is an interpolation of Parliament's 'Mothership Connection (Star Child)'. The portamento synth line, not the drums, is the true signature: it recurs across the record like a leitmotif.","The Chronic sold millions, won Dre a Grammy for 'Let Me Ride', and moved hip-hop's commercial centre from New York to California for several years. Critics praised its sound almost without reservation; its content, heavy with guns, weed and contempt for women, drew the same objections that followed gangsta rap generally. Later writers treat it as the founding document of G-funk and as a studio achievement that producers still study.","Start with 'Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang' and 'Let Me Ride' back to back and listen to the bass, which sits lower and rounder than anything on an East Coast record of the same year. Then follow the Moog whine through the album. Play it loud in a car if you can: it was mixed for exactly that. Afterwards try Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters for the synth-funk ancestry."],"dr-dre-nuthin-but-a-g-thang":["A four-minute duet in which Dr. Dre and Snoop Doggy Dogg trade verses over an unhurried, sunlit groove: a lazy electric-piano figure, a deep bass, handclaps and that high synthesizer whistle. Snoop's voice arrives first, conversational and sly, almost sung, and Dre answers in a flatter, harder tone. There is no dramatic build; the song simply rolls, which is the point. It is a party record about cruising through a Los Angeles afternoon.","Released as the lead single from The Chronic in late 1992, it introduced most listeners to Snoop, then an unknown from Long Beach. Its video, a backyard barbecue in South Central, sold a vision of the neighbourhood that had been on the television news for riot footage a few months earlier. The single became a top-ten pop hit and carried G-funk onto radio stations that had never played West Coast rap.","The groove is built on Leon Haywood's 1975 soul record 'I Want'a Do Something Freaky to You', slowed and smoothed until the original's lounge-soul keyboard sounds like it is melting in the heat. Listen for how Snoop places his syllables behind the beat, a fraction late, which makes everything sound relaxed even when the words are not. That rhythmic placement became as influential as the production.","It was an immediate hit and has never really left rotation; critics' lists of the greatest hip-hop singles almost always include it near the top. The debate it prompted was less about the music than about what its easygoing tone normalised. Musicians tend to single out the chemistry between the two voices, the rapper as bandleader and the protégé as soloist.","Listen once for Snoop alone: the pitch of his voice rises and falls almost like a melody. Then listen for the bass, which never hurries. Compare Grandmaster Flash's 'The Message' to hear how much the sound of a street record changed in a decade, and follow it with Snoop's own Doggystyle."],"dr-dre-2001":["Dre's second solo album trades the lazy funk of The Chronic for something colder and more monumental: stark piano riffs, orchestral stabs, tight live bass and drums so crisp they sound carved. 'Still D.R.E.' rests on a three-note piano figure that everyone recognises in two seconds; 'The Next Episode' swings on a spy-film guitar and horn; 'Forgot About Dre' is a rapid-fire showcase for Eminem. Guests carry much of the rapping while Dre acts as the architect.","By 1999 Dre had left Death Row, founded Aftermath, and seen a first compilation flop badly enough that critics wondered whether he was finished. He had also just signed and produced Eminem. 2001 was his answer, recorded with a team of co-producers and keyboard players at a time when digital recording was replacing tape, which helped give the album its unusually clean, separated sound.","'The Next Episode' is built on David McCallum's 1967 instrumental 'The Edge', a piece of orchestral library-funk by the actor best known from The Man from U.N.C.L.E. Listen too for how much of the album is played rather than sampled, with bass and keyboards recorded live and then quantised. Jay-Z helped write Dre's verses on 'Still D.R.E.', a fact that was barely hidden at the time.","The album sold enormously and re-established Dre as the most sought-after producer in rap, a position he held for the next decade. Reviewers praised the sound and were cooler about the words, much of it ghostwritten and often crude. Its piano-led minimalism shaped much commercial rap of the early 2000s, from 50 Cent to Eminem's own records.","Put on 'Still D.R.E.' and count how few elements there are: piano, drums, bass, voice. That restraint is the style. Then hear 'The Next Episode' for the swagger, and compare both with The Chronic to hear how the same producer moved from warm to steely in seven years."],"snoop-dogg-doggystyle":["Snoop Doggy Dogg's debut, produced by Dr. Dre, takes The Chronic's sound and makes it looser, sleepier and more playful. The grooves are soft Parliament-style funk, heavy on bass and handclaps, with sung hooks from the Death Row family. 'Gin and Juice' is a lazy house-party anthem, 'Who Am I (What's My Name)?' a self-introduction over a funk chant, and 'Lodi Dodi' a loving remake of an old New York story rap. Snoop's voice, languid and nasal, holds it together.","The album came out in November 1993 amid enormous anticipation and entered the album chart at number one, unheard of for a debut rap record then. Snoop was simultaneously facing a murder charge over a shooting in Los Angeles, of which he was acquitted in 1996, and the case hung over the album's reception. Death Row was at its peak of commercial power and notoriety.","'Lodi Dodi' is a near line-for-line remake of Slick Rick's 1985 'La Di Da Di', with Snoop adjusting the story to his own world: an unusual act of homage in a genre that prized originality. 'Who Am I (What's My Name)?' borrows the chant and bass of George Clinton's 'Atomic Dog', and the whole album's sense of humour owes much to Clinton's cartoon funk.","Reviewers loved Snoop's voice and found the music, sometimes, too close to The Chronic. Its misogyny was criticised then and is harder to ignore now. Yet the record remains one of the best-selling rap debuts and is usually ranked alongside The Chronic as the core of G-funk. Snoop's relaxed delivery influenced a generation of rappers who realised you did not have to shout.","Start with 'Gin and Juice' and 'Lodi Dodi', then play Slick Rick's 'La Di Da Di' right after to hear how a Long Beach teenager reworked a Bronx classic. Listen for the hooks sung by guest vocalists: this album brought soul singing back into gangsta rap."],"2pac-me-against-the-world":["2Pac's third album is his most inward: an hour of slow, mournful soul-based beats over which he talks about fear, grief, depression, poverty and the likelihood of an early death. There are party moments, but the tone is set by 'So Many Tears', 'If I Die 2Nite' and 'Dear Mama', a tribute to his mother. His voice is urgent and hoarse, pushing slightly ahead of the beat, as if he were short of time.","Recorded in 1994, a year in which 2Pac was shot five times in a New York studio lobby and convicted on a sexual abuse charge, the album was released in March 1995 while he was in prison. It went to number one on the album chart, the first time an artist had topped it while incarcerated. His paranoia about who had set up the shooting would soon fuel his feud with the Notorious B.I.G.","Listen to how often the beats reach for gospel and quiet-storm soul rather than funk: the producers were building a church-like frame around a confession. The title track's theme of isolation is answered, deliberately, by 'Dear Mama' and 'Temptations', where he turns outward to family and desire. The album is sequenced as a mood cycle rather than a set of singles.","Critics at the time called it his best and most mature record, and many still do, preferring it to the louder All Eyez on Me. It is often cited as the moment gangsta rap admitted to its own despair. Debate continues over how much its introspection was art and how much a man in crisis, and over whether the two can be separated.","Play it from the start, in order, preferably at night. Notice how the tempo rarely rises and how often a sung chorus relieves the rapping. Then compare Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, another soul record about fear and faith that turns private grief into public testimony."],"2pac-dear-mama":["A thank-you letter from a son to his mother, told in three verses over a warm, slow soul groove with a sung refrain. 2Pac describes growing up poor with a single parent, his own adolescent anger, her addiction and her recovery, and his gratitude, without softening any of it. The music is gentle, glowing electric piano and a sighing chorus, which makes the frankness of the words all the more striking.","His mother, Afeni Shakur, had been a member of the Black Panther Party and was acquitted in the Panther 21 trial while pregnant with him. The single came out early in 1995, just before Me Against the World, while 2Pac was about to go to prison, and it became one of the biggest hits of his career. It gave a public that knew him from headlines a very different picture.","The groove borrows from Joe Sample's 'In All My Wildest Dreams', a jazz-soul piano piece, and the Spinners' 'Sadie', itself a soul song addressed to a mother. So the sample is a quiet quotation: a son's tribute built on an older tribute. Listen too for how the verses move chronologically, from childhood to the present, like a short memoir.","It was widely praised on release, even by critics hostile to 2Pac's other work, and in 2010 the Library of Congress added it to the National Recording Registry, a rare honour for a rap single. Some listeners find it sentimental; most hear it as a model of how hip-hop could handle family love and damage in the same breath.","Listen for the moment each verse turns from the hard fact to the gratitude. Then play the Spinners' 'Sadie' to hear the source, and the Temptations' 'Papa Was a Rollin' Stone', a darker soul song about a parent, as a companion piece."],"2pac-california-love":["A celebration of the state, not a love song: a booming piano riff, a stomping beat, and Roger Troutman's robotic talk-box voice singing the title hook. Dr. Dre opens with a verse and 2Pac follows with the victory lap. It is loud, blunt and joyful, built for stadiums and car stereos rather than close listening, and one of the most recognisable openings in nineties pop.","This was 2Pac's first single after Suge Knight posted his bail and signed him to Death Row in late 1995, and it announced the label's new star. Its video, directed by Hype Williams, staged the two rappers in a post-apocalyptic desert modelled on Mad Max. The feud between Death Row and New York's Bad Boy Records was intensifying, and the song's West Coast pride was read as a salvo.","The piano figure comes from Joe Cocker's 1972 'Woman to Woman'. The talk-box voice belongs to Roger Troutman of Zapp, whose electro-funk records were a crucial ingredient in G-funk; bringing him in was Dre's way of acknowledging the debt. Two versions exist: the original single and a darker remix on All Eyez on Me.","It topped the Billboard Hot 100 in 1996 and was nominated for Grammys; it is now a staple of California sporting events and a regular entry on best-of-decade lists. Critics tend to rate it as a thrilling record rather than a deep one. It marks the moment G-funk became mainstream pop music.","Wait for the talk-box and notice how it turns a human voice into a synthesizer, the very sound that links Zapp's funk to Kraftwerk's robots. Compare the single mix with the album remix, then hear Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for the electro strand in the family tree."],"2pac-all-eyez-on-me":["A double album of more than two hours, recorded at speed after 2Pac's release from prison. It swings between party records such as '2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted' with Snoop Doggy Dogg, the wistful 'I Ain't Mad at Cha', the triumphant 'Ambitionz az a Ridah' and many tracks of defiance aimed at enemies real and imagined. The production, by Dr. Dre, Johnny J, Daz Dillinger and others, is glossy G-funk throughout.","Released in February 1996 as one of the first double albums of solo rap, it fulfilled much of 2Pac's Death Row contract in one go. He recorded compulsively, sometimes several songs a night. By September he had been shot in Las Vegas and died six days later, at 25, which turned the album into a last will almost overnight. The East–West rivalry was at its most toxic.","'I Ain't Mad at Cha' was recorded, by most accounts, on the very day he left prison, and its video, released after his death, shows him as a ghost looking down on his friends. Listen to how many tracks imagine his own funeral or legacy: the premonitions on this album are not hindsight, they were already there.","Commercially enormous and certified diamond, it was received as a blockbuster rather than a masterwork: critics admired the energy and grumbled about length and repetition. Opinion has moved in its favour, partly through nostalgia, partly because its best tracks have lasted. Arguments continue over whether Me Against the World or this is his defining statement.","Do not attempt it in one sitting at first. Start with 'Ambitionz az a Ridah', 'I Ain't Mad at Cha' and '2 of Amerikaz Most Wanted', three different 2Paks. Then play Me Against the World to hear the private man before the public persona took over."],"notorious-big-ready-to-die":["Biggie Smalls's debut is structured as a life story: an opening skit takes him from birth through childhood to prison release, the album passes through crime stories, bedroom comedy and radio hits, and it ends with a suicide note set to music. In between are 'Juicy', 'Big Poppa', 'Gimme the Loot' and 'Things Done Changed'. His voice, rich and unhurried, glides over the beats with conversational ease and an effortless sense of rhythm.","Christopher Wallace grew up in Clinton Hill and Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn, sold drugs as a teenager and was signed by Sean 'Puffy' Combs, first at Uptown Records and then at Bad Boy, the label Combs founded. Released in September 1994, the album pulled New York rap back toward the charts at a moment when G-funk dominated. Producers included Easy Mo Bee, DJ Premier and Combs's own team.","The album is split between two philosophies. Hard, dusty tracks like 'Gimme the Loot', where Biggie voices two characters by changing his pitch, sit next to glossy hits built on well-known R&B: 'Big Poppa' is set on the Isley Brothers' 'Between the Sheets'. Combs pushed for the hits; the tension between street record and pop record is audible, and it works.","Critics recognised immediately that Biggie was an exceptional technician and storyteller, and the album is routinely ranked among the best in hip-hop. The darker tracks were criticised for their violence; the closing track's portrait of depression was, for its time, remarkably frank. After his murder in 1997 the album's title and ending took on a meaning that has shaped every listening since.","Listen to 'Gimme the Loot' for the two-voice trick, then 'Juicy' for the rags-to-riches arc, then 'Everyday Struggle' for the weariness underneath. Compare Slick Rick, whose storytelling and character voices Biggie clearly admired. Then hear it as a whole, from the birth skit to the final track, because the sequencing is the story: a life laid out in order, with its ending announced in the title."],"notorious-big-juicy":["A rags-to-riches autobiography in three verses: the child who read rap magazines and taped radio shows, the young man hustling to pay the rent, the star who can now buy his mother a house. The groove is bright and gliding, a sampled early-eighties R&B keyboard line with a sung chorus. Biggie delivers it with a grin, dedicating the song at the start to everyone who doubted him.","Released in August 1994 as the lead single from Ready to Die, it was Sean Combs's calculated attempt to put a hardcore Brooklyn rapper on pop radio, and it worked. The song is also a love letter to hip-hop's own history, name-checking the magazines and pioneers of Biggie's childhood. It became the anthem of a new, upwardly mobile era in rap.","The sample is Mtume's 1983 hit 'Juicy Fruit', a slow electro-soul song by a band led by James Mtume, who had played percussion with Miles Davis in the electric period. So a jazz lineage runs underneath one of rap's biggest pop moments. Listen for Biggie's internal rhymes, which chain several sounds within a single line.","It was a hit and has become one of the most loved rap songs of all: frequently ranked at or near the top of lists of the best hip-hop singles, and heard at graduations and weddings. Some purists at the time thought it too polished; that argument has long since lost. Its narrative arc became a standard hip-hop form.","Follow the three verses as three chapters. Then play Mtume's 'Juicy Fruit' and hear how little was changed, and how much the new words transform it. Pair it with 2Pac's 'Dear Mama', released months later, for the two coasts' great songs of gratitude."],"notorious-big-life-after-death":["A sprawling double album that shows how wide Biggie's range had become: the pop-disco of 'Mo Money Mo Problems', the swaggering 'Hypnotize', the hard New York of 'Kick in the Door', the Midwestern double-time flow borrowed for 'Notorious Thugs' with Bone Thugs-n-Harmony, and West Coast gestures on 'Going Back to Cali'. Between them come crime narratives told with a novelist's control. It opens where Ready to Die ended.","Wallace was shot dead in Los Angeles on 9 March 1997; the album, already finished and titled, came out sixteen days later. Its release in the aftermath of both his and 2Pac's murders made it a national event. It went to number one and sold in huge numbers. Combs's Bad Boy was then the dominant commercial force in rap. Its title, chosen before his death, now reads as grim prophecy.","'Hypnotize' is built on Herb Alpert's 1979 instrumental 'Rise', and 'Mo Money Mo Problems' on Diana Ross's 'I'm Coming Out': proof of Combs's taste for recognisable hits. Listen to 'Notorious Thugs' for the real feat, as Biggie switches from his usual slow glide to the rapid, sung triplet flow of his Cleveland guests without losing clarity.","Reviewers praised the virtuosity and complained about length and filler, a pattern with double albums. Its reputation has grown, particularly for tracks like 'Somebody's Gotta Die' and 'I Got a Story to Tell', which show a storyteller at full stretch. Debate over whether it or Ready to Die is the greater record has no settled answer.","Try 'Hypnotize', 'Notorious Thugs' and 'I Got a Story to Tell' first: three different skills. Then listen to 'Kick in the Door', produced by DJ Premier, to hear him on classic New York ground. Compare 2Pac's All Eyez on Me, the other coast's double album."],"nas-illmatic":["Ten tracks in forty minutes, with no filler: a portrait of the Queensbridge housing projects in Queens by a twenty-year-old poet. Nas's rhymes are dense with internal rhyme and precise images, delivered in a cool, almost detached voice. The beats, by DJ Premier, Pete Rock, Large Professor, Q-Tip and L.E.S., are built from jazz piano and soul fragments over hard, dusty drums. 'N.Y. State of Mind', 'The World Is Yours' and 'Life's a Bitch' are the core.","Nas had been a prodigy since a guest verse on Main Source's 1991 'Live at the Barbeque'; Columbia signed him and assembled the best producers in New York for his debut. Released in April 1994, it earned a perfect rating from The Source. It sold modestly at first, overshadowed by West Coast and Wu-Tang success, but its reputation among rappers was immediate.","Nas's father, the jazz cornettist Olu Dara, plays the melancholy solo at the end of 'Life's a Bitch', a direct link between the album and the jazz it samples. The opening track uses audio from the 1983 film Wild Style, planting Illmatic in hip-hop's own origins. Listen to how each producer's style shifts the mood while Nas's voice stays constant.","Almost immediately it became the benchmark: the record that critics, rappers and fans cite as the perfect hip-hop album, and it is usually ranked at or near the top of lists of the genre's greatest. The only real debate is whether its perfection trapped Nas, whose later records were always measured against it. Few debuts have been dissected so closely, line by line, by later rappers.","Listen straight through; forty minutes is short. Start by following just the words on 'N.Y. State of Mind', then just the drums. Compare Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full, the model for Nas's style, and then try Ahmad Jamal, whose piano underpins 'The World Is Yours'."],"nas-the-world-is-yours":["The most hopeful song on Illmatic. Over a looped, rippling jazz piano phrase and hard drums, Nas reflects on ambition, the pressures of the projects and the possibility of rising above them, answering the idea of the title with a question about who the world belongs to. The chorus is a call and response. It is reflective rather than boastful, and the piano gives it a sunlit, wistful glow.","The title comes from the 1983 film Scarface, whose rise-and-fall gangster story was an obsession of nineties rap. Produced by Pete Rock, one of the great crate-diggers of the era, it was released as a single from Illmatic in 1994. Pete Rock's own records with CL Smooth had already shown how jazz and soul fragments could carry emotional weight in hip-hop.","The piano loop comes from Ahmad Jamal's 'I Love Music', a 1970 live performance, lifted from the introduction where Jamal is spinning out a free, rhapsodic figure. Pete Rock trims it to a few bars that seem to breathe. Listen for how the loop's slight irregularity, a live pianist's rubato, makes the track feel less mechanical than most sampled music of the time.","It was not a big hit, but it is among the most admired tracks on the album and among Pete Rock's finest productions. Critics often use it to explain why Illmatic is loved: its mix of hardness and tenderness. It is regularly cited in discussions of jazz's second life inside hip-hop.","Listen once only to the piano: it never quite repeats identically. Then find Ahmad Jamal's live recordings, starting with 'Poinciana', to hear the pianist whose space and restraint made him Miles Davis's favourite and, twenty years later, a producer's treasure."],"wu-tang-clan-enter-the-wu-tang":["Nine rappers from Staten Island on one raw, murky record produced by RZA. The beats sound as though they were made in a basement, and they were: grimy drums, out-of-tune piano loops, soul fragments and snatches of dialogue from kung fu films. Each member has a distinct voice, from Method Man's smoky charm to Ol' Dirty Bastard's howling eccentricity to GZA's precise cool. 'Protect Ya Neck', 'C.R.E.A.M.' and 'Da Mystery of Chessboxin'' are the landmarks.","RZA assembled the group in 1992 with a five-year plan: the collective would make its name, then each member would sign solo deals with different labels while remaining in the group. The independent single 'Protect Ya Neck' led to a deal with Loud Records, and the album appeared in November 1993. Its low-budget rawness stood against the polished sound of Los Angeles.","The kung fu dialogue comes largely from the 1983 Hong Kong film Shaolin and Wu Tang; the group's whole mythology, of chambers, disciplines and rival schools, is borrowed from those films and from the teachings of the Five Percent Nation. Listen for RZA's detuned samples: he often let loops drift slightly out of pitch, creating the album's queasy, haunted feel.","Initial sales were steady rather than spectacular, but its influence was immense: it revived the gritty New York sound, launched a string of classic solo albums, and made the business model of the collective famous. It regularly appears near the top of lists of hip-hop's greatest albums. Critics now treat RZA as one of the genre's great auteurs.","Start with 'Protect Ya Neck' and try to tell the nine voices apart; learning them is half the pleasure. Then 'C.R.E.A.M.' for the soul side. Afterwards, the solo albums: GZA's Liquid Swords and Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx. Listen on headphones for the hiss and grit left deliberately in the mix."],"wu-tang-clan-cream":["Two long verses of memory and a chorus that became a slogan. Raekwon recalls a teenage life of petty crime and poverty, Inspectah Deck a youth that led to prison and the lessons drawn from it, and Method Man delivers the hook about money ruling everything. The beat is a slow, melancholy soul piano loop with a mournful sung fragment, under hard drums. It sounds like a lament, not a boast.","The title is an acronym about cash, and the song was released as a single in early 1994. Where much rap of the time celebrated money, Wu-Tang presented it as a trap that shaped every choice in the Park Hill and Stapleton projects of Staten Island. The song was widely heard as a sober account of the crack era's economics.","The piano and wordless vocal come from the Charmels' 'As Long as I've Got You', a little-known 1967 Stax soul record. RZA loops only a few bars, pitch-shifted, so the source sounds like a ghost. Listen to how different the two verses are in pace: Raekwon storytelling, Inspectah Deck compressing more syllables into each line.","It is one of the most admired singles in hip-hop, often ranked among the best of the decade, and its acronym entered everyday speech. Critics note that its strength lies in its restraint: there is no triumph at the end. It is frequently used to illustrate the social realism of nineties New York rap.","Listen for the piano first; it never changes, and the song's sadness lives there. Then concentrate on Inspectah Deck's verse, often called the finest on the track. Compare Grandmaster Flash's 'The Message', the first great rap song about the projects."],"gza-liquid-swords":["GZA's second album, produced by RZA, is the darkest and most austere of the Wu-Tang solo records: slow, heavy beats, minor-key organ and piano, eerie synthesizer drones and swathes of samurai-film dialogue. GZA raps in a calm, measured voice, a chess player rather than a brawler, with dense metaphor and wordplay. 'Liquid Swords', 'Cold World', 'Shadowboxin'' and '4th Chamber' are high points; nearly every Clan member appears.","GZA, the oldest member of the Clan, had recorded a failed debut for Cold Chillin' in 1991. Released on Geffen in November 1995 under the collective's solo-deal strategy, this album came in the same run as Method Man's, Ol' Dirty Bastard's and Raekwon's solo debuts. The period was the peak of Wu-Tang's creative and commercial power. RZA was producing several of these albums at once, yet each has its own colour.","The long opening speech and other dialogue come from Shogun Assassin, a 1980 English-language re-edit of the Japanese Lone Wolf and Cub films, and its story of a father and son wandering through violence frames the album. The cover, by comic artist Denys Cowan, shows a chess game played with swords. Listen for the chess metaphors, which run through the lyrics like a hidden structure.","Reviews were strong and its standing has only risen; many fans and critics rank it as the best Wu-Tang solo album and among the best hip-hop records of the decade. GZA's reputation as the Clan's most cerebral lyricist rests on it. Some listeners find it forbidding; its admirers hear that as the point.","Play it at night and let the film dialogue set the scene. Focus on RZA's use of silence and space. Then compare Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx from the same year: one record wintry and philosophical, the other a crime film."],"raekwon-only-built-4-cuban-linx":["A crime film in album form. Raekwon and his partner Ghostface Killah, effectively co-lead, tell interlocking stories of dealing, betrayal and the dream of getting out, with cinematic skits and RZA beats that use lush soul strings and orchestral samples more than on any earlier Wu-Tang record. The language is a thick, coded slang. 'Criminology', 'Incarcerated Scarfaces', 'Ice Cream' and 'Guillotine (Swordz)' are among the landmarks.","Released in August 1995 on the original purple cassette that gave the album its nickname, it arrived as the Wu-Tang solo wave peaked. Its gangster-cinema imagery, drawn from Scarface, The Godfather and Hong Kong action films, helped launch the so-called mafioso wave in New York rap, with Nas, Jay-Z and others soon adopting similar personas. RZA produced every track himself.","Listen to how often the strings and horns sound like soundtrack music, not funk; RZA was building a score as much as a beat. The album's slang, full of Five Percent vocabulary and invented terms, was difficult even for American listeners, and part of the pleasure is decoding it. Ghostface's abstract, stream-of-consciousness verses would soon grow into his own solo style.","Hailed on release and now consistently ranked among the finest rap albums, it is often called the pinnacle of the Wu-Tang solo catalogue. Its influence on the mafioso style of the later nineties was enormous. Critics debate whether that influence was entirely healthy, since imitators took the glamour without the storytelling.","Listen to 'Criminology' and 'Ice Cream' first, then play the album through as if watching a film. Compare Curtis Mayfield's Super Fly and Isaac Hayes's Shaft, the soul soundtracks behind the crime-cinema imagination this record inherited. Notice how the skits work like scenes between songs, and how often Ghostface steals the spotlight."],"mobb-deep-the-infamous":["Queensbridge seen from street level by two teenagers: Prodigy and Havoc rap about survival, paranoia and violence in flat, cold voices, with none of the glamour of the mafioso records. The beats, mostly by Havoc, are sparse and frightening: detuned piano, eerie jazz fragments, heavy drums with space around them. 'Shook Ones (Part II)', 'Survival of the Fittest' and 'Eye for an Eye' are the core; Nas, Raekwon and Ghostface guest.","Mobb Deep's first album had failed; released in April 1995, this second one, recorded when the two were barely twenty, made them the voice of Queensbridge after Nas. Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest helped with production and mixing. The mid-nineties were a period of high crime in New York, and the record documents it without editorialising. Its bleakness was a direct response to what the two saw daily.","Havoc often built loops from obscure jazz records, pitched down until they sound almost unrecognisable: the album's chill comes from that deliberate degradation. Listen to the relationship between the two rappers: Prodigy vivid and fatalistic, Havoc blunter. Prodigy lived with sickle-cell disease his whole life, and illness and mortality shadow his verses.","It was an instant classic among New York listeners and is now counted among the definitive East Coast records of the decade. Some critics found it bleak to the point of nihilism; others heard it as the most honest reportage of the period. Its influence on hardcore rap production is considerable.","Start with 'Shook Ones (Part II)' and 'Survival of the Fittest', and notice how much of the menace comes from what is missing, not what is there. Then compare Illmatic: the same projects, the same year, a far different mood."],"mobb-deep-shook-ones-part-ii":["Five minutes of dread. A smeared, dissonant piano phrase and a low hum loop under hard drums, while Prodigy and Havoc warn off pretenders who talk tough but would not survive the real thing. The famous opening verse by Prodigy is delivered almost quietly, which makes it more threatening. The beat is one of the most recognisable in hip-hop and has been reused endlessly.","A remake of a less successful single, 'Part II' was released early in 1995 and became the anthem of The Infamous. In 2002 it reached a much wider audience when it played during the final rap battle of the film 8 Mile. The song's picture of fear masking itself as toughness spoke to a New York still gripped by crime and heavy policing.","The piano comes from Herbie Hancock's 'Jessica', a gentle ballad, pitched down and cut so that its consonances turn sinister. It is a striking case of a sampler changing a piece's emotional meaning entirely. Listen for the faint hiss of the original vinyl under the loop. The low hum under the piano is part of the menace.","Widely ranked among the greatest hip-hop singles, it is often cited by producers as the pinnacle of the dark East Coast style. Its lyrics are violent, and critics disagree about whether they describe or glamorise. Its beat has been rapped over, freestyled over and battled over so often that it functions as a kind of folk standard.","Listen once to the beat alone, then find Hancock's 'Jessica' and marvel at the distance travelled. Then compare Hancock's own Maiden Voyage, whose modal harmonies share the same haunted spaciousness in a gentler key. Notice how quiet Prodigy stays: menace without volume."],"outkast-southernplayalisticadillacmuzik":["OutKast's debut sounds like nothing from either coast: slow, humid, played-live Southern funk with wah-wah guitar, organ, live bass and loose drums, the Organized Noize production team treating hip-hop like a soul band would. André and Big Boi, still teenagers, trade verses in thick Atlanta accents about Cadillacs, weekends and the Georgia police. 'Player's Ball' and 'Git Up, Git Out' mark the range from party to sermon.","Signed to LaFace, the Atlanta label of L.A. Reid and Babyface, the duo came out of a collective known as the Dungeon Family, which recorded in a basement studio. The album appeared in April 1994. At the 1995 Source Awards in New York, amid boos when the duo won best new group, André declared that the South had something to say, a moment now seen as a turning point.","Listen for how little of the music is sampled: Organized Noize hired live players, giving the record the loose feel of Southern soul from Stax and Hi rather than the clipped loops of New York. 'Git Up, Git Out', featuring Cee-Lo Green, is a sermon about ambition and idleness that shows the moralist side of André long before his later experiments.","It sold well and was received warmly but with some condescension from East Coast media, which treated Southern rap as a regional curiosity. Its standing has risen steadily, and it is now counted as the founding album of a Southern hip-hop mainstream that eventually overtook both coasts. André's Source Awards speech is now quoted as prophecy.","Listen for the live band, especially the bass and organ. Then play Al Green or Curtis Mayfield to hear where the warmth comes from, and follow with Aquemini to hear how far the duo travelled in four years. Notice the accents too: nobody on either coast sounded like this."],"outkast-aquemini":["OutKast's third album is a sprawling, adventurous record that blends funk, gospel, blues, psychedelia and electro with rapping of great virtuosity. 'Rosa Parks' stops for a stomping harmonica breakdown; 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' is a slow horn-driven reverie with spoken narration; 'Da Art of Storytellin'' tells two tender, tragic stories; 'Liberation' is an eight-minute spiritual. The contrast between André's experimental impulses and Big Boi's grounded flow drives it.","Released in September 1998, it followed ATLiens, where the duo had begun producing themselves. The title combines their star signs, Aquarius and Gemini. By now Southern rap was selling heavily, but OutKast were treated as art-rap outliers. Rosa Parks sued over the use of her name in the single, a case eventually settled in 2005. The duo now produced much of their music themselves.","The harmonica breakdown in 'Rosa Parks' is a deliberate nod to country blues and church revival, sounds rarely heard in rap. 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' is built on a slow, drunken horn line and a spoken, almost Southern-Gothic narrative about a night in a club. Listen for Raekwon on 'Skew It on the Bar-B': an East–West–South truce in miniature.","Critics received it as a classic almost at once, and The Source gave it five mics. It has come to be regarded as one of the greatest hip-hop albums and proof that Southern rap could be both popular and experimental. Some fans still prefer its mix of adventure and grounding to the wilder Stankonia.","Start with 'Rosa Parks' for joy, then 'SpottieOttieDopaliscious' for mood, then 'Da Art of Storytellin' (Pt. 1)' for narrative. Compare Stevie Wonder's Innervisions for another Black American album that treats a record as a whole world. Give it time: the long tracks reward patience."],"outkast-stankonia":["OutKast's most explosive record. 'B.O.B.' hurtles at drum-and-bass tempo with distorted guitar, a gospel choir and rapid-fire verses; 'Ms. Jackson' is a gentle, rueful apology to the mother of an ex-partner; 'So Fresh, So Clean' is smooth funk. Elsewhere there is psychedelic rock, slow jams and electro. The duo produced most of it themselves with Earthtone III, and the energy is restless and playful.","Released on 31 October 2000, the album followed the duo's buildup of their own Atlanta studio, Stankonia. 'B.O.B.' came out before the September 2001 attacks but became tied to the Iraq wars by its title's reference to Baghdad. The album won the 2002 Grammy for best rap album, and 'Ms. Jackson' became a huge crossover hit. It was their commercial peak until then.","'B.O.B.' runs at roughly 155 beats per minute, borrowing the tempo of British drum and bass, a genre almost unknown in American rap. Listen at the end for the gospel choir and the wailing guitar solo, pulling the song from rave to church to Hendrix in a minute. Listen too for how 'Ms. Jackson' sets an apology over a lullaby-soft groove, a rare tenderness in rap about breakups.","Critics loved it; it topped many year-end polls, and 'B.O.B.' is often ranked among the finest songs of the decade. Its genre-blurring prefigured the duo's split into two solo discs on Speakerboxxx/The Love Below. Some fans felt it traded Aquemini's coherence for spectacle; that is the main dissent. Its reputation has only grown since.","Play 'B.O.B.' loud, then 'Ms. Jackson' right after, and marvel that the same duo made both. Then go back to Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' to hear the electro roots of their restlessness. Notice how the duo treat genre as a palette rather than a boundary, then compare Missy Elliott's 'Get Ur Freak On'."],"missy-elliott-supa-dupa-fly":["Missy Elliott's debut, produced with her childhood friend Timbaland, sounds like it arrived from the future: stuttering, syncopated drum programming, gurgling synthesizers, odd vocal ad-libs and huge amounts of empty space. Missy moves between rapping and singing, playful and warm. 'The Rain (Supa Dupa Fly)', 'Sock It 2 Me' and 'Beep Me 911' are the landmarks, with guests including Aaliyah, Busta Rhymes and Lil' Kim.","Missy and Timbaland, both from Virginia, had already written and produced for Aaliyah and Ginuwine, creating the lopsided R&B that dominated the late nineties. The album came out in July 1997 on Missy's own Elektra imprint. Hype Williams's video for 'The Rain', with Missy in an inflated black plastic suit shot through a fish-eye lens, made her an instant visual icon.","'The Rain' is built on Ann Peebles's 1973 Memphis soul hit 'I Can't Stand the Rain', whose ticking electric percussion Timbaland loops almost unchanged: Southern soul becomes futurism. Listen for Timbaland's trademark sounds throughout, such as baby gurgles, cricket chirps and human beatbox, used as drums. Missy herself wrote most of the hooks.","It was hailed immediately as a leap forward in sound and design, and Missy became one of the most distinctive and influential figures in pop. Some reviewers felt the songs were thinner than the production. Its reputation has grown with the recognition that Timbaland's rhythms reshaped both R&B and hip-hop for a decade.","Start with 'The Rain' and hear how the Peebles loop drives it. Then listen to the drums on any track and try to count them: they skip and stutter. Compare Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for an earlier electro vision of Black futurism."],"missy-elliott-get-ur-freak-on":["A dance record built on a nagging five-note riff played on a plucked single string, over tabla and stuttering electronic drums. Missy's delivery is gleeful and teasing, full of shouts, grunts and bursts of speed, and the whole thing has the manic energy of a rave in a street market. It sounds unlike any hip-hop single before it, and the sound of the riff alone announces it.","Released in 2001 as the lead single from Miss E... So Addictive, it was produced by Timbaland at the height of his fame. Its South Asian elements anticipated a wave of Western producers borrowing from bhangra and Bollywood; a remix with Nelly Furtado followed. It won Missy a Grammy for best rap solo performance. The video, with its crawling bodies and green light, was equally strange.","The riff is played on or imitates the tumbi, a one-string Punjabi instrument associated with bhangra, and the tabla patterns are close to North Indian playing. Timbaland had absorbed these sounds from Indian film and bhangra records. Listen for the handful of Japanese words at the very start, a jolt of global collage before the beat drops.","It was a critical and commercial triumph and is often ranked among the best singles of its decade. Later writers have also questioned the borrowing of South Asian sounds without credit to their sources, part of a wider debate about appropriation in Timbaland's work. Its sound has aged remarkably little.","Listen for the tabla and the one-string riff, then play some classic bhangra or Hindustani tabla to hear what Timbaland borrowed. Pair it with OutKast's 'B.O.B.' from a year earlier: two Southern futurists pulling in sounds from everywhere. Then notice how much space surrounds the riff."],"fugees-the-score":["The Fugees' second album blends hip-hop beats, soul, reggae and folk guitar with the contrasting voices of Lauryn Hill, Wyclef Jean and Pras Michel. Its hits were covers: 'Killing Me Softly', Roberta Flack's song set over a bare hip-hop beat and sung by Hill, and Bob Marley's 'No Woman, No Cry'. Originals such as 'Ready or Not', 'Fu-Gee-La' and 'The Mask' show the trio's fluid mix of rap and song.","Released in February 1996 after a debut that had sold poorly, it became one of the best-selling hip-hop albums, won two Grammys and made Hill a star. Wyclef and Pras were of Haitian descent, and the group's name was a reclaiming of 'refugee', a slur used against Haitians in New York. Its success showed rap could be mellow, musical and global.","'Ready or Not' samples Enya's 'Boadicea', a New Age vocal piece, a surprising source for a hip-hop hit. 'Killing Me Softly' was written by Charles Fox and Norman Gimbel and first recorded by Lori Lieberman before Roberta Flack's famous version. Listen for the kitchen-sink sounds and street recordings woven between tracks.","Critics welcomed its warmth and originality; purists grumbled that its hits were covers. It remains one of hip-hop's great crossover albums and set the stage for Hill's solo work. The trio split soon after, and its reunions were brief. Critics now also credit its mixture of Haitian, Caribbean and American influences as a quiet political statement about migration and belonging, well ahead of its time.","Listen to 'Ready or Not' and 'Killing Me Softly' first, then 'The Mask' to hear the trio as rappers. Follow with The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill to hear where Hill went next. Then listen to Roberta Flack's version of 'Killing Me Softly' to hear how much Hill kept and what she changed."],"jay-z-reasonable-doubt":["Jay-Z's debut is a cool, detailed study of hustling as a business: plush, jazzy soul samples, laid-back beats and a rapper whose flow is relaxed, witty and precise. 'Dead Presidents II', 'Can't Knock the Hustle' with Mary J. Blige, 'Brooklyn's Finest' with the Notorious B.I.G. and 'D'Evils' are the standouts. The mood is less violent than reflective, ambivalent about the life it describes.","Shawn Carter, from the Marcy projects in Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn, was in his mid-twenties and unable to find a label deal, so he founded Roc-A-Fella Records with Damon Dash and Kareem Burke. Released in June 1996, the album was distributed by Priority. Producers included DJ Premier, Ski and Clark Kent. It came at the height of the mafioso wave launched by Raekwon.","'Dead Presidents II' is built on Lonnie Liston Smith's 'A Garden of Peace', a gentle spiritual-jazz piece, and its hook takes a vocal fragment from Nas: an early sign of the rivalry to come. Listen to how Jay-Z raps around the beat with little effort, rarely stressing the downbeat, giving the flow its conversational ease.","It sold modestly at first but is now widely considered his best album and one of the essential New York records of its decade. Critics debate whether his later commercial peaks or this intimate debut is the real Jay-Z. It is often grouped with Illmatic and Ready to Die as the canon of nineties New York.","Start with 'Dead Presidents II' and 'Brooklyn's Finest', where Jay-Z and Biggie trade verses. Listen for the piano and horn samples, which give it a jazz-club feel. Then compare Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx for a grittier take on the same themes."],"gang-starr-daily-operation":["Gang Starr's third album is the purest statement of the duo's method: Guru's calm, monotone voice, smooth and authoritative, over DJ Premier's chopped jazz and soul samples, hard drums and scratched choruses. 'Take It Personal', 'Ex Girl to Next Girl', 'Soliloquy of Chaos' and 'I'm the Man', featuring the young Jeru the Damaja, show the range. The sound is sparse, jazzy and absolutely precise.","Guru, from Boston, and Premier, from Houston, met in New York in the late eighties. By 1992 Gang Starr were leaders in the jazz-rap movement alongside A Tribe Called Quest and De La Soul; Guru would soon launch Jazzmatazz, recorded with live jazz musicians. Released in 1992, the album came as Premier began producing for others, a role that would make him the most admired beatmaker in New York.","Listen for Premier's signature chorus technique: instead of a sung hook, he scratches together fragments of other rappers' lines, creating a collage that doubles as a history lesson. His drums are programmed slightly off the grid, which gives them their famous swing. Guru's voice, a flat tenor, was described by fans as the perfect match for jazz.","It was praised on release and is widely considered one of the finest records of the jazz-rap era, although Gang Starr's later Moment of Truth sometimes overshadows it. Premier's production has been influential far beyond the duo: his method of chopping and scratching became a template for East Coast hip-hop.","Listen to 'Take It Personal' and focus on Premier's drums. Then compare A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory, another jazz-rap album of the same moment, and hunt out the jazz records these producers mined: Bill Evans, Ahmad Jamal, Miles Davis."],"geto-boys-mind-playing-tricks-on-me":["A song about paranoia, depression and guilt. Over a slow, looped soul guitar figure, Scarface, Willie D and Bushwick Bill each describe a mind unravelling: sleeplessness, visions, suspicion of friends and a hallucinated fight. The tone is weary and haunted, the music soft and melancholy, which makes the subject all the more disturbing. It sounds like nothing else from its year.","The Geto Boys were from Houston, signed to James Prince's Rap-A-Lot Records, and notorious for their violent lyrics; their earlier album had been refused by its distributor. 'Mind Playing Tricks on Me', from We Can't Be Stopped (1991), became their biggest hit and brought Southern rap onto national radio before OutKast. The album cover showed Bushwick Bill after a shooting incident.","The guitar comes from Isaac Hayes's 'Hung Up on My Baby', from his 1974 soundtrack Tough Guys, one of Hayes's orchestral soul pieces. Listen to Scarface's verses, which are the song's emotional core: they hint at the frank treatment of mental illness that would mark his later solo work. The loop barely changes.","It was a hit and remains one of the most admired Southern rap songs, frequently ranked among the best hip-hop singles of all. Critics have singled it out as an early and frank treatment of mental illness in rap. Its influence on the psychological storytelling of later artists is widely acknowledged.","Listen for the guitar loop and how its gentleness frames the unease. Then play Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul to hear the orchestral soul world behind it, and follow with 2Pac's Me Against the World for another record of paranoia and dread."],"ugk-ridin-dirty":["UGK's third album is slow, heavy Texas soul-funk: bluesy guitars, deep bass, organ and swinging drums, played live more often than sampled. Pimp C, who produced most of it, sings and raps in a nasal drawl; Bun B raps with measured precision. 'One Day', about losing friends and family, 'Murder', 'Diamonds & Wood' and '3 in the Mornin'' are the core. Interludes feature phone calls from a friend in prison.","Pimp C and Bun B came from Port Arthur, Texas, a refinery town near the Louisiana border. Released in July 1996 on Jive, the album had no radio hit and little promotion, yet sold well through Southern word of mouth. It was a major influence on the Houston scene, and its slow tempos anticipated the chopped-and-screwed style of DJ Screw.","Pimp C studied music at school and played keyboards and bass himself, which explains the unusually sophisticated harmonies and live arrangements. Listen for the blues and gospel inflections, Southern roots that set UGK apart from the funk-loop approach of both coasts. The prison phone calls give the album a sober, documentary frame.","It was little noticed by national critics at the time but has come to be regarded as one of the definitive Southern rap albums and a foundation of the region's sound. Pimp C's death in 2007 prompted reassessment. Critics now rank it with OutKast's early albums as the heart of nineties Southern hip-hop.","Start with 'One Day' and 'Murder', and listen for the live guitar and organ. Play it slowly, in a car if possible, and notice the unhurried tempo. Compare the Geto Boys and OutKast's debut for the Houston and Atlanta sides of the story."],"eminem-the-marshall-mathers-lp":["Eighteen tracks, most produced by Dr. Dre with Mel-Man or by Detroit's Bass Brothers, and a sound that is surprisingly bare: a hard drum loop, a bouncing keyboard figure, a lot of empty space for the voice. The rapper plays several people at once — the leering comedian of The Real Slim Shady, the furious celebrity of The Way I Am, the doomed fan of Stan — and the whole record works as a hall of mirrors about what fame does to the man inside the persona.","The Slim Shady LP had made Eminem a star a year earlier, and a white rapper from Detroit's east side selling to suburban teenagers had become a moral panic of its own. This follow-up, released by Dre's Aftermath label in May 2000, answered the outrage directly and sold about 1.76 million copies in its first week in the United States, a record for a rap album at the time. It was made in the last months before file-sharing began to hollow out album sales.","Listen to how many tracks are really about the listener: the skits, the answering-machine messages, the fan letters, the parents and politicians named in The Way I Am. The album keeps staging its own reception. And beneath the jokes, the rhyme technique is extraordinary — long strings of internal rhymes that land on every stress in the bar, a density few rappers had attempted at pop tempo.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and was nominated for Album of the Year, while GLAAD and others protested its homophobic language and one track's graphic domestic violence fantasy; Eminem's duet with Elton John at the 2001 Grammys was staged as a reply. Critics still argue whether the persona excuses the content. Its technical brilliance and its influence on a generation of rappers are not in dispute.","Start with Stan and The Way I Am, the two tracks where the craft and the self-portrait are clearest, then play The Real Slim Shady to hear the same man as cartoon. Notice how Dre's beats leave the voice almost unaccompanied. Afterwards, go back to Dr. Dre's 2001 from the year before, where Eminem is a guest in the same sonic world."],"eminem-stan":["A song in letters. Over a sampled loop of the English singer Dido's Thank You — her voice drifting in for the chorus — and the sound of rain, thunder and a scratching pen, a fan writes to his idol three times, each letter angrier and more unhinged, until he drives off a bridge. In the final verse the star finally writes back, kindly and too late, and realises mid-letter who the fan was.","Produced by the veteran New Jersey producer The 45 King and released on The Marshall Mathers LP, it became a single late in 2000 and reached number one in Britain. Eminem was then receiving the kind of obsessive fan mail the song describes, and the track is partly a warning about taking his violent persona literally. Dido appeared in the video, and the song made her a star in the United States.","Listen to how the three letters are performed: the same rapper voices the fan, but the pitch, pace and breathing change with each verse, until the words are almost shouted over the loop. The reply verse drops back to Eminem's ordinary voice. The fan's name has since become a word — to stan is to be an obsessive supporter — and the Oxford English Dictionary added it in 2019.","Widely ranked among the greatest hip-hop singles, it earned praise even from critics hostile to the rest of the album for its narrative control. Some hear its ending as the album's moral centre; others note that it depicts the fan's violence against his pregnant girlfriend with the same cinematic relish it claims to deplore. Elton John's performance of it with Eminem at the 2001 Grammys fixed its place.","Listen once simply as a short story, following the four letters. Then listen again for the sound design: the rain, the pen, the car, the splash, each placed like a film's foley. Compare Dido's full recording of Thank You to hear how little of it the producer needed."],"eminem-lose-yourself":["A muted, palm-damped electric guitar riff, a spoken introduction, and then a relentless, rising build: piano chords hammered on every beat, drums entering, three long verses with no let-up. The song narrates a young rapper choking at a battle, then getting one more chance, and its hook is an exhortation to seize the single moment. It is built like a sports anthem and has been used as one ever since.","Written for 8 Mile, the 2002 film in which Eminem played a lightly fictionalised version of himself on Detroit's battle-rap circuit, it was reportedly written and recorded in breaks during filming, with production by Eminem, Jeff Bass and Luis Resto. It spent twelve weeks at number one in the United States and in 2003 became the first rap song to win the Academy Award for Best Original Song; Eminem did not attend the ceremony.","The music barely changes chords; its tension comes from layering and from the voice, which gets faster and more crowded with syllables as each verse goes on. Listen for the moment at the start of the final verse where the arrangement briefly drops away — the only real breath in five minutes. Eminem finally performed the song at the Oscars in 2020, seventeen years late.","Instantly canonical as a motivational anthem, it has been praised as the purest distillation of Eminem's craft without the shock tactics, and dismissed by some as his most conventional record. The Oscar win was read as a turning point in the establishment's acceptance of hip-hop.","Notice the structure first: a guitar that never resolves, piano that grows heavier, verses that accelerate. Then follow the story line by line. Compare Run-DMC's Walk This Way for an earlier fusion of rap and rock guitar, and watch the battle scenes in 8 Mile for the world it came from."],"kanye-west-the-college-dropout":["A debut built from soul records sped up until the voices squeak — the sound nicknamed chipmunk soul — with live strings, a gospel choir and comic skits about a school that tells students to stay in line. Kanye raps as the ordinary middle-class kid in a genre of hustlers: about student debt, consumer insecurity, faith, and the car crash that nearly killed him. Jesus Walks marches over a stomping gospel chant; Through the Wire was rapped through a jaw still wired shut.","By 2003 Kanye was a sought-after producer for Jay-Z's Roc-A-Fella label, but executives doubted a producer in a pink polo shirt could sell as a rapper. After a near-fatal car accident in October 2002 he recorded Through the Wire weeks later, sampling Chaka Khan's Through the Fire, and the label relented. The album appeared in February 2004 as mainstream rap was dominated by gangster personas.","All Falls Down borrows its melody from a Lauryn Hill song, re-sung by Syleena Johnson because the original could not be cleared. Listen too for how the drum sounds are softer and rounder than in most rap of 2004, closer to a 1970s soul session, and how often the samples are allowed to finish their phrases rather than being chopped into loops.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and topped many critics' lists for 2004; reviewers heard it as a sign that hip-hop's centre could accommodate doubt, religion and self-mockery. Some later listeners find the skits long. It is now seen as the launch of the most consequential producer-rapper career of the century.","Start with Jesus Walks and Through the Wire, then Spaceship and All Falls Down. Listen for the pitched-up soul voices that sit in the background of nearly every track. Pair it with Jay-Z's The Blueprint, where the same sampled-soul style first reached the charts."],"kanye-west-late-registration":["The second album keeps the soul samples but dresses them in orchestral colour: strings, harpsichord, French horns, marimba and brass arranged with the film composer Jon Brion. Touch the Sky rides a slowed-down horn fanfare from Curtis Mayfield; Gold Digger turns a Ray Charles sample, sung by Jamie Foxx, into a comic hit; Diamonds from Sierra Leone sets Shirley Bassey's Bond theme against the trade in conflict diamonds. Hey Mama and Roses are tender family portraits.","Kanye brought in Jon Brion, known for his work with Fiona Apple and his film scores, and the two shaped the arrangements through 2005. The album appeared that August; days later, during a televised benefit for Hurricane Katrina, Kanye went off script to say that President Bush did not care about Black people, and the record played against that argument.","Listen for how often the orchestra enters only for a bar or two — a flourish of strings or a harpsichord line — rather than playing through, so the tracks feel like soul records visited by a chamber band. Touch the Sky uses Mayfield's Move On Up at a slower tempo, which makes its horns sound heavier and more ceremonial than the original.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and was widely named album of the year by critics; some rate it Kanye's most complete record, others find it a lavish transitional step between The College Dropout and later reinventions. Gold Digger became his biggest single to that point.","Play Touch the Sky straight after Curtis Mayfield's Curtis to hear the source. Then listen to Diamonds from Sierra Leone for the collision of a Bond theme and politics, and to Roses for the strings at their most intimate. Wear headphones: the arrangements are full of small instrumental entries."],"kanye-west-808s-and-heartbreak":["Almost no rapping. Kanye sings, his voice run through Auto-Tune so that every wobble becomes a robotic slide, over the cavernous booms of a Roland TR-808 drum machine, cold synthesizer pads and pounding taiko drums. The songs — Say You Will, Heartless, Love Lockdown, Street Lights, Coldest Winter — are about loneliness, a broken engagement and grief, sung by a man who sounds numb. It is spare, slow and deliberately anti-triumphant.","In November 2007 Kanye's mother, Donda West, died after cosmetic surgery; soon after, his engagement ended. He recorded the album quickly during 2008, partly in Honolulu, drawing on 1980s synth-pop and new wave as much as rap. Auto-Tune was then a novelty associated with T-Pain's party hits; using it as the sound of grief was a startling choice from the biggest rapper in the world.","Coldest Winter borrows its melody from Tears for Fears' Memories Fade, and the whole album's minor-key synth palette owes more to British 1980s pop than to hip-hop. Listen to how Auto-Tune is pushed to exaggerate the voice's cracks rather than hide them: the machine becomes an expression of emotional flatness, not a correction. Love Lockdown alternates near-silence with thunderous drums.","Reviews were divided in 2008; many rap fans felt betrayed. Within a few years it was recognised as one of the most influential records of its time: Drake, Kid Cudi, The Weeknd, Frank Ocean and the whole wave of melodic, emotionally exposed rap and alternative R&B grew from it. The argument now is less about its merit than about how much it enabled.","Start with Love Lockdown and Heartless, then Street Lights. Listen for the space around the voice, and how few elements each track uses. Follow it with Drake's So Far Gone from the next year to hear the influence arrive almost at once."],"kanye-west-my-beautiful-dark-twisted-fantasy":["A maximalist album about excess, fame and self-loathing, stacked with choirs, prog-rock samples, strings and a crowd of guests: Jay-Z, Nicki Minaj, Rick Ross, Bon Iver's Justin Vernon, Rihanna, Pusha T and others. Power stomps over chanting and a King Crimson sample; All of the Lights piles up horns and a dozen voices; Runaway opens with a single piano note repeated and ends in a long, distorted, wordless solo on a vocoded voice.","After Kanye interrupted Taylor Swift at the 2009 MTV Video Music Awards he was widely vilified and withdrew to Hawaii, where he gathered producers, rappers and writers for months of communal sessions in Honolulu. He previewed songs through free weekly downloads, the GOOD Fridays series, and released the album in November 2010 alongside a thirty-five-minute film, Runaway.","Power samples the 1969 King Crimson track 21st Century Schizoid Man; Lost in the World builds on Bon Iver's Woods, and the album closes with Gil Scott-Heron reciting his poem Comment No. 1. Listen at the end of Runaway: the vocoder solo is Kanye's own voice, deliberately smeared into unintelligibility, a three-minute confession without words.","It received near-universal acclaim, including a rare perfect score from Pitchfork, and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album but was left out of the Album of the Year shortlist, which became a lasting grievance. It regularly appears near the top of decade and all-time lists; the debate now is inseparable from reassessments of its maker.","Hear it in order, with headphones. Start with the chant that opens Dark Fantasy, then Power and Runaway. Play King Crimson's In the Court of the Crimson King afterwards to hear where Power's riff and scream come from, and compare the stripped-down Yeezus three years later."],"kanye-west-yeezus":["Ten tracks in forty minutes of grinding industrial synthesizers, acid-house squelches, screams and sudden silences. Daft Punk co-produced the opening On Sight and Black Skinhead, with its glam-rock stomp; New Slaves attacks consumerism and prison profiteering; Blood on the Leaves sets a sample of Nina Simone singing Strange Fruit against a booming brass hook; the closing Bound 2 swerves into a sweet 1970s soul sample, as though the rest had been a nightmare.","Recorded partly in a Paris loft with a large team of producers including Daft Punk, Hudson Mohawke, Arca and Chicago drill artists, the album was stripped down in its final weeks by Rick Rubin, brought in as executive producer. It was released in June 2013 with no conventional singles or cover art — just a clear jewel case sealed with red tape.","Strange Fruit, the anti-lynching song sampled on Blood on the Leaves, is set under lyrics about a failed relationship, a juxtaposition many found jarring — and perhaps intended to be. Listen for the abrupt cuts: tracks change beat, drop to silence or collapse into noise mid-phrase, the editing itself becoming a rhythm. Bound 2 samples the Ponderosa Twins Plus One.","Critics largely admired its nerve; many fans and radio did not. Its abrasive minimalism was a direct rebuttal of the lavish Fantasy, and it opened space for noise and experimental electronics in mainstream rap. Its use of Strange Fruit remains disputed.","Play loud. Start with Black Skinhead and New Slaves, then Blood on the Leaves. Listen for how little is going on in each track, and how violently it changes. Then play Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Kanye's own Late Registration to feel how far he had travelled."],"kendrick-lamar-good-kid-maad-city":["Subtitled a short film, the album follows a seventeen-year-old Kendrick through one day and night in Compton: a crush, a borrowed van, peer pressure, a robbery, a friend shot, and finally a prayer. Voicemails from his parents stitch the scenes together. The music moves from hazy, woozy West Coast beats (Money Trees, Swimming Pools) to the split-screen menace of m.A.A.d city and the twelve-minute Sing About Me, I'm Dying of Thirst.","Kendrick had built a following through Top Dawg Entertainment's independent releases and the album Section.80 (2011); Dr. Dre's Aftermath co-signed him and released this, his major-label debut, in October 2012. It deliberately positioned him as heir to Compton's rap history while reversing its point of view — from gangster to observer.","The album is arranged as a flashback: the closing prayer is the moment the rest of the story has been leading towards, and the final track, Compton, with Dr. Dre, jumps forward to the adult star. Swimming Pools, a radio hit, is an anatomy of peer pressure and drinking disguised as a party song — one voice in the verse is his conscience.","Widely acclaimed, it was treated as an instant classic and compared with Nas's Illmatic as a coming-of-age portrait of one place; its commercial and critical success made Kendrick the defining rapper of the decade. Grammy voters preferred Macklemore for Best Rap Album, a result later widely regretted, including by Macklemore.","Listen in order, like an audiobook, and follow the voicemails. Start with Money Trees if you want one track. Then compare Illmatic and Dr. Dre's The Chronic, the albums it both honours and answers. Notice how the beats darken as the night goes on."],"kendrick-lamar-to-pimp-a-butterfly":["Sixteen tracks played largely by live musicians — Thundercat's melodic bass, Terrace Martin's alto saxophone, Robert Glasper's keyboards, Kamasi Washington's string arrangements, Flying Lotus and Sounwave's production — ranging across free jazz, P-Funk, spoken word and gospel. The album argues with fame, depression, American racism and Black self-image, from the funk strut of King Kunta to the drunken self-lacerating u, the protest chant Alright and the confrontational The Blacker the Berry.","After the success of good kid, Kendrick visited South Africa, including Robben Island, and returned while protests over police killings were spreading across the United States. He spent months with a Los Angeles circle of jazz-trained musicians rather than beatmakers. The album was released in March 2015, a week earlier than announced; George Clinton appears on the opening Wesley's Theory.","A poem is assembled one line at a time across the album: Kendrick recites a little more of it at the end of several tracks until, in Mortal Man, he reads it complete — and then appears to interview Tupac Shakur, using a 1994 radio interview with the Swedish broadcaster Mats Nileskär. The album ends with a question to the dead rapper that goes unanswered.","Reviewers called it the most ambitious rap album in years; it won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, was nominated for Album of the Year, and routinely heads polls of the decade's best albums. Some listeners find it dense and demanding, and its jazz turn helped revive public interest in Los Angeles' young jazz scene.","Give it a full, uninterrupted hour and twenty minutes. Start with King Kunta and Alright, then u and Mortal Man. Listen to the bass throughout. Then hear Kamasi Washington's The Epic, made by some of the same players that year, and Marvin Gaye's What's Going On."],"kendrick-lamar-alright":["Produced by Pharrell Williams and Sounwave, the track opens with a spoken passage and a sung refrain from Pharrell before Kendrick's verses weave in and out of a jittery, jazz-inflected beat with saxophone lines threading through. The song holds despair and resilience side by side: temptation, police violence and inner demons are all named, and the chorus answers them with a plain promise that things will, in the end, be alright.","Released on To Pimp a Butterfly in March 2015 and as a single that summer, it arrived during the protests that followed police killings of unarmed Black Americans in Ferguson, Baltimore and elsewhere. Kendrick performed it at the 2015 BET Awards standing on a graffiti-covered police car, which drew criticism from some commentators.","Within months, protesters were chanting its chorus at demonstrations, beginning with a widely filmed moment at Cleveland State University in July 2015; it became an unofficial anthem of the Black Lives Matter movement without having been written as one. Listen for how much of the track is Pharrell's calm singing, against which the rapping sounds urgent.","It won the Grammys for Best Rap Performance and Best Rap Song and is often ranked the most important protest song of its decade, compared with Public Enemy's Fight the Power and Curtis Mayfield's civil-rights hymns. The criticism it drew from conservative television hosts became part of its story.","Listen first for the chorus and imagine it chanted by a crowd, then go back for the verses, which are darker than the hook suggests. Compare Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready and James Brown's Say It Loud as earlier songs that crowds adopted."],"kendrick-lamar-damn":["Fourteen tracks with one-word, capitalised titles — BLOOD., DNA., YAH., ELEMENT., FEEL., LOYALTY., PRIDE., HUMBLE., LUST., LOVE., XXX., FEAR., GOD., DUCKWORTH. — played tighter and harder than To Pimp a Butterfly, with trap drums, sudden beat switches and guests from Rihanna to U2. DNA. flips halfway into a frantic new beat; HUMBLE. is a stark piano-and-bass hit; FEAR. is a long, slow meditation on dread.","Released in April 2017, after the sprawl of Butterfly, it was made with producers including Mike Will Made-It, Sounwave, 9th Wonder, The Alchemist and James Blake. In 2018 it won the Pulitzer Prize for Music, the first time the prize went to a work outside classical music and jazz; the committee called it a virtuosic song collection unified by vernacular authenticity and rhythmic dynamism.","The album is a loop about chance and fate. It opens with a staged shooting and closes with DUCKWORTH., a true story: the label head Anthony Tiffith once nearly robbed a fast-food restaurant where Kendrick's father worked, and spared him — the song then rewinds to the album's start. A later edition reverses the running order, and the album reads differently backwards.","It was a critical and commercial triumph and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album. The Pulitzer set off a debate about whether the prize was catching up with American music or conferring classical respectability on a form that needed none; most musicians welcomed it.","Start with DNA. and HUMBLE., then FEAR. and DUCKWORTH. — then listen to the whole album in reverse order to hear the alternative story. Compare To Pimp a Butterfly for the opposite method: live band instead of tight programming. Pay attention to YAH. and ELEMENT., where the beat drops out or changes under him, and to how often the same phrases and questions return from track to track, tying the album together like leitmotifs."],"madvillain-madvillainy":["Twenty-two tracks in forty-six minutes. Madlib's beats are brief, crackly loops drawn from jazz, Brazilian pop, soundtracks, old cartoons and radio drama; MF DOOM raps over them in a conversational murmur, rarely bothering with a chorus, stringing together puns, comic-book references and dense chains of rhyme. Tracks end abruptly, beats mutate mid-song, and short instrumentals interrupt. Accordion, All Caps, Figaro and Rhinestone Cowboy are the signposts.","Madlib, a producer on the Los Angeles label Stones Throw, and DOOM, reviving his career in the mask, began working together around 2002. An unfinished version leaked online in 2002, which reportedly discouraged them; they reworked much of it before the album's release in March 2004. Some of the beats were reportedly made on portable equipment while Madlib travelled in Brazil buying records.","Listen for the samples of old radio serials and superhero cartoons that introduce the villain persona — the album is structured like a comic book, with narration between scenes. Accordion is built on a loop by the electronic musician Daedelus. Many songs are shorter than two and a half minutes: brevity itself is the aesthetic, the opposite of the long, chorus-driven radio track.","Modest sales on release, but immediate critical acclaim; its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now routinely cited as the definitive underground hip-hop album, a touchstone for rappers and producers from Earl Sweatshirt to Tyler, the Creator. There is little debate beyond whether any follow-up could have matched it — none came.","Listen straight through; it is barely longer than a vinyl LP. Pay attention to the beats first on one pass, then to the rhymes on another. Afterwards play De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising and J Dilla's Donuts for kindred sample-collage records."],"mf-doom-mm-food":["A concept album loosely organised as a meal, with track titles that are food puns — Beef Rapp, Hoe Cakes, Kon Karne, Deep Fried Frenz, One Beer, Kookies. Most of the beats are DOOM's own, under his producer alias Metal Fingers: jaunty soul, soft-rock and easy-listening loops, some cheerfully cheesy. Over them he raps about friendship, rivalry and the rap business with deadpan wit, and several interludes are built from children's story records.","Released in November 2004 by the Minneapolis label Rhymesayers, the same year as Madvillainy and as his Viktor Vaughn and King Geedorah projects had kept him prolific. DOOM had re-emerged from years of obscurity after KMD's collapse and his brother's death, now in a metal mask, performing sometimes through stand-ins — a refusal of celebrity that became part of the legend.","The interludes lift dialogue from old Fantastic Four children's records, placing the rapper within the Marvel universe his persona came from. Listen to how often the samples come from gentle, unfashionable sources — soft-rock ballads, cartoon themes — and how DOOM's voice, heavy and laconic, turns them sinister or funny. One Beer was produced by Madlib.","Praised on release as DOOM's most personable solo album, it has become a cult favourite, often recommended as the easiest way into his catalogue. Some critics find the food conceit thin; admirers counter that the conceit is merely a frame for the rhyming.","Start with Hoe Cakes and Deep Fried Frenz, then Beef Rapp. Listen for the multi-syllable rhymes stacking up at the ends of lines. Compare Madvillainy, released months earlier, to hear the same rapper over someone else's beats. Listen, too, for the children's-record interludes and how they set up the next track like a comic-book panel, and try following a single verse on paper to see how far the rhyme chains run."],"j-dilla-donuts":["Thirty-one instrumental pieces in forty-three minutes, most under two minutes, made from chopped fragments of soul, funk and pop records. Voices stutter, drums lurch against the loops, sirens wail, and tracks end mid-phrase or melt into the next. There is no rapping; the sampled singers do the talking, in sentences cut up and reassembled. Workinonit, Don't Cry and Last Donut of the Night are the landmarks.","Dilla made much of Donuts while seriously ill with a rare blood disorder and lupus, some of it reportedly in a Los Angeles hospital bed with a small sampler and portable record player. Stones Throw released it on 7 February 2006, his thirty-second birthday; he died three days later. That timing has shaped how it is heard ever since.","The album begins with a track called Donuts (Outro) and ends with Welcome to the Show (Intro), so it loops back on itself like the ring in its title. Listen for fragments that seem to speak to his situation — phrases about crying, time and goodbyes, cut from love songs by the Jackson 5, Dionne Warwick and others — though how intentional this was is debated.","Little noticed outside hip-hop at first, it is now regarded as the high point of instrumental hip-hop and one of the most influential albums of its decade; producers from Flying Lotus to Knxwledge, and jazz drummers such as Chris Dave, trace their feel to it. The romantic deathbed myth has sometimes overshadowed the craft.","Play it through once without stopping; it is shorter than most albums. Listen for the drums that land slightly off where you expect them. Then hear D'Angelo's Voodoo and Erykah Badu's Mama's Gun, where the same loose feel was played by live musicians."],"jay-z-the-blueprint":["Jay-Z at his most relaxed and commanding, rapping over warm, looped soul records: the Jackson 5 on Izzo (H.O.V.A.), Bobby Bland on Heart of the City, the Doors and David Bowie on Takeover. Most of the beats are by the young Kanye West and Just Blaze, with Timbaland and Bink!; the only guest rapper is Eminem, on Renegade, which he also produced. Song Cry is a tender breakup confession; Takeover is a surgical attack on rivals.","Recorded in a few weeks in the summer of 2001 while Jay-Z faced criminal charges, and during a feud with Nas and Mobb Deep's Prodigy, it was released on 11 September 2001, the day of the attacks on New York. It sold strongly regardless. The style of pitched-up soul samples it popularised shaped mainstream hip-hop for years.","Takeover's jab at Nas provoked Ether, one of the most famous replies in rap, and the rivalry ran for years before the two reconciled. Listen for how the samples are left almost whole — the soul singers' own phrases carry the hooks — a sound that made Kanye's name as a producer before he ever rapped on an album.","Acclaimed on release and consistently placed among the best rap albums of the decade; many consider it Jay-Z's finest after Reasonable Doubt. It is often credited with shifting commercial rap from glossy club production back towards soul sampling. Later critics singled out its unhurried confidence, the sense of a rapper who no longer needed to prove anything, and the Takeover and Ether exchange is still studied as the high-water mark of the battle record played out on major-label albums.","Start with Heart of the City and Song Cry, then Takeover. Listen to how the voice sits calmly in the pocket of the beat. Follow it with Nas's Illmatic and Kanye's The College Dropout, the rival and the protégé. Pay attention to U Don't Know, where Just Blaze's sped-up soul loop and horns build a victory march, and to how rarely Jay-Z raises his voice anywhere on the album."],"jay-z-the-black-album":["Presented as a retirement album, it gives each track to a different star producer: Just Blaze's triumphal brass on December 4th and Public Service Announcement, Kanye's soul on Encore, Timbaland's minimalism on Dirt Off Your Shoulder, the Neptunes on Change Clothes and Allure, and Rick Rubin's rock guitar and crashing drums on 99 Problems. December 4th, named after his birthday, weaves in his mother's voice telling his life story.","Released in November 2003, it was followed by a farewell concert at Madison Square Garden filmed as Fade to Black. The retirement lasted three years. The record appeared as file-sharing and mixtape culture were reshaping hip-hop, and the label released its a cappella vocals — an invitation to remixers. Jay-Z gathered beats from producers across the industry, using a different one for nearly every song, and the album's sequence moves deliberately from childhood memories to career summary, like a spoken autobiography.","Those a cappellas were taken by Danger Mouse, who set them to samples of the Beatles' White Album to make The Grey Album (2004), an unauthorised mash-up that EMI tried to suppress and that became a landmark of internet remix culture. Listen too to 99 Problems, where Rubin, who produced Run-DMC and the Beastie Boys, brings back the 1980s rap-rock sound.","Widely praised as a strong farewell and a showcase of producers; 99 Problems became one of his signature songs, and critics have analysed its verse about a traffic stop in law-school classes on search and seizure. Some reviewers found the retirement framing self-regarding.","Start with December 4th and 99 Problems, then Encore. Notice how each producer's signature shapes the voice differently. Compare Run-DMC's Walk This Way and the Beastie Boys' Licensed to Ill for Rubin's earlier rap-rock. Then hear the whole album as a curated anthology of 2003 production styles."],"jay-z-4-44":["Ten tracks in thirty-six minutes, all produced by No I.D. from carefully chosen soul samples. Jay-Z, in his late forties, raps plainly about marital infidelity, therapy, money, race and inheritance. The Story of O.J. uses Nina Simone's Four Women to talk about Black wealth; Smile, built on Stevie Wonder's Love's in Need of Love Today, includes his mother Gloria Carter reading a poem about coming out as gay; the title track is an extended apology to his wife.","Released in June 2017 initially through Tidal, the streaming service he part-owned, it was widely heard as a reply to Beyoncé's Lemonade (2016), which had depicted a marriage damaged by betrayal. No I.D., a Chicago producer who had mentored Kanye West, pushed Jay-Z towards introspection and away from his usual guest-laden approach.","Listen to the samples as commentary: Nina Simone's song of four Black women's lives, Stevie Wonder's plea for love, Hannah Williams and the Affirmations' Late Nights and Heartbreak on the title track. Each is chosen for what its original lyric says, not only its sound. The album's brevity is itself a statement from a rapper once known for excess.","It drew Jay-Z's best reviews in over a decade and eight Grammy nominations, though it won none. Some found its advice on generational wealth tone-deaf; many heard it as a model of how a rapper might age honestly. With Lemonade and Everything Is Love it forms a public marriage trilogy.","Play it after Lemonade and hear the two sides. Start with The Story of O.J. and 4:44. Then play Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life to hear the source of Smile. Listen for how sparse the drums are, leaving the samples and the voice exposed, and how short the whole record is."],"drake-so-far-gone":["A free mixtape that sounds like a studio album: dim, muffled synthesizers and slow drums by Noah \"40\" Shebib and Boi-1da, with Drake half-rapping, half-singing about ambition, fame and women who got away. Best I Ever Had became a radio hit; Successful, with Trey Songz and Lil Wayne, and Houstatlantavegas set the mood of late-night melancholy. Several tracks rap over borrowed beats, including one from Kanye's 808s.","Drake, a former child actor from Toronto, released it free on his blog in February 2009. It spread so fast that labels competed for him; he signed with Lil Wayne's Young Money that summer. An abbreviated EP version was sold later. It was among the first mixtapes to launch a major star in the blog era, just as streaming and social media were displacing the old label pipeline.","Listen for how the production sounds as if it is heard through a wall — 40 filters out the high frequencies so the beats feel submerged and nocturnal. That filtered sound, not the lyrics, is the album's signature, and became the template for a decade of Toronto-born R&B and rap, from The Weeknd to PartyNextDoor.","Reviewers noticed at once that it took Kanye's 808s & Heartbreak into more commercial territory; some dismissed the emotional openness as whining, others heard a new vocabulary for rap. Its reputation now rests on its influence more than on any single track.","Start with Successful and Houstatlantavegas. Listen to the texture of the keyboards before the words. Then play 808s & Heartbreak and The Weeknd's House of Balloons to hear the circle of influence. Notice how often the hook is sung rather than rapped, and how slow the tempos are."],"drake-take-care":["Eighty minutes of hushed, late-night introspection. 40's production is muted piano, pillowy synthesizers and slow drums; Drake raps and sings about loneliness in success, old relationships and regrets. Marvins Room is a drunk phone call set to barely-there keyboards; the title track with Rihanna is a dance-pop duet; Headlines and The Motto are swaggering rap singles; Crew Love introduced The Weeknd to a mass audience.","Released in November 2011 on Young Money and Cash Money after his first album Thank Me Later, it was made partly at the Los Angeles studio once owned by Marvin Gaye, which gave Marvins Room its name. The title track was built from Jamie xx's remix of Gil Scott-Heron's I'll Take Care of U, an older soul-blues ballad.","Listen to how much of the album is sung rather than rapped, and how often the voice is doubled, whispered or pitched down — the voice becomes one more soft texture in the mix. The album also features a brief appearance from Stevie Wonder on harmonica, on Doing It Wrong.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and is widely considered Drake's best record, the one that fixed the template of his career. Some critics still find the self-pity cloying; others argue that its vulnerability made space for emotional candour across rap.","Start with Marvins Room and Doing It Wrong at night, then Crew Love. Listen for the empty space in the mixes. Follow with Sade's Love Deluxe, which Drake admires, and Frank Ocean's Channel Orange from the next year. Listen for the long, slow outros where the beat carries on after the voice stops, a 40 hallmark, and compare the loud singles Headlines and The Motto with the quiet album tracks around them."],"tyler-the-creator-flower-boy":["A sunny, lush, self-produced album of warm synthesizer chords, strings, jazz harmonies and soft sung hooks, over which Tyler raps about loneliness, driving, growing up and longing. See You Again, with Kali Uchis, floats on bright keyboards; Who Dat Boy is a grinding, horror-film counterpoint; Garden Shed, Boredom and 911 / Mr. Lonely are introspective and melodic. It sounds like a summer drive with the windows down and a lot on your mind.","Tyler first found fame with the Odd Future collective and records designed to shock. By 2017, with Frank Ocean, Steve Lacy and Kali Uchis among his collaborators, he had moved towards the jazzy, harmonically rich pop of his idols Pharrell Williams and Stevie Wonder. Released by Columbia in July 2017, it was widely read as a coming out, with several songs alluding to attraction to men.","Listen to the chords: Tyler, self-taught, favours stacked jazz harmonies and unexpected key changes more typical of 1970s soul than of rap. Garden Shed works as an extended metaphor — a hidden place where feelings are stored — and sets Estelle's singing against a slow guitar solo; the metaphor lets the song speak by suggestion rather than statement.","Critics hailed it as a breakthrough and a reinvention; it was nominated for the Grammy for Best Rap Album, losing to DAMN. It is now seen as the hinge of his career, and of a wider openness about sexuality in hip-hop.","Start with See You Again and Garden Shed, then Where This Flower Blooms. Listen for the key changes. Compare Frank Ocean's Blonde, made by his Odd Future colleague, and Stevie Wonder's Innervisions for the harmonic inheritance. Listen to 911 / Mr. Lonely for the way the song splits into two moods halfway through, a trick Tyler uses often, and notice how much of the album is instrumental colour rather than rapping."],"tyler-the-creator-igor":["A breakup album told by a character, Igor, sung mostly in a pitched and distorted voice, over fuzzy synthesizers, blown-out bass and soul samples. It moves from infatuation (EARFQUAKE, with its lilting keyboard hook) to jealousy and obsession (NEW MAGIC WAND, a grinding distorted rant) to acceptance (GONE, GONE / THANK YOU and ARE WE STILL FRIENDS?). There is little conventional rapping; it is closer to a psychedelic soul record.","Released in May 2019, it came with Tyler's own warning to listeners not to expect a rap album. He produced, arranged and wrote nearly everything himself; EARFQUAKE was reportedly first offered to Justin Bieber and Rihanna. It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, a category Tyler publicly criticised as a box into which Black artists were put regardless of genre.","A BOY IS A GUN* is built on the same Ponderosa Twins Plus One soul record that Kanye West used on Bound 2, heard very differently. Listen too for how the voice changes pitch across the album: as the character loses control, the vocals become more distorted, and they grow clearer as he lets go.","Widely praised as his most coherent album; its Grammy win and his speech about genre labels sparked a debate about how the industry categorises Black artists. Some listeners miss the rapping; many hear it as the culmination of the melodic turn.","Listen straight through, as a story. Start with EARFQUAKE if you want one song, then NEW MAGIC WAND. Play Kanye's Yeezus to hear the shared sample in its first setting, and Prince's Dirty Mind for another one-man-band funk album. Notice how the tracks bleed into one another without gaps, so the album plays like one long suite, and listen for the moment on GONE, GONE / THANK YOU when the fuzz lifts and a clean melody takes over."],"run-the-jewels-run-the-jewels-2":["Thirty-nine minutes of pounding, distorted, bass-heavy beats by El-P, over which he and Killer Mike trade verses at full volume. The mood swings from exuberant menace (Oh My Darling Don't Cry, Blockbuster Night Part 1) to direct politics: Early describes a police stop from both sides of the car, and Lie, Cheat, Steal attacks the powerful. Zack de la Rocha of Rage Against the Machine guests on one track.","The duo released the album free online in October 2014, amid the protests in Ferguson, Missouri, after the police killing of Michael Brown. On the night the grand jury declined to indict the officer, Killer Mike gave an emotional speech from the stage in St. Louis before a show, which spread widely and fixed the album to the moment.","Listen for El-P's production detail: layers of fuzz, dub-style delays and industrial clatter, closer to a noise-rock record than to a sample-based beat. Early is a dialogue, with the singer BOOTS voicing a fearful bystander. The duo later released Meow the Jewels, a remix album made from cat sounds, as a crowdfunding joke that raised money for charities.","It topped many critics' lists for 2014 and made the duo, both veterans in their late thirties, into festival headliners. It is often compared to Public Enemy for its political energy; some find the sonic assault relentless. Its politics sounded prophetic as protests spread through 2015, and later critics have argued it marked a moment when independent, free-release rap could command the kind of attention once reserved for major-label albums.","Start with Oh My Darling Don't Cry, then Early. Listen for the contrast between El-P's New York clip and Killer Mike's Atlanta drawl. Then play Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back. Pay attention to Blockbuster Night Part 1 for the sheer drum sound, then to Crown, a quieter confession where the aggression turns inward."],"danny-brown-atrocity-exhibition":["A disorienting, anxious album: detuned horns, lurching drums, clattering noise, psychedelic rock and Detroit dance samples, produced mostly by the British producer Paul White. Danny Brown's high, strained voice spirals through addiction, paranoia and depression, sometimes comic, often frightening. Ain't It Funny is a queasy carnival; Really Doe features Kendrick Lamar, Ab-Soul and Earl Sweatshirt over a tolling bell; When It Rain is a Detroit ghettotech rush.","Released in September 2016 on Warp, the British electronic label of Aphex Twin, it signalled Brown's move away from rap's commercial mainstream. He was then in his mid-thirties, touring constantly and, by his own account, struggling with substances and his mental health. Paul White, a London producer known for psychedelic, sample-heavy instrumental records, had worked with Brown before, and the pairing pushed the album away from Detroit rap conventions towards rock, noise and dub.","The title is borrowed from Joy Division's song Atrocity Exhibition, the opening track of their album Closer, which itself took it from J. G. Ballard's experimental novel. Listen for the post-punk inheritance: jagged guitars, dub-heavy bass and claustrophobia. Brown has named Joy Division, Talking Heads and Radiohead as inspirations for the record.","Many critics named it one of the best albums of 2016, praising its daring and the way it made rap sound like no other rap. Some found it too harrowing to return to; it is increasingly seen as a key record of experimental hip-hop in the 2010s.","Start with Really Doe and Ain't It Funny, then When It Rain. Listen for the horns, which never quite sit in tune. Afterwards, play Joy Division's Closer and the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat for the darker lineage. Notice the tempo: many tracks feel slightly too fast or too slow, deliberately uncomfortable. If it overwhelms, start with the calmer Rolling Stone and work outwards from there."],"little-simz-grey-area":["Ten tracks in thirty-five minutes, built with the producer Inflo from live bass, drums, guitar and strings rather than samples. Offence and Boss are confident, punchy openers; Selfish, with Cleo Sol, and Flowers, with Michael Kiwanuka, are slower and reflective; Venom is a furious answer to men who underestimate her, over horror-film strings. The album is about being in one's mid-twenties — neither young nor settled — and the conflicts that brings.","Little Simz, a north Londoner of Nigerian heritage, released it in March 2019 on her own label, Age 101, after years of independent mixtapes and albums. Inflo, a childhood friend, was then working with the secretive collective Sault. The record was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and won the Ivor Novello award for Best Album.","Listen for how rarely the music loops: parts change under her verses as a band would, and several tracks end in extended instrumental codas. Venom's orchestral stabs recall a 1970s thriller score. The phrase grey area refers to the in-between state she describes — a life no longer simple enough for black-and-white answers.","It was widely hailed as the best British rap album of its year and the one that made her a major figure; critics praised the concision and the live-band sound. It is often compared to Lauryn Hill's Miseducation for its mix of confidence and self-examination.","Start with Offence and Venom, then Selfish. Listen for the live bass. Follow it with her Sometimes I Might Be Introvert, which takes the same partnership to orchestral scale, and Lauryn Hill's The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill. Listen closely to the drums, which sound like a real kit in a room, and to 101 FM, a nostalgic portrait of growing up on London estates with pirate radio and video games."],"little-simz-sometimes-i-might-be-introvert":["A cinematic album that opens with Introvert, a fanfare of brass, strings and choir like a film's title sequence. Across nineteen tracks Inflo's arrangements range from orchestral grandeur to Afrobeat (Point and Kill, with Obongjayar), 1970s soul (Woman), and quiet piano; interludes narrated by the actor Emma Corrin frame the record like a fairy tale. The subject is her own life: family, fame, grief, her absent father, her introversion.","Released in September 2021 during the pandemic, the album's acronym, SIMBI, is her own name. Inflo, now famous through the anonymous collective Sault, arranged it with an orchestra and choir. It won the Mercury Prize in 2022, confirming her as the leading British rapper of her generation. It was recorded with a large studio orchestra and choir, an unusual expense for a self-released rap record, and the title reflects how she has described herself: an introvert who comes alive only on stage.","I Love You, I Hate You addresses her estranged father directly, and the orchestration pulls back to let the verse speak. Little Q is a two-part portrait of a cousin who survived being stabbed, told first in his voice and then in hers. Listen for how the interludes move from fairy tale to intimate counsel, a narrator speaking as an inner voice.","Critics called it a British counterpart to To Pimp a Butterfly for its ambition and its orchestral-soul palette. Some found its length and grandeur overwhelming; most praised its sustained focus. It is widely seen as a landmark of UK hip-hop.","Start with Introvert for the fanfare, then Woman and Point and Kill. Listen for how the orchestra enters and exits. Compare Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly and Lauryn Hill's Miseducation. Headphones help: the arrangements are full of small orchestral details beneath the voice."],"kae-tempest-everybody-down":["A novel told in twelve tracks: Becky, Harry and Pete, three young people in south-east London, meet, fall in love, deal drugs and get caught in something larger than themselves. Tempest raps and speaks each chapter in a breathless, rhythmic London voice over spare, bright electronic beats by Dan Carey, with synthesizer riffs that grow track by track. Each song advances the plot, and the album is best heard as a single story.","Tempest, already known as a poet and playwright, had won the Ted Hughes Award in 2013 for Brand New Ancients. Released on Ninja Tune's hip-hop imprint Big Dada in May 2014, the album was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize. Tempest later expanded the same characters into the novel The Bricks That Built the Houses (2016).","Listen for how the beats are constructed by Carey in his south London studio with analogue synthesizers and drum machines, often playing live with the voice rather than looping. The characters recur from track to track, and the voice shifts register for each of them as the story turns darker.","It was praised as a new kind of rap record, closer to spoken-word theatre, and compared with The Streets' A Grand Don't Come for Free; hip-hop purists sometimes doubted whether it counted. It established Tempest as a crossover figure between poetry and music.","Listen in order, as a story. Start with Marshall Law and Lonely Daze. Follow the characters' names. Afterwards read the novel, or play Let Them Eat Chaos for the next chapter of Tempest's method. Notice how the synthesizer lines build across the record."],"kae-tempest-let-them-eat-chaos":["One long poem in thirteen parts. At 4.18 a.m. on a London street, seven neighbours lie awake in seven flats — a nurse, a heartbroken young man, a builder, a woman grieving a friend — each given a track, until a storm brings them all out into the street. Dan Carey's music is spare synthesizer pulse and slow-building drums. The climax, Tunnel Vision, turns into an urgent address about climate, inequality and indifference.","Released in October 2016, months after the Brexit referendum and weeks before the American election, it was also published as a book. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2017. Tempest, a performer of great intensity, often played the whole piece live without a break. The title plays on the phrase attributed to Marie Antoinette, turning a remark about indifference to hunger into a comment on political distraction and the chaos of modern life.","Listen for how the album is structured as a single panning camera shot, moving from window to window. Some tracks are almost pure speech; others lock into a beat. The opening track sets the whole thing in cosmic scale — the earth seen from far away — before zooming onto one street.","Critics praised its ambition and topicality; some found it didactic. It is often compared to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On as a panorama of a society in distress, and to Under Milk Wood for its structure. It featured on many British critics' lists for 2016, and its live performance, an uninterrupted hour, drew praise as theatre as much as concert.","Listen late at night, in one sitting, as if it were a radio play. Pick a character to follow. Then play Marvin Gaye's What's Going On to hear a different panorama of a troubled nation. Notice how the beat drops out entirely in some passages, leaving the voice alone, and how the storm arrives in the music before it is named in the words."],"noname-telefone":["A short, gentle mixtape of warm electric piano, soft drums and gospel-tinged backing voices, produced by Chicago friends including Phoelix, Cam O'bi and Saba. Noname's delivery is quiet and rapid, a poet's cadence, and the subjects are serious beneath the softness: childhood in Chicago, police violence, grief, depression, and, on Bye Bye Baby, a pregnancy lost. Diddy Bop recalls childhood summers; Casket Pretty confronts young death.","Released free in July 2016 after long delays, it came out of a Chicago scene of young rappers and musicians around Chance the Rapper, Saba and Smino, many from the city's youth poetry programmes. Noname had built an audience from guest verses rather than any record of her own. She had performed slam poetry as a teenager, and the youth open-mic programmes of Chicago public libraries and arts centres shaped her quiet, dense style; Telefone took several years to finish.","Listen to the backing singers and keys: many songs are closer to a church or jazz lounge than a rap studio, and the joyful arrangements often sit under the saddest words — childlike melodies over mourning. The title evokes a phone conversation; the songs are meant to feel like intimate calls.","Critics praised its warmth and its poetry; it placed on many year-end lists despite being a free mixtape. It established Noname as a leading voice of Chicago's new rap, and some compared her to Erykah Badu for her mix of softness and pointedness.","Start with Diddy Bop and Casket Pretty. Listen for the contrast between sweet music and serious words. Then play Erykah Badu's Baduizm and her own Room 25. Listen with the words in front of you the first time; her delivery is quick and quiet, and the lines reward close attention to their images."],"noname-room-25":["Eleven tracks of live, jazzy instrumentation — piano trio, strings, flute, soft backing choirs — produced mainly by Phoelix. Noname's delivery is still quiet, but sharper and funnier than before: she writes about moving to Los Angeles, sex, Black womanhood, politics and self-discovery. Self is a witty opening statement; Blaxploitation and Prayer Song are political; Ace, with Smino and Saba, is playful.","Released independently in September 2018, it followed Noname's move from Chicago to Los Angeles and her first adult relationship, according to her interviews. She would later found Noname's Book Club and increasingly turn her attention from music to activism. Phoelix, a Chicago producer and multi-instrumentalist, again led the arrangements, with live strings and a small jazz band recorded rather than programmed, and guests from her Chicago circle, including Smino and Saba.","Listen to the bass and drums: the band plays like a jazz trio behind a singer, pushing and pulling tempo, and some tracks change groove mid-song. The title refers to a room she lived in; the album is about what happens in private spaces. Some tracks end with long instrumental passages, jazz tags rather than hip-hop fades.","It was widely named among the best albums of 2018, often praised as the best rap album by a woman that year. Critics highlighted its intelligence and musicianship; some noted the quietness might make it easy to underrate. Later assessments have treated it as a high point of the Chicago jazz-rap scene of the late 2010s, and her later ambivalence about the music industry has only added to its standing.","Start with Self and Blaxploitation, then Montego Bae. Listen for the band. Compare A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory for an earlier jazz-rap fusion, and Little Simz's GREY Area. Notice how the drummer shifts the groove under her, and how often she lands a joke at the end of a serious line."],"kraftwerk-autobahn":["The title track fills the whole first side: some twenty-two minutes of a motorway journey rendered in sound. A car door slams, an engine turns over, a horn sounds, and then a steady, rolling pulse sets off under a sunny major-key tune on the Minimoog, with voices chanting 'Wir fahr'n, fahr'n, fahr'n auf der Autobahn'. Passing traffic whooshes by in stereo. Side two holds shorter pieces, including the night-time 'Kometenmelodie' pieces and the pastoral 'Morgenspaziergang', with flute and birdsong.","Ralf Hütter and Florian Schneider had made three records of loose, organ-and-flute improvisation in the Düsseldorf Krautrock scene. For their fourth they bought a Minimoog and recorded with producer Conny Plank at his farmhouse studio near Cologne, joined by Klaus Röder and Wolfgang Flür. West Germany's motorways stood for postwar prosperity, and the group wanted a German modern music not borrowed from Anglo-American rock. An edited three-minute single found unexpected radio success in Britain and the United States.","The painter Emil Schult, who designed the cover, helped write the words, and the chanted refrain is often heard as a joke on the Beach Boys' 'Fun, fun, fun'. Listen for how little the harmony moves: the long track is built from a handful of chords, and its sense of travel comes from changing textures, a new synth line or filter sweep every minute or so, like scenery through the window.","Many British and American reviewers treated it as a novelty, a German oddity with a catchy hook. Within a decade the judgment had reversed: musicians from David Bowie to the Detroit techno producers cited it, and it is now usually named as the hinge where Kraftwerk left Krautrock behind. Debate continues over how much credit Conny Plank's engineering deserves, since the group later minimised his role.","Give the title track its full length, ideally on a drive or with headphones, and notice the stereo cars passing. Then compare the single edit to hear what was lost. The 2009 remaster is clean and punchy. Follow it with Trans-Europe Express to hear the pulse become fully mechanical, and with Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge to hear what the German electronic avant-garde had already done with tape."],"kraftwerk-trans-europe-express":["Seven tracks in two halves. Side one is a postcard of old Europe: the wistful 'Europe Endless', the eerie 'Hall of Mirrors' and the stiff-limbed 'Showroom Dummies'. Side two is one continuous rail journey: 'Trans-Europe Express' sets a clanking, metallic rhythm going, it flows into the harsher 'Metal on Metal', and then into 'Franz Schubert' and a short coda. The recognisable sound is the train itself, a pounding electronic beat with a melody that falls and rises like a whistle.","By 1977 the line-up was Ralf Hütter, Florian Schneider, Karl Bartos and Wolfgang Flür, working at their own Kling Klang studio in Düsseldorf. The album was an argument for European identity at a time when pop meant Britain and America; the TEE was a real network of luxury international trains. Punk was raging in London; Kraftwerk answered with suits, short hair and total control. Bowie and Iggy Pop, both in Berlin, were friends and admirers.","The lyric of the title track names Iggy Pop and David Bowie, whom the group had met. Listen too for the album's circularity: 'Europe Endless' opens it and the brief 'Endless Endless' closes it with a ghostly reprise, so the journey ends where it began. In 1982 Afrika Bambaataa and the Soulsonic Force rebuilt the title melody into 'Planet Rock', one of the founding records of electro and hip-hop production.","It sold modestly in Britain and America on release, and some reviewers found its coolness sinister. Its afterlife is enormous: through 'Planet Rock' it reached the Bronx, through Detroit radio it reached Juan Atkins and Derrick May, and through Bowie it reached British post-punk. Critics now often call it the album where electronic pop became a complete aesthetic, image and sound together.","Start with side two, the rail sequence, as one piece: the title track, 'Metal on Metal' and 'Franz Schubert'. Listen for the moment the beat turns from mechanical to almost industrial. Then play 'Planet Rock' to hear what the Bronx did with it, and Honegger's Pacific 231 for a much older musical locomotive. The German-language edition, Trans Europa Express, is worth comparing."],"kraftwerk-the-man-machine":["Six tracks, tighter and more danceable than anything the group had made. 'The Robots' opens with a vocoder voice declaring 'We are the robots' over a jerky, sequenced bass line. 'Spacelab' and 'Metropolis' are glittering instrumentals; 'The Model' is a three-minute pop song about a fashion model, with a cheerful synthesizer riff; 'Neon Lights' is a long, slow, glowing hymn to a city at night; the title track closes on a chanted mechanical groove.","Recorded at Kling Klang in Düsseldorf, it arrived as disco ruled the clubs and Giorgio Moroder's machine-driven productions were topping charts; Kraftwerk were listening. The cover, red and black with the four members in identical shirts and ties, borrowed the style of the Russian constructivist El Lissitzky. The group had begun presenting themselves as robots, later using mannequins as stand-ins, turning the human-machine question into both theme and theatre.","'The Model' was a minor track at first; reissued as a single in 1981 it reached number one in Britain, years after it was recorded. Listen for the Russian words whispered in 'The Robots', a nod to the constructivist theme. 'Metropolis' takes its name from Fritz Lang's 1927 film, whose robot Maria is a direct ancestor of the group's persona.","Reviewers who had mocked Kraftwerk as cold began to hear humour in the deadpan. The album is now regarded as the blueprint for British synth-pop: the Human League, Depeche Mode and Orchestral Manoeuvres in the Dark all took from it. Some fans prefer its sleek pop focus; others miss the long, landscape pieces of the earlier records.","Begin with 'Neon Lights', the most tender thing the group recorded, and notice how few elements it uses. Then play 'The Robots' loudly for its bass line. Compare with Yellow Magic Orchestra's Solid State Survivor, made a year later in Tokyo, and with Moroder's work of the same years. The 2009 remaster is the usual choice."],"kraftwerk-computer-world":["Seven tracks about the arrival of computers in everyday life. The title song lists 'interpol and Deutsche Bank, FBI and Scotland Yard' in a flat voice; 'Pocket Calculator' is played partly on a toy calculator; 'Numbers' is a stark rhythm track with a synthetic voice counting in several languages; 'Computer Love' sets a lonely-hearts lyric to one of the group's loveliest melodies. The whole record is lean and percussive, its beats sharper and more syncopated than before.","It took three years, as Kraftwerk rebuilt their studio to be portable for touring. Home computers were just appearing, and data surveillance was a live political issue in West Germany. Meanwhile, the Detroit DJ the Electrifying Mojo was playing Kraftwerk on the radio alongside funk, and in New York electro producers were listening closely. The record reached a new, Black American dance audience the group had never directly addressed.","The syncopated drum pattern of 'Numbers', combined with the melody of Trans-Europe Express, is the skeleton of Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock', and Juan Atkins and Derrick May, founders of Detroit techno, have named this album as a key influence. Listen for the counting in 'Numbers': it runs through German, French, Japanese, Russian and more, a small world tour of machine voices. Coldplay later built 'Talk' on the 'Computer Love' melody, with credit.","Reviews were respectful rather than excited; the record sold solidly without matching the singles success of the previous one. Its reputation has grown steadily, especially in the United States, as critics traced the line from 'Numbers' through electro and Miami bass to techno. It is now often called Kraftwerk's most prophetic album, predicting both the personal computer and the anxieties of data.","Play 'Numbers' first, loud, and try not to move. Then 'Computer Love' for the melody. Follow with 'Planet Rock' and an early Juan Atkins record to hear the inheritance directly. The 2009 remaster keeps the dry, close sound; the 1991 re-recordings on The Mix are a different, glossier experience."],"wendy-carlos-switched-on-bach":["Ten pieces by Johann Sebastian Bach realised entirely on a Moog synthesizer: the Sinfonia from Cantata No. 29, the Air on the G String, three Two-Part Inventions, 'Jesu, Joy of Man's Desiring', 'Wachet auf', two preludes and fugues from The Well-Tempered Clavier, and the whole Third Brandenburg Concerto. Each contrapuntal line has its own colour, some reedy, some bell-like, some buzzing, so the polyphony becomes vividly clear, as though an organ had been taken apart and every stop given its own speaker.","The Moog could play only one note at a time and drifted out of tune as it warmed up. Carlos and her producer Rachel Elkind, with musicologist Benjamin Folkman, built each piece line by line on an eight-track tape machine in a small New York studio, over months. Columbia expected a curiosity. Instead it became one of the best-selling classical records ever made and won three Grammy Awards, putting the word 'Moog' into popular vocabulary.","In the Third Brandenburg Concerto, Bach left only two chords between the fast movements, where players traditionally improvise; Carlos filled the gap with a short electronic improvisation of her own, the most audibly 'synthesizer' moment on the record. Listen for the touch-sensitivity problem too: the early Moog had no dynamics from the keyboard, so every crescendo and accent had to be shaped by hand with knobs and envelope settings.","Glenn Gould praised it warmly, and many classical listeners heard Bach's inner lines more clearly than ever before; some purists called it a gimmick, and rock fans were surprised to find they liked Bach. It triggered a wave of cheaper 'Moog plays the hits' records that did its reputation no favours for a while. Today it is recognised as the record that made the synthesizer a serious instrument in public eyes.","Start with the Third Brandenburg, then play a period-instrument recording straight after and notice what each makes clearer. The Air on the G String shows her lyrical side. Seek the remastered edition overseen by Carlos herself, released in the Switched-On Boxed Set. Compare Stockhausen's Kontakte to hear what the classical studio was doing with electronics at the same time."],"wendy-carlos-a-clockwork-orange":["Music for Stanley Kubrick's film of Anthony Burgess's novel, with an expanded album of Carlos's full-length versions. It opens with Purcell's Music for the Funeral of Queen Mary, turned into a slow, eerie synthesizer processional. Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, the hero Alex's obsession, appears with its choral finale sung by a vocoder, a machine voice singing Schiller's words. The original 'Timesteps' is a long, dark, searching piece, and Rossini's William Tell Overture is sped up to comic mania.","Kubrick heard Carlos and Rachel Elkind's work after Switched-On Bach and invited them onto a film already built around classical music. The novel's theme, a violent youth conditioned by the state to feel sick when he hears Beethoven, suited a score where familiar music is made strange by machines. Only parts of the music reached the finished film, so Carlos released a fuller album. It was released under the name Walter Carlos.","The vocoder, which splits a voice into frequency bands and uses them to shape a synthesizer, had begun as a telephone and wartime encryption technology; this was among its first uses in music, years before Kraftwerk made it famous. Listen for 'Timesteps', which Carlos had begun as an independent piece before the film project came along, which is why it feels less like a film cue and more like a concert work.","The film was hugely controversial, which drew attention to the score. Classical listeners debated whether synthesizer Beethoven was sacrilege or a sharp critique of how culture is consumed; film critics saw how the machine timbres mirrored Alex's conditioning. The full-length album has come to be valued as Carlos's most personal early statement, and 'Timesteps' as a real composition.","Start with 'Timesteps' for Carlos's own voice, then the vocoder Beethoven, and play the ending of the real Ninth Symphony next to it. Watch the film's opening credits to hear the Purcell over the red screen. The Wendy Carlos's Complete Original Score edition includes material the film did not use."],"wendy-carlos-sonic-seasonings":["A double album of four long pieces, one per season, each filling an entire side at around twenty minutes. Spring, Summer, Fall and Winter blend recorded natural sounds, rain, surf, birds, insects, wind and distant thunder, with slow synthesizer textures that sometimes imitate those sounds and sometimes float a melody above them. Nothing is dramatic: the pieces drift, change weather and fade, closer to a landscape seen from a window than to a composition that argues.","Carlos and Rachel Elkind made it in New York while working on commercial and film projects after the success of Switched-On Bach. Columbia released it as a double album in 1972, when there was no category for it; environmental recordings of surf and forest were selling as relaxation records, and minimalism was growing in downtown New York. Vivaldi's Four Seasons is the obvious ancestor in title, though not in sound.","Listen for the places where you cannot tell the recorded world from the synthesizer: a wind that is actually filtered noise, a bird call that is an oscillator. Carlos designed the synthetic sounds to sit inside the natural ones rather than above them, a quieter use of the Moog than anything on her Bach records, and in 'Summer' a gentle melody surfaces only after minutes of pure atmosphere.","It puzzled reviewers and sold modestly compared with the Bach records. Its reputation rose once Brian Eno named ambient music later in the decade; critics now regularly cite it as a proto-ambient, proto-new age record, arriving several years before Discreet Music. Some listeners find it too gentle; others treat it as the first ambient album in all but name.","Put on 'Spring' at low volume while you read or cook, and let it recede; then come back and listen hard to one five-minute stretch. Compare Eno's Music for Airports and Suzanne Ciani's Seven Waves. The Sonic Seasonings reissue from East Side Digital adds a later companion piece."],"delia-derbyshire-doctor-who-theme":["A galloping bass line, a swooping melody that climbs like a voice in a wind tunnel, and hissing bursts of filtered noise. It lasts a couple of minutes and has never been forgotten. Every sound was made without a synthesizer or keyboard: the bass is a single plucked string, recorded and then transposed to each pitch by changing tape speed; the melody is an oscillator shaped by hand; the washes are white noise swept through filters.","Ron Grainer wrote the theme for a new BBC children's science-fiction series, first broadcast in November 1963, handing over a single page with the tune and descriptions like 'wind bubble' and 'cloud'. Delia Derbyshire realised it at the BBC Radiophonic Workshop in Maida Vale, with Dick Mills assisting, cutting every note from tape and splicing them by the hundreds. Television needed sounds nobody had heard before, and the Workshop was built for exactly that.","Grainer is reported to have asked 'Did I write that?' on hearing it, and to have offered Derbyshire co-credit, which the BBC refused because Workshop staff were anonymous. She was not credited on screen. Listen for tiny differences between the bass notes: each pitch was a separate length of tape, cut and joined by hand. The melody's slightly unsteady swoops come from oscillators tuned by hand rather than played from a keyboard.","It was an instant fixture of British television and a favourite of children. Derbyshire's authorship became widely known only late in her life, and she has since become a heroine for electronic musicians, especially women, from Orbital, who played the theme live, to many younger producers. Musicologists now treat the original as a landmark of British tape music; later arrangements, however polished, are generally thought weaker.","Hear the 1963 original first, then any later version, and notice how the modern ones smooth away the handmade wobble. Try to separate the three layers: bass, melody and noise. Follow with Varèse's Poème électronique and Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge, tape-studio works from the continental studios that pointed the way."],"delia-derbyshire-the-dreams":["A radio collage in which ordinary people describe their dreams: of running, falling, drowning, of landscapes and of colour. Their voices are cut, overlapped and repeated, and around them Derbyshire built an electronic dream-space of hums, pulses, glassy tones and rushing noise. It moves through sections on distinct kinds of dream, so the result is closer to a composed piece of music for voices than to a documentary.","The writer Barry Bermange recorded the interviews and devised the idea; it was one of his Inventions for Radio, made with the BBC Radiophonic Workshop for the Third Programme, the BBC's arts channel. Radio in the early 1960s was still the home of adventurous sound work, and the Workshop had been founded in 1958 for exactly such radio drama. Derbyshire, then in her mid-twenties, was fresh from the Doctor Who theme.","Listen for how the edits themselves become rhythm: a phrase like 'running away' is chopped and looped until the words turn into music, the same trick Steve Reich was using at almost the same time in San Francisco. Derbyshire also transposed some voices down so that they merge with the electronic drones, blurring speaker and sound.","It passed almost unnoticed outside BBC radio listeners for decades. Revival came with the rediscovery of Derbyshire's archive after her death in 2001, and it is now valued as a key piece of British tape music and a predecessor of later sound collage and sampling. Some critics hear it as a British equivalent of the Paris musique concrète studio's radio work.","Listen in the dark with headphones, as it was made for radio, not for a concert hall. Follow the speakers' voices as if they were instruments. Compare Reich's early tape pieces and Lucier's I Am Sitting in a Room, where speech also turns into sound, and Luciano Berio's tape work if you know it."],"white-noise-an-electric-storm":["Seven tracks that begin as psychedelic pop songs and end in an electronic nightmare. 'Love Without Sound' opens with sped-up strings and close harmonies; 'My Game of Loving' layers breathy voices into an erotic tape collage; 'Firebird' and 'Your Hidden Dreams' are dreamy songs with strange electronic halos. The long closing piece, 'Black Mass: An Electric Storm in Hell', is pure noise and percussion, a swirl of screams, drums and oscillators.","David Vorhaus, an American bassist in London, heard Delia Derbyshire and Brian Hodgson of the BBC Radiophonic Workshop give a talk and proposed a band. They worked partly in Vorhaus's own small studio in Camden and partly at the Workshop after hours. Island Records had signed them on the strength of a demo and, reportedly, pressed for a quick finish, which is why the last piece was put together fast. It was 1969, the year of psychedelia's peak.","The gentle vocal harmonies are often not multiple singers at all but one or two voices speed-shifted and layered by tape, a technique straight out of the Radiophonic toolkit. Listen for how 'Black Mass' uses real drums alongside electronics, and for the stereo panning throughout: the group planned movements across the speakers rather than fixed placements, which gives the record its unsettled, circling feeling.","It sold slowly but steadily and gained a cult reputation over decades among musicians rather than critics. It has since been reclaimed as a key record of British electronic psychedelia, often cited by later groups who mixed pop song with vintage electronics. Critics sometimes find the songs thin, but the tape techniques remain striking and the closing track anticipates industrial music.","Start with 'Love Without Sound' for the fusion of song and tape, then brave 'Black Mass' at a decent volume. Compare the Beatles' 'Tomorrow Never Knows' era and Silver Apples. Later reissues on Island are cleaner; the original stereo mix is essential for the panning."],"tangerine-dream-phaedra":["Four instrumental pieces, led by the sixteen-minute title track. It begins with murky, shifting electronic tone before a Moog sequencer sets off a bubbling bass pattern that repeats and slowly mutates while Mellotron choirs and flutes drift above. Nothing like a song, it feels like travelling through a dark, glittering space. 'Mysterious Semblance at the Strand of Nightmares' is a slow Mellotron dream; 'Movements of a Visionary' and 'Sequent C'' are shorter, eerier sound studies.","Edgar Froese, Christopher Franke and Peter Baumann had just signed to Richard Branson's young Virgin label and recorded at the Manor, Virgin's studio in rural Oxfordshire. They had bought a Moog modular with a sequencer, an expensive and temperamental instrument. The record reached the British album charts with almost no radio play, carried by word of mouth and the new underground taste for German experimental rock.","The title track was reportedly shaped by accident: the Moog's oscillators drifted in pitch as the machine warmed up, and the group recorded the wandering tuning rather than fixing it. Listen for how the sequencer pattern very slowly changes pitch and filter over minutes, and for the tape-recorder hiss, part of the atmosphere. The whole track is essentially a single improvisation, lightly edited.","Some rock critics found it formless, but it sold unexpectedly well in Britain and was later seen as the founding document of the Berlin School. Its sequencer patterns anticipate trance and ambient techno by two decades, and producers from the early rave scene have named it. Debate continues over whether its looseness is a strength or a limitation compared with Kraftwerk's precision.","Listen to the title track in the dark, from the formless opening until the sequence emerges, and notice when you stop hearing it as repetition. Compare Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air for the organ-loop ancestor, and Klaus Schulze's Timewind. The remaster in the In Search of Hades box set of the Virgin years is well regarded."],"tangerine-dream-rubycon":["Two long parts, one per side, each around seventeen minutes. Part One opens with gong washes and prepared-piano clatter, then a sequencer pulse arrives and builds to a driving climax before dissolving; Part Two begins with dark, choral Mellotron chords, almost sacred, and then gathers another sequence under guitars and organ. The whole record feels like one slow, flowing current, more focused and more dramatic than Phaedra.","Made by the same trio of Edgar Froese, Christopher Franke and Peter Baumann, again for Virgin, in the wake of Phaedra's surprising success. The group had begun playing concerts in cathedrals in France and Britain, some with audiences of thousands, and their music was increasingly composed for performance rather than purely in the studio. Mid-1970s Britain was hungry for long instrumental albums from Pink Floyd to Mike Oldfield.","The title puns on the Rubicon, the river Caesar crossed on the point of no return, and the record is built like a crossing: a quiet shore, a swelling passage, and a changed landscape on the other side. Listen for the prepared piano, with objects placed on its strings, which links the album to John Cage's technique in a way few rock listeners expect.","It became the group's highest-charting album in Britain and is often named by fans as their best. Critics praise its cohesion over Phaedra's loose experiments. Its structure, long builds and releases over a continuous pulse, has been compared to later trance and to the minimalist process pieces of Reich and Glass, though it is more improvised than either.","Hear the whole of Part One without interruption and notice where the pulse first becomes regular. Then compare Glass's Music in Twelve Parts, from the same period and also built on patterns, but acoustic and exact. The remaster in the In Search of Hades box set of the Virgin years is well regarded."],"klaus-schulze-irrlicht":["Three long pieces of slowly shifting drone, subtitled a quadrophonic symphony for orchestra and electronic machines. There are no synthesizers: Schulze plays sustained organ chords and feeds a recording of an orchestra through filters until it becomes a glowing, unrecognisable haze. The sound swells and fades in waves, sometimes shining, sometimes grimly dark. There is no beat anywhere, only pressure and slow change, like light moving across a room.","Having drummed with Tangerine Dream and Ash Ra Tempel, Schulze went solo in 1971 with almost no money and no synthesizer. He recorded the orchestral material from a student orchestra rehearsal and processed it through damaged equipment in Berlin. It was released on the Ohr label, home of Krautrock's most experimental records, just as West German youth culture was inventing a music outside both Anglo-American rock and the classical avant-garde.","Listen for the orchestra, which you may not recognise at all: in places you can just make out bowed strings, but Schulze filtered them so heavily that the players become part of the drone. The title means will-o'-the-wisp, a ghostly light over marshes, and the record's glimmering, deceptive textures follow that image. Its clouds of sustained sound share something with Ligeti's orchestral writing of the previous decade.","It was barely reviewed outside Germany at first. Later critics have seen it as one of the first dark ambient or drone records, a precursor of later noise and isolationist ambient music. Some listeners find it too static; others hear it as Schulze's purest statement before sequencers took over his style.","Play it loud in a dark room and let the drones fill the space. Listen for the moment, in the first piece, when the organ chord slowly shifts. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères and Lux aeterna, which share the idea of sound as slowly moving clouds, and Tangerine Dream's Zeit from the same year."],"klaus-schulze-timewind":["Two side-long pieces, each about half an hour. 'Bayreuth Return' starts with a sequencer pattern that builds slowly, then Schulze solos and layers above it in waves of shimmering, droning sound. 'Wahnfried 1883' is darker and more static, a vast, mournful drone that ebbs and swells like an organ in a cathedral. The textures are rich and slightly unstable, the pulses hypnotic, and the length is part of the meaning.","Schulze now had synthesizers and a sequencer, and could at last make the long, pulsing pieces he had been reaching for with organ and tape. The titles are homage to Richard Wagner: Bayreuth is Wagner's festival town, Wahnfried his house there, and 1883 the year of his death. Schulze, like many German musicians of his generation, was working out what could be done with a German Romantic inheritance after the war.","Wagner's ideas of endless melody and slowly transforming harmony lie beneath the whole album; listen for how 'Bayreuth Return' keeps shifting without clear cadences, like a Wagnerian transition that never arrives. Schulze worked largely by improvising over running sequences, and small drifts and irregularities were kept rather than corrected, part of his belief that a performance's life matters more than precision.","It sold well across Europe and was especially admired in France, where Schulze had a devoted following. Critics consider it the album where his mature style arrived. Its Wagner homage has divided listeners, some finding it grandiose, others seeing it as an honest attempt to join electronic music to the European symphonic tradition. Its half-hour spans were later echoed in trance and ambient techno.","Hear 'Bayreuth Return' in one sitting, and notice how the slowly changing sequence keeps you waiting. Then play the Prelude to Wagner's Tristan und Isolde if you know it, to hear the model. Compare Tangerine Dream's Phaedra and Rubycon. Later CD reissues add bonus material from the same period."],"vangelis-chariots-of-fire":["The soundtrack album for Hugh Hudson's film about two British runners at the 1924 Paris Olympics. The title theme, 'Titles', rides on a repeated, slowly pulsing piano figure while the CS-80 plays a broad, brassy melody that swells to triumph. Other pieces are gentler: reflective, chiming, sometimes melancholy synthesizer pieces, with echoing piano. The album closes with an extended version of the main theme.","Hudson wanted modern music for a period film, and Vangelis recorded it alone at his Nemo Studios in London, improvising and building layers. The choice of synthesizers for a film set in the 1920s was bold, but it lifted the film out of its period. The main theme was released as a single and topped the American charts in 1982, and the score won the Academy Award.","Listen for the gentle piano figure underneath the main theme, which keeps going almost unchanged; the drama comes entirely from what the synthesizer layers above it. The CS-80's pressure-sensitive keyboard lets Vangelis swell each note, so the brassy crescendos are played by touch rather than by turning knobs. The anachronism is the point: a 1920s story scored with the sound of 1981.","It won the Oscar for best original score, a remarkable result for an electronic score at the time. Its theme became a cliché, used for any slow-motion triumph, and parodied endlessly; that fame has sometimes obscured its craft. Film-music writers now credit it with opening Hollywood to synthesizer scores through the 1980s.","Hear the main theme first and listen for the piano ostinato, then how the melody rides above it. Then try the quieter tracks for Vangelis's reflective side. Compare his Blade Runner, a year later and very different in mood, and Wendy Carlos's Clockwork Orange for an earlier synthesized film score."],"vangelis-blade-runner":["The score for Ridley Scott's science-fiction film, set in a rain-soaked Los Angeles of 2019. Wide, reverberant synthesizer chords evoke a city of towering buildings; the 'Main Titles' open with booming bells and a slowly rising fanfare; the 'Love Theme' is a sultry saxophone melody over soft synth; 'Tales of the Future' has an Arabic-inflected vocal line. Dialogue from the film threads through the album, and the ending theme drives forward on a pounding pulse.","Vangelis recorded the score at Nemo Studios in London, improvising to the images. For legal and business reasons the original score was not released on record in 1982; an orchestral re-recording by other musicians appeared instead, and fans circulated bootlegs for years. Vangelis finally released his own album in 1994. Early 1980s cinema was discovering synthesizers, and Vangelis had just won an Oscar for Chariots of Fire.","The 1994 album is not simply the film's music: Vangelis re-edited it and wove in lines of dialogue, making it a new work with the film's voices. Listen for the 'Love Theme', played on saxophone by Dick Morrissey, and for the singer Demis Roussos, Vangelis's old bandmate in Aphrodite's Child, on 'Tales of the Future'. The 'Memories of Green' track had appeared on an earlier Vangelis album.","The film was a commercial disappointment at first but became a cult classic, and its score's reputation grew with it. Critics now rank it among the most influential film scores of the 1980s, its sound of rain, neon and loneliness echoed across later science-fiction films and in electronic genres from synthwave to ambient.","Start with 'Main Titles' and the 'Love Theme', then the 'End Titles'. Watch the film's opening with the city at night to hear how score and image fuse. Compare Wendy Carlos's Tron and Glass's Koyaanisqatsi, from the same year. The 25th Anniversary three-disc edition adds unreleased music."],"jean-michel-jarre-oxygene":["Six parts played almost continuously. Part I opens with slowly shifting chords and whooshing filtered noise. Part IV, the famous one, sets a bouncy rhythm-box beat under a simple, catchy synthesizer tune, with swoops and bubbles above. Other parts are dreamy and slow. The sound is warm, spacious and melodic, closer to a pop record than to the experimental music Jarre trained in, but full of textural detail.","Jarre recorded it in a makeshift studio in his Paris flat, on a small budget, and several labels turned it down. Francis Dreyfus's small Disques Motors label released it in late 1976, and it became an international bestseller. Jarre had studied at the Groupe de Recherches Musicales under Pierre Schaeffer, founder of musique concrète, and brought the studio's tricks, filtered noise and tape effects, into a melodic setting.","The rhythm under Part IV comes from a cheap Korg preset rhythm box meant to accompany home organists, the kind of drum machine a serious studio would sneer at. Listen for the wind-and-sea whooshes, made with filtered white noise on the EMS synthesizer, a trick straight from the musique concrète studio. The cover painting by Michel Granger, of an earth peeling to reveal a skull, hints at the ecological theme.","Some critics dismissed it as lightweight, and admirers of the Paris experimental studio saw it as a turn to easy melody. But it sold in the millions and is often cited as one of the best-selling French albums. It opened a mass market for instrumental electronic music, and producers of 1990s ambient house cited its warmth and melody.","Start with Part IV, then go back to Part I to hear the slow opening that sets the mood. Listen for the whooshes and sweeps. Compare Tangerine Dream's Phaedra, darker and less tuneful, and Vangelis's Chariots of Fire. The later sequel albums, Oxygène 7–13 and 3, are interesting but less fresh."],"jean-michel-jarre-equinoxe":["Eight parts forming a continuous piece. The opening is misty and slow; Part IV introduces a pulsing sequencer pattern and a bright melody; Part V has a tune that sounds almost like a folk song. The last parts include a hurdy-gurdy-like fairground melody and a return to the opening mist. Sequencers now drive much of the music, more rhythmic and propulsive than Oxygène, and the textures are richer, with more layers and stereo movement.","After Oxygène's success Jarre had more money and new machines, including a custom sequencer built by his collaborator Michel Geiss. He recorded again in Paris, aiming for a darker, more rhythmic record. In 1979 he performed it at a free concert on the Place de la Concorde in Paris before a vast crowd, the first of his giant city events with lights and projections.","The cover, by Michel Granger, shows crowds of tiny figures with binoculars staring out, a hint that the album is about watching and being watched. Listen in the final parts for the sound of rain and a fairground organ, a return to Jarre's musique concrète training. The fairground tune is gently ironic, a nod to French popular traditions.","It sold well, though less than Oxygène, and critics found it more assured if less surprising. Fans often prefer it for its rhythmic drive, which points toward the sequenced dance music of the following decade. Some find its melodies too sweet; others hear in its pulsing patterns the missing link between the Berlin School and synth-pop. It remains one of his most admired records.","Hear Parts IV and V together to follow the move from pulse to melody, and listen for the rain and the fairground at the end. Compare Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, released the same year and more severe, and go back to Oxygène to hear how much the sequencer changed his music in two years."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-yellow-magic-orchestra":["The trio's debut, bright and cheeky. It opens with arcade-game bleeps and explosions, then launches into 'Firecracker', a cover of Martin Denny's 1950s exotica tune reimagined as clipped, sequenced synth-pop. 'Tong Poo' is a fast, Chinese-flavoured instrumental; 'La Femme Chinoise' pokes fun at Western clichés of East Asia. The rhythms are exact and bouncy, the melodies catchy, and the whole record is shot through with humour.","Haruomi Hosono formed the group with Yukihiro Takahashi and Ryuichi Sakamoto, with Hideki Matsutake programming a Roland MC-8 computer sequencer, one of the first. It was released on the Alfa label in Tokyo in 1978 and reissued in the United States the next year in a remixed version. Japan was at the peak of its electronics boom, and arcade games were becoming a craze.","The 'yellow magic' of the name answered 'black magic' and 'white magic', a way of claiming a new Asian pop identity. Listen for how 'Firecracker' keeps Denny's exotica tune but removes its jungle sounds, replacing them with video-game noises, a sly comment on how the West imagined Asia. The American mix differs noticeably from the Japanese original.","It sold modestly at first, then boomed after the group's international tour. Its 'Computer Game' opening is often named among the first pop uses of video-game sounds. Hip-hop and electro DJs in New York took notice, and Afrika Bambaataa played 'Firecracker' in his sets. It is now seen as the start of Japanese electronic pop and an influence on video-game music.","Hear 'Firecracker' first, then Martin Denny's original to hear the transformation. Listen for the arcade sounds at the opening and how quickly they give way to a tune. Compare Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, made the same year in Düsseldorf, and follow with Solid State Survivor. Try both the Japanese and American mixes."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-solid-state-survivor":["Eight tracks, tighter and more song-based than the debut. 'Technopolis' opens with a vocoder chanting 'To-ki-o', setting a fast, bright electronic beat; 'Rydeen' is a galloping instrumental with a sweeping, cinematic melody; 'Behind the Mask' has a stiff robotic groove and a haunting tune. They also cover the Beatles' 'Day Tripper' in a jerky, mechanised style. The playing is precise, but the melodies are warm.","Recorded in Tokyo in 1979 with Hideki Matsutake again programming the computer sequencer. The English lyrics were written by the poet Chris Mosdell. It became one of the best-selling albums in Japan that year and made the three members national celebrities; its success helped launch 'technopop' as a Japanese phenomenon. Tokyo was fast becoming a futuristic, neon-lit city, and the record celebrated it with wit rather than awe.","'Behind the Mask' had a long afterlife: Michael Jackson wrote new lyrics for it during the Thriller sessions, and his version was released only posthumously; Eric Clapton and Greg Phillinganes also recorded it. Listen for how 'Technopolis' treats Tokyo the way Kraftwerk treated the autobahn, as a subject for machine music.","It won strong praise in Japan and growing attention abroad, where listeners had assumed electronic pop was a German speciality. Critics today see it as the peak of the group's pop phase, the moment their humour and precision were in perfect balance. It has influenced British synth-pop, later J-pop and electro, and its bright melodies can be heard behind decades of Japanese game music.","Hear 'Technopolis' and 'Rydeen' first, listening for how the melodies ride above the exact machine rhythm, then 'Behind the Mask' for its stiff, eerie groove. Compare the Beatles' 'Day Tripper' with their cover and notice what the mechanised version reveals about the riff. Then play Kraftwerk's Computer World to hear the two groups in conversation."],"yellow-magic-orchestra-bgm":["A darker, stranger record than its predecessors. 'Ballet' is a slow, sad song with booming machine drums; 'Music Plans' and 'Cue' have muted, melancholy melodies; 'Rap Phenomena' is an early Japanese experiment with rap over electronic beats. The sound is deliberately murky and compressed, with heavy, booming kick drums and clicking percussion. It feels like background music, as the title (an abbreviation for 'background music') implies, turned eerie.","After two cheerful international successes the trio wanted to make something less commercial, and they recorded it in Tokyo early in 1981. The new Roland TR-808 drum machine, released in 1980, sits at the centre of the sound; BGM was among the first records built around it. Western synth-pop was booming, and the group were reacting against being seen simply as entertainers.","The 808, with its deep boom of a bass drum and crisp handclaps, would soon become the sound of hip-hop and electro; listen here for it in an entirely different context, slow and melancholy. The title is ironic: BGM is the Japanese abbreviation for background music, played in shops, and the group made a record that refuses to stay in the background.","Fans who loved the bright earlier hits were confused, and sales were lower. Critics now rate it among the group's most adventurous records, and it is cited for its early and creative use of the 808, before that machine became synonymous with hip-hop. It prefigured the darker, more introspective side of electronic pop in the 1980s, and many listeners now prefer it to the hits.","Start with 'Cue' and 'Ballet', and listen for the 808's deep, booming kick drum under the melancholy melodies. Then try 'Rap Phenomena' as a curiosity. Compare Kraftwerk's Computer World, released the same year, and any early electro record, such as 'Planet Rock', to hear what the same drum machine became in the Bronx."],"brian-eno-discreet-music":["Side one is a single piece, over half an hour long: two short, gentle synthesizer melodies, fed into a pair of tape recorders that repeat them with delays, so they overlap and slowly change against each other, filtered and softened. The result is calm, uneventful and quietly beautiful. Side two is three variations on Pachelbel's Canon, played by a small string ensemble conducted by Gavin Bryars, with the parts slowed, rearranged and overlapped.","Eno released it on his own Obscure label, set up to issue experimental music by composers such as Bryars and Michael Nyman. In the liner notes he explains the idea: lying in bed after an accident, he listened to a harp record played too quietly to hear properly, with rain outside, and heard music becoming part of the environment. He had also learned tape-loop techniques from working with Robert Fripp.","The tape-delay system, two machines passing sound between them, had been used by Terry Riley and was the heart of Eno's collaborations with Fripp. Listen for how the melodies sometimes drop out, change filter, or overlap with themselves, entirely produced by the system rather than composed note by note. Eno's liner notes include a diagram of the system, an invitation to copy it.","It passed almost unnoticed at first, but it is now seen as the first ambient album, the start of a lineage leading to Music for Airports, ambient house and much else. Some find the Pachelbel variations less successful than the title piece; others value them as a gently subversive commentary on overplayed classical music.","Play the title piece at low volume while doing something else, and notice when you start to hear it again. Then hear Pachelbel's Canon in its original form and compare the Eno variations. Follow with Music for Airports and Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air."],"brian-eno-ambient-1-music-for-airports":["Four pieces, titled only '1/1', '2/1', '1/2' and '2/2'. In '1/1', a few piano notes, played by Robert Wyatt and slowed on tape, repeat and overlap in a calm, spacious texture. '2/1' uses wordless women's voices, each on a tape loop of a different length, so they combine in ever-changing chords. '1/2' combines piano and voices; '2/2' uses soft synthesizer. The whole record is quiet, unhurried, and beautiful to pay attention to or to ignore.","Eno conceived the idea waiting in the Cologne-Bonn airport, disliking the music played there, and wondered what music might suit a place where people might be anxious about flying. He recorded the album partly in Cologne and London. In the liner notes he defined ambient music as music that must be 'as ignorable as it is interesting'. It was installed at LaGuardia Airport's Marine Air Terminal in New York.","The tape loops are of different, unrelated lengths, which is why the voices in '2/1' never repeat the same combination: the system composes a piece that could, in principle, run forever without exact repetition. Listen for the ghostly quality of the piano in '1/1', created by slowing the tape. The idea descends from Satie's 'furniture music' and from Steve Reich's early phasing tape pieces.","Critics at first found it slight, and it did not sell widely. Over time it has become one of the most influential albums of its era, defining ambient music and inspiring genres from ambient techno to modern classical. In 1998 the ensemble Bang on a Can recorded it with live instruments, a sign that it had become a concert-hall work.","Play '1/1' at low volume, and notice how your attention comes and goes. Then listen closely to '2/1' and follow the voices. Compare Satie's Gymnopédies and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians. Bang on a Can's live version shows the music in a new light."],"brian-eno-ambient-2-plateaux-of-mirror":["Ten short pieces for piano by Harold Budd, treated by Eno so that each note hangs in an echoing, glowing halo. Budd plays slowly, softly, with pauses, chords that drift and hover. The sound is so soft and blurred that it sounds as if the piano were made of glass or water. It is lyrical and dreamy, closer to Debussy or Satie than to experimental music, but the treatment makes it strange.","Harold Budd was a California composer who had renounced the avant-garde for what he called soft music, slow and consonant. Eno invited him to make the second record in his Ambient series, with Daniel Lanois engineering. Budd played while Eno shaped the sound around him in real time. The Ambient series was Eno's attempt to make ambient music into a genre shared with other artists, not just one album of his own.","Listen for how the echoes seem to lag slightly behind the notes, as if the piano were in a vast, reflective hall; the effect is electronic, added as Budd played, so he was in effect duetting with his own halo. Much of the music was improvised. The piece titles, like 'First Light' and 'Not Yet Remembered', read like fragments of poems, and set the mood as surely as the sound.","It was seen at first as a slight follow-up to Music for Airports, pretty rather than radical. Critics now value it as a key ambient work and the ancestor of a whole school of treated, reverberant ambient piano heard in film and in modern classical recordings. Some still find it too sweet; admirers answer that its restraint is exactly the point. Budd and Eno collaborated again on The Pearl in 1984.","Play 'First Light' and 'Not Yet Remembered' and listen for how the echoes hang after each chord. Keep the volume low, as with Music for Airports, and then try a second, attentive listen. Compare Satie's Gymnopédies and Pärt's Für Alina, both quiet piano music built on space. Follow with The Pearl for the next step."],"brian-eno-ambient-4-on-land":["Eight pieces, each named for a place, many from Eno's memories of the East Anglian coast where he grew up: 'Lizard Point', 'Dunwich Beach, Autumn, 1960', 'Lantern Marsh'. Unlike earlier ambient records, it is dark and murky: low drones, slithering noises, the croaking of frogs, rustlings and hums, rather than melody. It sounds like a landscape at dusk, imperfectly remembered.","Eno was living in New York, working with Talking Heads and experimenting with sound processing. He described the pieces as attempts to evoke places through recorded sounds, processed and layered, rather than through melody. Contributors include the trumpeter Jon Hassell, whose Fourth World music Eno admired. The record completed the Ambient series, after Laraaji's Day of Radiance as Ambient 3, and pushed ambient music away from calm and toward mystery.","Listen for the found sounds: frogs, rustling, sticks, chains and more, processed until they sound like wildlife or weather. Eno used the studio to make the sounds feel as if they are coming from a distance, like a place in memory. The trumpet in some tracks is Hassell's, heavily treated.","It was admired by critics but did not sell widely. Its reputation has grown, particularly among makers of dark ambient and field-recording music, and it is now often called Eno's most personal ambient record, the one where the idea of music as place is fully realised. Some listeners find it too dark and formless after the gentle clarity of Music for Airports.","Listen at night, in the dark, with headphones, and let the sounds place you somewhere. Start with 'Lizard Point', then 'Dunwich Beach, Autumn, 1960'. Compare Oliveros's I of IV and Feldman's Rothko Chapel, two other ways of making sound feel like a space. Follow with Apollo, the next Eno ambient record."],"brian-eno-apollo":["Twelve pieces made for a documentary about the Apollo moon missions. Some are slow, ethereal washes of synthesizer, evoking the silence of space; others feature Daniel Lanois's pedal steel guitar, with its country-music twang, softened into something dreamy and floating. 'An Ending (Ascent)' is a slow, rising, hymn-like piece of held chords; 'Deep Blue Day' is a gentle slide-guitar melody that seems to float without gravity.","Eno made the album with his brother Roger Eno and Daniel Lanois, the Canadian producer who later worked with Eno on U2's records. It was intended as the soundtrack for Al Reinert's documentary built from NASA footage, which was eventually released as For All Mankind in 1989. Eno wanted to evoke not only the missions but the astronauts' experience, and to avoid the portentous, heroic scoring usual for space footage.","The pedal steel guitar is a country instrument; Eno used it partly because astronauts had taken country music with them into space, so their own taste is folded into the score. Listen for how Lanois's playing blurs into the synthesizers until you cannot tell which is which. 'Deep Blue Day' was later used in the film Trainspotting, in an unexpected and dreamlike underwater scene.","It became one of Eno's most beloved records, and 'An Ending (Ascent)' is widely used in films, documentaries and television, often for moments of grief or wonder. Critics consider it Eno's most emotionally direct ambient work, warmer than the earlier Ambient series. It has influenced film music and ambient pop, and its country-tinged steel guitar opened a lineage of so-called ambient Americana.","Start with 'An Ending (Ascent)' and 'Deep Blue Day', and listen for the pedal steel sliding under the synthesizer. Then hear the album whole, ideally watching For All Mankind. Compare Vangelis's Blade Runner and Glass's Koyaanisqatsi, two other film scores of the early 1980s. The 2019 reissue adds a second disc of new pieces."],"suzanne-ciani-buchla-concerts-1975":["Recordings of two live concerts Ciani gave in New York in 1975, playing the Buchla modular synthesizer alone. The music is improvised and flowing: bubbling, pulsing sequences, sounds that swirl and spin in space, sudden bursts and swoops, and moments of warm, lyrical melody. It sounds nothing like a keyboard instrument; the Buchla has no conventional keyboard, and Ciani treats it as a living, breathing system to be steered in real time.","Ciani had studied with Don Buchla in California and moved to New York, where she funded her work with commercial sound design. In 1975 she played in venues of the downtown experimental scene, including the loft of composer Phill Niblock. The recordings remained unreleased until 2016, when the Finders Keepers label issued them, part of a revival of interest in early female electronic musicians.","Listen for how she keeps sequences running while she adjusts other parts of the patch, making the music change continuously without any stop. The Buchla, unlike the Moog, was designed by its inventor to encourage experimental, non-keyboard music, and Ciani's playing shows why: it sounds more like weather or water than an instrument.","Its release in 2016 caused excitement, revealing a lost piece of history and a performer whose reputation had rested on commercials and new age albums. Critics now see it as proof of Ciani's pioneering role in live electronic performance, at a time when few played synthesizers on stage at all. It has inspired a new generation of modular synthesists, and Ciani herself returned to live Buchla concerts.","Listen with headphones and follow the sounds as they move between the speakers, and notice that nothing ever stops. Compare Morton Subotnick's Silver Apples of the Moon, another Buchla landmark, and Oliveros's electronic work. Then hear Seven Waves to hear how Ciani turned the same instrument toward melody."],"suzanne-ciani-seven-waves":["Seven pieces, one for each wave, each with its own mood. The sound is warm and liquid, with sequences that bubble and flow, lyrical melodies, and textures that seem to wash in and out like water. Some pieces are gentle and dreamy, others brighter and more rhythmic. The melodies have a Mediterranean warmth, and the sounds move across the stereo field.","It was Ciani's first solo album, made in New York after a decade of funding her work through commercial sound design for advertisements, films and games. She now wanted to make music of her own and to show that a synthesizer could sing. The album was later marketed as new age music, a growing genre in the 1980s, which brought her a loyal audience but also critical condescension that lasted decades.","The waves are not only ocean waves: Ciani meant them as waves of energy and feeling, and the seven pieces trace an emotional arc rather than a seascape. Listen for the vocoder in places, turning a human voice into a synthesizer texture, and for sounds that move around you in the stereo field, a legacy of her quadraphonic Buchla concerts.","It found a modest audience at first but led to a long career of albums. Critics in the 1980s often dismissed new age as background music, and Ciani was filed with it. Since the 2010s it has been re-evaluated alongside the rediscovery of her Buchla work, and her early albums are now seen as important, melodic contributions to electronic music by one of its few women pioneers.","Listen to the whole album in order, and notice how the moods change from piece to piece, bright to reflective. Pay attention to how the sounds are placed in space. Compare Wendy Carlos's Sonic Seasonings for another synthesizer view of nature, and Eno's ambient records for a cooler approach to calm."],"laurie-spiegel-kepler-harmony-of-the-worlds":["A short piece of computer music that turns the orbits of the planets into sound. Each planet's orbital speed, as described by Johannes Kepler in 1619, is translated into a sliding pitch, so the planets sing, sliding up and down as they speed up near the sun and slow down far from it. The sound is eerie and beautiful, a slow chorus of wavering tones.","Spiegel made it at Bell Labs, where she was working on computer music. It was chosen for the Voyager Golden Record, a record of Earth's sounds and music launched into space in 1977 on the two Voyager spacecraft, now beyond the solar system. The record, assembled by a team led by Carl Sagan, included music from Bach to Chuck Berry, and Spiegel's piece opened its section of the sounds of Earth.","Kepler, the seventeenth-century astronomer, believed the planets made music, and wrote out their melodies in his book Harmonices Mundi of 1619. Spiegel's piece realises his idea literally, using his data. Listen for the slow, wavering glides: each one is an orbit, rising as a planet races near the sun and falling as it slows, so the piece is a sounding diagram of Kepler's laws.","It is little known as music but famous as part of the Voyager record, one of the most widely discussed recordings ever made. Critics now see it as an early example of data sonification, turning scientific measurement into sound, and as a symbol of Spiegel's pioneering role in computer music at a time when very few women worked in the field.","Listen while imagining the planets circling the sun, and follow the glides as orbits. It is short, so hear it several times. Compare Holst's The Planets for a very different, astrological take on the same bodies. Then hear Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for the music she made with the same Bell Labs tools."],"laurie-spiegel-the-expanding-universe":["A set of pieces of computer music, each generated by Spiegel's algorithms, rules the computer uses to generate and vary music. 'Patchwork' is a lively, folk-like piece of interlocking patterns; 'Appalachian Grove' is gentle and modal, reminiscent of folk fiddle music; the title piece is a slow, spacious, evolving drone. The music is gentle and lyrical, not at all what one might expect of computer music in 1980.","Spiegel made the pieces at Bell Labs between 1974 and 1979, using the GROOVE system, designed by Max Mathews and F. Richard Moore, which let a mainframe computer control analogue synthesizers in real time. It was released on Philo, a folk label, in 1980, an unusual home that suits the music. In 2012 it was reissued in expanded form by Unseen Worlds, bringing it to a new audience.","Listen for how the patterns in 'Patchwork' shift and recombine, like a quilt; each variation is generated by the computer according to rules Spiegel wrote, but she chose and steered the results in real time. The folk colour comes from her background as a guitarist and lutenist. The computer was a partner in improvisation, not a replacement for the composer.","It was little noticed on release outside the small computer-music world. The 2012 reissue brought wide acclaim, and critics now see it as an important early work of algorithmic music that anticipated generative ambient music and later computer composition. Many were surprised by its warmth, which overturned assumptions about how early computer music sounds. It is now cited by a new generation of electronic musicians.","Start with 'Patchwork' and 'Appalachian Grove', and listen for how the patterns shift without ever quite repeating. Then try the title piece for its slow drift. Compare Terry Riley's In C and Steve Reich's Piano Phase, which share the idea of pattern and process, and Eno's Discreet Music, a system of a different kind."],"larry-levan-live-at-the-paradise-garage":["A recording of Larry Levan at work in the booth of the Paradise Garage, issued on two discs. What you hear is not a playlist but a performance: disco, soul and early electronic records flowing into one another, voices rising out of drum breaks, long instrumental passages left to breathe. Levan lets songs run far longer than radio would, repeats sections, and builds the room's energy in slow waves. The thing to recognise is patience: the mix never hurries toward a peak.","The Paradise Garage opened in a former parking garage on King Street in Manhattan in the late 1970s and became the spiritual home of New York's black and Latino gay dance crowd. There was no alcohol licence; people came to dance until morning. Its sound system, built by Richard Long, was legendary, and Levan tuned it obsessively. Disco was at its commercial height in 1979, and was about to suffer a violent backlash in the rest of America; the Garage simply carried on.","Levan treated the sound system as an instrument. On recordings like this you can sometimes hear him cutting the bass or the treble for a few bars, then slamming it back in, a trick that made a packed room scream. Later house DJs copied the move so universally that it is easy to forget someone had to invent it. The way a vocal track suddenly drops to its bare rhythm is also often his own edit, not the record.","For years the Garage survived only as memory and myth, told by people who were there; this release, decades later, let listeners check the legend against the evidence. Some found it less wild than the stories, a reminder that a DJ set is shaped by a room and a night that no recording keeps. Historians now treat Levan, alongside David Mancuso and Frankie Knuckles, as a founder of the modern DJ's art.","Do not skip around. Put it on at length, ideally loud, and notice how the transitions feel rather than which song is playing. Listen for the moments when a record is allowed to strip down to drums before the next one enters. Then hear the Peech Boys' Don't Make Me Wait, which Levan produced, and a Frankie Knuckles record, to hear the Garage's sound travel to Chicago."],"larry-levan-dont-make-me-wait":["A single by the New York Peech Boys, the group Larry Levan formed and produced around the singer Bernard Fowler and keyboardist Michael de Benedictus. It opens with a voice almost alone, then a heavy synthesised bass and bouncing handclaps arrive, drenched in echo. The song is a plea to a lover for patience, but the record's real subject is space: drums that hang in the air, a bass that swells and recedes, sounds that seem to bounce off distant walls. The echoing claps are its fingerprint.","By 1982 disco had been declared dead in the American mainstream, but in New York clubs it was mutating into something sparer and stranger. Levan was hearing Jamaican dub in the same years as synthesisers and cheap drum machines, and the Peech Boys record carries both. West End, a small New York dance label, released it. It was designed, first and foremost, to sound overwhelming on the Paradise Garage's sound system.","Listen to how the echo is used as an instrument rather than a garnish: the handclaps repeat and decay so that the rhythm seems to double itself. This is a dub producer's thinking, borrowed from Kingston studios, applied to a New York dance record. There are several mixes, and part of the fun is hearing how differently the same material can be arranged, with the vocal almost removed or pushed forward.","It was a club hit rather than a pop hit, but it became one of the defining sounds of the post-disco underground, now usually filed as 'garage' after the club that made it famous. Critics have since heard it as a missing link between disco and house, and between dub and dance music. Its bass sound in particular was widely imitated.","Start with the long twelve-inch mix, and wait for the bass. Pay attention to the handclaps and how they seem to ricochet. Then play New Order's Blue Monday from the following year for another record that grew out of the same New York clubs, and Frankie Knuckles's Your Love to hear where this spaciousness went next."],"juan-atkins-cybotron-clear":["An instrumental by Cybotron, Juan Atkins's duo with Rick Davis. A crisp, clattering drum machine sets a quick electro beat; a bubbling synthesised bass locks in underneath; above, a chilly, stately melody moves in long lines, like a signal broadcast across empty space. Sounds pan and flicker between the speakers. There are no vocals, only the sense of a clean, geometric machine-world. It lasts around five minutes and never raises its voice.","Atkins was a teenager in Belleville, outside Detroit, as the city's car industry collapsed. He and Davis, a Vietnam veteran obsessed with futurism, formed Cybotron in 1981 and borrowed the language of Alvin Toffler's The Third Wave, whose phrase 'techno rebels' would give the coming music its name. Kraftwerk were heard constantly on Detroit radio, especially on the DJ Electrifying Mojo's show. Clear appeared on Cybotron's album Enter, on the Californian label Fantasy.","Clear is electro rather than techno in the strict sense, closer to Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock than to the four-to-the-floor thump of later Detroit records, yet every idea of techno is already here. Two decades later Missy Elliott built her 2005 hit Lose Control on it, and a new generation heard the melody without knowing where it came from.","On release it was a regional record, admired by DJs and dancers in Detroit and in the electro scene. As histories of techno were written in the 1990s, Cybotron were recognised as the missing ancestor, and Clear is now routinely cited among the key recordings of early electronic dance music.","Hear Kraftwerk's Computer World first, then Clear, and notice what Atkins adds: funk syncopation, a darker bass, a more restless rhythm. Listen on headphones for the stereo movement. Then play Model 500's No UFO's to hear the same mind two years on."],"juan-atkins-model-500-no-ufos":["Recorded under Atkins's alias Model 500, this track pits a nervous, clipped drum-machine groove against a rubbery synthesised bassline and glassy chord stabs. A cool, treated voice drifts in and out, half sung, half spoken, circling the idea of being watched from above and of truths withheld. The rhythm is taut and twitchy, the mood paranoid but danceable. It is the moment electro's sci-fi imagery begins to settle into the sleek pulse that would soon be called techno.","After Cybotron split, Atkins founded his own label, Metroplex, in 1985, and No UFO's was among its very first releases. Owning the label meant owning the music and the name, a principle that became a Detroit creed. The city around him was emptying: factories closing, downtown hollowed out. Atkins, May and Saunderson were making records for a handful of clubs and high-school party crews, and for Chicago, where DJs were hungry for anything new.","Listen to how little the track uses. A few synth voices and a drum machine are made to sound huge by arrangement alone: parts drop out and return, the bass changes its pattern slightly, and the effect is of a machine thinking. The title's refusal of alien visitors reads as a Detroit joke, pointing attention back to earthly power and surveillance.","It sold modestly but spread quickly through Chicago and later Europe, and it is now usually named, alongside Cybotron, as the first true Detroit techno record. Later critics have admired its restraint; it sounds less dated than many flashier electronic records of the mid-1980s.","Focus first on the bassline and how it pulls against the drum pattern, then count how few sounds are actually playing at any moment. Headphones help with the treated voice drifting across the stereo field. Play Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine beforehand to hear what Atkins learned from, and Derrick May's Strings of Life afterwards to hear how quickly the Detroit sound grew more emotional."],"marshall-jefferson-move-your-body":["A pounding drum machine, a bouncing bassline, and then the piano: a rolling, gospel-flavoured riff hammered out in blocks of chords, repeated until it becomes a chant of its own. A voice calls the dancers to move and declares allegiance to house music itself. The track is built from a few elements looped and layered, with breakdowns where the piano stands almost alone. That church-trained piano riff, bright and insistent, is what everyone remembers.","Jefferson worked at the post office in Chicago and started producing after hearing house at clubs such as the Music Box. He bought equipment on credit and taught himself. Chicago house in 1986 was stark and drum-driven, made for DJs rather than radio; Trax Records, run by Larry Sherman, was pressing it cheaply and quickly. The subtitle's claim to be the house music anthem was part boast, part manifesto, and it stuck.","Jefferson has said that other producers told him piano did not belong in house; its presence here, played rather than programmed, was a deliberate stand. Trax was notorious for pressing records on poor, recycled vinyl, so early copies are often noisy, and part of the record's sound in memory is that grit.","It became one of the most played records of the Chicago scene and, once house crossed the Atlantic, of British clubs too. Its piano-led model shaped a whole strand of later dance music, from Italian 'piano house' to countless 1990s hits. Critics now rank it among house's founding texts, though it received little money or attention at the time.","Listen for the moment the piano enters and how it reshapes the whole groove. Then compare Frankie Knuckles's Your Love, darker and hypnotic, and Phuture's Acid Tracks, which Jefferson produced the next year and which could hardly be more different."],"frankie-knuckles-your-love":["A synthesised arpeggio, a pattern of notes cycling up and down, spins endlessly at the centre of this track, over a steady drum machine and a deep, sustained bass. Jamie Principle's voice enters soft and intimate, part confession, part prayer to a lover. The record is slow by later standards and deeply hypnotic; very little changes, and the tension comes from that sameness. The spinning arpeggio, instantly recognisable, is the sound of house at its most romantic.","Jamie Principle, a young Chicago songwriter, wrote and recorded the song in the mid-1980s, and Frankie Knuckles began playing Principle's tape at his club, the Power Plant, long before it existed on vinyl. Knuckles reworked it and it was released on Trax in 1987 under both names. Knuckles had come from New York in 1977 to DJ at the Warehouse, the club whose name gave the music its label, and was by now Chicago's most respected DJ.","Years later the British producers of The Source laid Candi Staton's gospel-soul voice, singing a different song entirely, over this instrumental; the resulting You Got the Love became a British anthem through the 1990s and beyond. Many people who know that hit have never heard the Chicago record underneath it. Listen also for how quietly the track begins; it was built for a DJ to blend in.","Its credit has long been debated, with Principle as writer and Knuckles as producer and champion, and later releases have sometimes shifted the billing. Critically there is little argument: it is regularly named among the finest house records ever made, and its tenderness showed early that house could be about intimacy as much as physical release.","Let it play for several minutes before judging it; its power is cumulative. Listen for how the voice floats over the machine pattern without ever pushing it. Then hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love for its ancestor, and The Source's You Got the Love for its afterlife."],"phuture-acid-tracks":["Twelve minutes of a drum machine and a single little bass synthesiser playing a short looping pattern. But the loop does not stay still: its tone is continually twisted, becoming by turns rubbery, squelching, whining, snarling, as if the machine were alive and struggling. There is no melody in the usual sense and almost nothing else happening. The entire drama is timbre, the colour of one sound mutating in real time. That squelch is the acid sound, and it starts here.","The Roland TB-303 had been sold in the early 1980s as a bass accompanist for guitarists practising alone; it sounded nothing like a bass guitar, flopped, and was dumped cheaply second-hand. Phuture, three young Chicago friends, bought one and discovered that turning its filter knobs while a pattern ran produced strange, vocal sounds. The DJ Ron Hardy played their tape at the Music Box, reportedly several times in one night. Marshall Jefferson helped produce the record for Trax.","Listen for the filter being moved by hand: the changes are not programmed but played, which is why they are so uneven and expressive. The track is almost a live performance on a failed consumer gadget. Many later 'acid' records programmed the same moves perfectly; the slightly clumsy human gestures here are part of its strangeness.","It baffled some dancers at first; others went wild. Within a year the acid sound had crossed to Britain and set off the 1988 'second summer of love', with its smiley-face iconography and moral panic. Phuture's record is now counted among the most influential dance tracks ever made, a founding document of acid house and, through it, of rave.","Listen once while doing nothing else, following only the 303. Notice each time the tone opens or closes. Then play Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear another music where slow change within repetition is the whole point, and Orbital's Chime for where British rave took it."],"derrick-may-strings-of-life":["Released under May's alias Rhythim Is Rhythim, it opens with a piano figure, jagged and urgent, then orchestral string stabs crash in like a film score at its climax. Drums tumble and syncopate beneath rather than simply thumping. The track surges, stops dead, and surges again, with the strings calling out in short, dramatic bursts. It feels less like a dance track than a small symphony for machines. The first entrance of the strings, after the piano, is the unforgettable moment.","May, a friend of Juan Atkins since their teens in Belleville, launched Transmat in Detroit in 1986. He worked with cheap samplers and drum machines, sampling short fragments and rearranging them. Detroit in 1987 was a city in industrial collapse, and May has often described his music as an emotional response to its emptiness and to the machine age. In Chicago and then in Britain, the record was heard as a new kind of house with a heart of pure drama.","The piano figure is often said to derive from an improvisation by the keyboardist Michael James, which May cut into pieces and reassembled; if so, the record is a collage of a live performance rather than something programmed from scratch. Listen too for how often the drums disappear, leaving the strings hanging; those gaps are what make the returns so ecstatic.","It became an anthem in British clubs in 1988 and 1989, sometimes played by DJs who had no idea it came from Detroit. It is now probably the most celebrated Detroit techno record, and critics often point to it as proof that electronic dance music could carry emotion as intense as any orchestral score.","Wait for the strings and let them hit. Then listen again to the piano underneath; notice how uneven and human its rhythm is. Compare Juan Atkins's cooler Model 500 records, then the soaring strings of Underground Resistance's Knights of the Jaguar, a later Detroit echo."],"derrick-may-nude-photo":["A fast, skittering drum-machine rhythm, jittery hi-hats and a busy, bubbling synth line that seems to chase itself in circles. Chords flash on and off. The track is restless and nervy rather than euphoric, built from short cells that interlock and slide against each other, then drop away to reveal a bare groove. There are no words and no obvious chorus, just continual motion. It sounds like a city at night seen from a speeding car.","Nude Photo was one of the first releases on Transmat, the label May founded in Detroit, and he made it with his friend Thomas Barnett. Released under the Rhythim Is Rhythim name, it reached Chicago clubs quickly and then Britain, where the compilation Techno! The New Dance Sound of Detroit in 1988 first packaged the Detroit sound for Europe.","Listen to the percussion as closely as the synths. May programs the drums with a funk drummer's sense of anticipation, pushing hits just ahead of the beat, which is why the track feels so twitchy. The main synth line repeats but its accents shift as other layers enter and leave, so the pattern seems to change even when it does not.","It is less famous than Strings of Life, but many producers and critics consider it the purer statement of May's style, and it is a touchstone for the syncopated, funk-inflected side of Detroit techno. Its influence on British 1990s 'intelligent' techno was considerable.","Focus first on the hi-hats and how they drive the whole thing; tap along and you will find yourself landing slightly early, as May intended. Then follow the main synth line and notice how its accents seem to move as layers enter and leave. Play Strings of Life to hear the same producer in a grander mood, and Kraftwerk's Computer World for the machine aesthetic both grew out of."],"kevin-saunderson-inner-city-big-fun":["Released under the name Inner City, a bright, bouncing synth riff and a crisp drum-machine beat carry Paris Grey's clear, joyous voice. The song is simple: an invitation to join the party. But the backing is pure Detroit, with a hard, rolling bass and synth chords that hit like brass. It is techno reshaped as a three-minute-thrill pop song, and the riff under Grey's first entrance is the thing people hum.","Kevin Saunderson, youngest of the Belleville Three, launched KMS in 1987 and teamed up with Paris Grey, a singer from Chicago. The track was licensed to British labels at the moment house and acid house were exploding in the UK, and it became a British hit in the summer of 1988. For many British listeners it was the first Detroit record they ever heard, though they had no idea where it came from.","The instrumental version, without Grey, sounds far harder and closer to underground techno; Saunderson built a pop song on an underground frame, and the two coexist in the record. Listen for the moment the vocal drops out and the machines take over; the rave generation in Britain danced to exactly that switch.","Some Detroit purists at the time saw Inner City's pop success as a dilution; others saw it as proof that the new music could reach anyone. With hindsight it is widely regarded as a bridge between underground techno and mainstream dance-pop, and Saunderson as one of the most commercially astute of the pioneers.","Hear it next to Strings of Life from the year before: two very different ideas of what techno could be, one orchestral and stormy, one bright and sing-along. Listen under Paris Grey's voice for the hard, rolling bass, which is pure Detroit. Seek out the instrumental mix too, then play Inner City's Good Life, which followed within months."],"kevin-saunderson-inner-city-good-life":["Another Inner City song, this one warmer and more yearning than Big Fun. Paris Grey sings of a better life and a hopeful future over glistening synth chords, a gliding bassline and a steady, relaxed drum-machine groove. The chords shimmer and change underneath her like light on water. The mood is optimistic but tinged with longing, and the upward-lifting chorus is what stays with you.","Following the success of Big Fun, Saunderson and Grey released Good Life late in 1988, and it too became a British hit and an international club staple. It later anchored Inner City's album Paradise. Detroit itself was in deep economic decline; a song dreaming of the good life, made in that city, carries an undertow the lyrics never quite state.","Listen to how the harmony moves under the vocal; the chords are unusually rich for a dance record of the era, sliding through changes closer to soul ballad than to machine music. That harmonic warmth, not just the beat, is why the song has outlasted most of its rivals.","It has become one of the most enduring dance songs of the late 1980s, frequently remixed and revived. Critics now place it among the finest bridges between underground techno and dance-pop, and it helped establish the idea that Detroit could produce hits as well as cult records.","Listen for the chord changes under the chorus; they move more like a soul ballad than a dance record, and they are why the lift feels earned. Notice how relaxed the drum programming is compared with Big Fun. Then play Frankie Knuckles's Tears to hear Chicago reaching for the same tender mood, and Madonna's Ray of Light for how this sound entered 1990s pop."],"kevin-saunderson-reese-just-want-another-chance":["Released under the alias Reese, this is the dark twin of Saunderson's Inner City pop. A stark drum-machine pattern backs a bassline unlike anything before it: two slightly detuned tones rubbing against each other so the sound throbs, growls and seems to rotate in space. A voice fragment pleads for another chance. Almost nothing else happens. That menacing, churning bass is the whole record, and it would become one of the most copied sounds in electronic music.","In 1988 Saunderson was simultaneously scoring hits as Inner City and making heavy underground tracks under other names. Aliases let Detroit producers release more music and experiment without confusing audiences. This record was aimed squarely at club DJs, and its bass sound was designed to feel physical on a big system.","The bass sound became known simply as the 'Reese bass' after this alias. In the early 1990s British jungle and drum-and-bass producers seized on it, and through them it passed into dubstep and countless later genres. Many people have heard a 'Reese' without ever hearing Reese.","It was a modest underground release, and its reputation grew mostly after the fact, as producers traced the lineage of a sound that had become ubiquitous. It is now regarded as one of the most influential basslines in dance music.","Listen on good speakers or headphones, since laptop speakers lose almost everything here, and focus entirely on the bass: notice how it seems to wobble and spin as the two tones drift in and out of line. Then compare the pop sparkle of Inner City's Big Fun, made by the same man in the same year, and The Prodigy's Jilted Generation for the sound's British afterlife."],"frankie-knuckles-tears":["A slow, melancholy house track made with the Japanese producer Satoshi Tomiie and sung by Robert Owens. Soft piano chords, deep bass and a gentle drum-machine groove support Owens's rich, grieving voice, which sings of a love that has ended. The texture is spacious and elegant. Nothing is rushed. Where most dance records aim for euphoria, this one aims for heartbreak, and the piano's sad, circling chords are its signature.","By 1989 Knuckles was back in New York and house had become an international phenomenon, especially in Britain. The London label FFRR issued Tears as part of a wave of American house sold to British buyers. Tomiie was a young producer from Tokyo, and Owens a Chicago singer who had worked with Larry Heard. The track belongs to the 'deep house' current that brought jazz and soul harmonies back into the music.","Listen for how sparse the drum programming is; Knuckles leaves room for Owens's voice rather than filling every gap, a lesson learned from years of mixing soul records on the Warehouse's system. The piano part moves through richer harmonies than most 1980s house, a sign of Tomiie's jazz leanings.","It became a club classic and a template for a more sophisticated, song-based deep house, the strand later carried by producers such as Kerri Chandler. Critics consistently place it among Knuckles's finest productions, and among the records that showed house could express sorrow as convincingly as joy. It also launched Tomiie's long international career, an early sign that house was no longer only an American music.","Play it after Your Love to hear Knuckles's romanticism turn melancholy. Listen to how Owens phrases slightly behind the beat, letting lines trail over the bar, and to how the piano answers him in the gaps. It works best late at night at moderate volume. Then hear Inner City's Good Life for Detroit's warmer, more hopeful answer."],"orbital-chime":["A chiming melodic pattern, bright and bell-like, repeats over a skipping breakbeat and a warm, rounded bassline. The track builds slowly in layers, adding and removing sounds, and moves between rushing euphoria and quieter, reflective passages. It lasts about twelve minutes in its full form and never loses its sense of joyful discovery. The bell-like chime that gives it its name is the sound of British rave at its most innocent.","The brothers Phil and Paul Hartnoll recorded Chime at home in Sevenoaks, Kent, reportedly for next to nothing, and it came out first on a small label before FFRR reissued it in 1990 and it became a hit. They named the duo after the M25 orbital motorway that carried ravers to illegal parties in fields and warehouses around London. It was the high tide of rave, and the Conservative government was already moving against unlicensed parties.","When they performed it on television, the brothers reportedly wore T-shirts protesting the poll tax, a quiet sign of the politics that ran under British rave. Listen too for how much of the track is made from a single melodic cell, changed by adding voices and shifting the rhythm around it, a technique closer to minimalism than to pop song.","It was one of the first British rave records to become a genuine hit and made Orbital's name. It is still regarded as a defining record of its moment, and a proof that rave could be tender and melodic rather than merely hard.","Listen to the full twelve-inch version rather than an edit. Notice when the breakbeat drops out and the chime is left almost alone. Then compare Phuture's Acid Tracks to hear what the Chicago sound became once it crossed the Atlantic."],"frankie-knuckles-the-whistle-song":["A light, airy instrumental built around a playful melody on flute, whistling and fluttering over a smooth house groove. Soft keyboard chords and a gentle, rolling bass fill in beneath. It is cheerful, elegant and uncomplicated, closer to a gentle jazz-funk tune than to the stark machine tracks of early Chicago house. That bird-like flute tune is the whole identity of the record.","The track came from Knuckles's album Beyond the Mix, made for Virgin in the early 1990s after he had moved back to New York and joined David Morales in the Def Mix production team. Major labels were now signing house producers, and Def Mix remixes were polishing pop records for clubs. This record showed what house sounded like when it had money, studio time and live players.","Listen for how much of the track is played rather than programmed; the flute and keyboards breathe in ways a sequencer does not. That shift back toward live musicianship, in a genre born of drum machines, was part of what later came to be called 'soulful house'.","It was a big club hit and remains one of Knuckles's most loved records, though some critics found it too pretty and easy compared with the raw Chicago tracks of the 1980s. Today it tends to be heard as an early example of the polished, musical house of the 1990s.","Enjoy it for what it is: sunny, graceful dance music. Listen for the conversation between the flute and the keyboard, and for the moments the drums fall back and the tune floats alone. Then go back to Your Love to hear how far house had travelled in four years, from a hypnotic machine loop to something close to jazz-funk, and on to Daft Punk's Discovery for its sunny heirs."],"the-prodigy-charly":["Fast, chopped breakbeats, a buzzing synth riff and, cutting through them, a sample of a cartoon cat's meow and a child's voice from a British public-information film. The effect is manic and comic, a nursery rhyme dropped into a rave. The track moves quickly through sections, piling on sound. The meow, instantly recognisable to British listeners who grew up with the films, is its hook.","Liam Howlett made the track in Essex at the peak of the British rave boom, when records were being pressed and sold for weekend parties in fields and warehouses. The sample came from the Charley Says films, short safety cartoons shown on British television in the 1970s in which a cat warned children about strangers and dangers. XL Recordings released it in 1991, and it became a surprise hit.","The cartoon's safety messages, about not wandering off with strangers, took on a knowing double meaning at raves, where drug use was widespread; listeners heard both the childhood memory and the joke. Listen too for the breakbeats: they are chopped and sped up in a style that would soon grow into jungle.","Its success set off a rash of imitations sampling children's television, and critics derided the resulting 'toytown techno' as a cheap novelty. The Prodigy moved quickly away from the style, but the track is now remembered fondly as a period piece and a first sighting of their chaos.","Take it as a snapshot of 1991 rave: listen for the speed of the breakbeats and the delight in collage. Then hear Music for the Jilted Generation three years later to hear how much darker and more serious the band became."],"jeff-mills-waveform-transmission-vol-1":["An album of fast, relentless techno tracks built from pounding drum-machine kicks, clattering hi-hats and short, harsh synth loops that repeat and slowly mutate. There are no vocals and almost no melody; each track is a single idea pushed to its limit through repetition, with small changes creating huge tension. The effect is hypnotic and physical, like being inside a machine running flat out. The hard, dry sound of the 909 drum machine is everywhere.","Mills had left Underground Resistance and was working between Detroit and New York when Berlin's Tresor club and label, based in a vault beneath a former department store near the Berlin Wall's former line, released this album. Techno and the new reunited Berlin were growing up together, and Detroit producers found a devoted audience there. The album marks the moment Detroit techno turned harder and more minimal.","Listen to how Mills uses tiny changes: a loop may repeat for minutes with only a filter slowly opening or a hi-hat shifting position, and your ear starts to hear the change as dramatic. This is a DJ's music; many tracks were designed to be mixed and layered live rather than heard alone.","It became a touchstone for the harder, faster strand of 1990s techno, especially in Europe, and helped cement Mills's reputation as a producer of fierce minimalism. Some listeners find it abrasive and monotonous; admirers hear it as pure, disciplined intensity.","Play it loud and let the repetition work; resist skipping. Pick one sound in a track, a hi-hat or a synth stab, and follow it for several minutes: what seemed static turns out to be constantly shifting. Then compare Steve Reich's Drumming for another music where sustained repetition and gradual change create drama, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the even sparer path it opened."],"underground-resistance-hi-tech-jazz":["Released under the alias Galaxy 2 Galaxy, it is a soaring, melodic techno piece built around a bright synth lead that sings like a jazz horn over lush, shifting chords and a propulsive drum-machine groove. It builds, breaks down, and builds again, with the melody lifting over each new entry of the rhythm. It is uplifting and cinematic rather than hard. That singing lead line, climbing and falling like an improvised solo, is the signature.","Underground Resistance, led by 'Mad' Mike Banks, positioned themselves as a masked, militant collective opposed to the major-label music industry and to the neglect of Detroit. Yet alongside their aggressive records they made music of great beauty, and Hi-Tech Jazz is the most famous. The Galaxy 2 Galaxy project aimed to link techno to the heritage of Detroit jazz and funk, and to Sun Ra's cosmic Afrofuturism.","The title is a manifesto: Banks was arguing that techno belonged to the lineage of black American music, especially jazz, rather than to European electronic music alone. In later live performances the group played the piece with musicians, including a saxophonist, making that connection audible.","It has become one of Detroit techno's most loved records, frequently cited as proof that the genre can be as emotionally expressive as any jazz or soul. Critics have increasingly read UR's work as part of an Afrofuturist tradition that runs from Sun Ra through Parliament.","Listen to the lead melody as if it were a trumpet solo. Then play Sun Ra's Space Is the Place and Parliament's Mothership Connection to hear the cosmic lineage UR claimed, and Strings of Life for another Detroit record reaching for the sky."],"orbital-orbital-2":["Orbital's second album, known by the colour of its cover, flows as a continuous journey through long, evolving tracks. Warm, layered synths, rolling breakbeats and slowly unfolding melodies build from quiet ambience to euphoric climax. Tracks such as Lush 3, Halcyon + On + On and Impact (The Earth Is Burning) move between the dancefloor and contemplation. Halcyon's shimmering, looping vocal sample and bell-like synths are the album's emotional centre.","By 1993 British rave had moved from illegal parties into licensed clubs and festivals, and a new 'intelligent' or 'album' electronic music was taking shape for home listening. The Hartnolls were becoming known as an extraordinary live act, playing sequencers and synthesisers on stage rather than miming. The Conservative government was drafting the law that would target outdoor raves the following year.","Halcyon is named after the tranquilliser Halcion, and the Hartnolls have said it was written for their mother, who had been dependent on it; knowing this, the track's aching beauty takes on a different colour. The album also opens with a phased spoken fragment about time becoming a loop, which links back to their first album; the two records seem to circle into each other.","It was widely praised on release and is now regarded as one of the defining British electronic albums of the 1990s. Their live performance of Halcyon at Glastonbury in 1994 became a landmark in the acceptance of electronic music at rock festivals.","Listen straight through; the album is designed as a single arc. Start with Halcyon if you want one track. Then hear Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient mood, and Steve Reich for the method of slowly layered repetition."],"robert-hood-minimal-nation":["An album of radically stripped-down techno. Each track is built from a few elements: a steady kick drum, a ticking hi-hat, a single clipped chord or synth stab repeating in a tight, funky pattern. Almost nothing else. But the grooves are relentless, and tiny changes in accent and timing keep them alive. The music sounds skeletal, but it swings. Those short, repeating stabs, like a machine hiccuping in rhythm, are the sound of minimal techno.","Hood had been part of Underground Resistance and moved with Jeff Mills toward a leaner sound; Minimal Nation came out on Mills's Axis label. Hood has described minimalism as a response to Detroit itself, a stripping back to what was essential, a funk made from very little. The title named a subgenre that was about to spread across Europe, especially in Berlin and Cologne.","Listen for what is missing: basslines, melodies, breakdowns, vocals. Hood removes almost everything that dance music usually relies on, and yet the tracks remain funky because of precise rhythmic placement. The swing comes from how the hi-hats and stabs sit just off the beat, a drummer's sensibility carried into a machine.","On release it was a cult record; within a few years it was recognised as the foundation of minimal techno, a genre that dominated European clubs in the 2000s. Critics now treat it as one of the most important techno albums of the 1990s.","Pick one track and follow a single element, say the hi-hat, all the way through. Then compare Steve Reich's Clapping Music, another piece that finds drama in almost nothing, and Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission for the hard sound it grew out of."],"the-prodigy-music-for-the-jilted-generation":["The Prodigy's second album is harder, darker and more aggressive than their early rave singles. Heavy, chopped breakbeats, distorted guitars, snarling synth riffs and sampled voices collide in tracks such as Voodoo People, Poison, No Good (Start the Dance) and the multi-part Narcotic Suite. The mood is defiant and cinematic, closer to rock than to dance-pop. The big, lurching breakbeats, heavy enough to rival a rock drummer, are its signature.","The album was made as the British government pushed through the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act of 1994, which gave police powers against outdoor gatherings with music defined, notoriously, by repetitive beats. Rave culture felt criminalised. Liam Howlett responded with an album that took the side of the ravers, and whose sleeve artwork showed a raver cutting a rope bridge between his world and the police.","The track Their Law, made with the band Pop Will Eat Itself, is a direct protest against the Criminal Justice Act. Listen also for the album's breakbeats: many are borrowed from old funk and rock records, chopped and reassembled so completely that the sources are almost untraceable.","It was widely acclaimed and marked The Prodigy's transformation from a rave novelty act into a serious album band. Many critics now regard it as one of the key British records of the 1990s, and as a bridge between rave and rock that anticipated big beat.","Start with Voodoo People, then play the album in order. Listen for how the guitars and breakbeats interact. Compare Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions for another protest record built on sampled beats, and The Fat of the Land for what came next."],"underworld-dubnobasswithmyheadman":["Underworld's breakthrough album blends long, hypnotic techno and house grooves with Karl Hyde's murmured, fragmentary vocals. Tracks like Dark & Long, Mmm Skyscraper I Love You, Cowgirl and Dirty Epic stretch out over many minutes, with shimmering synths, pulsing basslines and guitars washed in echo. Hyde's voice drifts through, repeating phrases like overheard conversation. The mood is urban, nocturnal, dreamlike. The half-sung, half-spoken vocal over a relentless groove is unmistakable.","Hyde and Rick Smith had been an unsuccessful synth-pop band in the 1980s before reinventing themselves with the young DJ Darren Emerson. They were also members of Tomato, a London design collective whose visual style shaped the band's look. The album was released on the independent Junior Boy's Own label as British electronic music was moving toward albums and a wider audience.","Hyde's lyrics were often assembled from notes of conversations overheard in the street, snatches of signs and advertisements, and fragments from his notebooks, cut up and repeated. Listen for how the words work as rhythm and colour rather than a story; the effect is of drifting through a city at night catching pieces of other lives.","It was widely hailed as a breakthrough in British electronic music, showing that techno could carry songs, words and emotional depth without losing its hypnotic power. Rock critics who had ignored dance music took notice, partly because Hyde sounded like a singer rather than a sampled diva. It is frequently named among the best albums of the decade, and it opened the way for the band's festival-headlining years.","Start with Dark & Long or Cowgirl and let it run; the tracks need their full length to work. Listen for the voice as another instrument, a rhythm and a colour rather than a story, and for the guitars smeared in echo underneath. Then compare Orbital's Brown Album for a sister record, and Born Slippy .NUXX for where Underworld went next."],"underworld-born-slippy-nuxx":["It opens with washes of shimmering synth chords, slow and ambient; then a pounding, galloping beat bursts in, and Karl Hyde's voice chants and stammers fragments of a night out, repeating and circling. The track keeps building, dropping back to its floating chords, then surging again. It is ecstatic and exhausting at once. The moment the beat crashes in after the ambient opening is one of the most famous in dance music.","It first appeared as a B-side in 1995, then was reissued in 1996 after the director Danny Boyle used it at the climax of his film Trainspotting, about heroin users in Edinburgh. It became a huge hit and a symbol of British 1990s culture. The title reportedly comes from the name of a greyhound on which the band once placed a winning bet.","Hyde's vocal was written as a portrait of drunken self-destruction, the voice of someone lost in a night of heavy drinking; it was never meant as a celebration. Many crowds nevertheless chant its most famous word as a party anthem, which the band have found both funny and troubling. Listen for the desperation under the euphoria.","The song became inseparable from Trainspotting and from the image of 1990s Britain, and is often voted among the greatest dance records. Critics have debated whether its massive popularity obscured its darker meaning, turning a portrait of a man drowning in drink into a stadium singalong. It also fixed a public image of Underworld that the band spent years gently resisting, while still closing concerts with it.","Listen from the start and wait for the beat to drop; the long ambient opening is the setup, not an intro to skip. Pay attention to the voice as a rhythm, stammering and circling rather than singing a tune. Then play Dubnobasswithmyheadman for the album-length version of Underworld's world, and The Prodigy's The Fat of the Land for its louder contemporary."],"jeff-mills-live-at-the-liquid-room":["A recorded DJ set by Jeff Mills at a Tokyo club, where he mixes dozens of techno tracks at astonishing speed. Records are cut in and out every minute or two, layered and flipped, sometimes with a drum machine played live on top. The flow is relentless and dense, a surging wall of rhythm. It is less a playlist than a new composition built in real time from other people's records. The sheer velocity of the mixing is the point.","Mills was famous in Detroit in the 1980s as 'The Wizard', a radio DJ whose rapid mixing amazed listeners. By the mid-1990s he was an international figure, especially in Japan and Europe. Mix albums were becoming a major format, but few captured the athletic, improvisational side of DJing as vividly as this one, recorded at the Liquid Room in Tokyo.","Mills often used three turntables and a drum machine, allowing him to layer records and add his own rhythms; listen for moments when a beat appears that is on no record at all. Several of the tracks in the mix are his own, reworked on the fly.","It is widely regarded as one of the great DJ mix albums, and a benchmark of technical skill. Some listeners find it too relentless for home listening; admirers regard it as a document of DJing as a live art comparable to jazz improvisation.","Listen loud and try to count how often the records change; you will lose count quickly. Notice the moments where two records run together and create a rhythm neither has alone. Treat it like a live jazz album rather than background music. Then compare Larry Levan's Paradise Garage set for a completely different idea of the DJ's art: slow, spacious, patient."],"jeff-mills-the-bells":["A short, relentless techno track built on a pounding kick drum and a bright, ringing synth pattern that sounds like bells or a carillon chiming in endless loops. The pattern repeats insistently, shifting subtly as other layers enter, and the effect is both hypnotic and joyful. It is simple, but the tension between the bell-like melody and the driving rhythm makes it irresistible. The chiming figure is instantly recognisable.","Mills released it on his Purpose Maker label, a vehicle for funkier, more club-functional tracks. By then he was one of the most influential DJs in the world, and The Bells became one of the most played techno tracks of the late 1990s. It fitted his approach: minimal material, maximum impact.","In 2005 Mills performed The Bells with the Montpellier Philharmonic Orchestra, translating the synth pattern into orchestral bells and strings; hearing that version reveals how close the track is to minimalist concert music. Listen for how the pattern changes by adding rather than altering notes.","It became a club staple and is now considered one of the classic techno tracks. Critics have noted its bridge between minimal techno and minimalist composition, and it is often cited as an example of how techno can be both simple and deeply engaging.","Listen to how the bell pattern interacts with the kick drum, and notice that the pattern grows by adding notes rather than changing them, so the melody seems to assemble itself. Then seek out the orchestral version from Mills's Montpellier concert, and compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the minimal Detroit sound it grew from."],"daft-punk-homework":["Daft Punk's debut album is raw, looping French house: filtered disco samples, buzzing synths, thumping drum machines and robotic vocoder voices. Tracks such as Da Funk, Around the World and Revolution 909 repeat simple ideas until they become hypnotic. The sound is gritty and playful, made to be played loud in clubs. The filter sweep, in which the sound seems to open and close like a door, is the album's signature.","Thomas Bangalter and Guy-Manuel de Homem-Christo made Homework in Bangalter's Paris bedroom. French house was emerging from Paris clubs and labels such as Roulé and Crydamoure, drawing heavily on Chicago house and disco. The duo took their name from a British music press review that had dismissed their earlier indie band as 'daft punky'. Virgin released the album internationally.","The track Teachers is a roll-call of the Chicago and Detroit producers the pair learned from, a thank-you note to the house and techno pioneers. Listen for the repeated names as a sign of how consciously Daft Punk saw themselves as students of American black dance music.","It was a major critical and commercial success, bringing French house to global attention and helping define the late-1990s dance boom, helped by inventive videos from directors such as Spike Jonze and Michel Gondry. Some reviewers found it too long and repetitive for home listening. Critics now regard it as a key album in the internationalisation of house music, and as the moment Paris joined Chicago and Detroit on the map.","Start with Around the World, then Da Funk. Listen for the filter sweeps: the sound seems muffled behind a door, then the door swings open. Notice how few elements each track uses, repeated with a DJ's patience. Then compare Frankie Knuckles's Your Love for the Chicago sound Daft Punk loved, and Discovery for where they went next."],"the-prodigy-the-fat-of-the-land":["The Prodigy's third album fuses breakbeat electronics with punk and metal aggression. Keith Flint and Maxim snarl over huge, distorted beats, grinding synth riffs and guitar samples in tracks such as Firestarter, Breathe and Mindfields. The sound is loud, abrasive and theatrical, closer to a rock album than a dance record. The combination of sirens, chopped breakbeats and screamed vocals is unmistakable.","By 1997 the Prodigy had become a major international act, and The Fat of the Land went to number one in both Britain and the United States, the height of the 'big beat' and 'electronica' boom. One track's title and video drew protests and bans for their confrontational imagery, adding to the band's notoriety.","Firestarter samples a guitar figure from The Breeders and a fragment from the Art of Noise; listen for the rock sources inside the electronics. Many of the album's guitar sounds are sampled rather than played, a sign of how thoroughly the Prodigy treated rock as raw material.","It was a huge commercial success and critically acclaimed, though some dance purists saw it as a sell-out to rock. It is now regarded as a defining album of the big beat era and as a bridge between electronic dance music and rock audiences.","Start with Breathe, where the call-and-response between the two vocalists sits over a lurching, stop-start beat. Listen for how the beats are built from chopped fragments and how often a 'guitar' is really a sample. Play it loud, as intended. Then compare Music for the Jilted Generation to hear the transformation, and Underworld's Born Slippy for a very different British dance anthem."],"underground-resistance-knights-of-the-jaguar":["Credited to DJ Rolando of Underground Resistance, it is built on a sweeping string-like synth melody, grand and melancholy, over a propulsive, driving techno beat. The melody rises in long phrases, repeated and layered, while the rhythm pushes forward relentlessly. It feels heroic and wistful at once. That soaring string line, sounding like a film theme, is the track's signature.","Rolando Rocha, a Mexican-American DJ from southwest Detroit, made the track as part of UR. The title refers to the Jaguar Knights, an elite warrior order of the Aztec Empire, a reference to his heritage and to UR's warrior imagery. The track became a massive European club hit. UR refused to license it to a major label, which then released a sound-alike version; the resulting dispute became a celebrated case of underground integrity against the industry.","Listen for how simple the melody is; its power comes from repetition and the way it floats over the beat. The track's popularity, and the major label's cover version, became part of its meaning: a battle between the independent Detroit ethos and corporate imitation.","It is regarded as one of the greatest Detroit techno tracks and a late flowering of the genre's melodic side. Critics also see it as a symbol of UR's refusal to compromise, and of the Latino presence in Detroit's music.","Wait for the melody to fully unfold; it arrives in stages, each repetition adding weight. Listen to how the drums never let up underneath, so the grandeur is always moving forward. It rewards a big system or good headphones. Then compare Strings of Life for its Detroit ancestor, Hi-Tech Jazz for another soaring UR record, and Vangelis's Blade Runner for a kindred synthetic melancholy."],"daft-punk-discovery":["Daft Punk's second album is brighter and poppier, drawing on disco, soft rock and 1980s pop. Filtered samples, vocoder vocals, guitar solos and glittering synths fill tracks such as One More Time, Aerodynamic, Digital Love and Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger. The mood is nostalgic and joyful, like a childhood memory of pop music. The heavily processed, auto-tuned vocal on One More Time is the album's signature sound.","The duo described the album as an attempt to recapture the feelings of childhood discovery, when they first heard pop and disco. They adopted robot helmets and a fictional backstory to hide their faces. The album was accompanied by the animated film Interstella 5555, made with the Japanese artist Leiji Matsumoto. One More Time features the vocals of the house singer Romanthony.","The album is built from samples of obscure 1970s and 1980s records, disguised by filtering and chopping: Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger uses Edwin Birdsong's Cola Bottle Baby, and Digital Love draws on George Duke. Kanye West later sampled Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger for his hit Stronger. Listen for the disguised sources.","Initially some critics found it too cheesy and pop-oriented, objecting to the soft-rock guitar solos and the heavily processed vocals that dance purists considered kitsch. Its reputation has grown enormously since. It is now widely considered one of the most influential albums of the 2000s, shaping electronic pop, EDM and hip-hop production, and its embrace of guilty-pleasure sounds now looks prophetic.","Start with One More Time, then Digital Love, and listen for how the vocoder makes a machine sound lovesick. Hear the guitar solo in Aerodynamic, closer to heavy metal than to house. Play the album in order; it flows like a mixtape. Then compare Chic's Good Times for the disco sound that inspired them, and Random Access Memories for their later tribute."],"daft-punk-random-access-memories":["Daft Punk's final studio album abandons samples in favour of live musicians, recreating the lush sound of 1970s disco and soft rock. Nile Rodgers's guitar, Nathan East's bass and top session drummers drive tracks like Get Lucky, Lose Yourself to Dance and Giorgio by Moroder, with guest vocals from Pharrell Williams, Julian Casablancas and Paul Williams. The sound is warm, expansive and meticulous. Nile Rodgers's rhythm guitar on Get Lucky is the album's hallmark.","Recorded in expensive studios in Los Angeles, Paris and New York, the album was a reaction against the laptop-produced EDM that was dominating dance music in the early 2010s. Daft Punk wanted to honour the musicians and studios of the disco era. Columbia released it in 2013, and it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","On Giorgio by Moroder, the veteran producer tells his life story; the band recorded him with microphones from different decades, so the sound of his voice changes to match the era he is describing. Listen for that subtle shift in tone.","It was a massive commercial success and won major awards, and Get Lucky became one of the most played songs of its year. Some critics felt it was too nostalgic or overproduced, a museum piece for a vanished studio culture. Others heard a sincere argument for musicianship in a laptop age. It is now regarded as a celebration of dance music's history and of studio craft, and as the duo's farewell statement.","Start with Get Lucky, then Giorgio by Moroder, where Moroder's spoken story gives way to a long, building instrumental. Listen for the live drums and Rodgers's choppy rhythm guitar, and hear how the album sounds warmer than a sample-based record. Then compare Chic's Good Times and Donna Summer's I Feel Love for the sources they honour."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-85-92":["Thirteen instrumental tracks, most of them built from a slow techno pulse, a soft analogue bass line and one or two melodies that loop and drift rather than develop. 'Xtal' opens with a breathy, wordless pad over a lazy kick drum; 'Heliosphan' and 'Ageispolis' are brighter and more mechanical; 'Tha' stretches a murky groove past nine minutes. Everything sits under a faint layer of hiss, so the record sounds less like a club than like a bedroom at three in the morning.","Richard D. James made most of these pieces as a teenager in Cornwall, on cheap and modified synthesizers, recording straight to cassette. They were collected in 1992 by Apollo, the ambient offshoot of the Belgian techno label R&S, just as British rave was splitting into the dancefloor and the living room. Warp's 'Artificial Intelligence' series appeared the same year, and critics began to talk about electronic music made for listening rather than dancing.","The hiss is not a mastering fault that nobody caught; the album was taken from the cassette tapes, and the noise floor is part of the colour. Listen too for how little happens: many tracks repeat a four-bar idea for minutes, changing only a filter or one drum. 'We Are the Music Makers' borrows a line of film dialogue spoken by Gene Wilder, the only human voice on the record that says real words.","Reviewers in 1992 had few categories for it; 'ambient techno' was the label that stuck. Within a decade it had become a fixture on lists of the best electronic albums ever made, and it is often the first record people reach for when explaining what IDM meant before the term existed. The one lasting argument is over the word ambient itself, since most of these tracks keep a steady beat that Eno's ambient music avoids.","Start with 'Xtal' and 'Ageispolis' on headphones at moderate volume, and let the loops settle rather than waiting for a drop. Then compare Eno's Music for Airports, which has no pulse at all, to hear what James added. After that, Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children shows where the same warm, worn sound went next."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-volume-ii":["Two and a half hours of drones, slow chords and dim, half-heard textures, almost entirely without beats. Some tracks are a single organ-like chord sequence repeated with infinite patience; others are metallic rumbles, distant voices or tones that seem to be leaking from another room. The piece fans call 'Rhubarb', a few soft synthetic chords rising and falling for seven minutes, is the one most listeners recognise, and it could almost be a hymn.","Released on Warp in 1994, when James was already known for abrasive, jokey techno, it confounded almost everyone. The tracks carried no titles, only photographs of textures and objects keyed to a pie chart on the sleeve, and fans later assigned names of their own. James told interviewers that some of the material came to him in lucid dreams, a claim that may be partly mischief but which shaped how the record was heard.","Because the tracks are untitled, the album works like one continuous installation, and it rewards being played in order rather than shuffled. Beneath several pieces are real acoustic sources slowed and smeared beyond recognition. 'Rhubarb' uses almost nothing: a short cycle of chords, no melody to speak of, no change of speed. It is the same wager Satie and Arvo Pärt made, that a few plain sounds left alone long enough will start to feel alive.","The music press was divided in 1994, with some reviewers calling it empty and others hearing a new, darker kind of ambient music than Eno's. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often preferred to the first volume by listeners who come from classical minimalism or drone music. Debate continues over how much of the dream story to believe, and over whether the whole thing should ever be heard in one sitting.","Play 'Rhubarb' first, then Pärt's Für Alina, and listen to how both let a few notes ring for as long as they need. Then try a whole disc late at night, lights low, at a volume just above silence. Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel makes a revealing companion for the darker tracks."],"aphex-twin-richard-d-james-album":["A short record that crams sweet, almost nursery-rhyme melodies against drum programming so fast and fractured it sounds like a breakbeat being fed through a shredder. '4' opens with a floating keyboard tune over a skittering, impossibly busy rhythm; 'Girl/Boy Song' sets a tender string arrangement over jungle drums chopped into tiny slivers. The contrast between the lullaby on top and the chaos underneath is the whole idea.","By 1996 jungle and drum and bass were the sound of London, and James, now living there, took their chopped breakbeats and pushed the editing to an extreme that dancers could not follow. The style became known, half mockingly, as drill 'n' bass. Much of the album was programmed on a computer rather than played on hardware, which let him place drum hits at speeds no drummer or sampler performance could reach. His own grinning face filled the cover.","Several titles are Cornish place names, including 'Carn Marth' and 'Logan Rock Witch', a private map of the landscape he grew up in. Listen past the drums in 'Girl/Boy Song' and the melody is a simple, plaintive tune that would sit comfortably on a music box. In the fastest passages the drum hits are so close together that they stop being rhythm and become pitch, a buzzing tone of their own.","Received as James's most approachable record despite its violence, it sold well and turned the face on its sleeve into a kind of logo. Critics then and now split between those who hear real tenderness and those who hear a sophisticated joke. It is often cited as the moment IDM's technical ambition and its melodic warmth fitted together most tightly.","Begin with 'Girl/Boy Song' and follow only the strings the first time, then only the drums. After that, listen to '4' and notice how the melody never hurries while the percussion races. Compare Nancarrow's Studies for Player Piano, another composer who used a machine to play faster than hands can."],"aphex-twin-windowlicker":["A six-minute track that begins as a slinky, almost R&B groove, with a lazy swung beat and a honeyed synth line, then gradually warps. James's own voice, pitched and sliced until it sounds like a woman cooing, floats in and out. Midway the drums start to stutter, glitch and fold back on themselves, and the smooth surface curdles into something wonky and unsettling before sliding back into the groove.","Released as a single on Warp in 1999, it arrived with a long video by Chris Cunningham that parodied the conventions of hip-hop videos, pasting James's leering face onto a troupe of dancers. The video became a fixture of late-night music television. James had spent the late 1990s trading more and more on his public persona, and this was the peak of that absurdist image-making.","Put the end of the track through a spectrogram, a picture of sound frequencies, and a spiral appears; the B-side, titled with a mathematical formula, hides an image of James's face the same way. So the music contains pictures only a computer can see. The warped vocal is not a sampled singer but James himself, transformed.","It became one of the most widely heard pieces of experimental electronic music of its decade, helped by the video. Some critics hear a satire of commercial R&B; others hear a straightforward love of it bent through James's sensibility. The hidden images have become part of internet folklore, sometimes overshadowing the track itself.","Listen once without the video, following the drums as they move from smooth to stuttering and back. Then watch Cunningham's film. For the lineage, hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the machine-made groove this is partly descended from, and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock."],"aphex-twin-avril-14th":["Two minutes of solo piano: a gentle, rocking left hand and a short, tender melody that repeats with small variations and then simply stops. There are no drums, no synthesizers and no processing. The sound is close and slightly muffled, with the mechanism of the instrument audible, as if you were sitting inside the piano. It sits on the double album Drukqs among some of James's most violent drum programming, which makes its quiet all the more striking.","Drukqs, released by Warp in 2001, alternated frantic electronic tracks with pieces for prepared piano and for a Yamaha Disklavier, a piano that can be played by a computer. James programmed the notes rather than performing them, then recorded the real instrument. Many tracks on the album carry Cornish-language titles, and James said the release was hurried along after he left an MP3 player full of unreleased music on a plane.","Because a machine is playing, the dynamics are unusually even and the rubato is written in, not felt, which gives the piece its slightly uncanny, music-box quality. Listen for the soft thuds of the dampers and keys, left prominent in the mix. Kanye West built his 2010 song 'Blame Game' on this music, which introduced it to millions who had never heard of Aphex Twin.","Drukqs was received coolly in 2001, often called overlong and unedited, but this short piano piece escaped the criticism and became James's most widely known composition, used in films, adverts and countless cover versions. Pianists argue over whether to play it with the flat evenness of the original or to add the expressive shaping it seems to invite.","Listen first on headphones to hear the mechanism. Then play Satie's first Gymnopédie and notice the same rocking accompaniment and unhurried melody. Nancarrow's player-piano studies show the opposite use of the same idea, a machine playing what no human could."],"autechre-amber":["Eleven long instrumentals of slow, heavy, softly lit electronic music. The beats are there but restrained, often a muffled kick and a clicking pattern at walking pace; above them hang mournful, slowly shifting synthesizer chords. 'Foil' opens with a clanking rhythm that seems to be searching for its own groove, and 'Nil' closes with a long, warm, sad drift. The overall colour is exactly what the title suggests: something ancient, glowing and preserved.","Rob Brown and Sean Booth, two friends from Rochdale near Manchester, had grown up on electro and hip-hop mixtapes before Warp signed them. Their early tracks appeared on the label's 1992 'Artificial Intelligence' compilation, which framed a new kind of electronic music for home listening. Amber was their second album, and its sleeve showed an eroded rock landscape in Cappadocia in Turkey, a picture of slow geological time.","The rhythms are more complex than they first sound: several tracks layer patterns of different lengths so that the downbeat quietly drifts. Listen also for how often a track stays on one or two chords for its whole length, letting texture rather than harmony carry the change. The hip-hop breakbeat, the duo's first love, is still audible, but slowed down and blurred.","Amber was well received but overshadowed at the time by Aphex Twin's more flamboyant releases. It has since become the Autechre album most often recommended to newcomers, precisely because their later, harder abstraction has not yet arrived. Fans debate whether it is their warmest record or merely their least characteristic one.","Play 'Montreal' and 'Silverside' first; both balance melody and machinery perfectly. Listen in one go, as an album, in the evening. Compare Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works 85–92 for a brighter version of the same moment, and Kraftwerk's Computer World for the source of the clean electronic pulse."],"autechre-tri-repetae":["Harder and colder than Amber: ten tracks of metallic, echoing percussion and spare synthesizer lines, the sound of machinery in an empty hall. 'Dael' opens with a lopsided, clanging rhythm that keeps promising to settle; 'Clipper' is a long, grinding study in a single repeated figure; 'Rotar' and 'Leterel' pare the melodies down to a few notes. The music is repetitive in the way a factory is repetitive, but it keeps slightly changing shape.","Recorded in 1995 as Brown and Booth were acquiring more hardware and taking their studio sound in a more industrial direction, it came out on Warp that November. In America the album was expanded with two EPs as Tri Repetae++. British electronic music was splitting into factions, and Autechre were placing themselves firmly on the side of structure rather than the party.","The title suggests threefold repetition, and repetition with tiny changes is the method: listen for the moment a percussion sound shifts in timbre or position while everything else stays still. The reverb is part of the composition, not decoration; many tracks are as much about the space around the sounds as the sounds themselves.","It is now regarded by many fans and critics as Autechre's finest work of the 1990s, the point where they left dance-music conventions behind without yet going fully abstract. Some listeners find it austere and airless; others hear a hard, physical kind of beauty. It is often compared with the process music of Steve Reich.","Play it loud on good speakers rather than headphones so the reverb fills the room. Start with 'Dael' and follow one percussion sound through the whole track. Then listen to Reich's Piano Phase, where the same idea of gradual shift inside repetition is played by human hands."],"autechre-confield":["Nine tracks in which rhythm stops behaving like rhythm. Beats accelerate, decelerate, jam and splinter; sounds bounce around like objects in a closed box. 'VI Scose Poise' opens with a clean, almost pretty melody that is then broken apart by stuttering percussion, and 'Pen Expers' is a relentless, lurching pattern that never repeats exactly. There are no loops in the ordinary sense; the music feels as if it is thinking in real time.","By 2001 Brown and Booth were building their own software environments, using programs such as Max to create systems of rules that generated rhythms and textures as they played. They performed and recorded with these systems, adjusting parameters rather than writing notes. The laptop had just become a viable instrument, and the album arrived at the height of glitch and computer music's crossover into rock and pop circles.","Many of the patterns were not written but generated: the duo set conditions, such as how one drum should respond to another, and let the machine decide. That is why no two bars are quite the same. It is the same basic idea as Xenakis's use of mathematics or Cage's chance operations, arrived at from the dancefloor rather than the conservatory.","Confield divided listeners sharply. Some reviewers called it unlistenable, others the most radical record Warp had released. It is now widely seen as a turning point after which Autechre stood closer to the avant-garde of Stockhausen and Xenakis than to techno. The debate over whether it is music to feel or only to admire continues.","Start with 'VI Scose Poise', whose opening melody gives you something to hold. Treat the rest like a piece of contemporary chamber music rather than a dance record. Pair it with Xenakis's Persephassa or Stockhausen's Kontakte to hear the older lineage of rule-built sound."],"boards-of-canada-music-has-the-right-to-children":["An hour of slow, warm, slightly out-of-tune electronic music built on lazy hip-hop beats, wobbling synthesizer melodies and fragments of children's voices. 'Roygbiv' is a short, bright, instantly memorable tune; 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun' and 'Aquarius' rest on thick, soft drums; 'Olson' is a brief wistful fragment. Everything sounds as if it had been left in the sun on an old tape, the pitch wavering as the tape stretches.","The Scottish brothers Michael Sandison and Marcus Eoin had worked privately for years in the Pentland Hills near Edinburgh before Warp and the smaller Skam label released this in 1998. Their name comes from the National Film Board of Canada, whose educational films they watched as children during time spent in Canada. The album arrived just as sampling technology made it easy to rebuild the sound of old recordings.","The wavering pitch is deliberate: the brothers ran material through tape and old equipment to mimic the wow and flutter of worn cassettes and film soundtracks, so the music sounds like memory. Several tracks contain voices from children's television and documentary sources, and the sleeve's faded family photograph has the faces bleached out. The unease beneath the nostalgia is easy to miss on first hearing.","Critics praised it immediately, and it has grown into one of the most influential electronic records of its era, a source for chillwave, hauntology and much lo-fi hip-hop. Some listeners hear pure comfort; others find it quietly disturbing, a childhood recalled slightly wrong. That mixture of innocence and menace remains the thing people argue about.","Start with 'Roygbiv', then 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun'. Listen for the pitch drift in held notes, the signature of the whole record. Then hear Delia Derbyshire's The Dreams and her Doctor Who theme: the BBC Radiophonic Workshop's sound of 1960s and 70s British childhood is the ghost this album is chasing."],"boards-of-canada-geogaddi":["Twenty-three tracks, many under two minutes, that take the warm sound of the first album into darker territory. 'Music Is Math' sets a slow, heavy beat under a queasy, detuned melody and a child's voice counting; 'Dawn Chorus' is sunlit and hazy; 'Gyroscope' is claustrophobic. Short interludes of drones and voices connect the longer pieces, so the whole plays like a single, unsettled dream.","The brothers spent about four years making it in their rural studio, releasing it on Warp in early 2002 after a long near-silence. By now their first record had a devoted following that pored over every detail, and they responded with an album full of hints, numbers and references to nature, mathematics and the occult, seemingly designed to be decoded.","The album runs sixty-six minutes and six seconds, and that is no accident. It is full of patterns drawn from spirals, the golden ratio and the Fibonacci sequence, and listeners have found fragments that carry reversed voices. Titles such as 'A Is to B as B Is to C' point to proportion. The childlike surface hides a record about order, belief and fear.","Received warmly, though some reviewers found it too long and too uniform. Its reputation has risen as listeners have taken its darkness seriously, and it is often named alongside the debut as their best work. The decoding culture it inspired online, with fans mapping every number, is now part of how the record is known.","Listen straight through with no shuffle; the interludes matter. Start by noticing the running time, then listen to 'Music Is Math' for the way a simple beat becomes ominous. Pair it with Laurie Spiegel's Kepler's Harmony of the Worlds for older music built from mathematical ideas about nature."],"boards-of-canada-tomorrows-harvest":["Seventeen tracks that sound like the soundtrack to a lost science-fiction film about the end of the world. The warm nostalgia of earlier records gives way to bleak, pulsing synthesizer lines, cold arpeggios and long, desolate drones. 'Reach for the Dead' builds from a slow, sad theme to a heavy, rolling beat; 'Cold Earth' and 'Jacquard Causeway' are grim and hypnotic. The mood is of a deserted landscape after something terrible has happened.","Released on Warp in 2013 after eight years without an album, it was announced through an elaborate puzzle: a secret record slipped into a shop on Record Store Day, then numerical codes broadcast on radio, television and online that fans had to combine to unlock the release date. The hunt became a news story in its own right, a sign of how much mystique the brothers had built by saying almost nothing.","The palette is deliberately borrowed from late-1970s and early-1980s film scores, the synthesizer music of John Carpenter and Vangelis, a new source for the brothers. Titles hint at a story of famine, surveillance and collapse. The sleeve shows a city skyline bleached almost white, as if seen through heat haze or something worse, and the tracks move like scenes cut from a film you never see.","Critics welcomed it as a strong return, if less surprising than their early records. Some found it too consistent in mood; others heard their most coherent album, a single sustained vision. It is often described as a landscape record for an age of ecological anxiety, and it drew many younger listeners back to the brothers' earlier work.","Play it in one sitting, preferably while travelling or at dusk, and begin with 'Reach for the Dead'. Notice how rarely the beat arrives, and how much weight it carries when it does. Then hear Vangelis's Blade Runner score, the earlier synthesizer language this album echoes in spirit, and Tangerine Dream's Rubycon."],"squarepusher-feed-me-weird-things":["The debut: long tracks in which frantic, hyper-edited jungle breakbeats run under fluid, jazzy electric bass lines and glowing synthesizer chords. 'Squarepusher Theme' sets a funky, walking bass line over drums that race and stutter; 'Tundra' is spacious and melancholy, all cold pads and distant beats. The combination of a real bassist's phrasing and a programmer's impossibly fast drumming is what makes it unlike anything else of its year.","Tom Jenkinson, a young bass player from Essex who had grown up on jazz fusion and rave, was signed to Rephlex, the label co-founded by Aphex Twin. The album came out in 1996, when drum and bass was at the height of its fame and jazz was seeping into it from several directions. Warp signed Jenkinson soon afterwards, and the record has since been reissued there.","The bass is played, not programmed; listen for slides, ghost notes and small timing variations that no machine would produce, set against drums placed with inhuman precision. Jenkinson's debt to Jaco Pastorius, the fusion bassist of Weather Report, is audible in the singing, melodic lines high on the neck. The whole record is a duet between a human and a sequencer played by the same person.","It was received as a striking debut and an unusual meeting of jazz musicianship and electronic production. It is often ranked just behind his next record, though some fans consider its rawness more exciting. The question of whether its jazz is homage or parody has never quite been settled, and jazz critics have mostly left it alone.","Start with 'Squarepusher Theme' and follow only the bass, then go back and follow only the drums. Then hear Jaco Pastorius's solo debut and Weather Report's Heavy Weather to find the source of that fluid, singing bass tone, and Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album for the drum side of the family."],"squarepusher-hard-normal-daddy":["Jazz fusion played through a rave sound system: glossy electric-piano chords, virtuoso bass runs and drum programming so fast and detailed it seems to be improvising. 'Beep Street' opens with a bright, looping groove before the breaks begin to fracture. Other tracks slow into dreamy, 1970s-flavoured passages of keyboard harmony. It is the most playful and melodic of his early records.","His first album for Warp, released in 1997, it arrived as drill 'n' bass was briefly fashionable in British electronic music. Jenkinson was still in his early twenties, working alone at home, programming every drum hit and playing the bass parts himself. The record drew heavily on the fusion of the 1970s: Miles Davis's electric bands, Weather Report and Herbie Hancock.","The keyboard chords are voiced like a 1970s fusion record, rich and extended, while the drums are chopped from breakbeats at a speed no drummer could play. That collision between two decades is the whole joke and the whole beauty. Listen for moments when bass and drums lock into unison, a flash of perfect coordination between a human and a machine.","It is often cited as Squarepusher's best record and one of the high points of drill 'n' bass. Critics in 1997 enjoyed its humour; later listeners have taken its musicianship more seriously. Jazz critics have been more sceptical, hearing pastiche rather than real improvisation, and that argument sits at the heart of how he is judged.","Start with 'Beep Street'. Listen for the bass first, then the drums, then how they fit together. Compare Miles Davis's Bitches Brew, where the same fusion textures were improvised by a band in a room, and the Mahavishnu Orchestra for the speed and sheen."],"plaid-not-for-threes":["Bright, intricate, cheerful electronic music: chiming synthesizer melodies, clicking and popping rhythms, interlocking patterns that sound like mechanical toys. Björk sings on 'Lilith', a slow, dark and breathy song, and other guest voices appear, but most tracks are instrumental. Compared with Autechre, Plaid are melodic and warm; compared with Aphex Twin, they are tidy and precise, like a small chamber group of synthesizers.","Ed Handley and Andy Turner had been two-thirds of The Black Dog, a pioneering British home-listening techno group, before concentrating on Plaid. Not for Threes, released in 1997, was their first album for Warp and arrived in the label's richest period. They had already worked with Björk, which is how she came to sing on this record, a sign of how closely IDM and adventurous pop were then connected.","Many of the rhythms are built from overlapping patterns of different lengths, so the accent shifts from bar to bar, a technique shared with Steve Reich and with West African drumming. Listen for how the melodies are handed between synthesizer voices like instruments in a chamber group, each taking a phrase and passing it on.","It was well received, though Plaid have always stood somewhat in the shadow of their Warp colleagues. Many listeners now regard them as one of the most consistently melodic groups in IDM, and this album as their breakthrough. Critics sometimes find them too polite; admirers answer that craft and charm are rare in the genre.","Start with 'Lilith' for Björk, then move to the instrumental tracks and follow a single melody as it passes around. Compare Björk's Homogenic, released the same year, and Reich's Drumming, where the interlocking-pattern idea is played by live percussionists on bongos and marimbas."],"mu-ziq-lunatic-harness":["Big, lush, hummable synthesizer melodies over furiously edited jungle drums. 'Hasty Boom Alert' sets a soaring, almost orchestral tune against breakbeats chopped to shrapnel; other tracks are slower and more wistful, with long string-like pads. Where much IDM is cool and cerebral, this is emotional and even bombastic, closer to a film score played over a drum-and-bass rhythm section.","Mike Paradinas, a London producer, had been releasing records since the early 1990s and was a friend and sometime collaborator of Aphex Twin. Lunatic Harness appeared in 1997, when drill 'n' bass was briefly in fashion. Paradinas had founded the label Planet Mu in 1995, which he later ran independently; it became one of the most important homes for electronic music, from IDM to Chicago footwork.","Paradinas often builds melodies from a few very simple notes, repeated and harmonised in thick chords, which is why they are so memorable. Listen for how the melody stays steady and slow while the drums race around it. The drums themselves are edited with enormous care: every hit is placed by hand.","Reviewers in 1997 saw it as a highlight of the drill 'n' bass moment, and it has kept a strong reputation. Later listeners sometimes find it overripe; fans find it more emotionally direct than anything else in the genre. Paradinas's influence as a label owner has sometimes overshadowed his own music.","Start with 'Hasty Boom Alert'. Listen to the melody first and treat the drums as a storm around it. Then hear Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album, from the previous year, to compare two very different temperaments working in the same style."],"four-tet-rounds":["Warm, intricate music built from acoustic samples: plucked harps, bells, acoustic guitar, hand percussion, snatches of jazz drumming, all chopped and looped on a laptop. 'She Moves She' and 'My Angel Rocks Back and Forth' are gentle and intimate; 'Spirit Fingers' is jittery and bright; 'As Serious as Your Life' is propulsive. The record feels handmade, like electronic folk music played by one person at a kitchen table.","Kieran Hebden, a London musician who also played guitar in the post-rock band Fridge, made Rounds on a laptop at home and released it on Domino in 2003. The word 'folktronica' was coined partly to describe his sound, a label he disliked. Laptop production had just made it possible for one person to assemble a whole record from fragments of acoustic instruments.","Many of the drum patterns are sampled from jazz records and re-edited so they stumble and swing unpredictably; Hebden is a devoted fan of free and spiritual jazz. Listen for how acoustic sounds are looped with slight irregularities so they never quite repeat. The title 'As Serious as Your Life' borrows the title of Valerie Wilmer's classic book on free jazz.","Rounds was widely praised and has come to be seen as one of the defining electronic albums of the 2000s. Some critics found it too pretty; many musicians cite it as a model for the warm, sample-based electronic music of the following decade. Its reputation has only grown as the folktronica tag has faded.","Start with 'She Moves She' and listen for the harp and the loose jazz drums underneath. Then hear Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda for the harp and spiritual-jazz mood Hebden loves, and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the shimmer of many small patterns at once."],"four-tet-there-is-love-in-you":["A gentler, more dance-oriented record than Rounds. 'Angel Echoes' opens with a wordless female vocal fragment looped and layered into a shimmering chorus; 'Love Cry' is a nine-minute build over a steady, patient beat; 'Sing' and 'Plastic People' drift and pulse. The acoustic samples are still there, but now set to a house tempo, with long, gradual arcs of change.","By 2010 Hebden had become a club DJ with a residency at the London venue Plastic People, and his music had moved closer to the dancefloor. He had also begun collaborating with Burial, and the ghostly treatment of voices on this record shows that exchange. It was released on Domino in January 2010, just as British club music was dissolving the line between home listening and dancing.","'Love Cry' takes its title from an Albert Ayler album, a signal of the free-jazz devotion behind the calm surface. Listen to 'Angel Echoes' for how a single short vocal fragment is turned into a whole choir by layering and pitch-shifting. 'Plastic People' is named after the club where much of this music was first tested on a crowd.","It was widely praised as his most accomplished record, a bridge between his earlier home-listening music and the dancefloor. Some fans missed the rougher edges of Rounds; others heard a new maturity and patience. It is often credited with helping bring gentle, emotional house music back into critical favour after a decade of harder club sounds.","Start with 'Angel Echoes', then 'Love Cry'. Let 'Love Cry' play through without skipping: its change is slow, and the reward is in the last minutes. Compare Burial's Untrue for the same ghostly vocal technique used in a darker way, and Caribou's Swim from the same year."],"burial-burial":["Dark, rainy, sub-bass-heavy music at a slow dubstep tempo, full of crackle, hiss and fragments of distant voices. 'Distant Lights', 'Southern Comfort' and 'Night Bus' sound like south London at night: rain, empty streets, a half-heard song from a passing car. The beats are loose and shuffling rather than tight, and the bass is felt as much as heard.","William Bevan made the album in south London and released it in 2006 on Hyperdub, the label founded by the producer Kode9. He stayed anonymous for the first years of his career, refusing interviews and photographs until a tabloid threatened to unmask him in 2008, when he posted a picture of himself online. Dubstep was emerging from London's underground garage scene, and Burial was its most mournful voice.","Bevan built the tracks in a simple sound-editing program rather than a sequencer, placing sounds by eye without a grid, which is why his beats feel loose and human. The vinyl crackle and rain are part of the composition, not decoration. He has said that some of the clicks and textures came from video games he played.","The album was widely praised and placed high on many critics' lists for 2006. It was quickly seen as a defining document of dubstep, though Burial has always stood at an angle to the genre. Some critics hear pure melancholy; others hear a lament for the rave culture of the 1990s that Bevan was too young to have fully known.","Listen at night, ideally while travelling through a city, on headphones with decent bass. Start with 'Distant Lights', then 'Night Bus'. Compare Eno's Ambient 4: On Land, an earlier record that also turns a remembered place into sound, and Radiohead's Kid A for another dark turn of the same period's mood."],"burial-untrue":["Burial's second album brings voices to the front: fragments of R&B singing pitched up and down until they sound genderless and ghostly, floating over shuffling two-step garage rhythms and deep bass. 'Archangel' is the best-known track, a lovelorn vocal loop over a limping beat; 'Near Dark' and 'Etched Headplate' are darker and more fragmentary. The whole album sounds like someone walking home alone after a night out.","Released on Hyperdub in November 2007, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2008, and the attention sent tabloids after Bevan's identity. Dubstep was by now a recognised genre, and Untrue took its bass and space but mixed them with the swing of UK garage, a sound Bevan had loved as a teenager through an older brother's records and pirate radio.","The vocal samples come from R&B and pop recordings, but they are so transformed that most are nearly impossible to identify; the pitch-shifting makes men sound like women and women like children. The beats are placed by hand, without a grid, so the rhythm has a subtle, human drag. Listen for the sound of lighters, rain and footsteps between the drums.","Untrue was widely acclaimed and is now often named among the most important electronic albums of the century. Its influence runs through British electronic music, pop production and even film scoring. Critics still debate whether it is a dance record, an ambient record or something else entirely, and whether its nostalgia is for a real scene or an imagined one.","Start with 'Archangel' and listen for how the vocal loop bends and returns. Then play the album straight through on headphones late at night. Arvo Pärt's Cantus in Memoriam Benjamin Britten makes a surprising companion: another piece that turns grief into a descending line repeated until it becomes weather."],"jon-hopkins-immunity":["An hour-long arc from dense, pounding techno to quiet piano. 'Open Eye Signal' and 'Collider' are long, relentless, distorted tracks with a physical, almost crushing weight; the second half turns inward with 'Abandon Window' and 'Form by Firelight', where slow piano chords emerge; the title track closes with a hushed song sung by the Scottish songwriter King Creosote. The shape is like a night out and the long walk home.","Jon Hopkins, a classically trained pianist from south-west London, had played keyboards for Imogen Heap and worked alongside Brian Eno before making Immunity, released on Domino in 2013. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize that year. Home studios now allowed a single producer to make music with the weight and detail of a full band, and Hopkins used that freedom to shape a whole album as one piece.","Many of the percussion sounds began as everyday noises recorded in and around Hopkins's studio, struck and handled objects processed until they hit like drums. The album was conceived as a single journey, and tempo and intensity are carefully mapped across its hour. The piano in the second half is played softly with the pedal held down, so the notes blur into each other.","Immunity was one of the most acclaimed electronic records of its year, and many listeners came to it from outside dance music. Some critics found its dramatic arc too polished; others heard a model of how to make an electronic album that works as a whole. It is often credited with bringing techno to audiences who otherwise heard ambient and modern classical music.","Play it through in one go, ideally loud, and notice the shift in the middle from heavy to gentle. Start with 'Open Eye Signal' if you can only hear one track. Then compare Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for the stillness of the closing piano, and Eno's Apollo for the ambient side of Hopkins's education."],"jon-hopkins-singularity":["A sequel in shape to Immunity: a first half of massive, surging techno and a second half that dissolves into ambient drift and piano. 'Emerald Rush' rides a bright, rising synthesizer figure over a thudding beat; 'Everything Connected' is long, dense and euphoric. Then 'Feel First Life', sung by a small choir, wordless and hymn-like, opens the quiet half, and the closing piano pieces are slow and luminous.","Hopkins wrote the album after years of interest in meditation and altered states of consciousness, and presented it as music about connection between mind and world. It was released on Domino in 2018, five years after Immunity had made him one of the best-known British electronic musicians. By then the border between electronic music and contemporary classical composition had become very porous, and he moved freely across it.","Hopkins has described the album as beginning and ending on the same note, so the record forms a closed loop and can be played round and round. Listen for how the choir in 'Feel First Life' is treated almost like a synthesizer, the voices held and blended until the words no longer matter. The two halves mirror each other in length and shape.","It was widely praised, though some critics found it too close in design to Immunity. Many listeners regard its first half as among the most powerful electronic music of the decade, while the choral and piano pieces divided opinion between those who heard depth and those who heard spa music.","Play 'Emerald Rush' loud, then 'Feel First Life' quietly, and notice how the album earns its calm. Compare Ligeti's Lux Aeterna, a choral piece of pure, suspended sound, and Pärt's Summa for the plainness of the closing mood. Then go back to Immunity and hear how the same two-part plan has grown bigger."],"oneohtrix-point-never-replica":["Short loops cut from television adverts of the 1980s and 1990s, slowed, repeated and set against drifting synthesizer chords and a few piano notes. The title track is a slow, melancholy piano loop that circles like a memory you cannot quite place; 'Andro' is dense and hazy; 'Sleep Dealer' is a stuttering collage of breaths and syllables. Old commercial music, made to sell, becomes strangely sad and uncanny.","Daniel Lopatin, working in Brooklyn, released the album in 2011 on Software, the label he had co-founded. He built much of it from compilations of old TV adverts, hunting through them for fragments of music and speech. Internet culture was then preoccupied with nostalgia for late-twentieth-century commercial media, and Replica gave that preoccupation a serious artistic form, halfway between ambient music and collage.","The adverts are chopped so finely that their products are almost never named; you hear a breath, a vowel, a scrap of jingle. Listen for how a loop repeats just long enough to become hypnotic, then changes. The name Oneohtrix Point Never is itself a found sound: a pun on the frequency of a Boston radio station Lopatin grew up hearing.","Replica was widely praised and is now often seen as an important work in the history of sample-based ambient music, and a key record of what critics called hypnagogic music. Some found it too clever by half; others heard a genuine meditation on memory and consumer culture, the way advertising lodges in us like folk song.","Start with the title track and follow the repeated piano fragment as it shifts. Then hear Steve Reich's Different Trains, where recorded speech becomes melody, and Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children for an earlier record about media memory."],"oneohtrix-point-never-r-plus-seven":["Glassy, fragmented music built from the stock sounds of digital instruments: synthetic choirs, bell tones, bright plucked presets. Tracks such as 'Boring Angel', 'Zebra' and 'Still Life' move abruptly between ideas, cutting from a sustained chord to a burst of arpeggios to sudden silence. The result feels like a gallery of digital surfaces, beautiful and slightly hollow, with no beat to hold on to.","Lopatin's first album for Warp, released in 2013, it moved away from the sample collages of Replica towards a deliberately artificial palette. He was increasingly working with visual artists and on film, and the album shares their interest in the look and feel of digital technology: screensavers, stock imagery, the default settings of software. It marked his move from the underground into wider view.","Many sounds are the factory presets that come with software and keyboards, the very sounds most musicians avoid as clichés. Lopatin uses them precisely because they are cheap and familiar, and frames them so they sound strange and even holy. Listen for the abrupt cuts that break any sense of flow; the silences are part of the composition.","It was widely praised, though some listeners found it cold and arch. It is now seen as a key record in experimental electronic music's turn towards digital surfaces, and as the bridge to Lopatin's later work in film and pop. Whether its detachment is critique or pleasure is still argued.","Start with 'Zebra' and listen for the cuts, then 'Boring Angel' for the synthetic choir at its most hymn-like. Treat the silences as part of the music. Compare Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM, another record that treated digital sound as its subject, and Laurie Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for an earlier, gentler computer music."],"fennesz-endless-summer":["Warm guitar melodies and chords, some almost like pop songs, dissolving into digital static, crackle and glitch. 'Made in Hongkong' is all shimmering digital fizz; the title track sets a sunny, sad guitar figure against gathering distortion; 'Caecilia' is slow and glowing. The record sounds like a summer memory being slowly eaten by the machine that stores it, the tune always just visible through the noise.","Christian Fennesz, a Viennese guitarist, made the album on a laptop with software such as Max, processing his guitar as he played. It was released in 2001 on Mego, the Vienna label at the centre of glitch music, where most artists worked with pure digital noise. The title borrows from a Beach Boys compilation, and the record is full of affection for 1960s Californian pop.","Beneath the noise, many tracks rest on simple, pretty guitar chord sequences that could come from a Beach Boys song, and the melodies are allowed to surface for only a few seconds at a time. Listen for the moments when the static clears and the guitar is heard clean. The digital errors are deliberate and carefully shaped, like a painter's scumble.","Endless Summer was widely praised and is now seen as a landmark of glitch and laptop music, the record that showed noise could be tender. It persuaded many rock listeners that computer music could be warm. Some hear nostalgia; others hear loss, and the album seems designed to hold both at once.","Start with the title track and listen for the guitar under the noise. Then hear the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations for the harmonic world Fennesz is remembering, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the idea that melody can live inside distortion."],"fennesz-venice":["Denser and more mournful than Endless Summer: slow washes of guitar and noise that swell and recede like water against stone. Most tracks are instrumental, ranging from quiet, glowing drones to storms of distortion that break and ebb. David Sylvian sings on 'Transit', the album's one song, his low voice floating over a shimmering, unstable backdrop. The effect is of a city seen through mist.","Released in 2004 on the British label Touch, known for its sound-art and field-recording catalogue, the album took its name from the city where some of the material was developed. Fennesz was by now collaborating widely, with Sylvian and with Ryuichi Sakamoto among others, and his processed guitar had become one of the most recognisable sounds in experimental music.","Listen for how single guitar chords are stretched and smeared so that they last for minutes, the attack of each note removed so it seems to fade in from nowhere. The city of the title is present less as a picture than as a condition: water, reflection and slow decay. Venice has a long history of music written for its spaces, from the Gabrielis to Luigi Nono.","It was well received, though inevitably measured against Endless Summer. Many listeners prefer its darker, more enveloping tone, and 'Transit' is often singled out as one of the best meetings of song and glitch. Some critics found the instrumentals too similar to one another; admirers answer that the sameness is the point, a single weather system heard from different angles.","Start with 'Transit', then play the instrumentals in the dark at low volume. Compare Luigi Nono's La lontananza nostalgica utopica futura, another Venetian meditation in which a live instrument meets tape, and Eno's On Land for an earlier landscape of blurred sound."],"tim-hecker-harmony-in-ultraviolet":["Walls of distorted, luminous sound: chords that swell to overwhelming volume, then recede to fragile drones. The four-part 'Harmony in Blue' is a set of slow, glowing variations on a single mood; other tracks pile distortion onto soft, organ-like tones until they crack and fizz. The music is at once violent and tender, like looking at sunlight through a stained-glass window that is slowly shattering.","Tim Hecker, a Vancouver-born musician working in Montreal, released the album in 2006 on Kranky, the Chicago label that was home to much of North America's drone and ambient music. He was part of a wave of musicians who brought the textures of noise and shoegaze rock into ambient music, playing it at volumes Eno's ambient never intended.","Listen for the melodies buried under the distortion: often a simple, almost hymn-like chord progression is still audible beneath the roar. Hecker shapes the noise as carefully as a composer shapes an orchestra, so the swells have a clear architecture of build, peak and release. Some of the source sounds are organs and guitars, disguised almost completely.","It was widely praised and is often seen as the turning point in his career, the record where his style came fully into focus. Some listeners find it overwhelming or abrasive; others find it deeply moving. It helped establish loud, distorted ambient music as a genre in its own right.","Play it loud on speakers, not quietly in the background. Start with the 'Harmony in Blue' sequence. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères, another music of clustered, shimmering sound in which there is no melody, only colour and mass, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the rock side."],"tim-hecker-ravedeath-1972":["Built from recordings of a church pipe organ, processed into roaring, distorted drones and fragile, decaying chords. 'The Piano Drop' opens with an eruption of sound; the three-part 'In the Fog' is a long, slow storm; the 'Hatred of Music' pieces are bleak and hymn-like. The organ is recognisable throughout but transformed, like a church service heard through a broken radio.","Hecker recorded the organ in a single day in a church in Reykjavík, working with the producer Ben Frost, then processed the material at home in Montreal. The album was released on Kranky in 2011. Its cover shows students dropping a piano from a university roof in 1972, a picture of music as a physical object being destroyed, at a moment when music was dissolving into digital files.","Listen for moments when the organ's own sound breaks through the processing, a reminder of the stone room where it was captured. The title and cover point to a meditation on the end of music as a thing you could hold. The organ, the oldest machine in Western music, is here fed through the newest.","It was widely acclaimed and is often named Hecker's finest album and one of the key ambient records of its decade. Listeners from contemporary classical music have heard in it the massed organ sonorities of Ligeti and Messiaen; others hear the distortion of heavy rock. The argument over whether it is ambient at all is part of its fame.","Play it loud in a dark room and start with 'In the Fog I'. Compare Ligeti's Volumina, an organ piece of pure clustered sound, and Messiaen's L'Ascension for the organ as a vehicle of overwhelming awe. Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works Volume II is the gentler cousin."],"the-field-from-here-we-go-sublime":["Steady four-to-the-floor techno made from tiny fragments of pop songs, looped and layered into shimmering, blurred clouds. 'Over the Ice' and 'Sun & Ice' pulse with a gentle, hypnotic lift; tracks change slowly, adding and removing layers over six, eight, ten minutes. The kick drum never stops, but above it everything is soft focus, so the music is euphoric and dreamy at once.","Axel Willner, working in Stockholm, released the album on the Cologne label Kompakt in 2007. Kompakt was the centre of a soft, melodic kind of minimal techno, and Willner brought to it the washed-out textures of shoegaze rock and the looping methods of hip-hop producers. It was his debut album, and it made him one of the label's best-known artists almost overnight.","The loops come from pop and soul records but are so short, a fraction of a syllable, that the sources are almost unrecognisable; the title track lets its closing fragment, from a 1950s doo-wop hit, come briefly into focus. Listen for how the loops are set slightly out of step with one another, so they shimmer like light on water.","It was widely praised as one of the best electronic albums of its year and has remained a touchstone for melodic techno. Some listeners find it too repetitive; others hear a demonstration that repetition itself can be euphoric. Its influence on later ambient techno, and on producers such as Jon Hopkins, is widely acknowledged.","Start with 'Over the Ice' and listen for one loop at a time as it enters and leaves. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the same idea of pulsing, shimmering repetition made by players, and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for the machine pulse underneath."],"caribou-swim":["Liquid, off-kilter dance music with a soft human voice at its centre. 'Odessa' opens with a quirky, bouncing bass line and Dan Snaith's gentle falsetto; 'Sun' repeats a single word over a steadily building groove; 'Kaili' and 'Bowls' add horns, chimes and rattling percussion. The sounds seem to bend and melt as they play, as if heard underwater, while the beat stays firmly on the ground.","Dan Snaith, a Canadian with a doctorate in mathematics, had made psychedelic pop as Caribou and won Canada's Polaris Prize for Andorra. With Swim, released in 2010, he turned to the dance floor, influenced by nights in London clubs. He said he wanted dance music that sounded fluid, like water, rather than hard and mechanical, and he recorded much of it in his London home studio.","Many sounds are bent in pitch as they play, so notes slide rather than land, which is where the underwater feeling comes from. Listen for the small acoustic details that ride on top of the synthesizers: chimes, bells, breaths and struck metal. Behind the dance-floor surface the songs are about the end of relationships, sung without drama.","It was widely praised as his best record to that point and brought him a new audience in dance music. Some listeners missed his earlier psychedelia; others heard a songwriter finding the right form. It is often paired with Four Tet's album of the same year as a sign of electronica's warm turn.","Start with 'Odessa' and listen for the sounds that bend. Then play 'Sun' and notice how much comes from one repeated word. Compare Four Tet's There Is Love in You, from the same year, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for an earlier band that also made dance music out of looping, layered parts."],"caribou-our-love":["Warm, soulful house music about love, family and commitment. 'Can't Do Without You' builds a single repeated vocal phrase, pitched and filtered, into a surging, joyous climax; 'Silver' and 'All I Ever Need' are tender and slow; 'Second Chance' features the Canadian singer Jessy Lanza. The textures are warmer and rounder than on Swim, with R&B and garage in the rhythms.","Snaith made the album after becoming a father and after years of touring Swim, and he described it as a record about the people close to him. It was released in 2014, when his club side project Daphni had also taken him deeper into DJ culture. The album brought Caribou to festival headline slots and a much wider audience.","Listen for how 'Can't Do Without You' changes over four minutes with only a handful of elements: the vocal loop, a bass, a kick drum, a rising filter. The repetition is the meaning, a phrase said so often it becomes both obsession and devotion. Several tracks borrow the pitched-up vocal technique of UK garage.","It was widely praised and became his most popular record. Some critics found it softer and less adventurous than Swim; others heard the ideal balance of songcraft and club music. 'Can't Do Without You' became one of the best-loved dance tracks of its year, played equally in clubs and at weddings.","Start with 'Can't Do Without You' and listen to how the build is made from almost nothing. Then compare Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the ancestor of every euphoric electronic love song, and Burial's Untrue for a darker use of the same pitched vocal technique."],"the-winstons-amen-brother":["A brisk instrumental B-side built on a gospel tune, 'Amen', given a soul-band workout: organ and horns trade the melody over a busy, bright groove. It is pleasant, short and unremarkable until about halfway through, when everyone else drops out and the drummer, Gregory Coleman, plays four bars alone. Those six or so seconds of snare, kick and ride cymbal, with a skipping syncopation in the third and fourth bars, are the reason the record is known to millions who have never heard the rest of it.","The Winstons were a Washington, D.C. soul group led by Richard Lewis Spencer, and 'Amen, Brother' was the flip side of their gospel-tinged hit 'Color Him Father', released on Metromedia in 1969. Drum breaks like this were a normal bit of arrangement, a chance for the band to breathe before the last chorus. No one in the studio could have imagined that the break would be lifted off the vinyl, looped and chopped by machines that did not yet exist.","The break reached hip-hop producers in the mid-1980s through bootleg compilations of drum breaks, and by the early 1990s British producers were slicing it into single hits and rearranging them on samplers, then speeding it up far beyond anything a human drummer could play. Listen for the ghost notes on the snare and the loose, open ride cymbal: those small human irregularities are exactly what made it sound alive when chopped and re-sequenced.","For decades the record was a collector's footnote while its four bars became probably the most sampled drum passage in recorded music, the backbone of jungle and drum and bass. Neither Coleman, who died in 2006, nor Spencer received royalties for that use. In 2015 two British DJs raised a public fund of about twenty-four thousand pounds for Spencer, a gesture that made the break a standing case in arguments over sampling and credit.","Play it straight through once as a soul record, then wait for the break and count the four bars. Then put on Goldie's 'Terminator' or Shy FX's 'Original Nuttah' and try to find Coleman in there: sped up, reversed, stretched, reassembled. Compare James Brown's 'Funky Drummer', the other great donor of the sampling era."],"lee-perry-blackboard-jungle-dub":["Credited to Perry's house band, the Upsetters, this is a set of reggae rhythms stripped almost to the bone: bass and drums carry everything, while organ stabs, guitar chops, horn phrases and scraps of voice drop in and out as if someone were switching the lights on and off in a house. Echo trails hang in the air after each fragment. There are no songs here in the usual sense, only grooves being taken apart and put back together in real time.","By 1973 Kingston producers had been issuing instrumental 'versions' on the B-sides of singles for a few years, so sound-system selectors and DJs could toast over them. Perry, a veteran of Studio One and producer of the Wailers' early records, went further and made a whole album out of versions, working with King Tubby on the mixing. It appeared in a tiny Jamaican pressing, and is usually counted among the very first dub albums.","Early pressings reportedly carried a stark stereo mix, with instruments split hard between the two speakers, so on headphones the band seems to be playing in two different rooms. Notice how often the bass is left entirely alone: the mixing desk is being used as an instrument, and silence is one of its sounds. Several rhythms come from Perry's earlier productions, recycled into something unrecognisable.","Rarely heard outside Jamaica on release, the album was rediscovered through reissues and is now treated as a founding document of dub, alongside King Tubby's early work. Arguments continue about exactly who did what at the desk, Perry or Tubby, and which pressing is the true one; scholars of dub treat the question as partly unanswerable, since the music was made live in the mix.","Use speakers with real bass or good headphones. Pick one instrument, perhaps the organ, and follow when it is allowed in and when it is taken away; the drama is in the removals. Then hear Public Image Ltd's Metal Box or The Slits' Cut to see how quickly London's post-punks learned the lesson."],"king-tubby-dub-from-the-roots":["A set of dub mixes in which King Tubby takes finished reggae rhythms and rebuilds them at the mixing desk. Drums and bass form a steady floor; everything else, the offbeat guitar, a horn line, a piano figure, appears for a bar, is thrown into a long spring-reverb tail, and vanishes. The signature sound is a sudden sweep that thins the whole band to a tinny rattle before the bass drops back in with physical force.","Osbourne Ruddock ran a small studio in his home in Waterhouse, a poor district of Kingston, where he cut dub plates for his sound system and voiced singers over rhythms recorded elsewhere. He was an electronics repairman first, and he modified his equipment himself, including a mixing console bought from the Dynamic Sounds studio. Albums like this one were made quickly, for local sound systems and a small record market, not for posterity.","The sweeping effect that makes the band sound as if it were being sucked down a drain is a filter on Tubby's console: he could cut away everything below a chosen pitch and slide the cutoff by hand. He was reportedly also fond of slamming the spring reverb unit so it crashed like thunder. Listen for these two gestures; they are his fingerprints on almost every record he touched.","In the 1970s dub albums were cheap by-products and rarely reviewed. Since the 1990s writers on electronic music have treated Tubby as a figure comparable to the tape composers of Paris and Cologne, a claim sometimes pushed too far, since he worked inside a commercial, collaborative culture. Credits on his albums are notoriously loose, and the exact provenance of many rhythms is still argued over.","Start with any track and listen for the moments when the whole band thins out and then crashes back. Then play Stockhausen's Kontakte or Varèse's Poème électronique to hear, from a very different direction, the same idea: that the studio itself can compose. A good reissue with the original mono or early stereo mix is worth seeking."],"lee-perry-super-ape":["Dub at its thickest and most humid. Where earlier dub stripped rhythms bare, Super Ape piles them up: murmuring backing vocals, hand percussion, a thick swirl of phasing over the cymbals, horns that arrive muffled as if from another room. Voices chant and exhort in fragments. The record moves slowly and heavily, and its sound, a blurred, swampy haze with a bass line cutting through, is instantly recognisable as Perry's Black Ark studio.","Perry built the Black Ark behind his home in Washington Gardens, Kingston, around 1973–74, and by 1976 it was producing a stream of records on modest equipment: a four-track tape machine, a tape echo unit, a phaser. Island Records, flush with the international success of Bob Marley, released this album abroad, and its gorilla cover became an emblem of Perry's mythology. It was issued in Jamaica with a different title.","Much of the density comes from 'bouncing': Perry recorded several parts, mixed them down onto one track, and then recorded more on top, so each generation of tape adds a layer of hiss and blur. What sounds like an effect is partly the accumulated grain of his tiny studio. Listen also for the phaser on the hi-hats, a sweeping shimmer that became one of the Ark's trademarks.","Received abroad as a curiosity alongside Island's roots reggae releases, the album is now considered one of the summits of dub and a key text for the British musicians who were listening closely: punk and post-punk bands took both its sound and its attitude. Some reggae purists prefer Perry's leaner early work, but Super Ape is usually the record cited when producers talk about his studio as an instrument.","This is an album to play loud, at night, on a system with real low end. Listen for the way voices drift past like people in a crowd. Then hear The Clash's Complete Control, which Perry produced the following year, to hear the Ark's reach across the Atlantic."],"augustus-pablo-king-tubby-meets-rockers-uptown":["Augustus Pablo's melodica, a humble school instrument that sounds like a wheezing harmonium, floats over slow, heavy rhythms from his Rockers label, while King Tubby mixes them into dub. The mood is sombre and eastern-tinged, built on minor modes. The title track, a dub of Jacob Miller's song 'Baby I Love You So', is the best-known dub in the world: a stately drum pattern, a bass that seems to walk on the floor, and Miller's voice surfacing and sinking.","Horace Swaby took the name Augustus Pablo and a melodica sound he called 'far east' in the early 1970s. He ran his own small label, Rockers, and brought his rhythms uptown to Tubby's studio for mixing. The album, compiled from dubs made over the preceding years, was issued in 1976, when roots reggae and Rastafarian devotion were at their height in Kingston and reggae was beginning to reach London and New York.","The drums on the title track are among the most imitated in reggae, but listen to the space: Tubby repeatedly lets the reverb swallow the snare, so each backbeat is followed by a long, fading room. Miller's vocal is never allowed a whole line; it is a ghost. And the melodica is often doubled with organ, which is why it sounds larger than a small reed instrument should.","Initially a specialist release, the album has become the dub record most often recommended to newcomers, and appears on nearly every list of essential reggae. Some critics find its reputation tilts attention toward the title track at the expense of the moodier pieces around it. Its sound fed directly into British sound-system culture and, through it, the bass-heavy music of the 1990s and 2000s.","Start with the title track, then listen to the whole album in one go as a single mood. Follow the bass rather than the melody. Then play Mala in Cuba or Digital Mystikz's 'Anti-War Dub' to hear how London's dubstep producers kept this weight and space thirty years on."],"scientist-rids-the-world-of-the-evil-curse-of-the-vampires":["Ten dubs of hard, spare dancehall rhythms played by the Roots Radics band, mixed by the young engineer Scientist with a comic-horror theme. Creaking doors, howls and cackles are scattered through the tracks, but underneath is serious mixing: rimshot snares ricochet in echo, the bass is round and enormous, and instruments are cut in and out with a drummer's timing. Each track is named after a horror episode, such as a plot of zombies or a mummy.","Scientist, Overton Brown, trained at King Tubby's studio as a teenager and became its leading engineer at the turn of the 1980s, when the slower, rootsy style of the 1970s was giving way to dancehall. Producer Henry 'Junjo' Lawes supplied the rhythms. The London label Greensleeves issued a series of themed Scientist albums, with lurid comic-book covers, aimed at a British market that bought dub avidly.","The horror sound effects are a joke, but the rhythms are not: they are the same Roots Radics tracks that carried hit vocal records by singers of the day, so Jamaican listeners would have recognised the songs behind the dubs. Listen for how Scientist uses the echo on the snare to create a second, off-kilter rhythm that answers the real one.","Long dismissed as a novelty, partly because of its cover and title, the album is now respected as a showcase of early-dancehall dub mixing and as Scientist's best-known work. Debate over who deserves credit for the golden age of Tubby's studio, the master or his gifted engineers, often centres on records like this.","Enjoy the silliness, then listen past it to the drums and bass. Compare it with King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown: here the rhythms are tighter, drier, faster, and the echo is sharper. The comic-book cover is worth seeing on vinyl."],"4hero-mr-kirks-nightmare":["An early breakbeat hardcore track from North London: fast, jittering sampled breakbeats, a rubbery bass line, and a stark spoken-word sample in which a police officer tells a father that his son has died of an overdose. The effect is unsettling, a dance track that repeatedly stops to deliver bad news. Synthesizer stabs and rave sirens keep it moving.","4hero, Mark Clair and Dego McFarlane, co-founded Reinforced Records in Dollis Hill around 1989–90, when the acid-house rave boom was mutating in Britain into something faster and more breakbeat-driven. Pirate radio stations and warehouse raves were the channels; tracks circulated on white labels. This record was a hit in that underground and a template for hardcore's darker turn.","The drug narrative made the record controversial as a pro- or anti-drug statement; in fact it is ambiguous, and its bleakness points ahead to the 'darkside' hardcore of 1992–93 from which jungle grew. Listen for the way the breakbeat is chopped and rearranged rather than simply looped, an early sign of the techniques Reinforced's artists, including Goldie, would push much further.","Treated at the time as a rave anthem, it is now cited in histories of hardcore and jungle as one of the first records to put darkness and narrative into the music. 4hero themselves moved on to the jazz-inflected Parallel Universe and then broken beat, and their early hardcore is sometimes overshadowed by that later work.","Play it loud and notice how often the music is interrupted: the groove builds, the spoken scene cuts in, and the dancefloor has to wait. Listen for the break being re-sequenced rather than looped. Then hear Goldie's 'Terminator', also on Reinforced, to hear the breakbeat pulled further out of shape two years later, and Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for the electro ancestor."],"goldie-terminator":["Released under the name Metalheads (a Rufige Kru production), 'Terminator' is a track in which the drums seem to melt. Sampled breakbeats are sped up, slowed down and phased against each other while staying in time, so a snare roll suddenly stretches and warps like tape pulled through a machine, then snaps back. Dark synthesizer pads and film-derived vocal fragments set a sci-fi mood. It sounds like a drummer being bent by the forces of a black hole.","Goldie, a graffiti artist from the Midlands who had spent time in New York's hip-hop scene, fell in with the Reinforced Records crowd in London in the early 1990s. New samplers and computer software allowed time-stretching: changing a sample's speed without changing its pitch, or vice versa. Goldie and his engineers used the technique not to hide the process but to make it the point, in the thick of London's hardcore rave scene.","The warping sound is a side effect: early time-stretching algorithms produced a grainy, metallic smear when pushed hard, and Goldie used that artefact deliberately. Listen closely to the snare in the passages where it shifts: the timbre changes too, becoming more metallic and shimmering. That digital flaw became one of drum and bass's signature textures.","A landmark for producers rather than the public, it is widely credited as the first record to make time-stretching an expressive device, and it moved hardcore toward the darker, more intricate sound of jungle. Some historians caution that several producers were experimenting with the technique simultaneously; Goldie's record is the one that made it famous.","Ignore the pads at first and focus on the drums alone: wait for the moments when a snare roll stretches, goes metallic and snaps back into time. Those bends are the whole point. Then play the Winstons' 'Amen, Brother' to hear the kind of raw material being bent, and Goldie's Timeless to hear where the technique went three years later."],"origin-unknown-valley-of-the-shadows":["A jungle track that feels like drifting through a dark corridor. A spoken sample, a woman describing a near-death experience of travelling through a long dark tunnel, repeats over a sparse, rolling breakbeat, a deep sub-bass and wide, ghostly synthesizer pads. The track builds and releases without drama; it is patience and atmosphere, not aggression, that carry it.","Origin Unknown was the partnership of Andy C and Ant Miles, from Essex; Andy C was a teenager when they started Ram Records in 1992. The track came out in the transitional year when hardcore rave was splitting into jungle and the more atmospheric strain soon called drum and bass. Pirate radio and club DJs built its reputation long before most listeners knew who had made it.","The spoken fragment is looped and repeated until its meaning slips into sound, a technique familiar from Steve Reich's early tape pieces. Listen to how the breakbeat underneath is kept surprisingly low in the mix: the vocal and pads carry the foreground, which was unusual in a scene obsessed with drums.","Still one of the most requested tracks in drum and bass, often cited as the scene's first atmospheric classic and reissued many times. Andy C became one of the genre's biggest DJs, and critics sometimes contrast the track's restraint with the louder, harder sound he later championed.","Listen on headphones in the dark and follow the vocal as it turns from speech into texture; by the fifth repetition you hear its rhythm more than its story. Then notice the sub-bass entering underneath. Compare Steve Reich's Different Trains for another looped voice, and LTJ Bukem's 'Horizons', which took this atmospheric direction and made it lush."],"shy-fx-original-nuttah":["Ragga jungle at full tilt. A dancehall-style MC, UK Apachi, chants and boasts over hyperkinetic chopped breakbeats at about 160 beats per minute, while a sub-bass line moves at half that speed, like a reggae bass line trapped inside a drum and bass track. Sirens, gunshot samples and rewind cues from the sound-system world punctuate the chaos. It is loud, joyful and aggressive at once.","Andre Williams, Shy FX, was a teenager from North London when he made this. In 1994 jungle was at the peak of its first wave, Jamaican sound-system culture fused with rave: MCs, dubplates and bass weight from reggae, breakbeats from hardcore. The track was a club and pirate radio phenomenon, and it became one of the records that brought jungle into the national press.","The layering is the key: the fast breakbeats and the slow bass line are really two tempos, one double the other, which is how jungle keeps a reggae body under a rave pulse. Listen for how the MC phrases his lines against the bass, not the drums, just as a Kingston toaster would over a version on a sound system.","Celebrated as a ragga jungle anthem and sometimes criticised at the time for its violent imagery, which Shy FX later described as part of the sound-system language rather than a literal message. The ragga strand fell out of favour with drum and bass tastemakers in 1995–96, but the track's reputation has only grown.","Turn it up and listen for the two tempos at once: dance to the bass or to the drums. Then compare Scientist's dubs to hear where the bass weight comes from, and Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner to hear where the MCing went next."],"basic-channel-quadrant-dub":["A long, hypnotic piece of dub techno: a muffled kick drum, a single chord stab that echoes into infinity, a haze of hiss, and slow filter sweeps that open and close the sound like breathing. Over many minutes almost nothing changes, and yet the chord's echoes pile up and shift so that the texture is never static. It feels like listening to a techno track through a wall, or underwater.","Basic Channel was the duo of Moritz von Oswald and Mark Ernestus, who ran the Hard Wax record shop in Berlin and a small group of labels. In the early 1990s, after reunification, Berlin techno was raw and industrial; Basic Channel combined it with the techniques of Jamaican dub, which both men loved, releasing plain-sleeved records that set a quiet, severe template.","The chords are processed with the same tools King Tubby used, delay and filter, but applied to a single repeated gesture rather than a band. Listen for the tiny pitch and timbre shifts in the echoes: most of the music happens in the decaying tails, not the notes themselves. Their mastering studio, Dubplates & Mastering, was also where the pair cut their vinyl.","Influential far beyond its sales, the track and its siblings founded dub techno as a genre and shaped minimal techno for decades. Critics sometimes debate whether the style's austerity became a formula in lesser hands; the originals are widely treated as untouchable.","Put it on while doing something else and notice the moment you start hearing the echoes rather than the beat; that shift of attention is how the piece works. Play it at moderate volume on speakers, not earbuds, so the hiss fills the room. Then play King Tubby's Dub from the Roots and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express to hear the two parents of this sound."],"goldie-timeless":["A double album that treats drum and bass as a medium for grand statements. It opens with the twenty-one-minute 'Timeless' suite: Diane Charlemagne's soaring soul vocal on 'Inner City Life' floats over lush strings and a frantic, intricately edited breakbeat, which then plunges into darker, heavier movements before returning. The rest of the album moves between jazz-inflected softness and aggressive, metallic drum programming. Everything is dense, cinematic and deliberately ambitious.","By 1995 jungle was becoming a national story in Britain, and Goldie, with his gold teeth and graffiti background, was its public face. He signed to London Records' FFRR imprint and worked with engineer Rob Playford for months in the studio. The album appeared when major labels were competing to find the jungle album that would cross over, as trip-hop and Britpop were doing.","The orchestral sounds are mostly samples and synthesizers, arranged with a film composer's attention; listen for how the strings in 'Timeless' move in slow, sustained chords while the drums race, a double tempo borrowed from dub and ragga jungle but turned toward the symphonic. Much of the drum editing was done hit by hit, not loop by loop.","Hailed as the album that proved jungle could be art, it reached the British top ten and is often called the genre's first great long-form work. Some in the scene felt it was overproduced and too eager for respectability; that argument over ambition versus dancefloor function has followed drum and bass ever since.","Begin with the 'Timeless' suite, all twenty-one minutes, and let it play through. Then sample the rest. Compare Roni Size's New Forms, the next attempt at a big statement, and Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient side of Goldie's ambition."]});